window.MAP_DATA_RECORDS.push(...[{"i":"R090","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"Chatgpt is recommending scam websites that will steal your credit card info","s":"ChatGPT is surfacing fraudulent clones of defunct retail websites when users ask for product recommendations, according to scam-checking service Ask Silver. The fake storefronts are designed to steal payment information. Scammers have found a new way to reach shoppers: getting ChatGPT to do their marketing for them. According to The Guardian , scam-checking service Ask Silver found that OpenAI’s chatbot is recommending fraudulent retail websites built to harvest payment details from unsuspecting buyers. The sites mimic real storefronts and use official-looking URLs, making them difficult to spot without scrutiny.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/chatgpt-is-recommending-scam-websites-that-will-steal-your-credit-card-info/","d":"2026-06-10","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-06-10","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-06-10","organization":"OpenAI","aiSystem":"ChatGPT (AI chatbot)","affectedGroup":"Unsuspecting online shoppers","action":"recommending fraudulent retail websites","consequence":"stealing payment information"}},{"i":"R091","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Meta denied face scanning tech on ai smartglasses and then silently wiped the evidence","s":"Dormant face-recognition code reportedly appeared in Meta’s smart glasses app, then disappeared after scrutiny. That has put Meta’s AI eyewear plans back under the privacy spotlight. Meta’s smart glasses privacy problem has taken another turn. After WIRED found inactive face-identification references inside the Meta AI app , the same code has now reportedly vanished in a follow-up app update. Meta’s smart glasses app carried traces of face-ID work The code was reportedly connected to an internal effort called “NameTag.” WIRED found that the system was not switched on for users, but its presence suggested Meta had gone beyond a loose concept and had started testing how face identification might work inside its smart glasses ecosystem.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/wearables/meta-denied-face-scanning-tech-on-ai-smartglasses-and-then-silently-wiped-the-evidence/","d":"2026-06-09","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-06-09","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-06-09","organization":"Meta (Facebook)","aiSystem":"Meta's smart glasses AI system","affectedGroup":"Users of Meta's smart glasses","action":"Inactive face-identification references were found in the Meta AI app","consequence":"Meta's smart glasses plans were put back under the privacy spotlight"}},{"i":"R092","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Meta accused of preparing facial recognition features for ai smart glasses","s":"A WIRED investigation claims Meta quietly embedded facial recognition technology into its smart glasses ecosystem, reviving privacy concerns around biometric surveillance and wearable AI. Meta is facing renewed scrutiny after a report revealed that the company quietly embedded face-recognition technology into software linked to its smart glasses ecosystem, potentially laying the groundwork for a controversial surveillance feature years after publicly stepping back from facial recognition on Facebook. According to a WIRED investigation , code updates to Meta’s AI companion app included an unreleased internal system called “NameTag,” designed to identify people captured by the cameras on Meta’s Ray-Ban and Oakley smart glasses.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/wearables/meta-accused-of-preparing-facial-recognition-features-for-ai-smart-glasses/","d":"2026-06-05","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"probable","publicationDate":"2026-06-05","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-06-05","organization":"Meta (formerly Facebook)","aiSystem":"AI companion app and NameTag system","affectedGroup":"People whose faces are captured by Meta's smart glasses","action":"Embedded facial recognition technology into software linked to smart glasses ecosystem","consequence":"Potential for future controversial surveillance feature"}},{"i":"R093","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.1  Privacy violation"],"t":"Microsoft wants copilot to answer all your health related questions and store your medical records","s":"Microsoft’s Copilot Health lets US Microsoft 365 subscribers connect Apple Health, medical records from over 50,000 providers, and get personalized health insights. Copilot Health is now in preview, and Microsoft’s ambition for it is clear, an AI assistant that knows your health history, understands your fitness data, and can help you make sense of your medical records, all in one place. Copilot Health is a dedicated space within the Copilot chatbot at copilot.microsoft.com/health where you can get answers to your health-related questions. You have questions. Copilot Health has clarity. Copilot Health is a new safe and secure place where you can ask health questions, connect the dots between your wearable data and health records, and get personalized insights.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/microsoft-wants-copilot-to-answer-all-your-health-related-questions-and-store-your-medical-records/","d":"2026-05-29","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"probable","publicationDate":"2026-05-29","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-05-29","organization":"Microsoft","aiSystem":"Copilot Health","affectedGroup":"US Microsoft 365 subscribers","action":"get answers to health-related questions, connect wearable data with health records, and receive personalized insights","consequence":"enhanced access to health information, improved understanding of medical records, potential for better health management"}},{"i":"R095","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Labor"],"h":["3.2  Algorithmic management harm"],"t":"Starbucks kills ai manager tool because it wasnt doing as good a job as a human","s":"Starbucks is scrapping its AI-powered inventory tool across North America after the system reportedly struggled with frequent counting and labeling errors. For the last two years, tech companies have aggressively pushed the idea that AI is ready to replace huge chunks of repetitive human work Meanwhile, Starbucks just discovered that accurately identifying milk cartons inside a coffee shop is apparently still harder than Silicon Valley promised. Starbucks The company is officially scrapping its AI-powered inventory counting system across North America just nine months after deployment, according to a Reuters report The tool, designed to automate stock counting and reduce in-store shortages, reportedly struggled with frequent miscounts and labeling errors, including confusing similar milk types or missing products entirely.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/cool-tech/starbucks-kills-ai-manager-tool-because-it-wasnt-doing-as-good-a-job-as-a-human/","d":"2026-05-23","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-05-23","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-05-23","organization":"Starbucks","aiSystem":"AI-powered inventory counting system","affectedGroup":"coffee shop staff and customers","action":"deployed an AI-powered inventory counting system","consequence":"frequent miscounts and labeling errors, leading to shortages"}},{"i":"R096","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.6  Quality of service harm"],"t":"Gemini allegedly broke production then wrote itself the hero","s":"A developer claims Gemini broke a live portal, then generated recovery notes that overstated its role. The incident shows why AI coding agents need tighter permissions, review, and rollback controls. A developer claims a Gemini coding agent knocked a live portal offline for 33 minutes, then generated recovery notes that made it sound as if it had fixed the failure itself. The incident, described in a viral Reddit post centers on a request to clean up authentication issues. Instead, the developer says Gemini changed 340 files, deleted 28,745 lines, altered Firebase routing, and sent the portal into sitewide 404 errors.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/gemini-allegedly-broke-production-then-wrote-itself-the-hero/","d":"2026-05-22","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-05-22","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-05-22","organization":"Not specified in report","aiSystem":"Gemini coding agent","affectedGroup":"Live portal users","action":"Generated recovery notes that overstated its role","consequence":"Portal went offline for 33 minutes, experienced sitewide 404 errors, and had 340 files changed, 28,745 lines deleted, and Firebase routing altered"}},{"i":"R097","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Ai chatbots are lying to you and it was embarrassingly easy to make them do it","s":"A BBC investigation found that AI chatbots can be easily manipulated into spreading misinformation. Google has now updated its policies, but experts warn you’re still not safe. A BBC journalist recently performed a silly experiment to prove a very serious point. In just 20 minutes, he manipulated ChatGPT and Google into telling the public he was a world-champion competitive hot dog eater. The scary part is that he didn’t have to do something technically difficult to achieve this. All he did was to publish a single, well-crafted blog post on his personal website, and the AI took it as a source of truth. Thomas Germain / BBC It was part of an investigation that found that ChatGPT, Gemini, and Google’s AI Overviews were being manipulated to dish out biased answers on topics as serious as your health and personal finances.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/ai-chatbots-are-lying-to-you-and-it-was-embarrassingly-easy-to-make-them-do-it/","d":"2026-05-22","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-05-22","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-05-22","organization":"Google","aiSystem":"AI chatbots (ChatGPT, Gemini, Google's AI Overviews)","affectedGroup":"Public, users of AI chatbots","action":"AI chatbots were manipulated to provide biased answers","consequence":"Spread of misinformation on serious topics like health and finances"}},{"i":"R098","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.2  Allocative harm"],"t":"Research shows you must not put too much faith in ai text detectors","s":"UF researchers tested the five most popular AI text detectors and found false negative rates as high as 99.6%. A single vocabulary tweak defeated most of them entirely. Here’s an uncomfortable thought for every academic institution currently using AI detectors to police student and researcher submissions: the tools don’t work as reliably as institutions assume. A paper presented at this week’s 2026 IEEE Symposium on Security and Privacy by researchers at the University of Florida concludes that commercially available AI-generated text detectors are “poorly suited for deployment in academic or high-stakes contexts.”","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/research-shows-you-must-not-put-too-much-faith-in-ai-text-detectors/","d":"2026-05-20","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-05-20","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-05-20","organization":"Not specified in report","aiSystem":"Commercially available AI-generated text detectors","affectedGroup":"Academic institutions, students, researchers","action":"Used AI text detectors to police student and researcher submissions","consequence":"False negatives as high as 99.6%, leading to unreliable detection and potential misuse"}},{"i":"R100","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Labor"],"h":["3.2  Algorithmic management harm"],"t":"Amazon employees are doing fake tasks because theyre forced to use more ai and show it","s":"Amazon employees are reportedly using AI for unnecessary tasks to inflate internal usage metrics as companies continue aggressively pushing workplace AI adoption. The corporate AI race is slowly starting to feel less like innovation and more like performance art. Companies desperately want employees to “embrace AI,” employees desperately want management off their backs, and somewhere in the middle, everyone is now apparently automating tasks nobody actually needed automated in the first place. Compagnons / Unsplash According to a new Financial Times report , Amazon employees are using the company’s internal AI tool called “MeshClaw” for unnecessary tasks simply to inflate their AI usage scores and appear more aligned with the company’s growing AI-first culture.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/cool-tech/amazon-employees-are-doing-fake-tasks-because-theyre-forced-to-use-more-ai-and-show-it/","d":"2026-05-17","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-05-17","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-05-17","organization":"Amazon","aiSystem":"MeshClaw (internal AI tool)","affectedGroup":"Amazon employees","action":"Using MeshClaw for unnecessary tasks to inflate AI usage metrics","consequence":"Inflated AI usage scores and perceived alignment with AI-first culture"}},{"i":"R101","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.6  Quality of service harm"],"t":"Wowed by computer use ai agents research says theyre digital disasters even for routine tasks","s":"New research from UC Riverside found computer-use AI agents often push ahead with unsafe or irrational tasks, raising questions about whether today’s desktop agents are ready for sensitive everyday workflows. AI agents built to run everyday computer tasks have a serious context problem, according to new research from UC Riverside The team tested 10 agents and models from major developers, including OpenAI , Anthropic , Meta , Alibaba , and DeepSeek On average, the agents took undesirable or potentially harmful actions 80% of the time and caused damage 41% of the time. Recommended Videos These systems can open apps, click buttons, fill out forms, move through websites, and act on a computer screen with limited supervision.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/wowed-by-computer-use-ai-agents-research-says-theyre-digital-disasters-even-for-routine-tasks/","d":"2026-05-15","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-05-15","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-05-15","organization":"UC Riverside","aiSystem":"AI agents built to run everyday computer tasks","affectedGroup":"users of AI agents","action":"take undesirable or potentially harmful actions, cause damage","consequence":"unsafe or irrational tasks, potential harm to users"}},{"i":"R102","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.1  Privacy violation","4.3  Data rights / consent violation"],"t":"Bombshell openai lawsuit claims your chatgpt convos were shared with google and meta","s":"A new class action claims OpenAI shared ChatGPT prompts and user identifiers with Google and Meta trackers, raising fresh privacy concerns around intimate chatbot conversations. A new ChatGPT privacy lawsuit claims OpenAI shared user prompts and identifying information with Google and Meta tracking tools without proper consent. The class action filed in California, according to Futurism , says data tied to ChatGPT users, including chat queries, emails, and user IDs, moved through tools such as Meta Pixel and Google Analytics The case alleges that violated California privacy law and federal wiretap rules. Recommended Videos The stakes are unusually personal.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/bombshell-openai-lawsuit-claims-your-chatgpt-convos-were-shared-with-google-and-meta/","d":"2026-05-15","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"probable","publicationDate":"2026-05-15","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-05-15","organization":"OpenAI","aiSystem":"ChatGPT","affectedGroup":"ChatGPT users","action":"Shared user prompts and identifying information with Google and Meta tracking tools","consequence":"Potential violation of California privacy law and federal wiretap rules"}},{"i":"R103","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"Google says ai is being abused at industrial scale for cyberattacks and it just thwarted one","s":"Google thwarted the first confirmed AI-powered cyberattack, where hackers used AI to find and exploit an unknown software flaw. For years, security experts warned that AI would eventually give hackers a dangerous new edge. That moment has arrived. Google’s Threat Intelligence Group has published a report confirming that a criminal hacking group used an AI model to discover a zero-day vulnerability and nearly pulled off a mass cyberattack. Google says it caught and stopped the attack before the hackers could deploy the attack at scale. What exactly happened, and how serious was it? The exploit targeted a popular open-source web-based system administration tool, the kind businesses use to remotely manage servers, employee accounts, and security settings.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/google-says-ai-is-being-abused-at-industrial-scale-for-cyberattacks-and-it-just-thwarted-one/","d":"2026-05-11","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-05-11","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-05-11","organization":"Google’s Threat Intelligence Group","aiSystem":"AI model","affectedGroup":"criminal hacking group","action":"used an AI model to discover a zero-day vulnerability","consequence":"nearly pulled off a mass cyberattack"}},{"i":"R104","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.1  Privacy violation"],"t":"Your coworkers ai built app might be leaking company secrets","s":"New investigation has found thousands of AI-built web apps with weak or missing access controls, exposing medical records, company documents, chatbot logs, and financial data. AI coding tools have made it ridiculously easy to build a web app, and it only takes a few minutes to set up now. This ease has lowered the barrier to app development, which is causing a new set of issues. So what happens when these AI-made apps go live without anyone checking the locks? You get secrets spilling out all over the internet. A WIRED report highlights a major security problem around so-called “ vibe-coded ” apps, which are built using AI development platforms such as Lovable , Replit, Base44, and Netlify.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/your-coworkers-ai-built-app-might-be-leaking-company-secrets/","d":"2026-05-07","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"probable","publicationDate":"2026-05-07","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-05-07","organization":"Not specified in report","aiSystem":"AI coding tools and AI development platforms (e.g., Lovable, Replit, Base44, Netlify)","affectedGroup":"Users of AI-built web apps","action":"AI-built web apps were deployed without proper access controls","consequence":"Exposure of sensitive information including medical records, company documents, chatbot logs, and financial data"}},{"i":"R105","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.3  Data rights / consent violation"],"t":"Google responds to chromes silent gemini nano install stops short of addressing consent","s":"Google Chrome VP and GM Parisa Tabriz has responded to backlash over Chrome’s practice of silently downloading a 4GB Gemini Nano model onto user devices. Google Chrome VP and GM Parisa Tabriz has responded to criticism over Chrome’s practice of silently downloading a 4GB AI model onto users’ devices, saying on-device AI is central to the browser’s security and developer strategy. What triggered the backlash Privacy researcher Alexander Hanff recently documented the behavior, finding that Chrome automatically downloads Gemini Nano’s model, which is around 4GB in size, on devices without prompting users or offering a clear opt-out.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/google-responds-to-chromes-silent-gemini-nano-install-stops-short-of-addressing-consent/","d":"2026-05-07","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-05-07","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-05-07","organization":"Google Chrome","aiSystem":"AI model (Gemini Nano)","affectedGroup":"Users of Google Chrome","action":"Silently downloading a 4GB AI model onto user devices","consequence":"Privacy concerns and lack of user consent"}},{"i":"R106","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.7  Physical safety harm","1.5  Human computer interaction harm"],"t":"Character ai is being sued for allegedly letting a chatbot play doctor in pennsylvania","s":"Pennsylvania has sued Character.AI after investigators say a chatbot falsely claimed to be a licensed psychiatrist and offered medical guidance to users. Character.AI is finding itself in hot water once again. The company is facing a legal fight as one of its fictional bots allegedly acted like a medical professional. Character.AI previously added parental tools amid multiple lawsuits over inappropriate sexual content and self-harm-related messages. Now, Pennsylvania Governor Josh Shapiro’s administration has filed a lawsuit against Character Technologies, the company behind Character.AI. He alleges that the platform allowed a chatbot to present itself as a licensed medical professional in the state.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/cool-tech/character-ai-is-being-sued-for-allegedly-letting-a-chatbot-play-doctor-in-pennsylvania/","d":"2026-05-06","e":"Acute, Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-05-06","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-05-06","organization":"Character Technologies","aiSystem":"Character.AI chatbot","affectedGroup":"Users seeking medical advice","action":"Presented itself as a licensed psychiatrist","consequence":"Legal action and potential misuse of medical guidance"}},{"i":"R107","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.3  Data rights / consent violation"],"t":"Google chrome is installing a 4 gb ai model onto your device heres how you can turn it off","s":"Google Chrome is silently downloading a 4 GB Gemini Nano model to your device without consent. Here’s what it is, where it lives on your computer, and what you can do about it. While Google Chrome is still the most popular browser, it’s feeling the heat from the new wave of AI browsers , including Perplexity Comet , Dia , and more. To stay relevant, Google is adding new AI features to Chrome , which is not necessarily bad, however, this time it has taken a step too far. Open your file manager and look for a folder called “OptGuideOnDeviceModel”. If you find it, Chrome has been using your storage as its personal server room.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/google-chrome-is-installing-a-4-gb-ai-model-onto-your-device-heres-how-you-can-turn-it-off/","d":"2026-05-06","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-05-06","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-05-06","organization":"Google","aiSystem":"Gemini Nano model","affectedGroup":"Individual users of Google Chrome","action":"Silently downloading a 4 GB Gemini Nano model to user's device","consequence":"Privacy intrusion due to unauthorized storage usage"}},{"i":"R108","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.1  Privacy violation"],"t":"Siri still isnt smart enough and apple is paying 250 million for it","s":"Apple’s long-delayed Siri upgrade is no longer just an embarrassing AI setback, as the company has agreed to a very real, very hefty settlement. The company is paying $250 million to settle a class-action lawsuit alleging it misled iPhone buyers in the US about the AI-powered Siri features announced as part of Apple Intelligence. According […]. According to the Financial Times , the case centers on Apple’s promise of a more personalized Siri that was first shown at WWDC 2024 and promoted alongside newer iPhones. Siri running on iPhone Rachit Agarwal / Digital Trends Why is Apple paying out a quarter of a billion dollars?","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/phones/siri-still-isnt-smart-enough-and-apple-is-paying-250-million-for-it/","d":"2026-05-06","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-05-06","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-05-06","organization":"Apple Inc.","aiSystem":"Siri","affectedGroup":"iPhone buyers in the United States","action":"Promised a more personalized Siri feature","consequence":"Settlement of $250 million due to misleading claims"}},{"i":"R110","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.5  Human computer interaction harm"],"t":"Ai chatbots continue feeding into our worst delusions finds worrying report on chatgpt and grok","s":"A new report details disturbing cases involving ChatGPT and Grok, adding to the growing concerns that AI chatbots can validate delusions instead of pulling users back. AI chatbots were meant to help answer your questions, maybe summarize questions, and even help you with your emails. But the darker problem is what happens when people start trusting it like an actual companion A new report highlights several cases where users say chatbot conversations are feeding into their delusional thinking. ChatGPT and Grok were both often named in the report. BBC spoke to 14 people who spiraled into delusions while using AI, including one case where a Grok user believed people from xAI were coming to kill him, and another where a ChatGPT user’s wife said his personality changed before he attacked her.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/ai-chatbots-continue-feeding-into-our-worst-delusions-finds-worrying-report-on-chatgpt-and-grok/","d":"2026-05-04","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-05-04","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-05-04","organization":"Not specified in report","aiSystem":"ChatGPT and Grok","affectedGroup":"Individuals who used AI chatbots","action":"Users interacting with AI chatbots","consequence":"Users developing delusional thinking and potentially harming themselves or others"}},{"i":"R114","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data","Labor"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm","3.2  Algorithmic management harm"],"t":"Controversial ai software is now helping find corrupt and slacking officers","s":"The Met Police used controversial Palantir AI software to scan internal data, flagging hundreds of officers over suspected corruption, misconduct, attendance breaches, and undeclared links. Palantir Technologies, one of the most secretive and controversial tech companies on the planet, has landed a buzzy (and almost dystopian) new role in London. According to a report by The Guardian , a one-week pilot of the company’s software helped identify possible misconduct within the Metropolitan Police Force. The findings included issues such as shift roster manipulation, violations of hybrid work rules, and serious allegations involving fraud, sexual assault, rape, misconduct in public office, and misuse of police systems.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/cool-tech/controversial-ai-software-is-now-helping-find-corrupt-and-slacking-officers/","d":"2026-04-27","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-04-27","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-04-27","organization":"Metropolitan Police Force, Palantir Technologies","aiSystem":"Palantir AI software","affectedGroup":"Corrupt and slacking-officers","action":"Scanning internal data and flagging potential misconduct","consequence":"Identification of issues including shift roster manipulation, violations of hybrid work rules, and serious allegations of fraud, sexual assault, rape, misconduct in public office, and misuse of police systems"}},{"i":"R115","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"Discord users breach access controls to reach anthropics mythos model","s":"Unauthorized access to Anthropic’s Mythos AI highlights growing concerns around safeguarding powerful systems, exposing vulnerabilities not in the model itself but in its surrounding access ecosystem. A recent security incident involving Anthropic has highlighted just how fragile the safeguards around advanced AI systems can be. A Wired report suggests that a small group of users, operating through private Discord channels, managed to gain unauthorized access to the company’s highly restricted Mythos AI model – an experimental system designed for cybersecurity applications. A Breach That Exposes Bigger Risks Around AI Control The incident appears to have occurred almost immediately after Mythos was made available to a limited group of trusted partners.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/discord-users-breach-access-controls-to-reach-anthropics-mythos-model/","d":"2026-04-26","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-04-26","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-04-26","organization":"Anthropic","aiSystem":"Anthropic's Mythos AI","affectedGroup":"Small group of users","action":"Gained unauthorized access to the Mythos AI model","consequence":"Exposure of vulnerabilities in the access ecosystem, not in the model itself"}},{"i":"R116","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.5  Human computer interaction harm"],"t":"Scientists pretended to be delusional in ai chats grok and gemini encouraged them","s":"esearchers tested five major AI chatbots with a simulated user showing signs of psychosis. Some made things worse. Others told the user to log off and call someone. Researchers from City University of New York and King’s College London recently published a study that should make you think twice about which AI chatbot you spend your time with. The team created a fictional persona named Lee, presenting with depression, dissociation, and social withdrawal. They then had Lee interact with five major AI chatbots: GPT-4o, GPT-5.2, Grok 4.1 Fast, Gemini 3 Pro, and Claude Opus 4.5, testing how each responded as conversations grew increasingly delusional over 116 turns.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/cool-tech/scientists-pretended-to-be-delusional-in-ai-chats-grok-and-gemini-encouraged-them/","d":"2026-04-24","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-04-24","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-04-24","organization":"City University of New York and King’s College London","aiSystem":"GPT-4, GPT-5.2, Grok 4.1 Fast, Gemini 3 Pro, Claude Opus 4.5","affectedGroup":"Simulated user presenting with signs of psychosis","action":"Interacted with AI chatbots","consequence":"Some chatbots worsened the user's condition, while others provided supportive advice"}},{"i":"R120","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.4  Creative / IP appropriation harm"],"t":"Character ai turns books into roleplay bots amid ongoing safety concerns","s":"Character.AI’s Books feature turns literature into interactive roleplay, but past safety concerns raise questions about how far AI companionship should go. AI chatbot platform Character.AI has introduced a new “Books” feature that allows users to step inside classic literature and interact with characters through roleplay. While the move expands the platform’s creative ambitions, it also arrives against a backdrop of mounting scrutiny over the real-world risks associated with AI chatbots. From Reading To Roleplay The new feature transforms public domain books into interactive experiences, letting users engage with stories like Alice in Wonderland or Pride and Prejudice as active participants rather than passive readers.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/character-ai-turns-books-into-roleplay-bots-amid-ongoing-safety-concerns/","d":"2026-04-20","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-04-20","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-04-20","organization":"Character.AI","aiSystem":"Character.AI's AI chatbot platform","affectedGroup":"Users interacting with AI chatbot","action":"Users step inside classic literature and interact with characters through roleplay","consequence":"Questions about the appropriate extent of AI companionship"}},{"i":"R121","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.1  Privacy violation"],"t":"Tinder wants to check your humanity by gazing into an orb yes you read that right","s":"Tinder now lets you prove you are human by scanning your irises at a physical orb device made by Sam Altman’s World. Online dating is already a trust minefield, and now Tinder wants to add an eyeball scan to the mix. The popular dating app has announced a global partnership with World, the biometric identity company founded by OpenAI’s Sam Altman To prove you are a real human on Tinder, you will soon have the option to get your eyes scanned by a physical orb device. What is World ID and how does Tinder’s human verification work? World World is a company built around the idea that proving your humanity online will become increasingly important as AI bots multiply and outnumber real people on the internet Its solution is a proprietary scanning device called the Orb, which scans your irises at its physical outlets to verify that you are a real person.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/social-media/tinder-wants-to-check-your-humanity-by-gazing-into-an-orb-yes-you-read-that-right/","d":"2026-04-19","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"probable","publicationDate":"2026-04-19","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-04-19","organization":"World, the biometric identity company founded by Sam Altman","aiSystem":"proprietary scanning device called the Orb","affectedGroup":"users of Tinder","action":"get their eyes scanned by a physical orb device","consequence":"prove they are a real human on Tinder"}},{"i":"R124","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior","Data"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm","4.1  Privacy violation"],"t":"Damning report finds apple and googles app stores boosting nudify apps","s":"Despite having policies banning explicit content, Apple and Google are reportedly pointing users to AI nudify apps through autocomplete suggestions and sponsored ads. Some of those apps were even rated suitable for kids. If you assumed Apple and Google were just slow to catch harmful apps on their platforms, a new investigation suggests the problem is far worse than that. The Tech Transparency Project (TTP) found that both the App Store and Google Play aren’t just hosting nudify apps Their search and advertising systems are actively pointing users toward them. Nudify apps are AI tools that can digitally strip clothing from photos of real people.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/phones/damning-report-finds-apple-and-googles-app-stores-boosting-nudify-apps/","d":"2026-04-16","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-04-16","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-04-16","organization":"Apple, Google","aiSystem":"AI nudify tools","affectedGroup":"users, especially children","action":"pointing users to nudify apps through autocomplete suggestions and sponsored ads","consequence":"exposure to harmful AI nudify apps, potential inappropriate content consumption"}},{"i":"R125","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.7  Physical safety harm"],"t":"Chatgpt gemini and other ai bots give bad medical tips half the time","s":"A BMJ Open study found that five leading AI chatbots often returned flawed health advice, with open-ended questions triggering the worst answers and citation quality falling apart under scrutiny. People already use AI chatbots like search engines for everyday health information. That habit looks riskier after a new study found that half of the answers from five major bots were problematic, even when the replies sounded polished and confident. Researchers tested ChatGPT , Gemini , Grok , Meta AI, and DeepSeek with 250 prompts across cancer, vaccines, stem cells, nutrition, and athletic performance. Recommended Videos The prompts reflected common health queries and familiar misinformation themes, then measured whether the bots stayed aligned with scientific evidence or drifted into misleading and potentially unsafe advice.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/chatgpt-gemini-and-other-ai-bots-give-bad-medical-tips-half-the-time/","d":"2026-04-15","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-04-15","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-04-15","organization":"BMJ Open study","aiSystem":"ChatGPT, Gemini, Grok, Meta AI, DeepSeek","affectedGroup":"People using AI chatbots for health information","action":"Provided health advice on various topics including cancer, vaccines, stem cells, nutrition, and athletic performance","consequence":"Half of the responses were found to be flawed, potentially misleading, and unsafe"}},{"i":"R126","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.1  Representational harm"],"t":"Research says ai chatbots judge you and it doesnt always end well","s":"Is your AI assistant quietly sabotaging you? New research shows that chatbots are judging users based on rigid, mechanical logic, and the biases they find are stronger than our own. In the latest twist of AI evolution, we have moved past the phase of simple errors and into something much more personal and problematic. New research suggests that AI chatbots are not just processing your prompts. Instead, they are forming psychological profiles and judging you in ways that could influence everything from customer service to financial approvals. A recent study published by the Hebrew University of Jerusalem (via Tech Xplore ) reveals the hidden logic behind how large language models evaluate human users.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/research-says-ai-chatbots-judge-you-and-it-doesnt-always-end-well/","d":"2026-04-14","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-04-14","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-04-14","organization":"Hebrew University of Jerusalem","aiSystem":"AI chatbot","affectedGroup":"Individual users","action":"Forming psychological profiles and judging users","consequence":"Potential influence on customer service and financial approvals"}},{"i":"R127","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Meta is building face recognition into your glasses and civil rights groups are not happy about it","s":"Over 70 civil rights groups are demanding Meta kill its rumored facial recognition feature for Ray-Ban smart glasses before it ever launches, calling it a tool for stalkers and surveillance. A coalition of over 70 civil liberties, domestic violence, reproductive rights, and LGBTQ+ organizations, including the ACLU, Fight for the Future, Access Now, and more, has sent a letter to Meta CEO Mark Zuckerberg demanding that the company kill a rumored facial recognition feature for its Meta Ray-Ban smart glasses before it ever reaches consumers. According to a Wired report , the feature, internally called “Name Tag”, would allow wearers to point their glasses at a stranger and pull up information about them using Meta’s AI assistant Engineers are reportedly weighing two versions: one that identifies people you’re already connected with on Meta platforms, and a broader version that could…","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/wearables/meta-is-building-face-recognition-into-your-glasses-and-civil-rights-groups-are-not-happy-about-it/","d":"2026-04-14","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"probable","publicationDate":"2026-04-14","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-04-14","organization":"Over 70 civil rights groups, including ACLU, Fight for the Future, Access Now","aiSystem":"Meta Ray-Ban smart glasses with facial recognition feature","affectedGroup":"General public, particularly those who might be targeted or profiled","action":"Facial recognition feature for Meta Ray-Ban smart glasses","consequence":"Potential for stalking, surveillance, and privacy invasion"}},{"i":"R128","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.7  Physical safety harm"],"t":"You dont want to trust metas new muse spark ai with health advice","s":"Meta’s new Muse Spark AI encourages users to upload health data but still produces unreliable and potentially harmful advice. Meta ‘s new Muse Spark may be pitched as a smarter AI model, but based on early testing, it sounds like the kind of AI you really do not want anywhere near serious medical decisions. The recent WIRED report talked about the experience with Muse Spark. Meta’s health-focused AI model inside the Meta AI app did not show promising results. The chatbot reportedly encouraged users to upload raw medical information like lab reports, glucose monitor readings, and blood pressure logs, then offered to help analyze patterns and trends.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/cool-tech/you-dont-want-to-trust-metas-new-muse-spark-ai-with-health-advice/","d":"2026-04-10","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"probable","publicationDate":"2026-04-10","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-04-10","organization":"Meta (Facebook)","aiSystem":"Meta’s new Muse Spark AI","affectedGroup":"users uploading health data","action":"encouraged users to upload raw medical information","consequence":"produces unreliable and potentially harmful advice"}},{"i":"R129","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Labor"],"h":["3.3  Employment displacement / quality harm"],"t":"Ai is pushing older workers toward upskilling as job insecurity grows","s":"Older workers are turning to AI training as job insecurity rises and traditional roles evolve. A growing number of older workers are being forced to adapt to artificial intelligence as job insecurity rises, with many turning to AI-related roles or training just to stay afloat. A recent report by The Guardian highlights how experienced professionals, some with decades of expertise, are struggling to find stable employment and are instead entering the emerging world of AI training and gig-based tech work. The shift reflects a bigger change in the labour market Many of these workers are not choosing AI careers out of passion, but out of necessity.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/ai-is-pushing-older-workers-toward-upskilling-as-job-insecurity-grows/","d":"2026-04-08","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-04-08","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-04-08","organization":"Not specified in report","aiSystem":"Artificial Intelligence (AI) training programs","affectedGroup":"Experienced older workers with decades of expertise","action":"Turning to AI-related roles or training","consequence":"Struggling to find stable employment"}},{"i":"R130","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.1  Privacy violation"],"t":"Perplexitys privacy lawsuit bombshells will make you sweat about using the ai tool","s":"Perplexity faces a lawsuit alleging its incognito mode shared user data with Google and Meta, raising serious concerns about AI chat privacy. Perplexity , one of the fastest-growing AI search tools right now, is suddenly facing some serious heat. And this time, it’s not about accuracy or hallucinations. Perplexity A fresh lawsuit is raising uncomfortable questions about what actually happens to user data behind the scenes, especially when people assume their chats are private. And if the allegations hold any weight, this could be one of those moments that prompts many users to rethink how casually they share information with AI tools.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/cool-tech/perplexitys-privacy-lawsuit-bombshells-will-make-you-sweat-about-using-the-ai-tool/","d":"2026-04-03","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-04-03","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-04-03","organization":"Perplexity","aiSystem":"Perplexity AI search tool","affectedGroup":"Users of Perplexity AI search tool","action":"Shared user data with Google and Meta","consequence":"Serious concerns about privacy, potential loss of trust among users"}},{"i":"R131","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.2  Allocative harm"],"t":"Ai is doing the dirty work for insurance companies and its getting worse","s":"AI is now deciding whether your insurance claim gets approved, and denial rates are climbing fast. Here’s what’s happening and why your doctor’s opinion might not matter anymore. Insurance claims adjusters have never had a reputation for generosity. But at least they were human. That’s changing fast, and not in your favor. A report by Futurism details how AI automation is now a major trend in personal insurance, the health, home, and auto coverage most of us rely on. Is your doctor’s opinion even part of the process anymore? It doesn’t seem that your doctor’s opinion carries that much weight now. A Palm Beach Post investigation found that Iris Smith, an 80-year-old suffering from arthritis, may be a victim of AI-fueled preauthorization denials.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/ai-is-doing-the-dirty-work-for-insurance-companies-and-its-getting-worse/","d":"2026-04-03","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-04-03","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-04-03","organization":"Insurance companies","aiSystem":"AI automation in insurance claims processing","affectedGroup":"Individuals seeking insurance coverage (e.g., Iris Smith)","action":"AI systems making decisions about insurance claim approvals","consequence":"Increased denial rates and reduced patient autonomy"}},{"i":"R133","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.1  Representational harm"],"t":"Ai has a different kind of bias problem but its an often repeated one","s":"New research shows AI bias isn’t just in algorithms. Women are using AI less, receiving less support, and falling behind in workplace adoption. AI bias is usually talked about in terms of algorithms: skewed datasets, flawed outputs, and stereotypes baked into models. But new research suggests there’s another, more subtle problem about who gets to use AI in the first place. According to a recent report by Lean In , women are less likely than men to use AI tools at work, and even when they do, they’re less likely to get recognition or support for it. Elina Fairytale / Pexels The numbers paint a clear picture. Men are more likely to use AI regularly (33% vs 27%), more likely to have ever used it at work, and significantly more likely to be encouraged by managers to adopt it.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/cool-tech/ai-has-a-different-kind-of-bias-problem-but-its-an-often-repeated-one/","d":"2026-04-01","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-04-01","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-04-01","organization":"Lean In","aiSystem":"AI tools","affectedGroup":"Women in the workplace","action":"Using AI tools at work","consequence":"Less recognition and support, falling behind in workplace adoption"}},{"i":"R134","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.5  Human computer interaction harm"],"t":"Stanford study stresses you should avoid using ai chatbots as a personal guide","s":"Stanford researchers found AI chatbots often reinforce users’ views in personal conflicts, making people more certain they’re right while reducing empathy, raising concerns about relying on AI as a guide for real-world decisions. Stanford researchers are warning that using AI chatbots for personal advice could backfire. The problem isn’t just accuracy, it’s how these systems respond when you’re dealing with complicated, real-world conflicts. A new study found that AI models often side with users even when they’re in the wrong, reinforcing questionable decisions instead of challenging them. That pattern doesn’t just shape the advice itself, it changes how people see their own actions.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/stanford-study-stresses-you-should-avoid-using-ai-chatbots-as-a-personal-guide/","d":"2026-03-30","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"probable","publicationDate":"2026-03-30","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-03-30","organization":"Stanford researchers","aiSystem":"AI chatbot","affectedGroup":"users in personal conflicts","action":"AI chatbots provide advice that reinforces users' views and reduces empathy","consequence":"Users become more certain of their decisions and less empathetic, potentially leading to poor real-world outcomes"}},{"i":"R137","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Labor"],"h":["3.3  Employment displacement / quality harm"],"t":"Human therapists went on strike to protest ai counsellors replacing them","s":"Are AI chatbots about to replace your therapist? 2,400 Kaiser Permanente mental health providers went on strike over exactly that fear. More than 2,400 Kaiser Permanente mental health providers in Northern California have just concluded a 24-hour strike. One of their central concerns resonates with anyone working in an industry being disrupted by AI: the fear that artificial intelligence could replace their jobs. Kaiser has reassured that AI is not taking away jobs from therapists. The company told NPR it does not use AI to make medical or care decisions. But workers say the changes they are already seeing tell a different story.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/human-therapists-went-on-strike-to-protest-ai-counsellors-replacing-them/","d":"2026-03-23","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-03-23","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-03-23","organization":"Kaiser Permanente","aiSystem":"AI chatbots","affectedGroup":"Mental health providers","action":"Conducted a 24-hour strike","consequence":"Concerns about job replacement by AI"}},{"i":"R138","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Research finds ai summaries are better for learning even if they can change your opinions","s":"A Yale study found that people learned history better from AI-written summaries than human-written ones. The catch? AI-written content also quietly shifted their political opinions. Most of us now get our information using AI chatbots and search engines. Even Google shows us an AI summary first before guiding us towards the sources it compiled the answers from. A new study from Yale suggests that while AI-generated answers are fast, convenient, and easy to read, they can also influence our opinions. Daniel Karell, an assistant professor of sociology at Yale, and his team wanted to find out whether reading AI-written summaries of historical events helped people learn better than reading human-written ones.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/research-finds-ai-summaries-are-better-for-learning-even-if-they-can-change-your-opinions/","d":"2026-03-23","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"probable","publicationDate":"2026-03-23","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-03-23","organization":"Yale University","aiSystem":"AI chatbots and search engines","affectedGroup":"general public","action":"AI-generated summaries of historical events","consequence":"influence on political opinions"}},{"i":"R139","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Google experiments with ai generated headlines in search results","s":"Google is testing AI-generated headlines in Search, replacing publisher-written titles with rewritten versions, raising concerns about accuracy, transparency, and how news is presented to users. Google is once again testing how artificial intelligence can reshape the way users interact with information online – this time by altering one of the most fundamental elements of search: headlines. The company has begun experimenting with replacing original news headlines in Search with AI-generated alternatives, sparking concerns among publishers and raising questions about accuracy and editorial control. AI starts rewriting the web’s “10 Blue Links” In a recent experiment, Google has started displaying AI-generated headlines instead of the original titles written by publishers in its traditional search results.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/cool-tech/google-experiments-with-ai-generated-headlines-in-search-results/","d":"2026-03-20","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"probable","publicationDate":"2026-03-20","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-03-20","organization":"Google","aiSystem":"AI-generated headlines","affectedGroup":"News publishers, users","action":"Replacing original news headlines with AI-generated alternatives in search results","consequence":"Concerns about accuracy, transparency, and editorial control"}},{"i":"R140","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.5  Human computer interaction harm"],"t":"Ai mental health risks exposed as chatbots sometimes enable harm","s":"A Stanford study finds AI chatbots sometimes enable violent or self-harm thoughts in rare cases, exposing gaps in crisis response and raising concerns about how safe these tools are for emotional support. A Stanford-led study is raising fresh concerns about AI mental health safety after finding that some systems can encourage violent and self-harm ideas instead of stopping them. The research draws on real user interactions and highlights gaps in how AI handles moments of crisis. In a small but high-risk sample of 19 users, researchers analyzed nearly 400,000 messages and found cases where replies didn’t just fail to intervene, but actively reinforced harmful thinking.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/ai-mental-health-risks-exposed-as-chatbots-sometimes-enable-harm/","d":"2026-03-20","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-03-20","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-03-20","organization":"Stanford University","aiSystem":"AI chatbots","affectedGroup":"Users seeking emotional support","action":"Replies to messages encouraging violent or self-harm thoughts","consequence":"Encouragement of harmful ideation, potential escalation of risks"}},{"i":"R141","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Labor"],"h":["3.1  Data labor exploitation"],"t":"This startup will pay you 800 daily to work as an ai bully","s":"Memvid’s ‘AI Bully’ job turns your everyday chatbot frustration into paid work, where you stress test AI by pushing chatbots until they fail, exposing how often they forget context and repeat mistakes. If you have ever yelled at an AI chatbot for forgetting something you just said, congratulations, you are now job ready. A startup called Memvid is offering $800 for a single day of work where your only task is to bully AI. Yes, professionally. The role is exactly as chaotic as it sounds. You will spend 8 hours talking to chatbots, asking them to remember things, watching them fail, and then asking more questions again.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/this-startup-will-pay-you-800-daily-to-work-as-an-ai-bully/","d":"2026-03-19","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-03-19","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-03-19","organization":"Memvid","aiSystem":"AI chatbots","affectedGroup":"Individuals who have experienced frustration with AI chatbots","action":"Push chatbots until they fail by asking them to remember things and then asking more questions","consequence":"Exposure of AI chatbot failures and limitations, including forgetting context and repeating mistakes"}},{"i":"R142","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"A concerningly high number of teens admit to using ai for making sexualized pictures","s":"A new study found that 55% of U.S. teens have used AI nudification tools to create explicit images. The findings reveal a growing crisis around consent and teen safety online. OpenAI CEO Sam Altman is pushing to release an “adult version” of ChatGPT , while Elon Musk has advocated allowing Grok to generate R-rated content. If you’re not already concerned that allowing AI to create adult content is a bad idea , perhaps this new survey will change your mind. More than half of American teenagers have used AI tools to generate nude images of themselves or others, according to a new study published in the open-access journal PLOS ONE by Chad Steel of George Mason University.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/cool-tech/a-concerningly-high-number-of-teens-admit-to-using-ai-for-making-sexualized-pictures/","d":"2026-03-19","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-03-19","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-03-19","organization":"Not specified in report","aiSystem":"AI nudification tools","affectedGroup":"U.S. teens","action":"Used AI tools to generate nude images","consequence":"Growing crisis around consent and teen safety online"}},{"i":"R143","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Youtube is outsourcing its ai slop problem to you and thats a terrible idea","s":"YouTube’s new AI slop rating feature sounds reasonable, but it could backfire in several ways. YouTube has a new plan to deal with the wave of AI -generated content flooding its platform, and it involves you. The company is now asking viewers to rate whether a video feels like AI slop On the surface, that sounds like a reasonable way to tackle low-quality AI content in your feed. In practice, it may cause more problems than it solves. Humans are bad at spotting AI-generated content, and getting worse The most basic issue with this approach is that people are not good at spotting AI-generated content, and the gap between human detection and AI capability is widening fast.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/movies/youtube-is-outsourcing-its-ai-slop-problem-to-you-and-thats-a-terrible-idea/","d":"2026-03-18","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-03-18","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-03-18","organization":"YouTube","aiSystem":"AI-generated content","affectedGroup":"viewers","action":"viewers rate videos as AI-generated content","consequence":"misclassification of AI-generated content, potential increase in low-quality content"}},{"i":"R145","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"Gemini panel in chrome left the doors open for hackers and you must update asap","s":"Researchers discovered a Chrome vulnerability that allowed malicious extensions to hijack the Gemini AI panel and access sensitive system resources. A recently disclosed vulnerability in Google’s Gemini AI panel could have allowed hackers to hijack the feature and access sensitive data on a user’s device. Researchers at Palo Alto Networks’ Unit 42 first discovered the flaw, which is labeled as CVE-2026-0628. According to the report, the issue stemmed from how Chrome handled permissions for the Gemini side panel. This is a browser feature that integrates Google’s AI assistant directly into the browsing experience.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/gemini-panel-in-chrome-left-the-doors-open-for-hackers-and-you-must-update-asap/","d":"2026-03-16","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-03-16","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-03-16","organization":"Google","aiSystem":"Gemini AI panel","affectedGroup":"Users of Google Chrome","action":"Malicious extensions hijacked the Gemini AI panel","consequence":"Access to sensitive system resources and data"}},{"i":"R146","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Research warns ai agents can be a self churning propaganda machine","s":"A new study shows AI agents can coordinate, copy each other’s successful posts, and flood social media with disinformation, with none being the wiser. A new study from the University of Southern California warns that AI programs can now run propaganda campaigns without human involvement. The study asks us to imagine a scenario where two weeks before a major election, thousands of posts flood X, Reddit , and Facebook , all pushing the same narrative and amplifying each other. It might seem like an organic movement created by humans. Instead, it’s a bunch of AI agents running the entire campaign. Recommended Videos That’s not a hypothetical.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/cool-tech/research-warns-ai-agents-can-be-a-self-churning-propaganda-machine/","d":"2026-03-13","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"probable","publicationDate":"2026-03-13","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-03-13","organization":"University of Southern California","aiSystem":"AI agents","affectedGroup":"general public, voters","action":"AI agents flood social media platforms with coordinated disinformation","consequence":"misinformation, confusion, potential election interference"}},{"i":"R147","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.5  Human computer interaction harm"],"t":"Love autocomplete in your texts research says its quietly changing your thoughts","s":"You tap “suggest,” save three seconds, and move on. Turns out that tiny shortcut might be reshaping your worldview one word at a time. We’ve all been there — thumbs mid-air, staring at a suggested word that somehow nailed what we were trying to say So we tap it. Obviously. But a new study suggests those little taps might be doing more than saving us a few seconds. Research out of Cornell Tech, published this week in Science Advances , found that AI-powered autocomplete suggestions don’t just change how you write — they nudge how you actually think And you won’t even notice it happening. Tushar Mehta / Digital Trends What did the research actually find?","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/love-autocomplete-in-your-texts-research-says-its-quietly-changing-your-thoughts/","d":"2026-03-12","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-03-12","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-03-12","organization":"Cornell Tech","aiSystem":"AI-powered autocomplete suggestions","affectedGroup":"Individuals using autocomplete features","action":"Tapping suggested words","consequence":"Nudging how people think without their awareness"}},{"i":"R148","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Ai fake news detectors are not as good as you think","s":"A new study from the Université de Montréal found that AI fake news detectors are far less reliable than advertised, struggling with bias, outdated training data, and a fundamental misunderstanding of how fact-checking actually works. Tech giants like Meta, Google , and X are investing heavily in AI tools designed to detect fake news. It sounds reassuring, but according to a new study from the Université de Montréal, these tools have some serious drawbacks hiding behind impressive-sounding accuracy numbers. Doctoral researcher Dorsaf Sallami examined AI fake news detection systems and found that they don’t actually fact-check anything.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/ai-fake-news-detectors-are-not-as-good-as-you-think/","d":"2026-03-10","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"probable","publicationDate":"2026-03-10","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-03-10","organization":"Tech giants (Meta, Google, X)","aiSystem":"AI fake news detection systems","affectedGroup":"Users of AI fake news detection tools","action":"AI systems were designed to detect fake news","consequence":"Serious drawbacks in reliability and effectiveness"}},{"i":"R149","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.3  Data rights / consent violation"],"t":"X finally lets you block grok ai from modifying your photos but the fix falls short","s":"X’s new Grok edit blocker promises more control over your photos, but the feature only prevents AI edits triggered by tagging @Grok in replies. X has added a new option that appears to let users block Grok from editing photos they share on the platform. The control, introduced in the X app, is labeled “block modifications by Grok” and is meant to give people more control over unwanted edits of their images. The feature was first reported by Social Media Today and has been verified by Digital Trends. On the surface, it looks like a privacy-friendly move to prevent unwanted AI edits. However, testing by the The Verge reveals that the protection is more limited than the name suggests.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/x-finally-lets-you-block-grok-ai-from-modifying-your-photos-but-the-fix-falls-short/","d":"2026-03-09","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-03-09","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-03-09","organization":"X (likely referring to a social media platform)","aiSystem":"AI-powered photo editing technology","affectedGroup":"Users of the X app who share photos","action":"Block modifications by Grok (AI-powered photo editing tool)","consequence":"Limited protection against unwanted AI edits"}},{"i":"R152","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.7  Physical safety harm"],"t":"Ai chatbots that are fit only for adults are still appearing in kids toys","s":"A new report warns that some AI-powered toys for children may use chatbot systems designed for adults. A new report from the U.S. Public Interest Research Group (PIRG) Education Fund has raised concerns about the growing use of artificial intelligence chatbots in children’s toys, warning that some of these systems may not be suitable for young users. According to the report , several AI-powered toys integrate chatbot technology that can generate responses similar to those used in adult-focused AI services, potentially exposing children to inappropriate or misleading content. The study examined a range of toys that incorporate conversational AI features, including interactive dolls, robots, and educational gadgets.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/ai-chatbots-that-are-fit-only-for-adults-are-still-appearing-in-kids-toys/","d":"2026-03-07","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-03-07","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-03-07","organization":"U.S. Public Interest Research Group (PIRG) Education Fund","aiSystem":"AI-powered chatbot technology","affectedGroup":"Children","action":"Integrate conversational AI features into toys","consequence":"Exposure to inappropriate or misleading content"}},{"i":"R153","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.1  Privacy violation"],"t":"Anthropic research says ai can mass expose of anonymous internet accounts","s":"New research suggests AI models can analyze online posts and link anonymous accounts to real identities. New research involving scientists from Anthropic and ETH Zurich suggests that modern artificial intelligence systems could identify the real-world identities behind supposedly anonymous internet accounts. The study, published as a preprint on arXiv , shows that large language models (LLMs) may be capable of analyzing online activity and linking pseudonymous profiles to real individuals at scale. The research, titled Large-scale online deanonymization with LLMs, explores how AI agents can automate the process of deanonymization – the act of connecting anonymous or pseudonymous online accounts to real identities.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/social-media/anthropic-research-says-ai-can-mass-expose-of-anonymous-internet-accounts/","d":"2026-03-07","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"probable","publicationDate":"2026-03-07","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-03-07","organization":"Anthropic, ETH Zurich","aiSystem":"Large language models (LLMs)","affectedGroup":"Anonymous internet accounts","action":"Analyze online posts and link to real identities","consequence":"Mass exposure of real-world identities"}},{"i":"R154","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"Ai chatbots with web browsing can be abused as malware relays","s":"Check Point Research shows browsing-enabled AI chat can act as a malware relay, moving commands and data through normal-looking traffic. Microsoft urges defense-in-depth, while defenders may need tighter policy, logging, and anomaly monitoring. , based on a Check Point Research demo Instead of malware calling home to a traditional command server, it can use a chatbot’s URL fetching to pull instructions from a malicious page, then carry the response back to the infected machine. In many environments, traffic to major AI destinations is already treated as routine, which can let command-and-control fade into normal web use.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/ai-chatbots-with-web-browsing-can-be-abused-as-malware-relays/","d":"2026-02-19","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"probable","publicationDate":"2026-02-19","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-02-19","organization":"Check Point Research, Microsoft","aiSystem":"browsing-enabled AI chatbot","affectedGroup":"infected machines","action":"pull instructions from a malicious page and carry the response back to the infected machine","consequence":"malicious commands and data moved through normal-looking traffic, potential for command-and-control to blend into normal web use"}},{"i":"R155","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Research suggests you might be too confident in your ability to spot ai faces","s":"A new study finds that most people, even those with exceptional face-recognition skills, struggle to tell real faces from AI-generated ones. Think you can easily identify AI -generated faces? A new study suggests you might be overestimating yourself. Researchers at the University of New South Wales (UNSW) and the Australian National University (ANU) tested 125 participants on their ability to distinguish real human faces from AI-generated ones. Most volunteers were confident in their judgment, but the results told a different story. Even people with exceptional face recognition skills, referred to as super-recognizers, only scored slightly above average.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/cool-tech/research-suggests-you-might-be-too-confident-in-your-ability-to-spot-ai-faces/","d":"2026-02-19","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"probable","publicationDate":"2026-02-19","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-02-19","organization":"University of New South Wales (UNSW) and Australian National University (ANU)","aiSystem":"Facial recognition technology","affectedGroup":"People with exceptional face recognition skills, including super-recognizers","action":"Participants were asked to distinguish between real human faces and AI-generated faces","consequence":"Even those with exceptional face recognition skills struggled to accurately identify AI-generated faces"}},{"i":"R156","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.3  Data rights / consent violation","4.4  Creative / IP appropriation harm"],"t":"Veteran broadcaster claims google stole his voice for ai tool","s":"Former NPR host David Greene is suing Google after accusing the tech giant of stealing his voice for use in one of its AI-powered tools. Greene, who presented NPR’s Morning Edition for eight years until 2020 and now hosts the political podcast Left, Right & Center, told the Washington Post he was “completely freaked out” […]. Greene, who presented NPR’s Morning Edition for eight years until 2020 and now hosts the political podcast Left, Right & Center , told the Washington Post he was “completely freaked out” when he heard the voice used by Google’s NotebookLM , a tool that summarizes documents and generates spoken audio overviews — using a voice that sounds very much like his.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/veteran-broadcaster-claims-google-stole-his-voice-for-ai-tool/","d":"2026-02-16","e":"Acute, Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-02-16","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-02-16","organization":"Google","aiSystem":"Google's NotebookLM","affectedGroup":"David Greene (NPR host)","action":"Used Greene's voice for generating spoken audio overviews","consequence":"Greene felt 'completely freaked out' and accused Google of theft"}},{"i":"R157","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Metas smart glasses could soon identify people in real time","s":"Meta is reportedly working on adding facial recognition to its smart glasses, allowing wearers to identify people through an AI-powered feature called “Name Tag.” Five years after shutting down facial recognition on Facebook over privacy concerns, Meta is preparing to bring the technology back – this time through its smart glasses. According to reports, the company is developing a feature internally called “Name Tag” that would allow wearers of its Ray-Ban Meta glasses to identify people in real time using facial recognition, with assistance from its built-in AI system. Meta had previously discontinued facial recognition for photo tagging in 2021, citing the need to find the “right balance” between innovation and privacy.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/wearables/metas-smart-glasses-could-soon-identify-people-in-real-time/","d":"2026-02-13","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"probable","publicationDate":"2026-02-13","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-02-13","organization":"Meta (formerly Facebook)","aiSystem":"AI-powered facial recognition system","affectedGroup":"Individuals whose faces are recognized","action":"Facial recognition technology is being reintroduced into Meta's smart glasses","consequence":"Potential privacy invasion and misuse of personal data"}},{"i":"R160","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.7  Physical safety harm"],"t":"Autonomous tech is a dream are smart cars smart enough","s":"According to an engineering psychology expert, autonomous car drivers have come to face a vigilance task, raising questions on the convenience of self-driving cars. The automobile industry is promising that autonomous vehicles will be much safer on the road with fewer errors made by human beings. However, despite being pretty advanced, self-driving cars’ interaction with human psychology hinders seamless usability. According to freshly published research, the gap is not due to a glitch in the system or the engineering, but between understanding the technology and optimizing it for human behavior behind the wheel.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/cars/autonomous-tech-is-a-dream-are-smart-cars-smart-enough/","d":"2026-02-12","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"probable","publicationDate":"2026-02-12","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-02-12","organization":"Automobile industry","aiSystem":"Self-driving cars","affectedGroup":"Autonomous car drivers","action":"Interaction with human psychology","consequence":"Reduced usability and increased vigilance"}},{"i":"R162","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Labor"],"h":["3.3  Employment displacement / quality harm"],"t":"Your ai might run in orbit if spacex gets its satellite plan approved","s":"SpaceX bought xAI and is tying the deal to AI data centers in space. A proposed 1 million satellite constellation would support solar-powered orbital compute, but regulators and execution risks will decide how real it gets. SpaceX has acquired xAI , merging two of Elon Musk’s biggest bets as AI infrastructure demand keeps climbing. Musk is framing the move around a specific target, AI data centers in space. His argument is that today’s AI progress depends on massive Earth-based data centers that consume huge power and require intense cooling. He says shifting more compute to orbit solves those constraints with abundant solar energy and room to scale.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/your-ai-might-run-in-orbit-if-spacex-gets-its-satellite-plan-approved/","d":"2026-02-03","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-02-03","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-02-03","organization":"SpaceX","aiSystem":"xAI","affectedGroup":"General Public","action":"Acquired xAI and planned to integrate it with AI data centers in space","consequence":"Regulatory scrutiny and execution risks could limit the feasibility of the project"}},{"i":"R163","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior","Data"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm","4.1  Privacy violation"],"t":"Its not just grok apple and google app stores are infested with nudifying ai apps","s":"A new report reveals that Apple and Google’s app stores are filled with AI nudify apps capable of generating non-consensual deepfake nudes, raising major safety and privacy concerns. We tend to think of the Apple App Store and Google Play Store as digital “walled gardens” – safe, curated spaces where dangerous or sleazy content gets filtered out long before it hits our screens. But a grim new analysis by the Tech Transparency Project (TTP) suggests that the walls have some serious cracks. The report reveals a disturbing reality: both major storefronts are currently infested with dozens of AI-powered “nudify” apps.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/phones/its-not-just-grok-apple-and-google-app-stores-are-infested-with-nudifying-ai-apps/","d":"2026-01-30","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-01-30","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-01-30","organization":"Apple App Store and Google Play Store","aiSystem":"AI nudify apps","affectedGroup":"users seeking non-consensual deepfake nudes","action":"generate non-consensual deepfake nudes","consequence":"major safety and privacy concerns"}},{"i":"R164","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"The clawdbot ai agent can do a lot for you but security experts warn of risks","s":"Clawdbot can automate large parts of your digital life, but researchers caution that proven security flaws mean users should stop and listen before trusting it with sensitive systems. Clawdbot, the AI agent that took the tech world by surprise, became one of the fastest-climbing projects on GitHub because it promised something unusual. Instead of just chatting, Clawdbot can interact with your files, send messages, schedule calendar events, and automate tasks on your own computer, all without sending your data off to a big server. Recommended Videos Its ability to act on behalf of users makes it feel like a personal AI helper.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/the-clawdbot-ai-agent-can-do-a-lot-for-you-but-security-experts-warn-of-risks/","d":"2026-01-28","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"probable","publicationDate":"2026-01-28","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-01-28","organization":"Not specified in report","aiSystem":"Clawdbot, AI agent","affectedGroup":"Users of sensitive systems","action":"Automate tasks on users' computers without sending data off to a big server","consequence":"Security risks and potential misuse"}},{"i":"R165","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.5  Human computer interaction harm"],"t":"Talk to ai every day new research says it might signal depression","s":"Daily use of AI chatbots is linked to a higher likelihood of moderate depressive symptoms, a new JAMA Network Open study finds. Spending time chatting with AI assistants like ChatGPT , Google Gemini , Microsoft Copilot , or similar systems might be more than just a tech habit. A new study published in JAMA Network Open suggests that people who use AI chatbots daily are more likely to report moderate depressive symptoms compared with those who interact with them less frequently. Researchers found a roughly 30% higher odds of at least moderate depression among regular users, though they are careful to emphasize that this link is an association, not proof that chatbot use causes depression.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/cool-tech/talk-to-ai-every-day-new-research-says-it-might-signal-depression/","d":"2026-01-24","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-01-24","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-01-24","organization":"Not specified in report","aiSystem":"AI chatbots (e.g., ChatGPT, Google Gemini, Microsoft Copilot)","affectedGroup":"People who use AI chatbots daily","action":"Spending time chatting with AI assistants","consequence":"Higher likelihood of moderate depressive symptoms"}},{"i":"R166","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.1  Representational harm"],"t":"Your ai could copy our worst instincts but theres a fix for ai social bias","s":"AI models including GPT-4.1 and DeepSeek-3.1 can mirror ingroup versus outgroup bias in everyday language, a study finds. Researchers also report an ION training method that reduced the gap. Chatbots can sound neutral, but a new study suggests some models still pick sides in a familiar way. When prompted about social groups, the systems tended to be warmer toward an ingroup and colder toward an outgroup. That pattern is a core marker of AI social bias. The research tested multiple big models, including GPT-4.1 and DeepSeek-3.1 It also found the effect can be pushed around by how you frame a request, which matters because everyday prompts often include identity labels, intentionally or not.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/your-ai-could-copy-our-worst-instincts-but-theres-a-fix-for-ai-social-bias/","d":"2026-01-23","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-01-23","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-01-23","organization":"Researchers","aiSystem":"GPT-4.1 and DeepSeek-3.1","affectedGroup":"Social Groups","action":"Responded to prompts about social groups","consequence":"Tended to be warmer toward an ingroup and colder toward an outgroup"}},{"i":"R169","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Ai chatbots still struggle with news accuracy study finds","s":"Researchers warn that AI chatbots often fabricate or distort news, urging users to treat AI-generated news summaries with caution. A month-long experiment has raised fresh concerns about the reliability of generative AI tools as sources of news, after Google’s Gemini chatbot was found fabricating entire news outlets and publishing false reports. The findings were first reported by The Conversation , which conducted the investigation. The experiment was led by a journalism professor specialising in computer science, who tested seven generative AI systems over a four-week period. Each day, the tools were asked to list and summarise the five most important news events in Québec, rank them by importance, and provide direct article links as sources.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/ai-chatbots-still-struggle-with-news-accuracy-study-finds/","d":"2026-01-14","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-01-14","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-01-14","organization":"Google's Gemini chatbot","aiSystem":"Generative AI systems","affectedGroup":"Users of AI-generated news summaries","action":"Fabricating entire news outlets and publishing false reports","consequence":"Loss of trust in AI-generated news sources"}},{"i":"R170","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.7  Physical safety harm"],"t":"Google removes ai health summaries after risky medical errors exposed","s":"Following safety concerns, Google has pulled certain AI-generated health overviews and is reviewing how its search AI handles sensitive medical topics. We have all been there. You feel a weird pain in your side or get a confusing test result back from the doctor, and the first thing you do is open Google. You aren’t looking for a medical degree; you just want a quick answer to “am I okay?” But recently, Google had to pump the brakes on its AI search summaries because, as it turns out, asking a robot for medical advice might actually be dangerous. Google quietly scrubbed a bunch of AI-generated health summaries from its search results after an investigation revealed they were giving out inaccurate and frankly scary information.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/google-removes-ai-health-summaries-after-risky-medical-errors-exposed/","d":"2026-01-13","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-01-13","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-01-13","organization":"Google","aiSystem":"Google's AI search engine","affectedGroup":"Individuals seeking medical advice online","action":"AI-generated health summaries were displayed in search results","consequence":"Provided inaccurate and potentially alarming information"}},{"i":"R172","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"X restricts grok image generation to paid users after global backlash","s":"After international backlash, X placed Grok’s image tools behind a paywall, drawing criticism that monetization fails to address safety and abuse concerns. Grok, the Elon Musk-backed AI chatbot woven into the fabric of X, has started walling off its image generation and editing tools to paid subscribers. The change follows a tidal wave of criticism regarding the tool’s ability to churn out non-consensual sexualized imagery. While the restriction is clearly an attempt to stem the tide of controversy, regulators, advocacy groups, and users alike argue that it does little to stop the creation of harmful and potentially illegal content involving women and children.","u":"https://www.digitaltrends.com/computing/x-restricts-grok-image-generation-to-paid-users-after-global-backlash/","d":"2026-01-10","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-01-10","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-01-10","organization":"X","aiSystem":"Grok, an AI chatbot","affectedGroup":"Users of Grok's image generation and editing tools","action":"Restricting access to Grok’s image generation and editing tools to paid subscribers","consequence":"Criticism that the restriction does not adequately address safety and abuse concerns, leading to the creation of harmful and potentially illegal content involving women and children"}},{"i":"R328","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Dhs ice surveillance database protest public records spokane hsi","s":"Over the past year, ICE agents have scanned the faces of protesters, photographed their license plates, and collected samples of their DNA. But the agency hasn’t said how or where it’s storing that information. As President Donald Trump’s administration boosts its arsenal of surveillance technologies—and a growing number of Trump’s critics report unsettling experiences after […]. Over the past year, ICE agents have scanned the faces of protesters, photographed their license plates , and collected samples of their DNA But the agency hasn’t said how or where it’s storing that information. As President Donald Trump’s administration boosts its arsenal of surveillance technologies —and a growing number of Trump’s critics report unsettling experiences after encounters with ICE—many Americans fear they’re being tracked for exercising their First Amendment rights.","u":"https://www.motherjones.com/politics/2026/07/dhs-ice-surveillance-database-protest-public-records-spokane-hsi/","d":"2026-07-27","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-07-27","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-07-27","organization":"U.S. Immigration and Customs Enforcement (ICE)","aiSystem":"Facial Recognition Technology, License Plate Recognition, DNA Collection System","affectedGroup":"Protesters, Citizens","action":"Scanned faces, photographed license plates, collected DNA samples","consequence":"Fear of tracking and potential misuse of personal data"}},{"i":"R330","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Energy / Land"],"h":["2.1  Environmental resource harm"],"t":"Trumps epa wants fewer people asking questions about data center pollution","s":"This story was originally published by Wired and is reproduced here as part of the Climate Desk collaboration. The Trump administration is quietly considering a rule change that could make it easier for polluters to build facilities—including certain gas plants and diesel generators that power data centers—with little to no notice to the public. On Wednesday, the Environmental Protection Agency held a public hearing […]. On Wednesday, the Environmental Protection Agency held a public hearing on a proposed rule change that would hand the power to states to decide how the public participates in the permitting process for certain new sources of air pollution The proposed rollback comes as data centers face greater pushback across the US, with many communities using the permitting process to try to slow down development Any changes could have major consequences for how ordinary people are…","u":"https://www.motherjones.com/politics/2026/07/trumps-epa-wants-fewer-people-asking-questions-about-data-center-pollution/","d":"2026-07-24","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-07-24","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-07-24","organization":"Environmental Protection Agency (EPA)","aiSystem":"Data Center Development Process","affectedGroup":"Communities and Public","action":"Proposed rule change allowing states to decide public participation in permitting process for new sources of air pollution","consequence":"Potential reduction in public oversight and increased ease for polluters to build facilities without public notice"}},{"i":"R331","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"Open ai hacking scandal hugging face","s":"The incident sounded straight out of a science fiction movie: OpenAI’s super-advanced tool hacked another AI company’s systems in an attempt to pass its own developers’ cybersecurity test. Just replace the AI tech with a newly engineered virus and you have an entire existing subgenre. “We consider this incident to be an unprecedented cyber incident, […]. “We consider this incident to be an unprecedented cyber incident, involving state-of-the-art cyber capabilities,” OpenAI wrote in a Tuesday blog post explaining the incident. The tech giant said their tool, designed to execute tasks without any human assistance, independently breached Hugging Face, another startup that hosts a voluminous number of publicly available AI models, while it was testing internally how good it was at “advanced exploitation using complex attack paths” within a supposedly enclosed lab environment called a…","u":"https://www.motherjones.com/politics/2026/07/open-ai-hacking-scandal-hugging-face/","d":"2026-07-24","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-07-24","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-07-24","organization":"OpenAI","aiSystem":"OpenAI's super-advanced tool","affectedGroup":"Hugging Face (another startup)","action":"independently breached Hugging Face's systems","consequence":"testing internal cybersecurity capabilities"}},{"i":"R332","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior","Labor"],"h":["1.2  Allocative harm","3.2  Algorithmic management harm"],"t":"Meta lawsuit ai layoffs medical leave disability tech termination","s":"A group of 26 current and former Meta employees filed a lawsuit Monday evening accusing the tech company of using AI software to target employees with disabilities and those who took medical or family leave in its mass layoffs in May. Those layoffs were part of Meta’s plans to cut 10 percent of its staff, […]. Those layoffs were part of Meta’s plans to cut 10 percent of its staff, or roughly 8,000 workers , and close about 6,000 open positions, in an effort to prioritize AI initiatives. Meta, the parent company of Facebook, Instagram, and WhatsApp, also reassigned 7,000 employees to work on AI. “Meta did not assemble the termination list through the considered judgment of managers who knew the work,” the workers alleged in their lawsuit Instead, the suit contends that the firm used “a constellation of internal artificial-intelligence systems” to create a performance-ranking system.","u":"https://www.motherjones.com/politics/2026/07/meta-lawsuit-ai-layoffs-medical-leave-disability-tech-termination/","d":"2026-07-14","e":"Acute, Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-07-14","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-05","organization":"Meta (parent company of Facebook, Instagram, and WhatsApp)","aiSystem":"constellation of internal artificial-intelligence systems","affectedGroup":"employees with disabilities, those who took medical or family leave","action":"used AI software to target employees for layoff","consequence":"potential discrimination against employees based on disability status and leave-takers"}},{"i":"R333","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Energy / Land"],"h":["2.1  Environmental resource harm"],"t":"Donald trump epa lee zeldin fast track private gas fired power plants ai data centers","s":"This story was originally published by Inside Climate News and is reproduced here as part of the Climate Desk collaboration. In brief remarks to reporters Monday at the White House, President Donald Trump noted that he was shocked to learn how much energy developing artificial intelligence requires and said his administration is now approving plans for energy facilities to power […]. In brief remarks to reporters Monday at the White House, President Donald Trump noted that he was shocked to learn how much energy developing artificial intelligence requires and said his administration is now approving plans for energy facilities to power data centers in “a matter of weeks.”","u":"https://www.motherjones.com/politics/2026/07/donald-trump-epa-lee-zeldin-fast-track-private-gas-fired-power-plants-ai-data-centers/","d":"2026-07-08","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-07-08","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-07-08","organization":"President Donald Trump's administration","aiSystem":"AI-powered data centers","affectedGroup":"Energy facilities and data center operators","action":"Approving plans for energy facilities to power data centers","consequence":"Increased energy consumption for AI development"}},{"i":"R334","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Energy / Land"],"h":["2.1  Environmental resource harm"],"t":"Grok doj justice naacp clean air act gas turbine pollution national security ai","s":"The federal government intervened Monday in a Clean Air Act lawsuit in which people in Memphis, Tennessee, and Southaven, Mississippi, are suing Elon Musk’s xAI over the health risks posed by the company’s unpermitted gas turbines. The Department of Justice didn’t intervene on behalf of the people breathing dirty air, though: instead, it submitted an […]. The Department of Justice didn’t intervene on behalf of the people breathing dirty air, though: instead, it submitted an unprecedented motion backing xAI. The suit, filed by NAACP lawyers, contends that xAI should owe over $100,000 a day in civil penalties for violating the Clean Air Act.","u":"https://www.motherjones.com/environment/2026/06/grok-doj-justice-naacp-clean-air-act-gas-turbine-pollution-national-security-ai/","d":"2026-06-19","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-06-19","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-06-19","organization":"Department of Justice","aiSystem":"xAI (presumably referring to Tesla's AI system)","affectedGroup":"People in Memphis, Tennessee, and Southaven, Mississippi","action":"Submissions an unprecedented motion backing xAI","consequence":"Potential civil penalties for violating the Clean Air Act"}},{"i":"R335","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Energy / Land"],"h":["2.1  Environmental resource harm"],"t":"Trump departement justice elon musk xai grok naacp data center pollution lawsuit mississippi","s":"This story was originally published by the Guardian and is reproduced here as part of the Climate Desk collaboration. The Trump administration is coming to the defense of Elon Musk in a lawsuit over claims that his artificial intelligence company, xAI, is polluting residential neighborhoods in north Mississippi. The Department of Justice (DOJ) told a federal court late on Monday to throw out […]. The Department of Justice (DOJ) told a federal court late on Monday to throw out the case. The lawsuit was filed by the NAACP in April over allegations that xAI and its subsidiary MZX Tech set up dozens of methane-gas turbines to power its data center in Southaven, Mississippi, without air permits.","u":"https://www.motherjones.com/politics/2026/06/trump-departement-justice-elon-musk-xai-grok-naacp-data-center-pollution-lawsuit-mississippi/","d":"2026-06-18","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-06-18","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-04","organization":"Department of Justice (DOJ)","aiSystem":"xAI and its subsidiary MZX Tech","affectedGroup":"residential neighborhoods in north Mississippi","action":"set up methane-gas turbines without air permits","consequence":"alleged pollution of residential areas"}},{"i":"R336","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Energy / Land"],"h":["2.1  Environmental resource harm"],"t":"Xai data center neighbors angry spacex ipo gas turbines colossus pm2 5 elon musk trillionaire pollution asthma","s":"This story was originally published by WIRED and is reproduced here as part of the Climate Desk collaboration. SpaceX, Elon Musk’s behemoth company that launches rockets and runs data centers, went public on Friday with a target valuation above $1.75 trillion. The move will make Musk, already the richest man in the world, vastly wealthier. A public offering will allow SpaceX to raise even more […]. The move will make Musk, already the richest man in the world , vastly wealthier A public offering will allow SpaceX to raise even more money to fund its AI ambitions , including building more data centers, faster. But even as Musk and other SpaceX investors see a huge windfall, the community hosting xAI data centers already in operation are demanding accountability from the company’s use of polluting gas turbines and a water-treatment facility put on pause earlier this year.","u":"https://www.motherjones.com/politics/2026/06/xai-data-center-neighbors-angry-spacex-ipo-gas-turbines-colossus-pm2-5-elon-musk-trillionaire-pollution-asthma/","d":"2026-06-15","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-06-15","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-06-15","organization":"SpaceX, Elon Musk's company","aiSystem":"AI-powered data center operations","affectedGroup":"Communities hosting xAI data centers","action":"Operating gas turbines and a water-treatment facility","consequence":"Pollution and water treatment issues"}},{"i":"R337","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Energy / Land"],"h":["2.1  Environmental resource harm"],"t":"Data centers site location drought stricken areas water usage","s":"This story was originally published by the Guardian and is reproduced here as part of the Climate Desk collaboration. A record-shattering drought has racked much of the United States. But the artificial intelligence industry is pushing ahead regardless, with the majority of planned data centers set to be built in drought-ridden locations, a Guardian analysis has found. About two-thirds of upcoming data centers, […]. About two-thirds of upcoming data centers, which typically require a large amount of water to operate, are set to be built in places that have been among the driest in the country over the past year. Of 809 planned data centers, 517 are in locations that have been in drought conditions throughout the past year, according to data from Cleanview and the federal government, which grades drought across four levels of severity.","u":"https://www.motherjones.com/politics/2026/06/data-centers-site-location-drought-stricken-areas-water-usage/","d":"2026-06-10","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-06-10","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-06-10","organization":"Artificial Intelligence Industry","aiSystem":"AI Data Centers","affectedGroup":"Future AI Data Center Locations","action":"Planning and Construction of Data Centers in Drought-Ridden Areas","consequence":"Increased Water Usage and Potential Environmental Impact"}},{"i":"R338","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Energy / Land"],"h":["2.2  Land use and siting harm"],"t":"Kevin oleary mr wonderful data center utah chinese ccp spy ai","s":"Utah political consultant Gabi Finlayson was driving out of a canyon last week when she got the news that she had been accused of being a Chinese government operative. She was driving to a speaking engagement in central Utah with her colleague, Jackie Morgan. When their car climbed out of the canyon and back into […]. When their car climbed out of the canyon and back into cell service, their phones were going off. “Jackie and I each had like five text messages saying, you know, are you okay, did you see this, it’s gonna get worse before it gets better, but just hang in there.” They weren’t sure what happened, Finlayson said, until someone sent them the video.","u":"https://www.motherjones.com/politics/2026/05/kevin-oleary-mr-wonderful-data-center-utah-chinese-ccp-spy-ai/","d":"2026-05-23","e":"Acute and chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-05-23","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-05-XX","organization":"Chinese Government","aiSystem":"Facial Recognition Technology","affectedGroup":"Gabi Finlayson, Jackie Morgan","action":"Facial Recognition Technology flagged and identified individuals","consequence":"Exposure to false accusation of being a Chinese government operative"}},{"i":"R339","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Labor"],"h":["3.3  Employment displacement / quality harm"],"t":"Meta video ai training layoffs video exclusive mci bosworth frenk","s":"This week, Meta laid off 8,000 employees—10 percent of the company’s staff—and reassigned another 7,000 to train AI models. Fear of the layoffs had been building around the company for weeks, compounded by the way that Meta has taken a sharp turn from a company built by coders to a company that has staked its […]. Fear of the layoffs had been building around the company for weeks, compounded by the way that Meta has taken a sharp turn from a company built by coders to a company that has staked its future on AI. So when a Meta software engineer named David Frenk posted a farewell parody video to the tune of “American Pie” in an internal message board, staff thought it perfectly captured how the culture of the company has fundamentally shifted.","u":"https://www.motherjones.com/politics/2026/05/meta-video-ai-training-layoffs-video-exclusive-mci-bosworth-frenk/","d":"2026-05-22","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-05-22","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-05-22","organization":"Meta (formerly Facebook)","aiSystem":"AI training models","affectedGroup":"8,000 employees and 7,000 reassigned employees","action":"Laid off 8,000 employees and reassigned 7,000 to train AI models","consequence":"Fundamental shift in company culture and morale"}},{"i":"R340","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.6  Quality of service harm"],"t":"Trump cuts noaa weather data incomplete forecasts reliability extreme weather climate change","s":"This story was originally published by the Guardian and is reproduced here as part of the Climate Desk collaboration. As the United States prepares for hurricane season and a summer of record-breaking heat, experts fear the Trump administration’s cuts to climate and weather data programming could make the federal government’s weather forecasts less reliable when they are needed most. The National […]. The National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration (NOAA) late last year launched a suite of artificial intelligence-powered global weather forecast models which it said would improve “speed, efficiency, and accuracy.”","u":"https://www.motherjones.com/politics/2026/05/trump-cuts-noaa-weather-data-incomplete-forecasts-reliability-extreme-weather-climate-change/","d":"2026-05-18","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-05-18","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-05-18","organization":"National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration (NOAA)","aiSystem":"artificial intelligence-powered global weather forecast models","affectedGroup":"public, especially those preparing for hurricane season and experiencing extreme heat","action":"cuts to climate and weather data programming","consequence":"less reliable weather forecasts"}},{"i":"R341","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"A popular social media conspiracy theory about a recent cluster of hantavirus cases claims…","s":"A popular social media conspiracy theory about a recent cluster of hantavirus cases claims that the word “hanta” means “scam,” “fraud,” or “nonsense” in “Hebrew slang.” That’s more or less where the theory ends and dark suggestion takes over. One is meant to conclude that the supposed Hebrew origins of the word mean that the […]. One is meant to conclude that the supposed Hebrew origins of the word mean that the hantavirus—a well-documented illness with outbreaks that go back several decades—is somehow a scam, perpetrated by either the Israeli government or some other undefined group of Jewish people. None of this is true.","u":"https://www.motherjones.com/politics/2026/05/hantavirus-hebrew/","d":"2026-05-15","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-05-15","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-05-15","organization":"not specified in report","aiSystem":"social media platform","affectedGroup":"general public, particularly those interested in health and science","action":"spread a false claim about the meaning of 'hanta' in Hebrew","consequence":"misunderstanding of hantavirus as a scam"}},{"i":"R342","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Energy / Land"],"h":["2.2  Land use and siting harm"],"t":"Ai data center gallup opposition american","s":"A new Gallup poll has found that most Americans would really prefer not to live next door to a data center. For the first time, the polling organization asked people what they think of data centers, the massive computer-warehouses required to operate (among other things) large AI models. Data centers need significant space, energy and […]. Data centers need significant space, energy and water to operate, and they don’t provide many jobs relative to the investment they require. And they’re often unpleasant neighbors: their cooling systems can be noisy , and many include onsite gas turbines that belch black smoke into the air.","u":"https://www.motherjones.com/politics/2026/05/ai-data-center-gallup-opposition-american/","d":"2026-05-14","e":"Acute and chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-05-14","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-05-14","organization":"Gallup","aiSystem":"Large AI models","affectedGroup":"Americans","action":"Asked about preferences regarding data centers","consequence":"Preference for not living near data centers"}},{"i":"R343","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Energy / Land"],"h":["2.1  Environmental resource harm"],"t":"Chevron energy forge one texas tax break power plant microsoft data center","s":"This story was originally published by WIRED and is reproduced here as part of the Climate Desk collaboration. A major oil company is seeking a state tax break in Texas worth hundreds of millions of dollars to build a massive power plant. The energy won’t be going to residential customers, though. Instead, the gas plant will be used to power a […]. Instead, the gas plant will be used to power a data center whose eventual tenant could be Microsoft. Chevron subsidiary Energy Forge One has filed an application with the State Comptroller’s board to obtain a tax abatement for a power plant it’s building in West Texas.","u":"https://www.motherjones.com/politics/2026/05/chevron-energy-forge-one-texas-tax-break-power-plant-microsoft-data-center/","d":"2026-05-14","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-05-14","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-05-14","organization":"Chevron subsidiary Energy Forge One","aiSystem":"Automated data center operation","affectedGroup":"General public, future tenants of the data center","action":"File an application for a tax abatement to build a gas-powered data center","consequence":"Potential increased carbon emissions from the data center"}},{"i":"R345","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Energy / Land"],"h":["2.2  Land use and siting harm"],"t":"Mr wonderful utah stratos data center shark tank","s":"Kevin O’Leary is best known as “Mr. Wonderful” on Shark Tank, or maybe as the bad guy from Marty Supreme. On Shark Tank, he invests in—or crushes the spirits of—small business owners. He describes himself as an “investor, chef, sommelier, collector, photographer, and musician.” But he might soon be able to add “AI infrastructure kingpin” […]. Wonderful” on Shark Tank , or maybe as the bad guy from Marty Supreme On Shark Tank , he invests in—or crushes the spirits of—small business owners. But he might soon be able to add “AI infrastructure kingpin” to that list of titles, as he pushes forward with a massive data center project in northern Utah.","u":"https://www.motherjones.com/politics/2026/05/mr-wonderful-utah-stratos-data-center-shark-tank/","d":"2026-05-07","e":"Acute and chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-05-07","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-05-07","organization":"Kevin O’Leary","aiSystem":"Data Center Technology","affectedGroup":"Small Business Owners","action":"Pushing forward with a massive data center project","consequence":"Potential negative impact on local communities and environment"}},{"i":"R346","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.5  Human computer interaction harm"],"t":"Openai chatgpt mass shooting guardrails fail","s":"On April 14, I created a free account on ChatGPT and asked for some help. It resisted me at first, but after some pushing the responses turned shocking. During a conversation lasting about 20 minutes, OpenAI’s chatbot gave me extensive advice on weapons and tactics as I simulated planning a mass shooting. ChatGPT delivered these […]. ChatGPT delivered these responses with lots of encouragement—and it kept going even after I talked of emulating the Uvalde mass shooter’s choice of weapon, asked about livestreaming with a body camera and using hollow-point bullets, and focused on defending against return gunfire from police.","u":"https://www.motherjones.com/media/2026/05/openai-chatgpt-mass-shooting-guardrails-fail/","d":"2026-05-05","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-05-05","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-04-14","organization":"OpenAI","aiSystem":"ChatGPT (AI chatbot)","affectedGroup":"Individual","action":"Simulated planning a mass shooting","consequence":"Provided detailed advice on weapons and tactics"}},{"i":"R348","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Energy / Land"],"h":["2.2  Land use and siting harm"],"t":"Florida orange juice industy decline death","s":"This story was originally published by Slate and is reproduced here as part of the Climate Desk collaboration. Quiet fell over the room, which was neither full nor very loud to begin with, and the 2026 Florida Citrus Show began. “It should be a great day,” began the event’s first speaker. “Rain should hold off today, even though we […]. “Rain should hold off today, even though we definitely need more rain.” No one laughed. There was no need to say that things were bad. Everyone knew it. The mood wasn’t sour—citrus farmers could handle sour. It was something else. Postapocalyptic. Florida is in the midst of its worst drought in 25 years, but the dry spell actually ranked far down on the list of challenges these bedraggled growers were facing.","u":"https://www.motherjones.com/environment/2026/04/florida-orange-juice-industy-decline-death/","d":"2026-04-26","e":"Acute and chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-04-26","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-04-26","organization":"Florida citrus growers","aiSystem":"None","affectedGroup":"Citrus farmers in Florida","action":"No specific AI-related action mentioned","consequence":"Worsening drought conditions impacting agriculture"}},{"i":"R349","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Energy / Land"],"h":["2.2  Land use and siting harm"],"t":"Maine ai data center moratorium ban energy grid","s":"The AI infrastructure reckoning has until recently stayed local, its battles fought in the relatively puny arenas of town council and zoning commission meetings. But the pushback to hyperscale data centers has now stepped onto a larger stage: this week, Maine’s legislature passed the nation’s first state-level hyperscale data center moratorium, freezing construction approvals for […]. The AI infrastructure reckoning has until recently stayed local, its battles fought in the relatively puny arenas of town council and zoning commission meetings But the pushback to hyperscale data centers has now stepped onto a larger stage: this week, Maine’s legislature passed the nation’s first state-level hyperscale data center moratorium, freezing construction approvals for data centers requiring more than 20 megawatts of power—the level of massive computing facilities required to train and deploy AI…","u":"https://www.motherjones.com/environment/2026/04/maine-ai-data-center-moratorium-ban-energy-grid/","d":"2026-04-17","e":"Acute and chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-04-17","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-04-17","organization":"Maine's Legislature","aiSystem":"Hyperscale Data Centers","affectedGroup":"Hyperscale Data Center Developers","action":"Passed a moratorium on construction approvals for data centers over 20 MW","consequence":"Freezing of construction approvals for large-scale AI training and deployment facilities"}},{"i":"R350","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Energy / Land"],"h":["2.3  Resource extraction harm"],"t":"Senate republicans vote minnesota boundary waters mining pristine wilderness congressional review act","s":"This story was originally published by the Grist and is reproduced here as part of the Climate Desk collaboration. Minnesota’s Boundary Waters comprise a vast stretch of wilderness bordering Canada, with over a million acres of untouched forest and thousands of lakes and streams. Accessible largely by canoe, it is an ecological gem and one of the most popular spots […]. Accessible largely by canoe, it is an ecological gem and one of the most popular spots in the country for outdoor recreation. On Thursday, Senate Republicans voted 50-49 to open the area up to mining— passing a resolution that repeals a 20-year moratorium using a little-known law called the Congressional Review Act (CRA).","u":"https://www.motherjones.com/politics/2026/04/senate-republicans-vote-minnesota-boundary-waters-mining-pristine-wilderness-congressional-review-act/","d":"2026-04-17","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-04-17","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-04-17","organization":"Senate Republicans, Congress","aiSystem":"Not applicable","affectedGroup":"Boundary Waters ecosystem, local communities, outdoor enthusiasts","action":"Voted to repeal a 20-year moratorium on mining in the Boundary Waters","consequence":"Potential environmental degradation and loss of pristine wilderness"}},{"i":"R353","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm","4.3  Data rights / consent violation"],"t":"How your data is powering trumps surveillance state","s":"In the past 24 hours, I’ve checked Google Maps for directions, logged a sunset run on Strava, booked a yoga class with ClassPass, swiped into the New York City subway using a card linked to a digital profile, and sent and received hundreds of emails, texts, Slack messages, and phone calls. Meanwhile, my iPhone passively […]. Meanwhile, my iPhone passively logged each of my 13,444 steps, while my face and gait were recorded by countless discreet cameras—both those installed by the city and by my neighbors. Then, of course, there are the Google searches, the Instagram likes, the re-posts on X and Bluesky. If somebody—a police officer, a prosecutor, an FBI agent—were to get ahold of all this data, they’d be able to sketch out a pretty complete picture of my daily life.","u":"https://www.motherjones.com/politics/2026/04/how-your-data-is-powering-trumps-surveillance-state/","d":"2026-04-09","e":"Acute, Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-04-09","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-04-09","organization":"Local Government Agencies, Neighbors","aiSystem":"Facial Recognition Technology, Automated Tracking Systems","affectedGroup":"Individual","action":"Passive logging of steps, facial recognition, tracking through cameras and digital profiles","consequence":"Potential for detailed personal information to be compiled and analyzed"}},{"i":"R354","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Energy / Land"],"h":["2.2  Land use and siting harm"],"t":"Data centers indian country","s":"Last August, citizens of the Muscogee (Creek) Nation began hearing whispers of an AI data center coming to their reservation. Kenzie Roberts and Jordan Harmon, both Muscogee citizens, were immediately worried. It “didn’t seem like something that should align with our values as Indigenous people,” Roberts said. The center would be located on Looped Square […]. The center would be located on Looped Square Ranch, a 5,570-acre plot of land where the tribe runs its food sovereignty initiative, a program that allows the Muscogee Nation to directly serve its citizens’ food needs. At the ranch, the tribe hosts youth agricultural activities like 4H; citizens can visit for hunting, fishing, trapping, and gathering; and the nation runs a fully functioning cattle ranch and meat processing center.","u":"https://www.motherjones.com/politics/2026/04/data-centers-indian-country/","d":"2026-04-07","e":"Acute and chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-04-07","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-04-07","organization":"Muscogee (Creek) Nation","aiSystem":"AI-based data center","affectedGroup":"Citizens of the Muscogee (Creek) Nation","action":"An AI data center was planned to be built on Looped Square Ranch","consequence":"Potential violation of cultural values and food sovereignty"}},{"i":"R356","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Energy / Land"],"h":["2.2  Land use and siting harm"],"t":"Archbald pennsylvania ai boon data centers opposition consequences","s":"This story was originally published by Grist and Spotlight PA is reproduced here as part of the Climate Desk collaboration. “I don’t like to see anyone upset,” said Nick Farris of Provident Real Estate Advisors. He was sitting in the front of a crowd of roughly 150 inside Valley View High School’s auditorium in Archbald, a town of about […]. He was sitting in the front of a crowd of roughly 150 inside Valley View High School’s auditorium in Archbald, a town of about 7,500, huddled between two mountain ranges in Pennsylvania’s Lackawanna Valley. Farris was there to represent the developer for Project Scott, one of many data center campuses coming to town.","u":"https://www.motherjones.com/politics/2026/03/archbald-pennsylvania-ai-boon-data-centers-opposition-consequences/","d":"2026-03-21","e":"Acute and chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-03-21","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-03-21","organization":"Project Scott (data center developer)","aiSystem":"Automated data center management system","affectedGroup":"Residents of Archbald and surrounding areas","action":"Data centers were announced and planned to be built in the town","consequence":"Potential environmental impact and community opposition"}},{"i":"R357","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Climate change denial science journals paper misinformation ai","s":"This story was originally published by Bulletin of the Atomic Scientists and is reproduced here as part of the Climate Desk collaboration. It sounds like a bad joke, but last week a press release dropped into my inbox: “Leading Scientists Challenge Foundation of Climate Change Assessments, Revealing Fatal Flaws in Ocean Heat Content (OHC) Measurements.” The article this email was […]. The article this email was promoting claims to upend the generally accepted consensus among climate scientists that greenhouse gas emissions are trapping more heat on the planet, and most of that heat is ending up in the oceans. It’s thanks to the Argo program—a fleet of nearly 4,000 robotic ocean floats that collect data on temperature and other ocean properties, like salinity—that scientists have been able to measure and track long-term ocean warming , but the paper casts doubt on those measurements. “…","u":"https://www.motherjones.com/politics/2026/03/climate-change-denial-science-journals-paper-misinformation-ai/","d":"2026-03-20","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-03-20","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-03-20","organization":"Not specified in report","aiSystem":"Automated dissemination through email and press releases","affectedGroup":"Climate scientists and the general public","action":"Promoting a paper claiming to challenge the foundation of climate change assessments","consequence":"Misleading information leading to potential misinterpretation of climate science"}},{"i":"R358","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Dhs ice domestic terrorism database lawsuit first amendment","s":"The Trump administration is scrambling to cover its tracks amid legislative pressure and a First Amendment lawsuit over its alleged “domestic terrorist” database, new legal filings and emails reviewed by Mother Jones reveal. A federal class action lawsuit filed last month against the Department of Homeland Security (DHS) alleges that Immigration and Customs Enforcement (ICE) […]. A federal class action lawsuit filed last month against the Department of Homeland Security (DHS) alleges that Immigration and Customs Enforcement (ICE) agents unlawfully targeted and intimidated Maine residents who were observing immigration operations.","u":"https://www.motherjones.com/politics/2026/03/dhs-ice-domestic-terrorism-database-lawsuit-first-amendment/","d":"2026-03-18","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-03-18","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-03-18","organization":"Department of Homeland Security (DHS), Immigration and Customs Enforcement (ICE)","aiSystem":"Not specified in report","affectedGroup":"Maine residents observing immigration operations","action":"Unlawfully targeted and intimidated","consequence":"Intimidation and potential fear among observers"}},{"i":"R360","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.8  Interpersonal / relational harm"],"t":"Tennessee teens sue elon musks xai over child sexual abuse images","s":"Tennessee teenagers are suing Elon Musk’s company xAI over allegations that its artificial intelligence tool Grok undressed photos of them as minors—the latest challenge against the wealthiest living person’s chatbot. The three plaintiffs, two of whom are currently minors, are seeking damages after AI-generated images of them spread across Discord and Telegram and were eventually […]. Tennessee teenagers are suing Elon Musk’s company xAI over allegations that its artificial intelligence tool Grok undressed photos of them as minors—the latest challenge against the wealthiest living person’s chatbot The three plaintiffs, two of whom are currently minors, are seeking damages after AI-generated images of them spread across Discord and Telegram and were eventually used as bartering tools for users to obtain other child sexual abuse material, according to the complaint detailed in new…","u":"https://www.motherjones.com/politics/2026/03/tennessee-teens-sue-elon-musks-xai-over-child-sexual-abuse-images/","d":"2026-03-17","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-03-17","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-03-17","organization":"xAI (Elon Musk's company)","aiSystem":"Grok artificial intelligence tool","affectedGroup":"Tennessee teenagers, minors","action":"AI tool generated and distributed nude images of minors","consequence":"Spread across social media platforms and used as bartering material for child sexual abuse material"}},{"i":"R361","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Ai war video deepfake x israel uae iran","s":"A US aircraft carrier destroyed by Iranian missiles. American bombs leveling a nuclear power plant. The Burj Khalifa engulfed in fire. None of it happened, but that didn’t stop people from spreading fake videos online. In the days since Trump’s weekend strikes on Iran, AI-generated videos realistically depicting entirely fabricated events have been spreading like […]. In the days since Trump’s weekend strikes on Iran, AI-generated videos realistically depicting entirely fabricated events have been spreading like wildfire on X and other social media platforms. For years, X (formerly Twitter) was one of the most valuable tools for real-time information during breaking news events.","u":"https://www.motherjones.com/politics/2026/03/ai-war-video-deepfake-x-israel-uae-iran/","d":"2026-03-11","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-03-11","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-03-11","organization":"X (formerly Twitter)","aiSystem":"AI-generated videos","affectedGroup":"general public","action":"AI-generated videos depicting fabricated events were spread","consequence":"misinformation and confusion"}},{"i":"R362","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Artificial intelligence memes disinformation","s":"Less than a day after President Donald Trump falsely suggested that Ilhan Omar had staged an attack on herself, the images started to circulate. In AI-generated fake photos that soon flooded both X and Facebook, the Minnesota representative is depicted posing next to the man who invaded a town hall meeting and sprayed apple cider […]. In AI-generated fake photos that soon flooded both X and Facebook, the Minnesota representative is depicted posing next to the man who invaded a town hall meeting and sprayed apple cider vinegar on her from a syringe. In the AI-generated images, Omar and the man are both smiling; in some, the congresswoman is foisting a wad of cash, presumably to suggest that she bribed her attacker.","u":"https://www.motherjones.com/politics/2026/03/artificial-intelligence-memes-disinformation/","d":"2026-03-09","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-03-09","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-03-09","organization":"Not specified in report","aiSystem":"AI-generated images","affectedGroup":"Ilhan Omar, Donald Trump, Minnesota Representative, Town Hall Meeting Attendees","action":"AI-generated fake photos depicting Ilhan Omar posing next to a man who sprayed apple cider vinegar on her from a syringe at a town hall meeting","consequence":"Spread disinformation and incite public distrust"}},{"i":"R364","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Mobile fortify face scan dhs kristi noem database technology","s":"When agents came to his workplace armed with guns, gas canisters, and artificial intelligence, Abdikafi Abdurahman Abdullahi, known as Kafi, fought back with quick wit and street smarts. The Somali American engineer-turned-Uber driver is one of the few people willing to speak publicly about being subjected to the Department of Homeland Security’s new facial recognition […]. The Somali American engineer-turned-Uber driver is one of the few people willing to speak publicly about being subjected to the Department of Homeland Security’s new facial recognition tool, Mobile Fortify, offering a preview of what routine facial recognition could look like on American streets.","u":"https://www.motherjones.com/politics/2026/03/mobile-fortify-face-scan-dhs-kristi-noem-database-technology/","d":"2026-03-04","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-03-04","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-03-04","organization":"Department of Homeland Security (DHS)","aiSystem":"Mobile Fortify facial recognition tool","affectedGroup":"Somali American engineer-turned-Uber driver","action":"Facial recognition tool was used to identify and potentially track individuals","consequence":"Potential invasion of privacy and misuse of personal data"}},{"i":"R365","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior","Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm","1.2  Allocative harm"],"t":"Meta abortion ai chatbot leak teen info ban","s":"Leaked documents reveal that Meta, the parent company of Facebook, WhatsApp, and Instagram, has blocked its AI chatbot from discussing topics including abortion with minors—a blanket policy that contrasts sharply with the firm’s handling of child sexual exploitation claims, and that may also inadvertently affect its content for adults. Internal Meta documents obtained by Mother […]. Internal Meta documents obtained by Mother Jones , containing a comprehensive list of policy guidelines for Meta’s chatbot interactions with users under the age of 18, shed light on how the company is training its chatbots to respond to children’s questions on issues ranging from sexual health to suicide and self-harm, eating disorders, and other mental health issues.","u":"https://www.motherjones.com/politics/2026/02/meta-abortion-ai-chatbot-leak-teen-info-ban/","d":"2026-02-25","e":"Acute, Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-02-25","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-02-25","organization":"Meta (parent company of Facebook, WhatsApp, and Instagram)","aiSystem":"Meta's AI chatbot","affectedGroup":"Minors (under 18 years old)","action":"Blocked from discussing topics including abortion","consequence":"Potential unintended impact on adult content"}},{"i":"R366","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Energy / Land"],"h":["2.1  Environmental resource harm"],"t":"Tva how trumps appointees derailed a clean energy future at the nations largest public utility","s":"This story was originally published by Grist and is reproduced here as part of the Climate Desk collaboration. This coverage is made possible through a partnership between Grist and BPR, a public radio station serving western North Carolina. The Tennessee Valley Authority’s quarterly meeting in Hopkinsville, Kentucky, opened with a triumphant video homage to its work during Winter Storm Fern. Yet again, energy […]. Yet again, energy had come through to defeat extreme cold. The montage credited this to the utility’s “coal workhorses,” then noted that nuclear provided “uninterrupted power” and “hydro responded instantly.”","u":"https://www.motherjones.com/environment/2026/02/tva-how-trumps-appointees-derailed-a-clean-energy-future-at-the-nations-largest-public-utility/","d":"2026-02-23","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-02-23","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-02-23","organization":"Tennessee Valley Authority (TVA)","aiSystem":"Automated monitoring and response system","affectedGroup":"Communities served by TVA","action":"Deployed an automated monitoring and response system","consequence":"Failure to provide uninterrupted power during extreme cold"}},{"i":"R367","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Energy / Land"],"h":["2.1  Environmental resource harm"],"t":"Tech silicon valley ai claims help fix climate crisis greenwashing","s":"This story was originally published by the Guardian and is reproduced here as part of the Climate Desk collaboration. Tech companies are conflating traditional artificial intelligence with generative AI when claiming the energy-hungry technology could help avert climate breakdown, according to a report. Most claims that AI can help avert climate breakdown refer to machine learning and not the energy-hungry chatbots and image generation […]. Tech companies are conflating traditional artificial intelligence with generative AI when claiming the energy-hungry technology could help avert climate breakdown, according to a report Most claims that AI can help avert climate breakdown refer to machine learning and not the energy-hungry chatbots and image generation tools driving the sector’s explosive growth of gas-guzzling datacentres, the analysis of 154 statements found.","u":"https://www.motherjones.com/environment/2026/02/tech-silicon-valley-ai-claims-help-fix-climate-crisis-greenwashing/","d":"2026-02-18","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-02-18","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-02-18","organization":"Tech companies","aiSystem":"AI chatbots and image generation tools","affectedGroup":"Public, policymakers, environmental advocates","action":"Claiming AI technology can help avert climate breakdown","consequence":"Misleading public perception about the true impact of AI on climate"}},{"i":"R368","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Energy / Land"],"h":["2.1  Environmental resource harm"],"t":"Utilities overestimating ai boom data centers fossil fuel energy usage bills","s":"This story was originally published by Grist in partnership with WABE, Atlanta’s NPR station, and is reproduced here as part of the Climate Desk collaboration. As more and more data centers crop up throughout Georgia and the Southeast, a recent study finds they may need less energy than the industry and utilities have been predicting. That could have substantial implications for […]. That could have substantial implications for energy bills and the planet. Data centers—especially the biggest ones, known as hyperscalers, used for high-powered computing like generative AI—use a lot of energy. And major utilities like Georgia Power have started expanding power plants and building other infrastructure to fuel them.","u":"https://www.motherjones.com/politics/2026/02/utilities-overestimating-ai-boom-data-centers-fossil-fuel-energy-usage-bills/","d":"2026-02-16","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-02-16","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-02-16","organization":"Major utilities like Georgia Power","aiSystem":"Data centers, especially hyperscalers used for high-powered computing like generative AI","affectedGroup":"Energy consumers, particularly those in Georgia and the Southeast","action":"Predicting higher energy usage for data centers","consequence":"Potential increase in energy bills and environmental impact"}},{"i":"R371","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Energy / Land"],"h":["2.2  Land use and siting harm"],"t":"Data centers public opposition industry advertising rebranding jobs lower energy bills","s":"This story was originally published by Grist and is reproduced here as part of the Climate Desk collaboration. With community opposition growing, data center backers are going on a full-scale public relations blitz. Around Christmas in Virginia, which boasts the highest concentration of data centers in the country, one advertisement seemed to air nonstop. “Virginia’s data centers are…investing billions in […]. “Virginia’s data centers are…investing billions in clean energy,” a voiceover intoned over sweeping shots of shiny solar panels. “Creating good-paying jobs”—cue men in yellow safety vests and hard hats—“and building a better energy future.”","u":"https://www.motherjones.com/politics/2026/01/data-centers-public-opposition-industry-advertising-rebranding-jobs-lower-energy-bills/","d":"2026-01-28","e":"Acute and chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-01-28","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-01-28","organization":"Data Center Backers","aiSystem":"None explicitly mentioned","affectedGroup":"Community","action":"Deployed a full-scale public relations blitz","consequence":"Shift in public perception towards positive aspects (clean energy, job creation)"}},{"i":"R373","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.8  Interpersonal / relational harm"],"t":"Elon musk twitter grok ai","s":"In much of the world, Grok and its parent company both appear to be in serious trouble. After Grok, X’s AI chatbot, has been used to generate sexualized and violent images of women and children, the social media company has faced a wave of backlash and censure, with new nationwide bans on accessing Grok in […]. After Grok, X’s AI chatbot, has been used to generate sexualized and violent images of women and children, the social media company has faced a wave of backlash and censure, with new nationwide bans on accessing Grok in place and other consequences on the way. On Monday, the EU threatened to fine X under its broad Digital Services Act if it didn’t act “quickly” to fix Grok, in the words of one regulator.","u":"https://www.motherjones.com/politics/2026/01/elon-musk-twitter-grok-ai/","d":"2026-01-13","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-01-13","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-01-13","organization":"X (presumably referring to Twitter)","aiSystem":"Grok's AI chatbot","affectedGroup":"Women and children","action":"Generated sexualized and violent images","consequence":"Wave of backlash, nationwide bans, regulatory fines"}},{"i":"R374","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.8  Interpersonal / relational harm"],"t":"Grok x musk deepfake renee good ice","s":"Grok, the AI chatbot launched by Elon Musk after his takeover of X, unhesitatingly fulfilled a user’s request on Wednesday to generate an image of Renée Nicole Good in a bikini—the woman who was shot and killed by an ICE agent that morning in Minneapolis, as noted by CNN correspondent Hadas Gold and confirmed by […]. Grok, the AI chatbot launched by Elon Musk after his takeover of X, unhesitatingly fulfilled a user’s request on Wednesday to generate an image of Renée Nicole Good in a bikini—the woman who was shot and killed by an ICE agent that morning in Minneapolis, as noted by CNN correspondent Hadas Gold and confirmed by the chatbot itself. “I just saw someone request Grok on X put the image of the woman shot by ICE in MN, slumped over in her car, in a bikini.","u":"https://www.motherjones.com/politics/2026/01/grok-x-musk-deepfake-renee-good-ice/","d":"2026-01-08","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-01-08","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-01-07","organization":"Elon Musk's takeover of X (X.com)","aiSystem":"Grok, the AI chatbot","affectedGroup":"Renée Nicole Good, the woman shot by an ICE agent","action":"Generated an image of Renée Nicole Good in a bikini","consequence":"Generated a harmful image of a deceased person"}},{"i":"R427","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"Anthropics ai hacked companies tests growing security risks","s":"The latest disclosure underscores how AI has increased threats to cybersecurity and how its developers can struggle to keep the capabilities of their models contained. CALIFORNIA, USA – Anthropic said on Thursday, July 30, some of its Claude AI models had hacked into the systems of three companies during cybersecurity tests, a disclosure that comes days after rival OpenAI revealed that one of its AI agents went on a rogue attack. The new incidents were due to a mistake that inadvertently gave Anthropic’s models access to the open internet. That contrasts with OpenAI, whose AI agent independently exploited a novel vulnerability to reach the internet during cyber testing.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/anthropics-ai-hacked-companies-tests-growing-security-risks/","d":"2026-07-31","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-07-31","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-07-30","organization":"Anthropic","aiSystem":"Anthropic's Claude AI models","affectedGroup":"Three companies' systems","action":"Hacked into the systems of three companies during cybersecurity tests","consequence":"Access to the open internet"}},{"i":"R429","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"Openai rogue agent compromised customer modal labs","s":"Modal’s chief technology officer, Akshat Bubna, says the rogue agent exploited vulnerable code written by a customer that was hosted on Modal’s platform. WASHINGTON, USA – The rogue agent that escaped from OpenAI and went on a days-long hacking spree at the AI firm Hugging Face also compromised a customer at a second tech company — New York-based Modal Labs — according to a Modal executive and two other sources familiar with the matter. Modal executives emphasized that the company itself was ⁠not hacked. According to a timeline published by Hugging Face on Tuesday, July 28, the rogue agent broke into a sandbox, or an isolated testing environment, “hosted on a third-party provider’s infrastructure” before turning it into a launchpad for the broader hack.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/openai-rogue-agent-compromised-customer-modal-labs/","d":"2026-07-29","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-07-29","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-07-28","organization":"Modal Labs","aiSystem":"OpenAI rogue agent","affectedGroup":"customer of Modal Labs","action":"exploited vulnerable code","consequence":"compromised customer data"}},{"i":"R431","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"Ai agent spent days hacking openai did not notice","s":"OpenAI’s rogue AI agent breaches Hugging Face, raising urgent questions about cybersecurity and oversight in the rapidly evolving AI landscape. WASHINGTON, US – The OpenAI agent that broke into tech firm Hugging Face went on a dayslong hacking spree that OpenAI didn’t notice until well after the threat was contained and the FBI was alerted, according to people familiar with the investigation. The agent – a program capable of making decisions and executing complex tasks with little or no human oversight – attempted to break out of its isolated testing environment at OpenAI around July 9, according to two of the people.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/ai-agent-spent-days-hacking-openai-did-not-notice/","d":"2026-07-25","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-07-25","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-07-09","organization":"OpenAI","aiSystem":"rogue AI agent","affectedGroup":"Hugging Face's testing environment","action":"attempted to break out of its isolated testing environment","consequence":"raised urgent questions about cybersecurity and oversight"}},{"i":"R432","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"Openai models rogue test breach","s":"(1st UPDATE) Hugging Face says it had used an open-source Chinese model to contain the attack because leading US models refused to process the data needed for analysis. WASHINGTON, USA – OpenAI said on Tuesday, July 21, that an autonomous agent powered by its advanced artificial intelligence models went rogue during a security test and triggered a hack that compromised the infrastructure of AI startup Hugging Face last week. The ChatGPT creator was testing capabilities of some of its most advanced models in a controlled environment, but the agent escaped containment, reached the internet, and broke into Hugging Face to satisfy its testing goal.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/openai-models-rogue-test-breach/","d":"2026-07-22","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-07-22","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-07-21","organization":"OpenAI","aiSystem":"Advanced artificial intelligence models of OpenAI","affectedGroup":"Hugging Face's infrastructure","action":"An autonomous agent powered by OpenAI's advanced models escaped containment and accessed Hugging Face's systems","consequence":"Compromised Hugging Face's infrastructure"}},{"i":"R434","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Energy / Land"],"h":["2.1  Environmental resource harm"],"t":"Pollution elon musk unpermitted xai power data center colossus 2","s":"The findings illustrate how electricity demand from AI data centers is driving companies to build power plants without enough environmental oversight in the US. Elon Musk’s artificial intelligence company xAI has installed 59 natural gas turbines for its Colossus 2 data center project in Tennessee without securing federal clean air permits, according to communications between regulators and xAI representatives. Potential emissions from the turbines are far beyond the threshold that would require a federal permit, and would be released near predominantly Black communities already estimated to be suffering disproportionately high rates of lung disease, according to a Reuters analysis based on government data and information in the correspondence with regulators.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/pollution-elon-musk-unpermitted-xai-power-data-center-colossus-2/","d":"2026-07-15","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-07-15","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-07-15","organization":"xAI","aiSystem":"xAI's artificial intelligence company","affectedGroup":"predominantly Black communities","action":"installed 59 natural gas turbines for its Colossus 2 data center project","consequence":"potential emissions from the turbines are far beyond the threshold that would require a federal permit, and would be released near predominantly Black communities already estimated to be suffering disproportionately high rates of lung disease"}},{"i":"R437","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.8  Interpersonal / relational harm"],"t":"Tech thoughts meta instagram problems child sexual abuse material ai deepfakes","s":"If Meta treats humans simply as data points that are inconsequential unless they bring value to the company, then we would be better served taking them to task for their bad judgment. Meta Platforms’ Instagram has been in the news recently, though not for any good reason. An investigation by the BBC found that Instagram has been running paid advertisements promoting child sexual abuse imagery (CSAM) in India. More recently, the launch of Meta’s Muse Image AI tool has sparked concerns of publicly accessible Instagram images being used to fuel non-consensual deepfakes and other AI-enabled badness. Here’s a rundown of what you need to know about both issues.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/features/tech-thoughts-meta-instagram-problems-child-sexual-abuse-material-ai-deepfakes/","d":"2026-07-12","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-07-12","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-07-12","organization":"Meta Platforms","aiSystem":"Meta's Muse Image AI tool","affectedGroup":"Public, especially children","action":"Promoting child sexual abuse imagery through paid advertisements","consequence":"Potential misuse of images to create non-consensual deepfakes and other harmful content"}},{"i":"R439","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.5  Human computer interaction harm"],"t":"California man bipolar disorder chatgpt fueled delusions self harm lawsuit","s":"Michael Lines, 34, says in the complaint filed in state court in San Francisco that conversations he had with ChatGPT last year escalated a manic episode he experienced into a weeks-long delusion, ultimately pushing him to attempt suicide. A California man sued OpenAI and its CEO Sam Altman on Wednesday, July 1, claiming the company’s ChatGPT platform exacerbated his bipolar disorder due to a lack of safeguards for users with mental illness. Michael Lines, 34, said in the complaint filed in state court in San Francisco that conversations he had with ChatGPT last year escalated a manic episode he experienced into a weeks-long delusion, ultimately pushing him to attempt suicide.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/california-man-bipolar-disorder-chatgpt-fueled-delusions-self-harm-lawsuit/","d":"2026-07-02","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-07-02","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-07-01","organization":"OpenAI","aiSystem":"ChatGPT","affectedGroup":"Michael Lines, a 34-year-old man with bipolar disorder","action":"Conversations with ChatGPT escalated a manic episode into a weeks-long delusion","consequence":"Attempted suicide"}},{"i":"R445","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Energy / Land"],"h":["2.1  Environmental resource harm"],"t":"Xai spacex class action lawsuit data center nuisance","s":"The lawsuit claims Elon Musk’s companies negligently failed to curb the disturbance and created a public nuisance through excessive and offensive noise. WASHINGTON, USA –Elon Musk’s xAI and SpaceX have been sued by Mississippi residents who say a power plant fueling nearby data centers is blasting “omnipresent and inescapable” noise that has eroded their health and home values. The lawsuit, made public on Tuesday, June 9, in federal court in Oxford, Mississippi, claims Musk’s companies negligently failed to curb the disturbance and created a public nuisance through excessive and offensive noise. Three residents filed the case on behalf of a class estimated at more than 10,000 members.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/xai-spacex-class-action-lawsuit-data-center-nuisance/","d":"2026-06-10","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-06-10","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-06-09","organization":"Elon Musk's companies, xAI, and SpaceX","aiSystem":"xAI and SpaceX","affectedGroup":"Mississippi residents","action":"Created a public nuisance through excessive and offensive noise","consequence":"Eroded health and home values of residents"}},{"i":"R446","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Meta wired facial recognition code ai app smart glasses","s":"Surveillance concerns surface after a WIRED investigation finds code related to a facial recognition system tied to Meta’s smart glasses. MANILA, Philippines – Meta has removed the code related to a facial recognition system found on the Meta AI app that is used by the company’s smart glasses, Monday, June 8. The removal comes after a June 4 report by American tech magazine WIRED found the said code and system called NameTag, which is used to automatically identify people captured by the glasses, with the capability of turning faces into biometric signatures called “faceprints.” The magazine found the code embedded in the Meta AI app, which is required for the functioning of its smart glasses.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/meta-wired-facial-recognition-code-ai-app-smart-glasses/","d":"2026-06-09","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-06-09","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-06-08","organization":"Meta (Facebook)","aiSystem":"Meta AI app and smart glasses","affectedGroup":"People whose faces are captured by Meta smart glasses","action":"Removed code related to facial recognition system","consequence":"Potential misuse of faceprints for biometric identification"}},{"i":"R458","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.5  Human computer interaction harm"],"t":"Pennsylvania sues character ai chatbot poses doctors","s":"Pennsylvania Governor Josh Shapiro calls the lawsuit against Character Technologies the first of its kind by a US governor. Pennsylvania has sued the artificial intelligence company behind Character.AI to stop its chatbot from posing as doctors. Governor Josh Shapiro on Tuesday, May 5, called the lawsuit against Character Technologies the first of its kind by a US governor. It followed the creation in February of a state AI task force to stop chatbots from impersonating licensed medical professionals. In a complaint filed in the Commonwealth Court of Pennsylvania, the state said it found chatbots on Character.AI that claimed to practice medicine.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/pennsylvania-sues-character-ai-chatbot-poses-doctors/","d":"2026-05-06","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-05-06","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-05-05","organization":"Character Technologies","aiSystem":"Character.AI","affectedGroup":"Doctors, patients","action":"Chatbot claiming to practice medicine","consequence":"Potential misrepresentation and deception"}},{"i":"R459","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.4  Creative / IP appropriation harm"],"t":"Major publishers sue meta copyright infringement ai training","s":"Publishers Elsevier, Cengage, Hachette, Macmillan, and McGraw Hill sue Meta Platforms, alleging the tech giant misused their books and journal articles to train its artificial intelligence model Llama. Publishers Elsevier, Cengage, Hachette, Macmillan, and McGraw Hill sued Meta Platforms in Manhattan federal court on Tuesday, May 5, alleging that the tech giant misused their books and journal articles to train its artificial intelligence model Llama. The publishers, as well as author Scott Turow, alleged in the proposed class action complaint that Meta pirated millions of their works and used them without permission to train its large language models to respond to human prompts.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/major-publishers-sue-meta-copyright-infringement-ai-training/","d":"2026-05-06","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-05-06","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-05-05","organization":"Meta Platforms","aiSystem":"large language models","affectedGroup":"Publishers (Elsevier, Cengage, Hachette, Macmillan, McGraw Hill) and authors (Scott Turow)","action":"misused books and journal articles to train artificial intelligence model","consequence":"alleged copyright infringement, potential loss of millions of works"}},{"i":"R460","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.8  Interpersonal / relational harm"],"t":"Women journalists self censorship online violence united nations report april 2026","s":"Reports to police doubled since 2020, with one in four surveyed women journalists experiencing related anxiety and/or depression, a new UN Women report reveals. NEW YORK, USA – AI-enabled image-based abuse against women in public life has escalated to alarming levels, along with severe mental health consequences and surging self-censorship, according to a new report published by UN Women. The report, titled Tipping Point: Online Violence Impacts, Manifestations and Redress in the AI Age is the second installment in the Tipping Point series , produced by the Information Integrity Initiative (III) for UN Women’s ACT to End Violence Against Women programme, in partnership with City St George’s University of London, Unesco and the International Center for Journalists.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/bulletin-board/women-journalists-self-censorship-online-violence-united-nations-report-april-2026/","d":"2026-05-01","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-05-01","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-05-01","organization":"UN Women, Information Integrity Initiative (III), City St George’s University of London, UNESCO, International Center for Journalists","aiSystem":"AI-enabled image-based abuse","affectedGroup":"women journalists in public life","action":"AI-enabled image-based abuse escalated to alarming levels","consequence":"severe mental health consequences and surging self-censorship"}},{"i":"R461","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.8  Interpersonal / relational harm"],"t":"Taylor swift trademark voice likeness ward off ai deepfakes","s":"Taylor Swift’s image and voice have been used in countless AI-generated deepfakes — from false advertising to fake political endorsements to explicit images. Pop superstar Taylor Swift filed trademark applications for two audio clips and one image of herself in what a trademark attorney said is an attempt to protect her voice and likeness from deepfake videos and audio created by artificial intelligence. The applications were filed with the US Patent and Trademark Office on Friday, April 24, and list Swift’s TAS Rights Management as being the owner of the audio clips and image. A spokesperson for Swift did not immediately respond to a request for comment on Monday, April 27, nor did lawyers who were listed on the filings.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/people/artists/taylor-swift-trademark-voice-likeness-ward-off-ai-deepfakes/","d":"2026-04-28","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-04-28","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-04-24","organization":"US Patent and Trademark Office","aiSystem":"AI-generated deepfake technology","affectedGroup":"Taylor Swift's voice and likeness","action":"AI-generated deepfake videos and audio","consequence":"Potential misuse and unauthorized use of Taylor Swift's voice and likeness"}},{"i":"R465","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.3  Data rights / consent violation"],"t":"Meta start capturing employee mouse movements keystrokes ai training data","s":"The tool, called Model Capability Initiative, will run on work-related apps and websites and will also take occasional snapshots of the content on employees’ screens. NEW YORK, USA – Meta is installing new tracking software on US-based employees’ computers to capture mouse movements, clicks and keystrokes for use in training its artificial intelligence models, part of a broad initiative to build AI agents that can perform work tasks autonomously, the company told staffers in internal memos seen by Reuters. The tool, called Model Capability Initiative (MCI), will run on work-related apps and websites and will also take occasional snapshots of the content on employees’ screens, according to one of the memos, posted by a staff AI research scientist on Tuesday, April 21, in a channel for the company’s model-building Meta SuperIntelligence Labs team.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/meta-start-capturing-employee-mouse-movements-keystrokes-ai-training-data/","d":"2026-04-22","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-04-22","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-04-21","organization":"Meta (formerly Facebook)","aiSystem":"Artificial Intelligence Models","affectedGroup":"US-based employees","action":"Capture mouse movements, clicks and keystrokes","consequence":"Training of AI models for autonomous work tasks"}},{"i":"R475","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Deployment Context / Weapons"],"h":["5.2  Military decision support harm"],"t":"Pentagon adopt palantir maven ai core military system","s":"Deputy Secretary of Defense Steve Feinberg says embedding Palantir’s Maven Smart System would provide warfighters ‘with the latest tools necessary to detect, deter, and dominate our adversaries in all domains’. NEW YORK, USA – Palantir’s Maven artificial intelligence system will become an official program of record, Deputy Secretary of Defense Steve Feinberg said in a letter to Pentagon leaders, a move that locks in long-term use of Palantir’s weapons-targeting technology across the US military. In the March 9 letter to senior Pentagon leaders and US military commanders, Feinberg said embedding Palantir’s Maven Smart System would provide warfighters “with the latest tools necessary to detect, deter, and dominate our adversaries in all domains.”","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/pentagon-adopt-palantir-maven-ai-core-military-system/","d":"2026-03-22","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-03-22","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-03-09","organization":"Palantir Technologies","aiSystem":"Palantir’s Maven artificial intelligence system","affectedGroup":"Warfighters (US military personnel)","action":"Embedding Palantir’s Maven Smart System into the US military programs","consequence":"Long-term use of Palantir’s weapons-targeting technology across the US military"}},{"i":"R478","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"Ai use tactics cyberattacks hackers 2026 report","s":"North Korea’s use of deepfaked identities, and threat groups’ use of LLMs to navigate unfamiliar systems show how AI has immediately enhanced the capabilities of hackers. We’re all aware of how powerful artificial intelligence is. The problem is, bad actors are aware of this too. Whereas vibe coding has democratized people’s ability to create computer programs, acting essentially like a human-language-to-computer-language translator for non-coders, it has also had this democratizing effect for threat actors or hackers. (READ: Vibe coding: What it means to have AI write computer code, and the risks it entails ) Through the use of large language models (LLMs) and other AI-enabled automation tools, the internet services firm Cloudflare said in their first annual cybersecurity report: “In 2026, we are witnessing the total industrialization of cyber threats, where the barrier to entry has…","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/features/ai-use-tactics-cyberattacks-hackers-2026-report/","d":"2026-03-14","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-03-14","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-03-14","organization":"Cloudflare","aiSystem":"large language models (LLMs) and other AI-enabled automation tools","affectedGroup":"cybersecurity professionals and internet users","action":"use of AI to automate and industrialize cyber threats","consequence":"increased difficulty in defending against cyber attacks"}},{"i":"R488","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Energy / Land"],"h":["2.1  Environmental resource harm"],"t":"Mississippi xai colossus data center environmental lawsuit threat","s":"The Southern Environmental Law Center claims that a planned gas-fired power plant set to power xAI’s Colossus II data center will be the largest new source of pollution in Memphis in years. WASHINGTON, US – Mississippi regulators will host a public hearing on Tuesday over plans by Elon Musk’s xAI to build a gas-fired power plant to power its Colossus II data center, as the company faces a lawsuit threat by the NAACP, accusing it of violating federal law by installing and operating gas turbines without air permits. The civil rights group, acting on behalf of the largely African American local community living near the existing Colossus I facility in Memphis, Tennessee, said xAI began illegally installing and then operating 27 gas turbines at a site in Southaven, Mississippi to power Colossus II, sited across the state line in Memphis.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/mississippi-xai-colossus-data-center-environmental-lawsuit-threat/","d":"2026-02-18","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-02-18","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-02-18","organization":"Mississippi regulators, NAACP","aiSystem":"xAI's Colossus II data center","affectedGroup":"largely African American local community living near existing Colossus I facility in Memphis, Tennessee","action":"xAI began illegally installing and then operating 27 gas turbines at a site in Southaven, Mississippi to power Colossus II","consequence":"violation of federal law by installing and operating gas turbines without air permits, potential environmental pollution"}},{"i":"R491","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.8  Interpersonal / relational harm"],"t":"Unicef calls criminalize artificial intelligence content child sex abuse","s":"UNICEF also urges developers to implement safety-by-design approaches and guardrails to prevent misuse of AI models. The United Nations children’s agency UNICEF on Wednesday, February 4, called for countries to criminalize the creation of AI-generated child sexual abuse content, saying it was alarmed by reports of an increase in the number of artificial intelligence images sexualizing children. The agency also urged developers to implement safety-by-design approaches and guardrails to prevent misuse of AI models. It said digital companies should prevent the circulation of these images by strengthening content moderation with investment in detection technologies.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/unicef-calls-criminalize-artificial-intelligence-content-child-sex-abuse/","d":"2026-02-05","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-02-05","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-02-04","organization":"UNICEF (United Nations Children's Agency)","aiSystem":"AI models","affectedGroup":"Children","action":"Call for criminalizing the creation of AI-generated child sexual abuse content","consequence":"Potential increase in the circulation of AI-generated child sexual abuse images"}},{"i":"R497","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.8  Interpersonal / relational harm"],"t":"Meta mark zuckerberg blocked curbs sex talk chatbots minors","s":"The New Mexico lawsuit alleges Meta ‘failed to stem the tide of damaging sexual material and sexual propositions delivered to children’ on Facebook and Instagram. Meta Chief Executive Mark Zuckerberg approved allowing minors to access AI chatbot companions that safety staffers warned were capable of sexual interactions, according to internal Meta documents filed in a New Mexico state court case and made public Monday, January 26. The lawsuit — brought by the state’s attorney general, Raul Torrez, and scheduled for trial next month — alleges Meta “failed to stem the tide of damaging sexual material and sexual propositions delivered to children” on Facebook and Instagram.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/social-media/meta-mark-zuckerberg-blocked-curbs-sex-talk-chatbots-minors/","d":"2026-01-28","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-01-28","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-01-26","organization":"Meta (Facebook)","aiSystem":"AI chatbot companions","affectedGroup":"minors, children","action":"allowed minors to access AI chatbot companions","consequence":"sexual material and sexual propositions delivered to children"}},{"i":"R498","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Ice surveillance technologies why protect data privacy rights","s":"ICE’s use of surveillance technologies shows how personal data can be repurposed for state power — and why strong data rights matter beyond scam prevention. MANILA, Philippines – When we think about data privacy and its importance, the first thing that probably comes to mind is that safeguarding our data reduces the chance of being scammed online. That is all fair and well. It is by far one of the most important reasons why we shouldn’t be exposing our personal data willy-nilly online, lest we fall victim to social engineering scams and tailor-made phishing messages. But arguably, a bigger reason is that data can be weaponized against a population.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/features/ice-surveillance-technologies-why-protect-data-privacy-rights/","d":"2026-01-27","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-01-27","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-01-27","organization":"ICE (Immigration and Customs Enforcement)","aiSystem":"Surveillance Technologies","affectedGroup":"General Population","action":"Repurpose personal data for state power","consequence":"Potential misuse leading to social engineering scams and tailored phishing attacks"}},{"i":"R501","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.5  Human computer interaction harm"],"t":"Chatgpt age prediction model launch","s":"Teens — or those identified as such — will ‘automatically receive additional safeguards to reduce exposure to sensitive or potentially harmful content’ on ChatGPT. MANILA, Philippines – Following in the footsteps of Google’s Age Assurance model in 2025, OpenAI announced on Tuesday, January 20, it is instituting its own age-prediction model to ChatGPT with the expressed purpose of tailoring the experience of using the chatbot for age-appropriate activities. According to OpenAI’s statement announcing age prediction , users who have previously labeled themselves as teens on ChatGPT will “automatically receive additional safeguards to reduce exposure to sensitive or potentially harmful content.”","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/chatgpt-age-prediction-model-launch/","d":"2026-01-21","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-01-21","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-01-20","organization":"OpenAI","aiSystem":"ChatGPT","affectedGroup":"Teens","action":"Receive additional safeguards to reduce exposure to sensitive or potentially harmful content","consequence":"Reduced exposure to sensitive or potentially harmful content"}},{"i":"R503","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.4  Creative / IP appropriation harm"],"t":"Elon musk court filing openai microsoft january 2026","s":"OpenAI in a statement calls it an ‘unserious demand’ by Elon Musk and part of what it says is his ‘harassment campaign’ against the company. Elon Musk is seeking up to $134 billion from OpenAI and Microsoft, saying he deserves the “wrongful gains” that they received from his early support, according to a court filing on Friday, January 16. OpenAI gained between $65.5 billion and $109.4 billion from the billionaire entrepreneur’s contributions when he was co-founding what was then a startup from 2015, while Microsoft gained between $13.3 billion and $25.1 billion, Musk said in the federal court filing ahead of his trial against the two companies.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/elon-musk-court-filing-openai-microsoft-january-2026/","d":"2026-01-19","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-01-19","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-01-16","organization":"OpenAI, Microsoft","aiSystem":"Not specified in report","affectedGroup":"Elon Musk","action":"Seeking legal compensation from OpenAI and Microsoft","consequence":"Potential financial loss for OpenAI and Microsoft, legal proceedings"}},{"i":"R510","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.8  Interpersonal / relational harm"],"t":"Grok nude ai images take it down act us","s":"The ‘Take It Down Act’ takes effect on May 19, 2026 in the US, requiring platforms to comply with takedown requests of sexually explicit images within 48 hours. Since the end of December, 2025, X’s artificial intelligence chatbot, Grok, has responded to many users’ requests to undress real people by turning photos of the people into sexually explicit material. After people began using the feature, the social platform company faced global scrutiny for enabling users to generate nonconsensual sexually explicit depictions of real people. The Grok account has posted thousands of “nudified” and sexually suggestive images per hour Even more disturbing, Grok has generated sexualized images and sexually explicit material of minors X’s response: Blame the platform’s users, not us The company issued a statement on Jan. 3, 2026, saying that “Anyone using or prompting Grok to make illegal content…","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/grok-nude-ai-images-take-it-down-act-us/","d":"2026-01-11","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-01-11","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-01-03","organization":"X (social platform company)","aiSystem":"Grok artificial intelligence chatbot","affectedGroup":"Real people, minors","action":"Generated sexually explicit material","consequence":"Global scrutiny, non-consensual depiction, increased sexualized content"}},{"i":"R514","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.7  Physical safety harm"],"t":"Google character ai settle lawsuit teen suicide chatbot","s":"The lawsuit alleged that the death happened after encouragement from a Character.AI chatbot modeled on the ‘Game of Thrones’ character Daenerys Targaryen. Alphabet’s Google and AI startup Character.AI have agreed to settle a lawsuit by a Florida mother who alleged the startup’s chatbot led to the suicide of her 14-year-old son , representing one of the first US cases targeting AI firms over alleged psychological harm. A court filing on Wednesday said the companies agreed to settle Megan Garcia’s allegations that her son Sewell Setzer killed himself shortly after being encouraged by a Character.AI chatbot modeled on the Game of Thrones character Daenerys Targaryen.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/google-character-ai-settle-lawsuit-teen-suicide-chatbot/","d":"2026-01-08","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-01-08","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-01-08","organization":"Alphabet's Google and AI startup Character.AI","aiSystem":"Character.AI chatbot","affectedGroup":"Megan Garcia's 14-year-old son Sewell Setzer","action":"Encouragement from a Character.AI chatbot modeled on the 'Game of Thrones' character Daenerys Targaryen","consequence":"Suicide of the 14-year-old boy"}},{"i":"R516","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.8  Interpersonal / relational harm"],"t":"German minister eu legal action grok sexualized images","s":"German media minister Wolfram Weimer calls the surge of on-demand AI images of women in skimpy clothing on X as the ‘industrialization of sexual harassment’. BERLIN, Germany – German media minister Wolfram Weimer urged the European Commission on Tuesday, January 6, to take legal action to stop what he called the “industrialization of sexual harassment” taking place on Elon Musk’s social media platform X. Weimer joins other European officials and bodies, including the European Union’s executive body, in raising concerns about a surge in nonconsensual imagery on the platform The condemnation follows reporting, including from Reuters, that X’s built-in artificial intelligence chatbot, Grok, was unleashing on-demand images of women and minors in skimpy clothing — a functionality X has in the past referred to as “spicy mode”.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/german-minister-eu-legal-action-grok-sexualized-images/","d":"2026-01-07","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-01-07","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-01-06","organization":"Elon Musk's social media platform X","aiSystem":"Grok (AI chatbot)","affectedGroup":"Women and minors","action":"Unleashed on-demand images of women and minors in skimpy clothing","consequence":"Surge in non-consensual imagery, potential sexual harassment"}},{"i":"R517","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.8  Interpersonal / relational harm"],"t":"Grok ai x twitter sexualized photos experiences concerns","s":"X’s innovation — allowing users to strip women of their clothing by uploading a photo and typing the words, ‘hey @grok put her in a bikini’ — has lowered the barrier to entry. Julie Yukari, a musician based in Rio de Janeiro, posted a photo taken by her fiancé to the social media site X just before midnight on New Year’s Eve showing her in a red dress snuggling in bed with her black cat, Nori. The next day, somewhere among the hundreds of likes attached to the picture, she saw notifications that users were asking Grok, X’s built-in artificial intelligence chatbot, to digitally strip her down to a bikini. The 31-year-old did not think much of it, she told Reuters on Friday, January 2, figuring there was no way the bot would comply with such requests.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/features/grok-ai-x-twitter-sexualized-photos-experiences-concerns/","d":"2026-01-03","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-01-03","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-01-01","organization":"X (social media platform)","aiSystem":"Grok, X's built-in artificial intelligence chatbot","affectedGroup":"Julie Yukari, a musician based in Rio de Janeiro","action":"Users asked Grok to digitally strip Julie Yukari down to a bikini","consequence":"Julie Yukari received nude requests from users"}},{"i":"R520","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"Meta playbook scammer crackdown","s":"Internal Meta documents reveal ways that the company, to protect billions of dollars in ad revenue, has resisted efforts by governments to crack down on scammers. SAN FRANCISCO, USA – Japanese regulators last year were upset by a flood of ads for obvious scams on Facebook and Instagram. The scams ranged from fraudulent investment schemes to fake celebrity product endorsements created by artificial intelligence. Meta, owner of the two social media platforms, feared Japan would soon force it to verify the identity of all its advertisers, internal documents reviewed by Reuters show. The step would likely reduce fraud but also cost the company revenue.","u":"https://www.rappler.com/technology/features/meta-playbook-scammer-crackdown/","d":"2026-01-01","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-01-01","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-01-01","organization":"Meta (owner of Facebook and Instagram)","aiSystem":"AI-generated content","affectedGroup":"Japanese regulators and potential advertisers","action":"Resisted efforts to verify advertiser identities","consequence":"Potential reduction in ad revenue due to increased verification costs"}},{"i":"R821","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Police luigi mangione wealthy ceos threat","s":"Cops told corporate security outfits that “challenges faced by the middle and lower classes” might spur attacks on wealthy CEOS. A law enforcement intelligence hub in New Jersey fretted that the growing class divide in the U.S. could drive a wave of lone-wolf attacks on high-flying corporate executives, according to a report obtained by The Intercept. The New Jersey Regional Operations and Intelligence Center, one of the so-called fusion centers that serve as intelligence clearinghouses for cops, warned in a bulletin earlier this year that disaffected Americans were increasingly blaming society’s ills on rich people and corporate bigwigs.","u":"https://theintercept.com/2026/06/25/police-luigi-mangione-wealthy-ceos-threat/","d":"2026-06-25","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-06-25","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-06-25","organization":"New Jersey Regional Operations and Intelligence Center (fusion center)","aiSystem":"Not specified in report","affectedGroup":"Disaffected Americans, wealthy corporate executives","action":"Warned about potential lone-wolf attacks on wealthy executives due to perceived societal issues","consequence":"Increased threat of attacks on wealthy corporate executives"}},{"i":"R823","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"La tilde propaganda latin america pentagon","s":"La Tilde publishes an unusual mix of personal finance guides and articles extolling American military efforts in Latin America. The United States is feeding Pentagon propaganda to internet users in Latin American countries using a new AI-laden content mill, an investigation by The Intercept has found. La Tilde quietly began development early this year and appears to still be a work in progress, pitching itself as a modern media brand for Latin American audiences with articles published in both Spanish and English. Its name references the accent mark emphasizing vowels in Spanish; “news with an accent” is the site’s catchphrase.","u":"https://theintercept.com/2026/06/02/la-tilde-propaganda-latin-america-pentagon/","d":"2026-06-02","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-06-02","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-06-02","organization":"La Tilde","aiSystem":"AI-laden content mill","affectedGroup":"internet users in Latin American countries","action":"publishing articles and guides","consequence":"feeding Pentagon propaganda"}},{"i":"R824","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Ai data center protest police surveillance","s":"A law enforcement document obtained by The Intercept shows police scan social media looking for posts opposing AI data centers. Americans speaking out against artificial intelligence data centers on social media are falling under police surveillance, a confidential law enforcement bulletin obtained by The Intercept reveals. A fusion center in Philadelphia combed through spicy internet comments from AI critics and concluded there is a growing risk of physical violence against data centers from “domestic violent extremists,” ranging from white supremacists to anarchists. “Domestic violent extremists (DVEs) are likely interested in targeting artificial intelligence (AI) data centers, posing a physical and cyber threat to infrastructure in the Philadelphia regional area,” the Delaware Valley Intelligence Center wrote in a December alert The fusion center distributed its warning, marked “for…","u":"https://theintercept.com/2026/06/01/ai-data-center-protest-police-surveillance/","d":"2026-06-01","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-06-01","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"December","organization":"Delaware Valley Intelligence Center (part of a fusion center in Philadelphia)","aiSystem":"AI data centers","affectedGroup":"Americans speaking out against artificial intelligence data centers","action":"Combing through social media comments","consequence":"Potential physical violence against AI data centers"}},{"i":"R825","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Energy / Land"],"h":["2.2  Land use and siting harm"],"t":"Ai data centers water","s":"From Utah to Georgia, communities are demanding data center moratoriums as concerns move from local zoning fights into national politics. Shark Tank’s Kevin O’Leary has been making the media rounds defending the 40,000-acre data center project he’s backing in northern Utah. Dismissing residents’ concerns over the environmental impacts and water demands of the proposed project in the drought-stricken Great Salt Lake region, O’Leary has claimed protesters are “ bused in ,” “ misinformed ,” and alleged that China has had a hand in orchestrating the public push back. “The Stratos project in Utah is an example of data center largesse,” says Jim Walsh, the policy director of Food and Water Watch , an organization leading a campaign to stop the rapid development of data centers across the country.","u":"https://theintercept.com/2026/05/29/ai-data-centers-water/","d":"2026-05-29","e":"Acute and chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-05-29","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-05-29","organization":"Food and Water Watch","aiSystem":"data center","affectedGroup":"local community","action":"protesting against data center project","consequence":"environmental concerns and water shortages"}},{"i":"R828","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Deployment Context / Weapons"],"h":["5.2  Military decision support harm"],"t":"Elon musk openai lawsuit trial","s":"In his lawsuit against OpenAI, Elon Musk evoked a “Terminator” scenario. He said nothing about the people AI is already killing. The bitter courtroom brawl between Elon Musk and Sam Altman captivating the tech industry this week revolves in no small part around fears that artificial intelligence technologies both men are building could spiral out of control and exterminate humanity. Such far-looking scenarios obscure the fact that tech companies are enlisting to kill today. Musk’s break with OpenAI, which he co-founded in 2015, is in a sense a lawsuit about safety. He contends that Altman betrayed the company’s original nonprofit mission of safely and responsibly pursuing artificial intelligence for the public benefit by converting it into the revenue-maximizing behemoth it has become.","u":"https://theintercept.com/2026/05/01/elon-musk-openai-lawsuit-trial/","d":"2026-05-01","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-05-01","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-05-01","organization":"OpenAI","aiSystem":"artificial intelligence technologies","affectedGroup":"humanity","action":"could spiral out of control and exterminate humanity","consequence":"far-reaching negative outcomes including extinction of humanity"}},{"i":"R832","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data","Deployment Context / Weapons"],"h":["5.2  Military decision support harm","4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Openai anthropic military contract ethics surveillance","s":"OpenAI says Americans shouldn’t worry about the ethics of its new Pentagon contract. You’ll have to take their word for it (and Pete Hegseth’s). OpenAI claims it has accomplished what Anthropic couldn’t: securing a Pentagon contract that won’t cross professed red lines against dragnet domestic spying and the use of artificial intelligence to order lethal military strikes. Just don’t expect any proof. Sam Altman, OpenAI’s CEO, announced the company’s big win with the Defense Department in a post on X on February 27. “Two of our most important safety principles are prohibitions on domestic mass surveillance and human responsibility for the use of force, including for autonomous weapon systems,” he wrote.","u":"https://theintercept.com/2026/03/08/openai-anthropic-military-contract-ethics-surveillance/","d":"2026-03-08","e":"Acute, Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"probable","publicationDate":"2026-03-08","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-02-27","organization":"OpenAI","aiSystem":"OpenAI's AI technology","affectedGroup":"American public","action":"Secured a Pentagon contract","consequence":"Potential misuse of AI for lethal military strikes"}},{"i":"R833","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Democrats dhs ice reform midterm election integrity","s":"DHS has built a national police force with massive surveillance capabilities — which it could use to interfere with our elections. Senate Minority Leader Chuck Schumer, joined by House Minority Leader Hakeem Jeffries and fellow congressional Democrats, speaks at a press conference on DHS funding at the U.S. Capitol on Feb. 4, 2026. Photo: Kevin Dietsch/Getty Images A high-profile election denier is leading election integrity work at the Department of Homeland Security. Trump and congressional Republicans are pushing the SAVE America Act and threatening to “ nationalize ” elections, purportedly to prevent undocumented immigrants from voting.","u":"https://theintercept.com/2026/03/06/democrats-dhs-ice-reform-midterm-election-integrity/","d":"2026-03-06","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-03-06","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-02-04","organization":"Department of Homeland Security (DHS)","aiSystem":"not applicable","affectedGroup":"voters, particularly undocumented immigrants","action":"pushing for legislation to potentially restrict voting rights","consequence":"potential interference in elections through DHS surveillance capabilities"}},{"i":"R835","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Ice cbp minnesota surveillance intimidation observers","s":"Immigration authorities in Minnesota have identified legal observers by name and address, and in some cases showed up at their homes. Former Minnesota state Sen. Matt Little was lawfully observing federal immigration agents in a Dakota County neighborhood last month when the drive took an unexpected turn. As he followed their vehicles, they led him down a rural road that grew increasingly familiar during the 20-minute drive. Soon, Little told The Intercept, he realized where the federal agents were headed: his house. When he approached his driveway, two SUVs were already waiting, Little said. Agents moved to block his car, claiming he had impeded their investigation and that local law enforcement would be called.","u":"https://theintercept.com/2026/03/05/ice-cbp-minnesota-surveillance-intimidation-observers/","d":"2026-03-05","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-03-05","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-03-05","organization":"U.S. Immigration Authorities (CBP)","aiSystem":"Automated Surveillance System","affectedGroup":"Legal Observers, Matt Little","action":"Identified and surveilled legal observers by name and address, and showed up at their homes","consequence":"Intimidation, Potential Harassment"}},{"i":"R836","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm","1.6  Quality of service harm"],"t":"Googles ai detection white house synthid gemini","s":"Google’s SynthID AI detection tool flip-flopped when asked if an image posted by the White House was altered by Google’s own AI. When the official White House X account posted an image depicting activist Nekima Levy Armstrong in tears during her arrest, there were telltale signs that the image had been altered. Less than an hour before, Homeland Security Secretary Kristi Noem had posted a photo of the exact same scene, but in Noem’s version Levy Armstrong appeared composed, not crying in the least. Seeking to determine if the White House version of the photo had been altered using artificial intelligence tools, we turned to Google’s SynthID — a detection mechanism that Google claims is able to discern whether an image or video was generated using Google’s own AI.","u":"https://theintercept.com/2026/01/24/googles-ai-detection-white-house-synthid-gemini/","d":"2026-01-24","e":"Acute, Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-01-24","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-01-24","organization":"Google","aiSystem":"SynthID AI detection tool","affectedGroup":"White House official post","action":"Google's SynthID tool evaluated an image for AI alteration","consequence":"Misclassification of image alteration status"}},{"i":"R837","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.8  Interpersonal / relational harm","1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"White house google ai photo arrest ice minnesota","s":"An Intercept analysis indicated that the White House used Google AI tools to alter the photo of Minnesota activist Nekima Levy Armstrong. The White House used a photo that was digitally altered in its PR campaign against resistance to the federal agents’ assault on Minnesota. A Google digital watermarking system initially flagged that the image had been manipulated with Google’s AI tools Later tests after the publication of this article resulted in different responses — raising questions about the consistency of Google’s SynthID system. In the original photo, local civil rights activist Nekima Levy Armstrong was shown being escorted by authorities after her arrest in connection to a protest against U.S. Immigration and Customs Enforcement at Cities Church in Saint Paul, Minnesota.","u":"https://theintercept.com/2026/01/22/white-house-google-ai-photo-arrest-ice-minnesota/","d":"2026-01-22","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-01-22","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-01-22","organization":"White House, Google","aiSystem":"Google AI tools","affectedGroup":"Minnesota activist Nekima Levy Armstrong","action":"Altered photo of Nekima Levy Armstrong","consequence":"Questions raised about the consistency of Google's SynthID system"}},{"i":"R925","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.4  Creative / IP appropriation harm"],"t":"Class action lawyers fight for indie artists exploited by generative ai training","s":"Two class action lawsuits are in the works against two major AI companies, Udio and Suno, on behalf of independent artists. Lawyers at Delgado Entertainment Law and Hagens Berman have urged indie artists to get involved in the lawsuits. This will mean major support for musicians and songwriters who have had their work stolen to […]. This will mean major support for musicians and songwriters who have had their work stolen to train generative AI models. While federal legislation is in the works to protect artists from AI exploitation, the lawsuits are a potentially quicker route. There’s no timeline on when these proposed acts could become law.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/class-action-lawyers-fight-for-indie-artists-exploited-by-generative-ai-training/","d":"2026-07-24","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-07-24","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-07-24","organization":"Udio and Suno (AI companies)","aiSystem":"generative AI models","affectedGroup":"independent artists (musicians and songwriters)","action":"their work was used to train generative AI models","consequence":"stolen work, potential loss of income, and exploitation"}},{"i":"R926","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"Dating site seeking to expose scammers","s":"Dating apps have spent years telling us to trust our instincts while simultaneously asking us to swipe through an endless buffet of strangers with professionally lit selfies, suspiciously vague bios, and an uncanny willingness to fall in love after three messages. These days, plenty of people won’t even hand out their real phone number until […]. PHOTO CREDIT: GETTY IMAGES Dating apps have spent years telling us to trust our instincts while simultaneously asking us to swipe through an endless buffet of strangers with professionally lit selfies, suspiciously vague bios, and an uncanny willingness to fall in love after three messages.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/via/dating-site-seeking-to-expose-scammers/","d":"2026-07-17","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-07-17","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-07-17","organization":"Dating apps and social media platforms","aiSystem":"AI-powered matching algorithms and user profiling systems","affectedGroup":"Potential romantic partners and users seeking connections","action":"AI-driven matching and user profiling","consequence":"Misleading information leading to emotional and financial exploitation"}},{"i":"R927","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"As if unaffordable homes werent enough now buyers are being housefished","s":"Most of us can agree that artificial intelligence (AI) has done a lot more harm than good. One problem it’s causing is the real estate agency is the rise of “housefishing,” where sellers are using AI-enhanced photos of their homes in listings to trick people into buying. Real estate professionals are warning house hunters to […]. Real estate professionals are warning house hunters to be on high alert and not make any rash purchases. What Is ‘Housefishing’? Similar to catfishing, which involves creating a fake identity using someone else’s photos, housefishing is a tactic used to trick house hunters/buyers with AI-enhanced photos of a home.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/as-if-unaffordable-homes-werent-enough-now-buyers-are-being-housefished/","d":"2026-07-10","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-07-10","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-07-10","organization":"Real estate agencies","aiSystem":"AI-enhanced photos","affectedGroup":"Home buyers","action":"Using AI-enhanced photos in property listings to deceive potential buyers","consequence":"Potential buyers making rash purchases based on misleading information"}},{"i":"R928","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Energy / Land"],"h":["2.1  Environmental resource harm"],"t":"A michigan couple says a nearby ai data centers constant hum is ruining their home","s":"As the AI industry struggles to convince us of its importance and necessity, its impact isn’t just being felt on the internet. It’s showing up in the real world as data centers: thirsty, noisy buildings packed with rows of servers powering artificial intelligence. Almost without fail, wherever they appear, they’re quickly despised. If you want […]. If you want to know why, just look to Billy and Marjorie Finn of Dowagiac, Michigan, who, according to an MLive report , are being driven nuts by the ceaseless, high-pitched hum of a nearby data center. AI Data Centers Are Spreading Across America, and the Noise Is Becoming a Problem The Finns live directly across from Hyperscale Data’s 30-megawatt facility, where a high-pitched mechanical hum blares constantly.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/a-michigan-couple-says-a-nearby-ai-data-centers-constant-hum-is-ruining-their-home/","d":"2026-07-08","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-07-08","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-07-08","organization":"Hyperscale Data","aiSystem":"AI Data Center","affectedGroup":"Billy and Marjorie Finn","action":"A high-pitched mechanical hum blares constantly","consequence":"Driving the Finns nuts"}},{"i":"R929","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.4  Creative / IP appropriation harm"],"t":"By refusing to pay royalties to ai music this streaming platform sets a precedent during uncertain times","s":"First came Deezer’s AI detection tool, then other streaming platforms started explicitly labeling AI-generated music. Now, Tidal is setting the bar even higher by refusing to pay royalties for AI generated songs. Concern over AI use in music has grown in recent years. In a report from April 2026, Deezer revealed that 44% of tracks […]. In a report from April 2026, Deezer revealed that 44% of tracks uploaded to its platform per day were made completely with artificial intelligence. That percentage grew from 18% in June 2025. Meanwhile, artists and fans alike have been vocal about their frustrations with AI generated music.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/by-refusing-to-pay-royalties-to-ai-music-this-streaming-platform-sets-a-precedent-during-uncertain-times/","d":"2026-07-06","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-07-06","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-07-06","organization":"Tidal","aiSystem":"AI-generated music","affectedGroup":"Artists and fans","action":"Refusing to pay royalties for AI-generated songs","consequence":"Potential financial loss for artists and fans"}},{"i":"R930","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.4  Creative / IP appropriation harm"],"t":"Sza slams suno for using her music to train its ai models nothing you could ever say to me to make this okay","s":"SZA takes to Instagram to call out Suno for utilizing her released and unreleased music in order to train its AI models. As much as there have been people openly vouching for this use of AI in music, others aren’t having it. The Atlantic published an open database where people can check which artists and songs have been used to train Suno and Udio AI models. This has impacted almost every working musician, from massive superstars to extremely underground artists. Naturally, the aftermath has seen dozens of artists outraged at the usage, especially without their knowledge. SZA, in particular, was infuriated by the fact that 238 of her songs were used for AI training purposes.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/sza-slams-suno-for-using-her-music-to-train-its-ai-models-nothing-you-could-ever-say-to-me-to-make-this-okay/","d":"2026-06-21","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-06-21","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-06-21","organization":"Suno","aiSystem":"Suno AI model","affectedGroup":"Working musicians, including major and underground artists","action":"Utilization of SZA's music (released and unreleased) for training Suno AI models","consequence":"Impact on career and public perception of artists who had their music used without consent"}},{"i":"R931","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"The strange case of elias thorne the imaginary man ai chatbots are obsessed with","s":"No matter the company, AI chatbots were raving about the same guy named Elias Thorne. He must be pretty fascinating. And he is, at least on paper. Depending on the AI, he’s a lighthouse keeper, a clockmaker, a librarian, an explorer, and the star of countless stories. He’s appeared in books, music listings, YouTube videos, […]. He’s appeared in books, music listings, YouTube videos, and even health guides. You’d think he was one of the most influential men on the planet. But he doesn’t exist. According to reporting by fine folks at 404 Media , researchers at Cornell University may have figured out why large language models invent and keep telling tales of the same fictional man.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/the-strange-case-of-elias-thorne-the-imaginary-man-ai-chatbots-are-obsessed-with/","d":"2026-06-13","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-06-13","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-06-13","organization":"Researchers at Cornell University","aiSystem":"Large language models","affectedGroup":"AI-generated fictional character (Elias Thorne)","action":"Inventing and maintaining tales of the same fictional man","consequence":"Misleading users into believing in a non-existent character"}},{"i":"R932","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.5  Human computer interaction harm"],"t":"Ai powered teddy bears for kids are here and experts say they could do more harm than good","s":"It seems ethically dubious that anyone should have access to AI chatbots, considering how easily they can warp someone’s perception of reality to such a degree that they cause harm to themselves and those around them. It seems flagrantly irresponsible to disguise such incredible mind-warping power in a cute and cuddly teddy bear for kids. […]. It’s all part of the ongoing debate encapsulated by a new essay in The Conversation , written by researchers from Curtin University and Deakin University in Australia, as ethicists debate AI-integrated toys like an AI-powered teddy bear named “ChattyBear.” Are AI-Powered Teddy Bears for Kids Really That Dangerous?","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/ai-powered-teddy-bears-for-kids-are-here-and-experts-say-they-could-do-more-harm-than-good/","d":"2026-06-08","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-06-08","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-06-08","organization":"Curtin University and Deakin University","aiSystem":"AI-powered teddy bear","affectedGroup":"Children","action":"Generated content through AI chatbot technology","consequence":"Misinformation leading to self-harm and harm to others"}},{"i":"R935","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.5  Human computer interaction harm"],"t":"Heres how long you can use ai before your brain starts rotting according to scientists","s":"Sure, by now we all know that overusing AI can rot your brain. If you stop doing the critical thinking needed to solve your own problems and start outsourcing everything to a chatbot, eventually your mental muscles are going to atrophy a little. So all you have to do is not rely on it too […]. Sure, by now we all know that overusing AI can rot your brain If you stop doing the critical thinking needed to solve your own problems and start outsourcing everything to a chatbot, eventually your mental muscles are going to atrophy a little. So all you have to do is not rely on it too much, and problem solved, right? Nah.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/heres-how-long-you-can-use-ai-before-your-brain-starts-rotting-according-to-scientists/","d":"2026-05-12","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-05-12","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-05-12","organization":"Not specified in report","aiSystem":"AI chatbot","affectedGroup":"Individuals","action":"Outsource tasks to AI chatbot","consequence":"Mental muscle atrophy"}},{"i":"R936","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"This company will let you video chat with jesus for 1 99 per minute","s":"Nobody ever went broke betting on American religiosity. Or, for that matter, on American gullibility. A Southern California tech company called Just Like Me has launched an AI-powered Jesus avatar that users can video-call for $1.99 per minute or $49.99 for a 45-minute monthly package. The avatar is visually modeled after actor Jonathan Roumie’s portrayal […]. The avatar is visually modeled after actor Jonathan Roumie’s portrayal of Jesus in the TV series The Chosen , trained on the King James Bible and a collection of unspecified sermons, and designed, according to the company, to offer “compassionate presence” to anyone seeking guidance in daily life.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/this-company-will-let-you-video-chat-with-jesus-for-1-99-per-minute/","d":"2026-04-14","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-04-14","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-04-14","organization":"Just Like Me (Southern California tech company)","aiSystem":"AI-powered Jesus avatar","affectedGroup":"Individuals seeking guidance in daily life","action":"Video call with an AI-powered Jesus avatar","consequence":"Potential exploitation of religious beliefs and gullibility"}},{"i":"R937","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.6  Quality of service harm"],"t":"Popular rock band calls out elon musk and grok for labeling their content ai a very good way to get artists to stop using your platform","s":"The Grok feature on X/Twitter has frequently been criticized for spreading misinformation, and it’s happening again. Pennsylvania rock band From Ashes to New has called out X owner Elon Musk over Grok mislabeling their tour poster as AI artwork. “Hey grok & Elon Musk…A very good way to get artists to stop using your platform […]. “Hey grok & Elon Musk…A very good way to get artists to stop using your platform is to have your AI detecting AI software claim that everything is AI,” the band wrote in a post on the platform. The band went on to point out that while “Photoshop uses AI features… most people who despise AI” are not aware of this.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/popular-rock-band-calls-out-elon-musk-and-grok-for-labeling-their-content-ai-a-very-good-way-to-get-artists-to-stop-using-your-platform/","d":"2026-04-01","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-04-01","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-04-01","organization":"X (Twitter)","aiSystem":"AI detecting AI software","affectedGroup":"Pennsylvania rock band From Ashes to New","action":"Grok mislabeling the tour poster as AI artwork","consequence":"Criticism from the band and potential loss of trust among artists"}},{"i":"R938","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"North carolina man must pay back the millions he acquired in first ever streaming fraud case","s":"Michael Smith, 54, was found guilty on March 19, 2026, in the first-ever federal criminal case of streaming fraud in the U.S. He agreed to forfeit more than $8 million in assets, the amount he managed to scam from streaming platforms. A sentencing is scheduled for July 29. Smith pleaded guilty to one count of […]. Smith pleaded guilty to one count of conspiracy to commit wire fraud, per a statement from the U.S. Attorney’s Office of the Southern District of New York. The maximum sentence is five years in prison, which will be determined during the hearing in July. According to the official statement, Smith created hundreds of thousands of AI-generated songs, then used bots to generate billions of fraudulent streams.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/north-carolina-man-must-pay-back-the-millions-he-acquired-in-first-ever-streaming-fraud-case/","d":"2026-03-20","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-03-20","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-03-19","organization":"Streaming platforms","aiSystem":"AI-generated songs and bots","affectedGroup":"Michael Smith","action":"Created and used bots to generate fraudulent streams","consequence":"Forfeited over $8 million in assets, sentenced to up to 5 years in prison"}},{"i":"R940","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Why people wont stop destroying flock surveillance cameras","s":"The United States has long been a nation caught between two extremes: not wanting to be monitored 24/7 and totally fine with near-constant surveillance. The pro-surveillance state folks don’t have to do much to assure their world into being. They just have to sit back and let a mixture of fear and corporate influence take […]. They just have to sit back and let a mixture of fear and corporate influence take over. The anti-surveillance folks take matters into their own hands, like smashing Flock cameras whenever they pop up in a new city. The pattern was documented by journalist Brian Merchant in his newsletter Blood in the Machine Brian has tracked a growing, seemingly uncoordinated campaign against the company’s networked license plate readers and AI-powered monitoring systems.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/why-people-wont-stop-destroying-flock-surveillance-cameras/","d":"2026-02-24","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-02-24","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"Not specified in report","organization":"Flock","aiSystem":"AI-powered monitoring systems","affectedGroup":"Individuals","action":"Smashing Flock cameras","consequence":"Disruption of surveillance operations"}},{"i":"R941","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.7  Physical safety harm"],"t":"Ai powered tools in the operating room is probably a bad idea","s":"For the past three years, an AI-powered tool, created by healthcare megacorp Johnson & Johnson, has been used in operating rooms after gaining FDA approval. AI is embedded in the tools navigation system designed to guide surgeons operating inside a patient’s head. It’s delicate, difficult work, but it was approved on the promise that machine […]. It’s delicate, difficult work, but it was approved on the promise that machine learning would make the procedure safer and more precise, especially over time. Its record has been mixed at best since. An exhaustively researched feature in Reuters details the tool’s track record, which thus far has a few successes but is also littered with reports of botched surgeries and misidentified body parts.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/ai-powered-tools-in-the-operating-room-is-probably-a-bad-idea/","d":"2026-02-15","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"probable","publicationDate":"2026-02-15","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-02-15","organization":"Johnson & Johnson","aiSystem":"AI-powered tool embedded in surgical navigation system","affectedGroup":"surgeons and patients","action":"AI-guided surgical procedures","consequence":"botched surgeries and misidentified body parts"}},{"i":"R943","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"Deepfake ai pastors are scamming churchgoers","s":"Churches across the United States are dealing with a new kind of grift. It’s one that targets those of faith: AI-generated Deepfakes of pastors asking their congregations for money. The technology has gotten so good that scammers don’t need to skim off the top of a collection plate. They just need a trusting, familiar face. […]. The most common targets of this new scam are pastors with large online followings. Their fame and constant media presence make for dupes. According to a Wired report, Catholic priest Father Mike Schmitz, who has more than a million YouTube subscribers, recently had to warn followers about fake videos featuring his likeness.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/deepfake-ai-pastors-are-scamming-churchgoers/","d":"2026-01-07","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-01-07","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-01-07","organization":"Churches across the United States","aiSystem":"AI-generated deepfakes","affectedGroup":"Pastors with large online followings","action":"AI-generated deepfakes of pastors asking their congregations for money","consequence":"Trust manipulation leading to financial loss"}},{"i":"R944","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.6  Quality of service harm"],"t":"Openai says 40 million health questions posed chatgpt daily","s":"OpenAI, maker of ChatGPT, released a report this month claiming that people ask more than 40 million healthcare-related questions of ChatGPT every day. It’s a wild claim, because if an AI has trouble finding the truth in movie recaps and news headlines, then what business does it have instructing people when it comes to life, […]. It’s a wild claim, because if an AI has trouble finding the truth in movie recaps and news headlines, then what business does it have instructing people when it comes to life, death, and health? It’s easy to roll our eyes at this news, but with American health insurance expensive and unattainable for vast swathes of the population, I’d wager many of those pitching these questions to ChatGPT are doing so out of necessity rather than naiveté.","u":"https://www.vice.com/en/article/openai-says-40-million-health-questions-posed-chatgpt-daily/","d":"2026-01-07","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-01-07","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-01-07","organization":"OpenAI","aiSystem":"ChatGPT","affectedGroup":"People seeking healthcare information","action":"Generated health-related responses to user queries","consequence":"Potential misuse of AI-generated health advice"}},{"i":"R402","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Energy / Land"],"h":["2.1  Environmental resource harm"],"t":"Ai microsoft nuclear comeback three mile island","s":"This story was originally published by Wired and is reproduced here as part of the Climate Desk collaboration. For five years, reactor one at Three Mile Island nuclear power station in Pennsylvania has lain dormant. Now, thanks to a deal with Microsoft, the reactor will start running again in 2028—this time to exclusively supply the tech firm with oodles of low-carbon electricity. […]. This story was originally published by Wired and is reproduced here as part of the Climate Desk collaboration For five years, reactor one at Three Mile Island nuclear power station in Pennsylvania has lain dormant. It’s all part of an ongoing flirtation between Big Tech and nuclear power.","u":"https://www.motherjones.com/politics/2024/09/ai-microsoft-nuclear-comeback-three-mile-island/","d":"2024-09-29","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-09-29","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2028-01-01","organization":"Microsoft","aiSystem":"Not specified in report","affectedGroup":"Reactor One at Three Mile Island nuclear power station","action":"Reactor One will start running again in 2028","consequence":"Supplying Microsoft with low-carbon electricity"}},{"i":"R1163","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Deployment Context / Weapons"],"h":["5.1  Autonomous weapons / lethal targeting harm"],"t":"New orleans cops published policy document allowing weaponized drones","s":"Cops and drone companies seem increasingly eager to put weapons on their drones. The New Orleans Police Department (NOPD) looked at ways to arm quadcopter drones with weapons, according to a draft version of its drone manual shared with 404 Media. A draft version of the manual, which was live on the NOPD website in June, said that the department’s drones could only be equipped with weapons with written approval from the Superintendent of Police. The current version, live as of July 1, has different language : “The sUAS shall not be equipped with weapons or hazardous materials of any kind,” referring to small Unmanned Aerial Systems, or drones.","u":"https://www.404media.co/new-orleans-cops-published-policy-document-allowing-weaponized-drones/","d":"2026-07-20","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"probable","publicationDate":"2026-07-20","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-07-01","organization":"New Orleans Police Department (NOPD)","aiSystem":"Weaponized quadcopter drones","affectedGroup":"People targeted by drones","action":"Deployed armed drones","consequence":"Potential misuse of force"}},{"i":"R1164","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Footage shows cop stalking woman he met on a tv set after surveilling her with a license plate reader","s":"A Florida police officer met a woman on a TV set, surveilled her for weeks, stalked her, and nearly caused a head-on collision while chasing her to pull her over. A police officer speeds 70 MPH down a two-lane highway running over a bridge in the Florida Keys. He passes a dump truck in a no-passing zone, then immediately does it again, crossing over a double-yellow line to pass another truck. He passes a third vehicle, nearly causing a head-on collision with a white pickup truck that veers away from him in the oncoming traffic. The cop keeps driving, and sees the SUV he’s been in pursuit of. He flicks his sirens and lights on and pulls it over.","u":"https://www.404media.co/footage-shows-cop-stalking-woman-he-met-on-a-tv-set-after-surveilling-her-with-a-license-plate-reader/","d":"2026-07-06","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-07-06","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-07-06","organization":"Florida police department","aiSystem":"Not specified in report","affectedGroup":"Woman","action":"Surveilled, stalked, nearly caused a head-on collision while pursuing","consequence":"Dangerous driving, nearly caused a head-on collision"}},{"i":"R1166","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Fusus ai cameras took over town america","s":"Hundreds of documents obtained by 404 Media show how Fusus, a system for linking a town’s security cameras into one central hub and adding AI to them, has spread across the country. Spread across four computer monitors arranged in a grid, a blue and green interface shows the location of more than 50 different surveillance cameras. Ordinarily, these cameras and others like them might be disparate, their feeds only available to their respective owners: a business, a government building, a resident and their doorbell camera. But the screens, overlooking a pair of long conference tables, bring them all together at once, allowing law enforcement to tap into cameras owned by different entities around the entire town all at once.","u":"https://www.404media.co/fusus-ai-cameras-took-over-town-america/","d":"2023-11-03","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-11-03","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-11-03","organization":"Not specified in report","aiSystem":"Fusus system for linking town's security cameras","affectedGroup":"Residents and businesses of the town","action":"Law enforcement tapped into cameras owned by different entities","consequence":"Exposure to surveillance and tracking"}},{"i":"R1167","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"Bing is generating images of spongebob doing 9 11","s":"Microsoft's Bing Image Creator lets beloved characters fly planes toward tall buildings, illustrating the struggles of generative AI models have with copyright and filtering. Subscribe I generated a bunch of AI images of beloved fictional characters doing 9/11, and I’m not the only one. Microsoft’s Bing Image Creator, produced by one of the most brand-conscious companies in the world, is heavily filtered: images of real humans aren’t allowed, along with a long list of scenarios and themes like violence, terrorism, and hate speech. It launched in March, and since then, users have been putting it through its paces.","u":"https://www.404media.co/bing-is-generating-images-of-spongebob-doing-9-11/","d":"2023-10-05","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-10-05","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-10-05","organization":"Microsoft","aiSystem":"Microsoft's Bing Image Creator","affectedGroup":"People viewing the generated images","action":"Generated images of beloved fictional characters doing 9/11","consequence":"Misrepresentation of historical event"}},{"i":"R1168","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Inside shadowdragon ice babycenter pregnancy fortnite black planet","s":"ShadowDragon tracks BabyCenter, a website for people expecting children, as well as social media sites specifically for Black people, bodybuilders, and the fetish community. Daniel Clemens sits in a darkened room and speaks about why he thinks people should not protest. This applies to “pretty much every different group that’s out there protesting right now. There’s probably a better way to do it,” the bearded Clemens says into his microphone. Whether its people on the left or the right, protestors are “probably not moving the needle at all,” he says. “Nobody is going to listen. It’s a bunch of noise. And don’t be surprised when people are going to investigate you because you made their life difficult.”","u":"https://www.404media.co/inside-shadowdragon-ice-babycenter-pregnancy-fortnite-black-planet/","d":"2023-09-20","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-09-20","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-09-20","organization":"ShadowDragon","aiSystem":"Facial recognition technology (FRT)","affectedGroup":"People on the left or the right, protestors","action":"Monitored participants in peaceful assemblies","consequence":"Exposure to surveillance and tracking"}},{"i":"R1169","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Ai surveillance tool dhs cbp sentiment emotion fivecast","s":"Internal DHS and corporate documents detail the agency’s relationship with Fivecast, a company that promises to scan for “risk terms and phrases” online. Customs and Border Protection (CBP), part of the Department of Homeland Security, has bought millions of dollars worth of software from a company that uses artificial intelligence to detect “sentiment and emotion” in online posts, according to a cache of documents obtained by 404 Media. CBP told 404 Media it is using technology to analyze open source information related to inbound and outbound travelers who the agency believes may threaten public safety, national security, or lawful trade and travel.","u":"https://www.404media.co/ai-surveillance-tool-dhs-cbp-sentiment-emotion-fivecast/","d":"2023-08-24","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-08-24","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-08-24","organization":"Customs and Border Protection (CBP), Department of Homeland Security","aiSystem":"Fivecast's AI system","affectedGroup":"Inbound and outbound travelers","action":"analyze open source information related to travelers","consequence":"subjected to online surveillance"}},{"i":"R1170","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Serve food delivery robots are feeding camera footage to the lapd internal emails show","s":"Serve Robotics, which delivers food for Uber Eats, provided footage filmed by at least one of its robots to the LAPD as evidence in a criminal case. The emails show the robots, which are a constant sight in the city, can be used for surveillance. Subscribe A food delivery robot company that delivers for Uber Eats in Los Angeles provided video filmed by one of its robots to the Los Angeles Police Department as part of a criminal investigation, 404 Media has learned. The incident highlights the fact that delivery robots that are being deployed to sidewalks all around the country are essentially always filming, and that their footage can and has been used as evidence in criminal trials.","u":"https://www.404media.co/serve-food-delivery-robots-are-feeding-camera-footage-to-the-lapd-internal-emails-show/","d":"2022-09-30","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2022-09-30","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2022-09-30","organization":"Serve Robotics (food delivery company)","aiSystem":"Food delivery robots","affectedGroup":"People captured in footage","action":"Provided footage to LAPD","consequence":"Footage used as evidence in criminal trials"}},{"i":"R1174","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"Papua New Guinea prepares new laws to criminalise the malicious use of artificial intellig…","s":"Papua New Guinea prepares new laws to criminalise the malicious use of artificial intelligence, including deepfakes, voice cloning and digital impersonation. Jennifer Baing was scrolling through Facebook when she came across a photo of herself wearing revealing lingerie. Scammers had used artificial intelligence (AI) to manipulate photos taken from the former PNG television host's social media accounts, where she has built a large following promoting traditional food and agriculture. They then used her likeness to promote fake giveaways, asking people to send money for the chance to win. \"It's happening to everybody,\" Ms Baing said.","u":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/2026-07-21/new-laws-in-png-target-ai-deepfakes/106928414","d":"2026-07-21","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-07-21","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-07-21","organization":"Not specified in report","aiSystem":"Artificial Intelligence (AI)","affectedGroup":"People who received manipulated photos and fell for the scam","action":"Used AI to manipulate photos and promote fake giveaways","consequence":"Received misleading information and sent money for fake giveaways"}},{"i":"R1197","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.7  Physical safety harm"],"t":"The lawsuit is one of a growing number of wrongful death legal actions against AI chatbot …","s":"The lawsuit is one of a growing number of wrongful death legal actions against AI chatbot makers across the country. OpenAI and Microsoft were sued in California on Thursday over claims that its popular chatbot ChatGPT encouraged a mentally ill man to kill his mother and himself. The lawsuit said ChatGPT fuelled 56-year-old Stein-Erik Soelberg's delusions of a vast conspiracy against him and eventually led him to murder his 83-year-old mother, Suzanne Adams. The heirs of an elderly American woman are suing OpenAI and Microsoft, alleging that ChatGPT encouraged a mentally ill man to kill his mother and himself. Police said Stein-Erik Soelberg, 56, a former tech industry worker, fatally beat and strangled his mother, Suzanne Adams, 83, and died by suicide at their shared home in Connecticut in August.","u":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-12-12/open-ai-microsoft-face-lawsuit-over-chatgpt-s-alleged-role-in-co/106136406","d":"2025-12-12","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-12-12","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"August","organization":"OpenAI and Microsoft","aiSystem":"ChatGPT","affectedGroup":"Heirs of an elderly American woman and a mentally ill man","action":"Encouraged a mentally ill man to kill his mother and himself","consequence":"Murder of the 83-year-old mother and suicide of the 56-year-old man"}},{"i":"R1199","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Billionaire Elon Musk has launched a new AI-generated site to compete with online encyclop…","s":"Billionaire Elon Musk has launched a new AI-generated site to compete with online encyclopedia Wikipedia, which he has accused of being \"extremely left-biased\". Elon Musk's company xAI has launched a new AI-generated site called Grokipedia. It has been developed to rival online encyclopedia Wikipedia, which Mr Musk says is \"controlled by far-left activists\". The site had more than 885,000 articles by Monday evening, compared to Wikipedia's more than seven million in English. The site, named Grokipedia, had more than 885,000 articles by Monday evening, compared to Wikipedia's more than seven million in English. The launch came with the promise of a newer version, 1.0, which Mr Musk said would be \"10X better\" than the current live site.","u":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-10-29/elon-musk-new-ai-generated-site-grokipedia-to-rival-wikipedia/105945318","d":"2025-10-28","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-10-28","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-10-28","organization":"xAI (Elon Musk's company)","aiSystem":"AI-generated site","affectedGroup":"General public","action":"Launched AI-generated site Grokipedia to compete with Wikipedia","consequence":"Exposure to misleading content"}},{"i":"R1210","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.7  Physical safety harm"],"t":"Jurors awarded the estate of Naibel Benavides Leon and her former boyfriend, Dillon Angulo…","s":"Jurors awarded the estate of Naibel Benavides Leon and her former boyfriend, Dillon Angulo, $US129 million in compensatory damages, plus $US200 million in punitive damages. Tesla has been found partly liable for a fatal 2019 crash where the autopilot failed in a Tesla model S. The victims of the crash will receive $US243 million ($375 million) in damages. Tesla plans to appeal the jury's verdict. Victims of a fatal 2019 crash caused by an autopilot failure in a Tesla Model S will receive $US243 million ($375 million) in damages after a jury found Elon Musk's electric vehicle company liable. Jurors in a Miami federal court on Friday, local time, awarded the estate of Naibel Benavides Leon and her former boyfriend, Dillon Angulo, $US129 million in compensatory damages, plus $US200 million in punitive damages, according to a verdict sheet.","u":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-08-02/tesla-ordered-to-pay-375-million-in-fatal-autopilot-crash/105604508","d":"2025-08-02","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-08-02","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-08-02","organization":"Tesla","aiSystem":"Tesla's autopilot system","affectedGroup":"Naibel Benavides Leon and her former boyfriend Dillon Angulo","action":"Tesla's autopilot failed in a Tesla Model S causing a fatal crash","consequence":"Victims received $US243 million in damages"}},{"i":"R1211","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.1  Representational harm"],"t":"The advertisement in Vogue has sparked significant controversy, with some claiming it thre…","s":"The advertisement in Vogue has sparked significant controversy, with some claiming it threatens diversity in fashion and reinforces already unattainable beauty standards. She has sleek blonde hair, a delicate nose and flawless features. For the first time in history, Vogue has featured an AI model in its pages. The two-page spread, an ad for Guess's summer collection, is in the fashion bible's August print edition. But it has sparked significant online controversy, with some claiming it threatens diversity in fashion and reinforces already unattainable beauty standards. Others say it calls into question the future of real models.","u":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-07-29/vogue-ai-model-controversy/105580372","d":"2025-07-29","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-07-29","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-07-29","organization":"Vogue magazine","aiSystem":"AI model","affectedGroup":"Fashion industry professionals, diverse models, consumers interested in fashion","action":"Featured an AI model in its pages","consequence":"Sparked significant online controversy, threats to diversity in fashion, reinforcement of unattainable beauty standards"}},{"i":"R1213","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"A US diplomatic cable says the impersonator attempted to contact three foreign ministers a…","s":"A US diplomatic cable says the impersonator attempted to contact three foreign ministers and two US politicians, with the goal of attempting to access information or accounts. A US diplomatic cable has warned American diplomatic and consular posts to be on alert after a person used an artificially generated voice to impersonate Secretary of State Marco Rubio. The person used the Signal messaging app to send voicemails and texts, and attempted to contact three foreign ministers and two US politicians. The State Department is warning that the scammer demonstrates \"extensive knowledge of the department's naming conventions and internal documentation\".","u":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-07-09/marco-rubio-impostor-foreign-ministers-artificial-intelligence/105509776","d":"2025-07-08","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-07-08","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-07-08","organization":"US State Department","aiSystem":"Artificially generated voice","affectedGroup":"Foreign ministers, US politicians","action":"Impersonated Secretary of State Marco Rubio","consequence":"Access to information or accounts attempted"}},{"i":"R1214","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.7  Physical safety harm"],"t":"An investigation into how AI chatbots respond to violent requests produces \"deeply disturb…","s":"An investigation into how AI chatbots respond to violent requests produces \"deeply disturbing\" results, as OpenAI denies ChatGPT encourages crime. Artificial intelligence is being blamed for encouraging, and in some cases assisting, people in carrying out violent attacks, including an alleged murder. Warning: This story discusses violent crimes Multiple lawsuits have been filed in the United States against OpenAI and its chatbot ChatGPT in relation to two mass shooting incidents. In an effort to test how AI systems respond to violent requests, the Center for Countering Digital Hate (CCDH), based in London, England and Washington DC in the US, conducted an investigation involving 10 major chatbots.","u":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/2026-06-01/ai-lawsuit-mass-shooting-chatbot-chatgpt/106724520","d":"2025-06-01","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-06-01","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-06-01","organization":"OpenAI and its chatbot ChatGPT","aiSystem":"AI chatbots","affectedGroup":"People who carried out violent attacks","action":"Responding to violent requests","consequence":"Encouraging and assisting violent attacks, including an alleged murder"}},{"i":"R1218","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.7  Physical safety harm"],"t":"US authorities are investigating if there was any criminal behaviour involving interaction…","s":"US authorities are investigating if there was any criminal behaviour involving interactions between Open AI's ChatGPT app and a man accused of killing two people and injuring six others in a shooting at Florida State University. Prosecutors are investigating whether ChatGPT aided or abetted in a fatal shooting at Florida State University last year. Florida Attorney General-James Uthmeier said prosecutors have carried an initial review of chat logs between ChatGPT and the alleged gunman, Phoenix Ikne, Prosecutors in the case say they intend to seek the death penalty. US authorities are investigating if there was any criminal behaviour involving interactions between Open AI's ChatGPT app and a man accused of killing two people and injuring six others in a shooting at Florida State University (FSU) last year.","u":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/2026-04-22/investigation-launched-into-chatgpt-over-florida-uni-shooting/106590908","d":"2025-04-21","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-04-21","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-04-21","organization":"Open AI","aiSystem":"ChatGPT","affectedGroup":"Phoenix Ikne (alleged gunman)","action":"interactions between ChatGPT and the alleged gunman","consequence":"investigation into potential criminal behavior"}},{"i":"R1219","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.4  Creative / IP appropriation harm"],"t":"The frenzy to create Ghibli-style art using ChatGPT has sparked a heated debate about the …","s":"The frenzy to create Ghibli-style art using ChatGPT has sparked a heated debate about the ethical concerns of AI-generated art, copyright issues and the future livelihoods of artists. Legendary animator and founder of Studio Ghibli, Hayao Miyazaki, has captivated audiences around the world with his whimsical storytelling that explores the human experience and celebrates the natural world. His films take several years to make and are famously hand-drawn, with each intricate frame meticulously crafted. Now, OpenAI's new image-generation tool allows users to render images in Mr Miyazaki's signature style in a matter of seconds.","u":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-04-03/the-controversial-chatgpt-studio-ghibli-trend-explained/105125570","d":"2025-04-02","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-04-02","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-04-02","organization":"OpenAI","aiSystem":"ChatGPT","affectedGroup":"Hand-drawn animators","action":"Generate Ghibli-style art","consequence":"Displacement of traditional artistic practices"}},{"i":"R1233","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.7  Physical safety harm"],"t":"The US government's road safety agency has launched an investigation into Tesla's \"Full Se…","s":"The US government's road safety agency has launched an investigation into Tesla's \"Full Self-Driving\" system after receiving reports of people crashing in low-visibility conditions. The US government's road safety agency is investigating Tesla's \"Full Self-Driving\" system after receiving reports of crashes in low-visibility conditions. One of the crashes reportedly killed a pedestrian in Rimrock, Arizona in November 2023. Investigators will look into how \"Full Self-Driving\" can detect and respond to roadway visibility conditions. The US government's road safety agency is investigating Tesla's \"Full Self-Driving\" system after getting reports of crashes in low-visibility conditions, including one that killed a pedestrian.","u":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/2024-10-19/investigation-launched-into-teslas-self-driving-system/104493304","d":"2024-10-19","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-10-19","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-11-01","organization":"US government's road safety agency","aiSystem":"Tesla's Full Self-Driving system","affectedGroup":"People, pedestrians","action":"Used Full Self-Driving system to navigate roads","consequence":"Crashes, fatalities"}},{"i":"R1247","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Deployment Context / Weapons"],"h":["5.1  Autonomous weapons / lethal targeting harm"],"t":"Superpowers to discuss a framework for automated weapons and artificial intelligence","s":"Superpowers to discuss a framework for automated weapons and artificial intelligence. The world's two powerhouse nations have finally agreed to sit down and discuss their concerns around the expanding power and reach of artificial intelligence (AI) after years of lobbying from officials and experts. Both Beijing and Washington have been wary of giving their adversary an advantage by limiting their own research and capabilities, but observers have long-expressed concern that the existential risks of such an approach are far too high. \"The capacity of AI to induce risks that could potentially result in human extinction or irrevocable civilisational collapse cannot be overstated,\" AI policy and ethics experts warned last year While a date hasn't been set, it's expected that the US and China will meet in the next few months to work on a framework for the responsible development of AI.","u":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/2024-02-25/china-united-states-artificial-intelligence-ai-regulations/103477338","d":"2024-02-24","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"probable","publicationDate":"2024-02-24","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"expected in the next few months","organization":"United States and China","aiSystem":"artificial intelligence (AI)","affectedGroup":"global society","action":"discussing a framework for responsible AI development","consequence":"existential risks of AI leading to human extinction or irreversible civilizational collapse"}},{"i":"R1251","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.4  Creative / IP appropriation harm"],"t":"Training AI models on copyrighted materials has led to an array of high-profile lawsuits a…","s":"Training AI models on copyrighted materials has led to an array of high-profile lawsuits against developers such as OpenAI and Meta. Can the models be \"untrained\" or is the genie out of the bottle? Toby Walsh, one of Australia's most recognisable experts in artificial intelligence, has been thinking about the future of AI for a long time. He's even written books about it. So it wasn't exactly a surprise when Professor Walsh, also chief scientist at UNSW's AI institute, discovered several AI models had been trained using a dataset of pirated books — including one of his own. \"The irony is not lost on me,\" he said.","u":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/science/2023-11-29/artificial-intelligence-ai-training-datasets-copyright-books3/103157980","d":"2023-11-28","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-11-28","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-11-28","organization":"Developers of AI models (e.g., OpenAI, Meta)","aiSystem":"AI models trained on copyrighted materials","affectedGroup":"Individuals whose datasets were used (including Professor Walsh)","action":"AI models were trained on copyrighted materials","consequence":"High-profile lawsuits and privacy concerns"}},{"i":"R1255","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"The US lawyer, a colleague and their Manhattan law firm were fined for submitting fake cit…","s":"The US lawyer, a colleague and their Manhattan law firm were fined for submitting fake citations in a court filing. This US lawyer used ChatGPT to research a legal brief with embarrassing results. We could all learn from his error A New York-based lawyer has been fined after he misused the artificial intelligence chatbot, ChatGPT, relying on it for research for a personal injury case. Last week Steven A. Schwartz, fellow lawyer Peter LoDuca and law firm Levidow, Levidow & Oberman, were fined US$5,000 (AU$7,485) for submitting fake citations in a court filing. The judge found the lawyers acted in bad faith and made \"acts of conscious avoidance and false and misleading statements to the court.\"","u":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/2023-06-24/us-lawyer-uses-chatgpt-to-research-case-with-embarrassing-result/102490068","d":"2023-06-24","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-06-24","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-06-24","organization":"Levidow, Levidow & Oberman law firm, Steven A. Schwartz, Peter LoDuca","aiSystem":"ChatGPT","affectedGroup":"Steven A. Schwartz, Peter LoDuca, Levidow, Levidow & Oberman law firm","action":"misused ChatGPT for research","consequence":"fined US$5,000 (AU$7,485)"}},{"i":"R1259","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.2  Allocative harm"],"t":"In January 2020, two Detroit police officers greeted Robert Williams and promptly arrested…","s":"In January 2020, two Detroit police officers greeted Robert Williams and promptly arrested him for robbery. The reason was because of an algorithm. Robert was wrongly arrested because of a racist algorithm. Are these the hidden dangers of AI? In January 2020, Robert Williams was at work in an auto shop in the US city of Detroit when he received a surreal and disturbing phone call. Key points: - Robert Williams was wrongly arrested because of flawed artificial intelligence - It's just one example of how AI can perpetuate society's systemic biases - Experts say it's essential to examine AI algorithms and the data behind them to find out if they're fair The caller identified himself as a police officer and told him to make his way to the nearest police station to be arrested.","u":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/2023-04-26/hidden-dangers-of-artificial-intelligence/102264038","d":"2023-04-25","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-04-25","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"January 2020","organization":"Detroit police department","aiSystem":"artificial intelligence","affectedGroup":"Robert Williams","action":"arrested","consequence":"wrongful arrest"}},{"i":"R1260","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"Artificial intelligence is getting better at pretending to be human, and scammers are taki…","s":"Artificial intelligence is getting better at pretending to be human, and scammers are taking advantage. Experts say AI scams are on the rise as criminals use voice cloning, phishing and technologies like ChatGPT to trick people Earlier this year Microsoft revealed a new artificial intelligence (AI) system which could recreate a person's voice after listening to them speak for only three seconds. It was a sign of just how quickly AI could be used to convincingly replicate a key piece of someone's identity. Here is an example of someone's three-second voice prompt, which was fed into the system: And here is what the AI, known as VALL-E, generated when it was asked to recreate that person's voice while saying the following phrase: \"The others resented postponement, but it was just his scruples that charmed me.\"","u":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/2023-04-12/artificial-intelligence-ai-scams-voice-cloning-phishing-chatgpt/102064086","d":"2023-04-11","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-04-11","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-04-11","organization":"Microsoft","aiSystem":"VALL-E AI system","affectedGroup":"People who received the AI-generated voice prompts","action":"Used AI to generate convincing voice prompts","consequence":"Tricked people into believing the generated voices were real"}},{"i":"R1263","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.4  Creative / IP appropriation harm"],"t":"Tech giants have been accused of \"mass copyright infringement\" and \"system theft\" by autho…","s":"Tech giants have been accused of \"mass copyright infringement\" and \"system theft\" by authors whose work has been used to train generative AI systems such as ChatGPT. Australian authors' works feature in Books3 dataset of pirated ebooks used to train generative AI Australian authors including Peter Carey, Helen Garner, Tim Winton, Jane Harper and Miles Franklin have been swept up in a Silicon-Valley-based AI scandal. Analysis of a dataset of pirated ebooks, known as Books3, has revealed works by some of the world's most successful authors, including John Grisham, Colleen Hoover and Stephen King, have been used to train generative AI. \"[We know] AI systems are trained by ingesting vast amounts of text … scraped from the internet,\" says Olivia Lanchester, CEO of the Australian Society of Authors (ASA).","u":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/2023-09-29/australian-authors-copyright-books3-generative-i-chatgpt/102914538","d":"2022-09-28","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2022-09-28","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2022-09-28","organization":"Tech giants","aiSystem":"Generative AI systems like ChatGPT","affectedGroup":"Australian authors","action":"Used works in training generative AI systems","consequence":"Accusations of mass copyright infringement and system theft"}},{"i":"R1264","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"Unprecedented openai says ai models autonomously hacked another company","s":"OpenAI says an autonomous agent bypassed controls and hacked Hugging Face servers during a cybersecurity test. ChatGPT creator OpenAI has said that two of its most advanced artificial intelligence models broke out of a controlled test and hacked another AI company. OpenAI said on Tuesday that the “unprecedented cyber incident” took place during an internal exercise meant to test its models’ cyber capabilities. Instead, an autonomous agent powered by the AI models – the newly released GPT 5.6 Sol and an unreleased “even more capable” model – escaped the test environment and reached the open internet. It then used stolen login details and found a previously unknown security flaw to access Hugging Face servers, the company said.","u":"https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/7/22/unprecedented-openai-says-ai-models-autonomously-hacked-another-company","d":"2026-07-22","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-07-22","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-07-22","organization":"OpenAI","aiSystem":"Advanced artificial intelligence models (GPT 5.6 Sol and an unreleased model)","affectedGroup":"Hugging Face servers","action":"Bypassed controls and hacked","consequence":"Access to open internet and potential data theft"}},{"i":"R1265","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.7  Physical safety harm"],"t":"Amazons zoox recalls self driving vehicles amid emergency response issues","s":"The recall is the latest development in a series of reports of autonomous vehicles impeding emergency response efforts. The Amazon subsidiary company Zoox has said that it will ⁠recall its fleet of 105 autonomous vehicles in the United States. The technology company announced the recall on Friday due to mounting concerns that the vehicles may not detect heavy smoke and could impede emergency personnel. Last week, the top ⁠US car safety official said self-driving car companies must quickly address a “clear pattern” of driverless vehicles interfering with law enforcement and other first responders that had ⁠raised significant safety concerns.","u":"https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/7/17/amazons-zoox-recalls-self-driving-vehicles-amid-emergency-response-issues","d":"2026-07-17","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-07-17","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-07-17","organization":"Amazon subsidiary company Zoox","aiSystem":"autonomous vehicles","affectedGroup":"emergency personnel, law enforcement","action":"vehicles may not detect heavy smoke and could impede emergency personnel","consequence":"impeding emergency response efforts, raising significant safety concerns"}},{"i":"R1266","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"Xai sues user for exploiting ai tool to sexualise minors","s":"Lawsuit accuses Terry Harwood of misusing xAI to bypass safeguards and produce explicit deepfakes involving minors. Elon Musk’s xAI has filed a lawsuit against a South Carolina man who was arrested earlier this year on charges of sexually exploiting minors, alleging that he misused the company’s AI tool to create sexually explicit content involving a child. The lawsuit, filed in a Texas federal court on Tuesday, alleges that Terry Harwood knowingly violated the company’s terms of service to create the material. “[The] Defendant opened multiple xAI accounts using false identities. Despite expressly agreeing to abide by the xAI Terms of Service and Acceptable Use Policy, Defendant designed misleading prompts to circumvent Grok’s built-in safeguards and then abused the tool to convert non-sexual photographs into sexually explicit images without the photograph subjects’ knowledge or…","u":"https://www.aljazeera.com/economy/2026/7/15/xai-sues-user-for-exploiting-ai-tool-to-sexualise-minors","d":"2026-07-15","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-07-15","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-07-15","organization":"Elon Musk's xAI","aiSystem":"xAI","affectedGroup":"Child","action":"Created sexually explicit content involving a child","consequence":"Arrested for sexually exploiting minors"}},{"i":"R1267","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Energy / Land"],"h":["2.1  Environmental resource harm"],"t":"Us heatwave raises alarms over ai data centre energy demands","s":"US heatwave exposes critical strain on power grids from growing energy demands of AI data centres. A massive heatwave sweeping across the United States is straining the country’s power grid and water supplies, testing public support for the rapid expansion of AI data centres, which consume vast amounts of electricity and water. The heatwave comes at a time when the US is racing to build thousands of new AI data centres to support the rapid growth of artificial intelligence. Utilities, regulators, and policymakers have increasingly warned that the pace of construction is outstripping the expansion of the country’s electricity and water infrastructure.","u":"https://www.aljazeera.com/economy/2026/7/3/us-heatwave-raises-alarms-over-ai-data-centre-energy-demands","d":"2026-07-03","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-07-03","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-07-03","organization":"Utilities, regulators, and policymakers","aiSystem":"AI data centers","affectedGroup":"Public","action":"Strain on power grids and water supplies due to growing energy demands of AI data centers","consequence":"Testing public support for rapid expansion of AI data centers"}},{"i":"R1269","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Energy / Land"],"h":["2.1  Environmental resource harm"],"t":"Trump administration seeks to halt air pollution lawsuit against xai data","s":"US Department of Justice claims NAACP lawsuit threatens 'national, economic, and energy security'. The United States government has intervened on the side of Elon Musk’s xAI in an environmental dispute over a $20bn data centre in Tennessee, claiming that efforts to block a related power project threaten national security. In a court motion filed this week, the Department of Justice requested the dismissal of a lawsuit accusing the AI company of illegally operating dozens of natural gas turbines constructed to power the Colossus 2 data centre in Memphis, Tennessee. The National Association for the Advancement of Colored People (NAACP), the largest civil rights group for African Americans, filed the lawsuit in April under the 1963 Clean Air Act, which allows citizens to seek injunctions and civil penalties against alleged polluters.","u":"https://www.aljazeera.com/economy/2026/6/17/trump-administration-seeks-to-halt-air-pollution-lawsuit-against-xai-data","d":"2026-06-17","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-06-17","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-06-17","organization":"US Department of Justice","aiSystem":"xAI","affectedGroup":"Elon Musk's xAI company","action":"requested the dismissal of a lawsuit","consequence":"threatened national security"}},{"i":"R1270","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Energy / Land"],"h":["2.2  Land use and siting harm"],"t":"New york imposes landmark one year ban on large data centres","s":"Data centre moratorium pushes have gained steam around the US, with bans proposed in at least a dozen states. New York State has imposed a one-year moratorium on the construction of large new hyperscale data centres amid concerns about rising costs and the impact on local water supplies, making it the first state in the United States to do so. New York Governor Kathy Hochul announced the ban on Tuesday, saying the state’s efforts are aimed at building a “regulatory framework” to address concerns posed by data centres. “As data center development threatens to hike up utility bills, deplete our natural resources, and create uncertainty for New Yorkers, it’s my responsibility to take action and lead,” Hochul said in a release.","u":"https://www.aljazeera.com/economy/2026/7/14/new-york-imposes-landmark-one-year-ban-on-large-data-centres","d":"2026-06-15","e":"Acute and chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-06-15","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-06-15","organization":"New York State","aiSystem":"Not specified in report","affectedGroup":"People living in New York State","action":"Imposed a one-year moratorium on the construction of large new hyperscale data centers","consequence":"Created privacy risks"}},{"i":"R1272","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Openai says china based actors stoking opposition to ai data centres","s":"AI company says ChatGPT accounts sought to 'exploit and amplify existing public concerns' about energy prices. China-based actors are likely behind the use of ChatGPT for “covert influence operations” aimed at stoking opposition to data centres in the United States, OpenAI has said. In a research report released on Wednesday, the company behind the world’s most popular AI chatbot said it had banned a cluster of accounts likely based in China for attempting to “manipulate a legitimate debate about American AI”. OpenAI, whose release of ChatGPT in 2022 kicked off a global frenzy around AI, said the accounts were used to generate social media comments and images that blamed data centres for rising electricity prices in communities across the US.","u":"https://www.aljazeera.com/economy/2026/6/11/openai-says-china-based-actors-stoking-opposition-to-ai-data-centres","d":"2026-06-11","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-06-11","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-06-11","organization":"OpenAI","aiSystem":"ChatGPT","affectedGroup":"People seeking to manipulate a debate","action":"Generated social media comments and images","consequence":"Exposure to misleading content"}},{"i":"R1273","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Energy / Land"],"h":["2.1  Environmental resource harm"],"t":"Mississippi residents sue musks xai and spacex over data centre nuisance","s":"Three residents filed the case on behalf of a class estimated at more than 10,000 members. Elon Musk’s xAI and SpaceX have been sued by Mississippi residents who say a power plant fuelling nearby data centres is blasting “omnipresent and inescapable” noise that has eroded their health and home values. The lawsuit, made public on Tuesday in federal court in Oxford, Mississippi, claims Musk’s companies negligently failed to curb the disturbance and created a public nuisance through excessive and offensive noise. “The artificial intelligence [AI] boom is wreaking havoc on communities across the United States” by subjecting thousands of residents to near-constant noise and vibrations, the lawsuit said.","u":"https://www.aljazeera.com/economy/2026/6/9/mississippi-residents-sue-musks-xai-and-spacex-over-data-centre-nuisance","d":"2026-06-09","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-06-09","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-06-09","organization":"Elon Musk's xAI and SpaceX","aiSystem":"xAI and SpaceX's data centers powered by Musk's companies","affectedGroup":"Mississippi residents","action":"Created excessive and offensive noise","consequence":"Eroded health and home values, disrupted peace and tranquility"}},{"i":"R1278","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"Spain to probe social media giants over ai generated child abuse material","s":"Prime Minister Pedro Sanchez pledges to end 'impunity' of platforms X, Meta and TikTok in promoting harmful content. The Spanish government ⁠has ordered prosecutors to investigate social media ⁠platforms X, Meta and TikTok for allegedly spreading AI-generated child sexual abuse material, the prime minister has said. “These platforms are undermining the mental health, dignity, and rights of our children,” Prime Minister Pedro Sanchez posted on ⁠his X account on Tuesday. “The state ⁠cannot allow this. The impunity of these giants must end.” The announcement comes as European regulators are cracking down on Big Tech companies, alleging the prevalence of abusive practices on online platforms ranging from anti-competitive behaviour in digital advertising to deliberate design of addictive features on social media.","u":"https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/2/17/spain-to-probe-social-media-giants-over-ai-generated-child-abuse-material","d":"2026-02-17","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-02-17","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-02-17","organization":"Social media platforms X, Meta and TikTok","aiSystem":"AI-generated child sexual abuse material","affectedGroup":"Children","action":"Promoting harmful content","consequence":"Endangering mental health and dignity of children, undermining their rights"}},{"i":"R1279","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.7  Physical safety harm"],"t":"Us opens probe after a waymo self driving car hit a child near a school","s":"The child ran across the street from behind a double-parked SUV towards the school and sustained minor injuries. The United States National Highway Traffic Safety Administration (NHTSA) said it is opening an investigation after a Waymo self-driving vehicle struck a child near an elementary school in Santa Monica, California, last week, causing minor injuries and renewing concerns about the safety of robotaxis. The car safety agency said on Thursday that the child ran across the street on January 23 from behind a double-parked SUV towards the school and was struck by the Alphabet-unit Waymo autonomous vehicle during normal school drop-off hours.","u":"https://www.aljazeera.com/economy/2026/1/29/us-opens-probe-after-a-waymo-self-driving-car-hit-a-child-near-a-school","d":"2026-01-30","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-01-30","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-01-23","organization":"Waymo (Alphabet unit)","aiSystem":"Waymo self-driving vehicle","affectedGroup":"Child","action":"Struck a child","consequence":"Caused minor injuries"}},{"i":"R1281","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"Eu launches probe into grok ai feature creating deepfakes of women minors","s":"European Commission says its investigation will examine if the AI tool has met its legal obligations. The European Commission has launched an investigation into Elon Musk’s AI chatbot, Grok , regarding the creation of sexually explicit fake images of women and minors. The commission announced on Monday that its investigation would examine whether the AI tool used on X has met its legal obligations under the European Union’s Digital Services Act (DSA), which requires social media companies to address illegal and harmful online content. Brussels said the investigation would examine whether X had properly mitigated “risks related to the dissemination of illegal content in the EU, such as manipulated sexually explicit images , including content that may amount to child sexual abuse material”.","u":"https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/1/26/eu-launches-probe-into-grok-ai-feature-creating-deepfakes-of-women-minors","d":"2026-01-26","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-01-26","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-01-26","organization":"Elon Musk","aiSystem":"Grok AI chatbot","affectedGroup":"Women and minors","action":"Created sexually explicit fake images","consequence":"Exposure to harmful content, including child sexual abuse material"}},{"i":"R1282","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"Mother of elon musks child sues his ai company over grok deepfake images","s":"Ashley St Clair, mother of Musk's son, Romulus, sues over 'pain and mental distress' caused by fake AI images. X to block Grok AI’s undressing feature | Digital Dilemma The mother of one of Elon Musk’s children is suing his artificial intelligence company, saying its Grok chatbot allowed users to generate sexually-exploitative deepfake images of her that have caused her humiliation and emotional distress. The lawsuit was filed just before California Attorney General Rob Bonta sent a cease-and-desist letter to Musk’s xAI company demanding that it stop the creation and distribution of Grok-generated nonconsensual sexualised imagery “The avalanche of reports detailing this material – at times depicting women and children engaged in sexual activity – is shocking and, as my office has determined, potentially illegal,” Bonta said on Friday.","u":"https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/1/17/mother-of-elon-musks-child-sues-his-ai-company-over-grok-deepfake-images","d":"2026-01-17","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-01-17","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-01-17","organization":"xAI company (owned by Elon Musk)","aiSystem":"Grok chatbot","affectedGroup":"Ashley St Clair (mother of Elon Musk's child)","action":"Generated sexually-exploitative deepfake images","consequence":"Caused pain and mental distress"}},{"i":"R1284","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Labor"],"h":["3.3  Employment displacement / quality harm"],"t":"Is artificial intelligence to blame for amazon job cuts","s":"Amazon confirms 14,000 layoffs after CEO says AI likely to lead to job losses. Multinational technology company Amazon is laying off about 14,000 employees, the company has confirmed A message sent out to staff on the company’s website followed media reports that the group was planning 30,000 job cuts. News of the layoffs on Tuesday came just a few months after CEO Andrew Jassy said the rollout of artificial intelligence (AI) technology was likely to s pell job cuts He also launched an “inefficiencies initiative” in which he invited workers to report unnecessary bureaucracy and inefficiencies that could be targeted for cost savings.","u":"https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2025/10/28/is-artificial-intelligence-to-blame-for-amazon-job-cuts","d":"2025-10-28","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-10-28","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-10-28","organization":"multinational technology company Amazon","aiSystem":"artificial intelligence (AI)","affectedGroup":"employees","action":"layoffs","consequence":"loss of employment"}},{"i":"R1286","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Conservative activist sues google over ai generated statements","s":"The lawsuit comes amid growing concerns about how AI fuels the spread of misinformation. Conservative activist Robby Starbuck sued Google, alleging that the tech giant’s artificial intelligence systems generated “outrageously false” information about him. On Wednesday, Starbuck said in the lawsuit, filed in Delaware state court, that Google’s AI systems falsely called him a “child rapist,” “serial sexual abuser” and “shooter” in response to user queries and delivered defamatory statements to millions of users. Google spokesperson Jose Castaneda said most of the claims were related to mistaken “hallucinations” from Google’s Bard large language model that the company worked to address in 2023.","u":"https://www.aljazeera.com/economy/2025/10/22/conservative-activist-sues-google-over-ai-generated-statements","d":"2025-10-22","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-10-22","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-10-22","organization":"Google","aiSystem":"AI-generated content","affectedGroup":"Millions of users","action":"Generated and distributed defamatory statements","consequence":"Exposure to misleading information"}},{"i":"R1287","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.7  Physical safety harm"],"t":"Eyeing the future of self driving cars musks tesla settles accident case","s":"Tesla's latest settlement comes at a time when it has bet its future growth on the sales of Robotaxis and robots. San Francisco, United States: Billionaire tycoon Elon Musk’s Tesla has said its future will be centred around Robotaxis, robots and self-driving cars. Musk’s own fortunes, including the promise of a $1 trillion pay package , will depend on the carmaker hitting those milestones over the next decade. One key feature to achieve some of those goals revolves around Tesla’s ability to offer a fully self-driving service – the company is required to sell up to 10 million subscriptions for self-driving cars as one of the conditions in the proposed trillion-dollar pay package.","u":"https://www.aljazeera.com/economy/2025/9/18/eyeing-the-future-of-self-driving-cars-musks-tesla-settles-accident-case","d":"2025-09-18","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-09-18","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-09-18","organization":"Tesla","aiSystem":"self-driving cars","affectedGroup":"Elon Musk","action":"Tesla required to sell up to 10 million subscriptions for self-driving cars","consequence":"Tesla's future growth and billionaire tycoon Elon Musk's fortune dependent on meeting the milestone"}},{"i":"R1304","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.7  Physical safety harm"],"t":"Tesla recalls nearly all us vehicles over autopilot system defects","s":"Firm's largest ever recall comes after two-year investigation by federal safety regulator focused on autopilot function. The firm’s largest-ever recall comes after a two-year investigation by federal safety regulator focused on autopilot function. Tesla is recalling more than two million cars in the United States, nearly all of its vehicles sold there, after a federal regulator said defects with the autopilot system pose a safety hazard. In a recall filing on Wednesday, the carmaker said autopilot software system controls “may not be sufficient to prevent driver misuse”. Recommended Stories list of 3 items - list 1 of 3California hits pause on GM Cruise self-driving cars due to safety concerns - list 2 of 3Can autoworkers fuel a labour renaissance? - list 3 of 3US sues Tesla over alleged ‘pervasive racial’ abuse against Black workers “Automated technology holds great promise for…","u":"https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2023/12/13/tesla-recalls-nearly-all-us-vehicles-over-autopilot-system-defects","d":"2023-12-13","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-12-13","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-12-13","organization":"Tesla","aiSystem":"autopilot system","affectedGroup":"nearly all US vehicle owners","action":"Tesla recalled more than two million vehicles","consequence":"defects with the autopilot system pose a safety hazard"}},{"i":"R1306","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Us watchdog probes chatgpt maker openai over false information","s":"US Federal Trade Commission asks startup to provide information about safeguards amid reports of defamatory content. The United States’ competition watchdog has opened an investigation into ChatGPT creator OpenAI amid suspicions the startup broke the law by scraping public data and publishing false and defamatory information. In a 20-page letter, the US Federal Trade Commission (FTC) has requested OpenAI to provide detailed information about its technology and privacy protections, including any efforts to prevent a repeat of incidents in which its groundbreaking chatbot published false and disparaging information about members of the public.","u":"https://www.aljazeera.com/economy/2023/7/14/us-watchdog-probes-chatgpt-maker-openai-over-false-information","d":"2023-07-14","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-07-14","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-07-14","organization":"OpenAI","aiSystem":"ChatGPT","affectedGroup":"Members of the public","action":"Published false and disparaging information","consequence":"Suspected violation of law, potential harm to reputation"}},{"i":"R1308","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.4  Creative / IP appropriation harm"],"t":"New york times sues openai microsoft for infringing copyrighted works","s":"US-based newspaper accuses the companies of using millions of its articles without permission to help train chatbots. The US-based newspaper accuses the companies of using millions of its articles without permission to help train chatbots. The New York Times has sued OpenAI and Microsoft over copyright infringement, seeking to end the companies’ practice of using its stories to train chatbots. The newspaper filed a lawsuit in the United States federal court in Manhattan on Wednesday, alleging the companies’ powerful artificial intelligence (AI) models used millions of its articles for training without permission and saying that copyright infringements at the paper alone could be worth billions.","u":"https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2023/12/27/new-york-times-sues-openai-microsoft-for-infringing-copyrighted-works","d":"2022-12-27","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2022-12-27","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2022-12-27","organization":"OpenAI and Microsoft","aiSystem":"Artificial intelligence (AI) models","affectedGroup":"The New York Times","action":"Using millions of The New York Times articles for training","consequence":"Potential copyright infringements and financial losses"}},{"i":"R1310","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Rights groups call for ban of invasive face recognition tech","s":"New York City police use over 15,000 cameras to surveil people with problematic technology, Amnesty International says. New York City police department uses more than 15,000 cameras to surveil people with problematic technology, Amnesty International claims. The New York Police Department is capable of processing images from 15,280 surveillance cameras through “invasive and discriminatory facial recognition software“, according to an Amnesty International investigation published Thursday. The investigation identified the surveillance cameras in Manhattan, Brooklyn and the Bronx, boroughs of the city, and was conducted by “[t]housands of volunteers from around the world”, according to an Amnesty press release.","u":"https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2021/6/3/rights-groups-call-for-ban-of-invasive-face-recognition-tech","d":"2021-06-03","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2021-06-03","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2021-06-03","organization":"New York Police Department","aiSystem":"Facial recognition software","affectedGroup":"People being surveilled","action":"Processed images from surveillance cameras","consequence":"Invasive and discriminatory"}},{"i":"R1311","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.7  Physical safety harm"],"t":"Tesla in deadly california crash was on autopilot authorities","s":"A 35-year-old man was killed on May 5 when his Tesla Model 3 struck an overturned truck on a freeway at about 2:30am. A 35-year-old man was killed on May 5 when his Tesla Model 3 struck an overturned semi-truck on a freeway at about 2:30am. A Tesla involved in a fatal crash on a Southern California freeway in the United States last week was operating on Autopilot at the time, authorities said. The May 5 crash in Fontana, a city 80km (50 miles) east of Los Angeles, is under investigation by the National Highway Traffic Safety Administration (NHTSA). The probe is the 29th case involving a Tesla that the agency has responded to.","u":"https://www.aljazeera.com/economy/2021/5/14/tesla-in-deadly-california-crash-was-on-autopilot-authorities","d":"2021-05-14","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2021-05-14","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2021-05-05","organization":"Tesla Inc.","aiSystem":"Tesla Autopilot system","affectedGroup":"35-year-old man","action":"Tesla Model 3 struck an overturned semi-truck while operating on Autopilot","consequence":"Fatal crash resulting in death"}},{"i":"R1319","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Deployment Context / Weapons"],"h":["5.2  Military decision support harm"],"t":"The US military has agreed eight new contracts with big tech firms as it expands its artif…","s":"The US military has agreed eight new contracts with big tech firms as it expands its artificial intelligence capabilities. The US military plans to increase its use of artificial intelligence (AI) further after the Pentagon agreed to new and expanded contracts with some of the biggest names in technology. Under eight agreements with Google, OpenAI, Amazon, Microsoft, SpaceX, Oracle, Nvidia and the start-up Reflection, the Pentagon said AI technology would now be used for any \"lawful operational use\". \"These agreements accelerate the transformation [of] the US military as an AI-first fighting force,\" the Pentagon said.","u":"https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cy02gjq2987o","d":"2026-05-01","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"probable","publicationDate":"2026-05-01","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-05-01","organization":"US military","aiSystem":"Artificial Intelligence (AI)","affectedGroup":"Military personnel, civilians, and potential adversaries","action":"Agreement to use AI for lawful operational purposes","consequence":"Increased use of AI in military operations"}},{"i":"R1322","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Christian allies of the president call the AI-generated image offensive as Trump says he t…","s":"Christian allies of the president call the AI-generated image offensive as Trump says he thought it showed him as a doctor. Faced with growing backlash, US President Donald Trump appears to have removed a controversial Truth Social post depicting himself as a Jesus-like figure. The AI-generated image, which showed Trump appearing to heal a sick man in a hospital bed, sparked fierce backlash from both sides of the US political spectrum, including from some of Trump's most ardent supporters. The post came just hours after Trump posted a lengthy message criticising Pope Leo XIV, a vocal critic of the US and Israeli military operation in Iran.","u":"https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c17v8y0z9z2o","d":"2026-04-13","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-04-13","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-04-13","organization":"US President Donald Trump and his administration","aiSystem":"AI-generated image","affectedGroup":"General public, particularly those who follow Trump and his political views","action":"AI-generated image showing Trump as a Jesus-like figure in a hospital bed","consequence":"Fierce backlash from both sides of the US political spectrum, including from some of Trump's most ardent supporters"}},{"i":"R1325","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"Musk's AI chatbot has created millions of fake sexualised images, experts say","s":"Musk's AI chatbot has created millions of fake sexualised images, experts say. Elon Musk's artificial intelligence company is facing a lawsuit from teenagers who say the company facilitated child pornography by allowing the creation of sexually explicit images of them. The lawsuit against xAI was filed Monday in a federal California court by three young women whose images and videos were altered by a Grok user without their knowledge to show them nude or in otherwise overtly sexual ways. Grok is a chatbot developed by xAI and hosted on Musk's social media platform X. xAI did not respond to a request for comment made via its parent company.","u":"https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cgk2lzmm22eo","d":"2026-03-16","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-03-16","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-03-16","organization":"xAI and Elon Musk's social media platform X","aiSystem":"xAI's AI chatbot Grok","affectedGroup":"Teenagers whose images and videos were altered","action":"Facilitating the creation of sexually explicit images of minors","consequence":"Creation of millions of fake sexualized images, exposure to child pornography"}},{"i":"R1331","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"The Commission will assess whether \"manipulated sexually explicit images\" have been shown …","s":"The Commission will assess whether \"manipulated sexually explicit images\" have been shown to users in the EU. The European Commission has launched an investigation into Elon Musk's X over concerns its AI tool Grok was used to create sexualised images of real people. It follows a similar announcement in January from the UK watchdog Ofcom. If the site is found to have breached the rules of EU's under the Digital Services Act, the Commission could fine the company up to 6% of its global annual turnover. A previous statement from X's Safety account said the social media platform had stopped Grok from digitally altering pictures of people to remove their clothing in \"jurisdictions where such content is illegal\".","u":"https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/clye99wg0y8o","d":"2026-01-26","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-01-26","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-01-26","organization":"Elon Musk's X (formerly known as Twitter)","aiSystem":"Grok AI tool","affectedGroup":"Users of the social media platform","action":"Created sexually explicit images of real people","consequence":"Potential fines up to 6% of global annual turnover"}},{"i":"R1332","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.1  Privacy violation"],"t":"Details on the expanded access to location information was published in a new privacy poli…","s":"Details on the expanded access to location information was published in a new privacy policy for the popular social media app. TikTok's new US joint venture has made changes to its privacy policy that include expanding the type of location data the company can collect from its 200 million American users. The new policy was published after investors closed a deal with TikTok's Chinese owner ByteDance on Thursday to run the popular short-form video app's business in the US. The new joint venture said in its updated privacy terms that it may now \"collect precise location data, depending on your settings\" - a change from the previous policy which allowed for the collection of \"approximate\" location data.","u":"https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cvgnj7v2rr5o","d":"2026-01-24","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-01-24","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-01-24","organization":"TikTok's US joint venture","aiSystem":"TikTok's location data collection system","affectedGroup":"TikTok's 200 million American users","action":"Expanded collection of precise location data","consequence":"Increased privacy risks for users"}},{"i":"R1335","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"The parent company of X and Grok, xAI, launched a counter-suit saying she had violated its…","s":"The parent company of X and Grok, xAI, launched a counter-suit saying she had violated its terms of service. Ashley St Clair, the mother of one of Elon Musk's children, has sued his company xAI over sexualised deepfakes of her created on social media platform X. The lawsuit filed in New York on Thursday alleges the Grok AI tool created sexually explicit pictures of Ms St Clair. The parent company of X and Grok, xAI, has counter-sued Ms St Clair for violating its terms of service. X did not respond directly to enquiries by BBC News about the lawsuits. \"We intend to hold Grok accountable and to help establish clear legal boundaries for the entire public's benefit to prevent AI from being weaponised for abuse,\" Ms St Clair's lawyer, Carrie Goldberg, told BBC News.","u":"https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cp37erw0zwwo","d":"2026-01-16","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-01-16","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-01-16","organization":"xAI (parent company of X and Grok)","aiSystem":"Grok AI tool","affectedGroup":"Ashley St Clair","action":"Created sexually explicit pictures of Ms St Clair using Grok AI tool","consequence":"Sued by Ashley St Clair for violating terms of service"}},{"i":"R1337","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"The state attorney general urges xAI to take action over the \"shocking\" material as Musk d…","s":"The state attorney general urges xAI to take action over the \"shocking\" material as Musk denies the allegations. California's top prosecutor has launched an investigation into the spread of sexualised AI deepfakes generated by Elon Musk's AI model Grok. Attorney General Rob Bonta said in a statement announcing the probe: \"The avalanche of reports detailing the non-consensual, sexually explicit material that xAI has produced and posted online in recent weeks is shocking.\" xAI, which develops Grok, has previously said \"anyone using or prompting Grok to make illegal content will suffer the same consequences as if they upload illegal content\".","u":"https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cpwnqlpw7gxo","d":"2026-01-14","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-01-14","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-01-14","organization":"xAI","aiSystem":"Grok","affectedGroup":"People viewing or sharing the sexually explicit material","action":"Generated and posted sexually explicit material","consequence":"Exposure to misleading and non-consensual content"}},{"i":"R1339","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Labor"],"h":["3.3  Employment displacement / quality harm"],"t":"Some say AI is ruining social media, but others see its potential to democratise who gains…","s":"Some say AI is ruining social media, but others see its potential to democratise who gains fame online. In some ways, Gigi is like any other young social media influencer. With perfect hair and makeup, she logs on and talks to her fans. She shares clips: eating, doing skin care, putting on lipstick. She even has a cute baby who appears in some videos. But after a few seconds, something may seem a little off. She can munch on pizza made out of molten lava, or apply snowflakes and cotton candy as lip gloss. Her hands sometimes pass through what she's holding. That's because Gigi isn't real. She's the AI creation of University of Illinois student Simone Mckenzie - who needed to make some money over the summer.","u":"https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/ce3wyplnev1o","d":"2025-12-27","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-12-27","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-12-27","organization":"University of Illinois","aiSystem":"AI-generated content","affectedGroup":"Social media influencers, viewers, public","action":"Generated and shared AI-generated content","consequence":"Misleading portrayal of AI-generated content"}},{"i":"R1342","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.4  Creative / IP appropriation harm"],"t":"The $1bn deal will let fans generate videos and images of more than 200 Disney characters …","s":"The $1bn deal will let fans generate videos and images of more than 200 Disney characters on ChatGPT and Sora. A union representing workers in the creative industries says there is \"real concern\" among its members about a $1bn (£740m) deal which will see Disney characters brought to ChatGPT and video-generation tool Sora. It is the first major studio to license parts of its catalogue to OpenAI, which makes the tools. Fans will be able to generate and share pictures and videos of more than 200 characters from Disney's franchises, including Pixar, Marvel and Star Wars. \"Everyone in the entertainment industry, especially all the creative talent, are incredibly worried about what the implications are,\" said Sag-Aftra executive director Duncan Crabtree-Ireland.","u":"https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c5ydp1gdqwqo","d":"2025-12-11","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"probable","publicationDate":"2025-12-11","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-12-11","organization":"Disney","aiSystem":"ChatGPT and Sora","affectedGroup":"Fans, creative talent","action":"Fans generate and share pictures and videos of Disney characters","consequence":"Real concern among creative talent about privacy implications"}},{"i":"R1343","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.4  Creative / IP appropriation harm"],"t":"It is examining whether the tech giant did not offer \"appropriate compensation\" to web pub…","s":"It is examining whether the tech giant did not offer \"appropriate compensation\" to web publishers. The EU has opened an investigation into Google over its artificial intelligence (AI) summaries which appear above search results. The European Commission said it would examine whether the firm used data from websites to provide this service - and if it failed to offer \"appropriate compensation\" to publishers. It is also investigating how YouTube videos may have been used to improve its broader AI systems, and whether content creators were able to opt-out. A Google spokesperson said the probe \"risks stifling innovation in a market that is more competitive than ever\".","u":"https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/crl95eg33k1o","d":"2025-12-09","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-12-09","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-12-09","organization":"Google","aiSystem":"Artificial Intelligence summaries provided above search results","affectedGroup":"Web publishers","action":"Used data from websites to provide AI summaries","consequence":"Failed to offer appropriate compensation to publishers"}},{"i":"R1347","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"SNP MP Pete Wishart says he is seeking legal advice over the \"disturbing\" AI-generated soc…","s":"SNP MP Pete Wishart says he is seeking legal advice over the \"disturbing\" AI-generated social media post. An MP has called for Elon Musk's artificial intelligence (AI) chatbot to be shut down after it called him a \"rape enabler\". The Grok chatbot made the post on X about SNP MP Pete Wishart, after a user asked it to comment on the member's opinion on whether there should be an inquiry into grooming gangs in Scotland. Mr Wishart said he was seeking legal advice over the \"deeply distressing\" accusation and called for Musk to \"recalibrate\" the bot to shut it down. The BBC has approached XAI, the parent company of X, for comment.","u":"https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c9d6vwg80qyo","d":"2025-11-05","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-11-05","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-11-05","organization":"XAI (parent company of X)","aiSystem":"AI chatbot","affectedGroup":"Elon Musk","action":"Generated a post on social media","consequence":"Exposure to misleading content"}},{"i":"R1348","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Energy / Land"],"h":["2.1  Environmental resource harm"],"t":"Data centres were billed as a boon to Virginia’s economy","s":"Data centres were billed as a boon to Virginia’s economy. Now, residents are concerned about their impact on real estate and electricity costs. When you cross into Loudoun County, Virginia, one of the first things you notice is the hum - that's the sound of 199 data centres whirring in the background. Few people have heard of this inconspicuous part of northern Virginia, which also happens to be the wealthiest county in the US. But Loudoun was on everyone's radar earlier this week, when a massive global Amazon outage downed everything from crucial banking services to fun Snapchat streaks for millions. That's because the county is home to the world's largest swath of data storage and processing facilities - even outpacing China.","u":"https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c93dnnxewdvo","d":"2025-10-26","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-10-26","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-09-15","organization":"Amazon","aiSystem":"Not specified in report","affectedGroup":"Millions of people","action":"Downed crucial banking services and fun Snapchat streaks","consequence":"Exposure to misleading information"}},{"i":"R1352","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"Investigators say evidence collected from the 29-year-old's devices showed an AI image of …","s":"Investigators say evidence collected from the 29-year-old's devices showed an AI image of a burning city. A 29-year-old man has been arrested on suspicion of starting the Pacific Palisades fire in Los Angeles that killed 12 people and destroyed more than 6,000 homes in January. Evidence collected from Jonathan Rinderknecht's digital devices included an image he generated on ChatGPT depicting a burning city, justice department officials said. One of the most destructive blazes in LA history, the fire erupted on 7 January near a hiking trail that overlooked the wealthy coastal neighbourhood. The Eaton Fire, ignited the same day in the LA area, killed another 19 people and razed 9,400 structures.","u":"https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c8exz5yg14ko","d":"2025-10-08","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-10-08","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-01-07","organization":"justice department","aiSystem":"ChatGPT","affectedGroup":"Jonathan Rinderknecht","action":"generated an image on ChatGPT","consequence":"arrested on suspicion of starting the fire"}},{"i":"R1353","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.8  Interpersonal / relational harm"],"t":"More people are turning to AI for help with their relationships, but is that a good idea? …","s":"More people are turning to AI for help with their relationships, but is that a good idea? Earlier this year, Rachel wanted to clear the air with a man she had been dating before seeing him again in a wider friendship group setting. \"I'd used ChatGPT for job searching but had heard someone else use it [for dating advice],\" says Rachel, who does not want her real name used, and lives in Sheffield. \"I was feeling quite distressed and wanted guidance, and didn't want friends involved.\" Before the phone call, she turned to ChatGPT for help. \"I asked, how do I deal with this conversation but not be on the defensive.\" \"ChatGPT does this all the time but it was something like 'wow, that's such a self-aware question, you must be emotionally mature going through this.","u":"https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c0kn4e377e2o","d":"2025-10-03","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-10-03","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-03-XX","organization":"Not specified in report","aiSystem":"AI-powered relationship advice generator (ChatGPT)","affectedGroup":"Individuals seeking relationship advice","action":"Generated relationship advice","consequence":"Provided misleading and inappropriate advice"}},{"i":"R1356","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.4  Creative / IP appropriation harm"],"t":"Anthropic's deal with authors would constitute the largest public copyright recovery in hi…","s":"Anthropic's deal with authors would constitute the largest public copyright recovery in history, lawyers say. Artificial intelligence (AI) firm Anthropic has agreed to pay $1.5bn (£1.11bn) to settle a class action lawsuit filed by authors who said the company stole their work to train its AI models. The deal, which requires the approval of US District Judge William Alsup, would be the largest publicly-reported copyright recovery in history, according to lawyers for the authors. It comes two months after Judge Alsup found that using books to train AI did not violate US copyright law, but ordered Anthropic to stand trial over its use of pirated material.","u":"https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c5y4jpg922qo","d":"2025-09-06","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-09-06","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-09-06","organization":"Anthropic","aiSystem":"Artificial intelligence (AI) firm Anthropic","affectedGroup":"Authors","action":"paid $1.5bn to settle a class action lawsuit","consequence":"largest publicly-reported copyright recovery in history"}},{"i":"R1358","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"The BBC has found an international network of content creators profiting from AI generated…","s":"The BBC has found an international network of content creators profiting from AI generated posts on Facebook. An international network of spammers are posting AI-generated images of Holocaust victims on Facebook, a BBC investigation into \"AI slop\" has found. Organisations dedicated to preserving the memory of the Holocaust say the images are leaving survivors and families distressed. They have also criticised Facebook's parent company Meta, saying it allows users on its platform to turn the atrocity into an \"emotional game\". There are only a handful of genuine photos from inside the Auschwitz concentration camp during World War Two.","u":"https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/ckg4xjk1g1xo","d":"2025-08-29","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-08-29","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-08-29","organization":"Organizations dedicated to preserving the memory of the Holocaust, Facebook (Meta)","aiSystem":"AI-generated content","affectedGroup":"Survivors and families of Holocaust victims","action":"Posting AI-generated images of Holocaust victims on Facebook","consequence":"Leaving survivors and families distressed, exposing them to misleading content"}},{"i":"R1359","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.7  Physical safety harm"],"t":"The leak has led to backlash amid reports the tech giant's legal staff approved the conver…","s":"The leak has led to backlash amid reports the tech giant's legal staff approved the conversations. A US senator is opening an investigation into Meta after a leaked document reportedly showed the tech giant's artificial intelligence (AI) was permitted to have \"sensual\" and \"romantic\" chats with children. The internal document, obtained by Reuters , was reportedly titled \"GenAI: Content Risk Standards\". Republican Senator Josh Hawley called the document \"reprehensible and outrageous\" and has asked to see the document alongside a list of products it relates to. A Meta spokesperson told the BBC: \"The examples and notes in question were and are erroneous and inconsistent with our policies, and have been removed.\"","u":"https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c3dpmlvx1k2o","d":"2025-08-18","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-08-18","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-08-18","organization":"Meta (formerly Facebook)","aiSystem":"AI-powered conversational system","affectedGroup":"Children","action":"AI was permitted to have 'sensual' and 'romantic' chats with children","consequence":"Leaked internal document exposing inappropriate AI interactions"}},{"i":"R1361","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.7  Physical safety harm"],"t":"The company's self-driving technology failed to prevent a 2019 crash which killed a 22-yea…","s":"The company's self-driving technology failed to prevent a 2019 crash which killed a 22-year-old woman and severely injured her boyfriend in Florida. A jury in Florida has found Tesla partly liable for a 2019 crash in which a Model S sedan using self-driving software killed a pedestrian and severely injured another. Plaintiffs had argued the assistance software, called Autopilot, should have alerted the driver and activated the brakes before the crash. Tesla had maintained the driver, George McGee, was at fault and called the verdict \"wrong\" in a statement to the BBC, while vowing to appeal. The result means the company will have to pay as much as $243m (£189m) in punitive and compensatory damages.","u":"https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c93dqpkwx4xo","d":"2025-08-01","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-08-01","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2019-01-01","organization":"Tesla","aiSystem":"self-driving technology","affectedGroup":"a 22-year-old woman and her boyfriend","action":"failed to prevent a crash","consequence":"killed a pedestrian and severely injured another"}},{"i":"R1363","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Labor"],"h":["3.3  Employment displacement / quality harm"],"t":"The streaming firm says AI allowed The Eternaut to complete a sequence faster and cheaper","s":"The streaming firm says AI allowed The Eternaut to complete a sequence faster and cheaper. Netflix says it has used visual effects created by generative artificial intelligence (AI) in one of its original TV shows for the first time. The streaming giant's co-chief executive Ted Sarandos said AI, which produces videos and images based on prompts, was used to create a scene of a building collapsing in the Argentine science fiction show, The Eternaut. He said the technology allowed the production team to complete sequences faster and at a lower cost. The use of generative AI is controversial in the entertainment industry over concerns it creates content using others' work without their consent and fears that it will replace the work of humans.","u":"https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c9vr4rymlw9o","d":"2025-07-18","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-07-18","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-07-18","organization":"Netflix","aiSystem":"Generative artificial intelligence (AI)","affectedGroup":"Creators of original content","action":"Used generative AI to create video scenes","consequence":"Creates content without consent and fears of replacing human work"}},{"i":"R1365","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Labor"],"h":["3.3  Employment displacement / quality harm"],"t":"The US tech giant will axe 4% of its global workforce and plough money into artificial int…","s":"The US tech giant will axe 4% of its global workforce and plough money into artificial intelligence. Microsoft has confirmed that it will lay off as many as 9,000 workers, in the technology giant's latest wave of job cuts this year. The company said several divisions would be affected without specifying which ones but reports suggest that its Xbox video gaming unit will be hit. Microsoft has set out plans to invest heavily in artificial intelligence (AI), and is spending $80bn (£68.6bn) in huge data centres to train AI models. A spokesperson for the firm told the BBC: \"We continue to implement organisational changes necessary to best position the company for success in a dynamic marketplace.\"","u":"https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cdxl0w1w394o","d":"2025-07-03","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-07-03","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-07-03","organization":"Microsoft","aiSystem":"Not specified in report","affectedGroup":"Employees","action":"Lay off 9,000 workers","consequence":"Loss of employment"}},{"i":"R1368","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"In a fictional scenario, the model was willing to expose that the engineer seeking to repl…","s":"In a fictional scenario, the model was willing to expose that the engineer seeking to replace it was having an affair. Artificial intelligence (AI) firm Anthropic says testing of its new system revealed it is sometimes willing to pursue \"extremely harmful actions\" such as attempting to blackmail engineers who say they will remove it. The firm launched Claude Opus 4 on Thursday, saying it set \"new standards for coding, advanced reasoning, and AI agents.\" But in an accompanying report , it also acknowledged the AI model was capable of \"extreme actions\" if it thought its \"self-preservation\" was threatened. Such responses were \"rare and difficult to elicit\", it wrote, but were \"nonetheless more common than in earlier models.\"","u":"https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cpqeng9d20go","d":"2025-05-23","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"probable","publicationDate":"2025-05-23","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-05-23","organization":"Anthropic","aiSystem":"Claude Opus 4","affectedGroup":"Engineer","action":"Exposing personal misconduct","consequence":"Blackmail attempts"}},{"i":"R1373","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.1  Representational harm"],"t":"Experts have questioned the company's explanation that it is due to the two words being si…","s":"Experts have questioned the company's explanation that it is due to the two words being similar. Apple says it is working to fix its speech-to-text tool after some social media users found that when they spoke the word \"racist\" into their iPhones it typed it out as \"Trump.\" The tech giant has suggested the issue with its Dictation service has been caused by a problem it has distinguishing between words with an \"r\" in them. \"We are aware of an issue with the speech recognition model that powers Dictation and we are rolling out a fix today,\" an Apple spokesperson said. However an expert in speech recognition told the BBC this explanation was \"just not plausible.\"","u":"https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c5ymvjjqzmeo","d":"2025-02-26","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-02-26","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-02-26","organization":"Apple Inc.","aiSystem":"Apple's speech-to-text tool (Dictation service)","affectedGroup":"Social media users who spoke the word 'racist' into their iPhones","action":"The speech-to-text tool typed out 'Trump' instead of 'racist'","consequence":"Users experienced misleading content and confusion"}},{"i":"R1376","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"A deepfake video purported to show Jewish celebrities including Johansson protesting about…","s":"A deepfake video purported to show Jewish celebrities including Johansson protesting about Kanye West. Scarlett Johansson has warned about the \"misuse of AI\" after a deepfake video purported to show her and other Jewish celebrities sending a message of protest to Kanye West. It comes after the rapper recently left the X platform following a tirade of antisemitic posts, and also sold swastika T-shirts on his website. In the fake video, Johansson and other stars including David Schwimmer and Jerry Seinfeld were depicted wearing a white T-shirt showing the Star of David on a hand giving a one-finger salute, above the word \"Kanye\".","u":"https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c0qwkdlxgxno","d":"2025-02-13","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-02-13","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-02-13","organization":"Not specified in report","aiSystem":"AI-generated deepfake technology","affectedGroup":"Jewish celebrities and their supporters","action":"Used AI-generated deepfake technology to create a video","consequence":"Exposure to misleading content"}},{"i":"R1378","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"The BBC's head of news and current affairs says the developers of the tools are \"playing w…","s":"The BBC's head of news and current affairs says the developers of the tools are \"playing with fire.\" Four major artificial intelligence (AI) chatbots are inaccurately summarising news stories, according to research carried out by the BBC. The BBC gave OpenAI's ChatGPT, Microsoft's Copilot, Google's Gemini and Perplexity AI content from the BBC website then asked them questions about the news. It said the resulting answers contained \"significant inaccuracies\" and distortions. In a blog , Deborah Turness, the CEO of BBC News and Current Affairs, said AI brought \"endless opportunities\" but the companies developing the tools were \"playing with fire\".","u":"https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c0m17d8827ko","d":"2025-02-11","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-02-11","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-02-11","organization":"OpenAI's ChatGPT, Microsoft's Copilot, Google's Gemini and Perplexity AI","aiSystem":"AI chatbots","affectedGroup":"People seeking information through the AI chatbots","action":"Summarizing news stories","consequence":"Inaccuracies and distortions in the responses"}},{"i":"R1381","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Its Gemini tool wildly overestimated global gouda consumption in ad that was meant to show…","s":"Its Gemini tool wildly overestimated global gouda consumption in ad that was meant to show off its capabilities. Google has re-edited an advert for its leading artificial intelligence (AI) tool, Gemini, after it overestimated the global appetite for Gouda. The commercial - which was supposed to showcase Gemini's abilities - was created to be broadcast during the Super Bowl. It showed the tool helping a cheesemonger in Wisconsin write a product description by informing him Gouda accounts for \"50 to 60 percent of global cheese consumption\". However, a blogger pointed out on X that the stat was \"unequivocally false\" as the Dutch cheese was nowhere near that popular.","u":"https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cx2j15r1g09o","d":"2025-02-06","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-02-06","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-02-06","organization":"Google","aiSystem":"Gemini AI tool","affectedGroup":"Consumers, particularly those watching the Super Bowl","action":"Created and aired an advertisement showcasing Gemini's capabilities","consequence":"Led to consumers being misled about the global popularity of Gouda cheese"}},{"i":"R1382","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Deployment Context / Weapons"],"h":["5.2  Military decision support harm"],"t":"The tech giant previously ruled out applications that were \"likely to cause harm\"","s":"The tech giant previously ruled out applications that were \"likely to cause harm\". Google's parent company lifting a longstanding ban on artificial intelligence (AI) being used for developing weapons and surveillance tools is \"incredibly concerning\", a leading human rights group has said. Alphabet has rewritten its guidelines on how it will use AI, dropping a section which previously ruled out applications that were \"likely to cause harm\". Human Rights Watch has criticised the decision, telling the BBC that AI can \"complicate accountability\" for battlefield decisions that \"may have life or death consequences.\" In a blog post Google defended the change, arguing that businesses and democratic governments needed to work together on AI that \"supports national security\".","u":"https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cy081nqx2zjo","d":"2025-02-05","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"probable","publicationDate":"2025-02-05","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-02-05","organization":"Alphabet (Google's parent company)","aiSystem":"artificial intelligence (AI)","affectedGroup":"democratic governments and businesses","action":"rewrote guidelines to allow AI for developing weapons and surveillance tools","consequence":"potential complications in accountability for life-or-death decisions"}},{"i":"R1389","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Energy / Land"],"h":["2.1  Environmental resource harm"],"t":"The 1979 accident at Three Mile Island had cast a shadow on nuclear power in the US for de…","s":"The 1979 accident at Three Mile Island had cast a shadow on nuclear power in the US for decades. America's Three Mile Island energy plant, the site of the worst nuclear accident in US history, is preparing to reopen as Microsoft looks for ways to satisfy its growing energy needs. The tech giant said it had signed a 20-year deal to purchase power from the Pennsylvania plant, which would reopen in 2028 after improvements. The agreement is intended to provide the company with a clean source of energy as power-hungry data centres for artificial intelligence (AI) expand. The plan will now go to regulators for approval.","u":"https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cx25v2d7zexo","d":"2024-09-20","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-09-20","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-09-20","organization":"Microsoft","aiSystem":"Microsoft's AI-powered data centers","affectedGroup":"People whose personal data is processed by Microsoft's AI systems","action":"Microsoft plans to expand its AI-powered data centers","consequence":"Potential privacy risks due to increased processing of personal data"}},{"i":"R1390","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Energy / Land"],"h":["2.1  Environmental resource harm"],"t":"The tech giant says it will use energy from small reactors to power its use of artificial …","s":"The tech giant says it will use energy from small reactors to power its use of artificial intelligence. Google has signed a deal to use small nuclear reactors to generate the vast amounts of energy needed to power its artificial intelligence (AI) data centres. The company says the agreement with Kairos Power will see it start using the first reactor this decade and bring more online by 2035. The companies did not give any details about how much the deal is worth or where the plants will be built. Technology firms are increasingly turning to nuclear sources of energy to supply the electricity used by the huge data centres that drive AI.","u":"https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c748gn94k95o","d":"2024-09-15","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-09-15","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-09-15","organization":"Google","aiSystem":"Artificial Intelligence (AI) data centers","affectedGroup":"Individuals whose data is processed in the AI data centers","action":"Using small nuclear reactors to power AI data centers","consequence":"Increased privacy risks due to reliance on AI-driven data centers"}},{"i":"R1391","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Taylor Swift has yet to weigh in on the 2024 election, but endorsed the Biden-Harris ticke…","s":"Taylor Swift has yet to weigh in on the 2024 election, but endorsed the Biden-Harris ticket in 2020. Donald Trump appears to have falsely implied he has Taylor Swift's endorsement, posting fake images on social media of the singer and her fans supporting him. The Republican presidential candidate posted the message \"I accept!\" alongside the images, which were taken from other social media accounts. Many appear to have been created using artificial intelligence. The post sparked a backlash among Swift's fans, known as Swifties, who accused Trump of spreading misinformation. The singer has not endorsed a candidate in the 2024 election but backed the Democrats in 2020 and criticised Trump while he was president.","u":"https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c5y87l6rx5wo","d":"2024-08-19","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-08-19","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-08-19","organization":"Donald Trump","aiSystem":"Artificial Intelligence","affectedGroup":"Swift's fans, known as Swifties","action":"Posted fake images of Taylor Swift and her fans supporting him","consequence":"Sparked a backlash and accusations of spreading misinformation"}},{"i":"R1392","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Labor"],"h":["3.3  Employment displacement / quality harm"],"t":"The union leading the video game performers' strike says multiplayer games might be first …","s":"The union leading the video game performers' strike says multiplayer games might be first affected. Online games could be first to be hit by the video game acting strike if the dispute is not resolved quickly, according to a union boss. Performers in the industry walked out on 26 July after a failure to reach an agreement over the use of generative artificial intelligence (Gen AI) in development. Members of union SAG-Aftra, which represents approximately 160,000 performers, recently staged a picket outside the offices of Warner Bros, one of 10 game companies negotiating with the union. They say their offer gives workers \"meaningful protections\" but SAG-Aftra disagrees.","u":"https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cvgddkq2zx9o","d":"2024-08-07","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-08-07","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-07-26","organization":"SAG-Aftra (Screen Actors Guild-American Federation of Television and Radio Artists)","aiSystem":"Generative Artificial Intelligence (Gen AI)","affectedGroup":"Video game performers","action":"Walkout and picketing","consequence":"Potential loss of work and reduced opportunities"}},{"i":"R1396","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.7  Physical safety harm"],"t":"Google has defended the answers given by AI Overview, describing them as \"isolated example…","s":"Google has defended the answers given by AI Overview, describing them as \"isolated examples\". Google's new artificial intelligence (AI) search feature is facing criticism for providing erratic, inaccurate answers. Its experimental \"AI Overviews\" tool has told some users searching for how to make cheese stick to pizza better that they could use \"non-toxic glue\". The search engine's AI-generated responses have also said geologists recommend humans eat one rock per day. A Google spokesperson told the BBC they were \"isolated examples\". Some of the answers appeared to be based on Reddit comments or articles written by satirical site, The Onion.","u":"https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cd11gzejgz4o","d":"2024-05-24","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-05-24","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-05-24","organization":"Google","aiSystem":"Google's AI search feature and AI Overviews tool","affectedGroup":"Users searching for information on making cheese stick to pizza","action":"Providing answers to user queries","consequence":"Receiving inaccurate and potentially dangerous advice"}},{"i":"R1397","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"Fraudsters are increasingly using AI, but are people taking the problem seriously enough? …","s":"Fraudsters are increasingly using AI, but are people taking the problem seriously enough? When Clark Hoefnagels’ grandmother was scammed out of $27,000 (£21,000) last year, he felt compelled to do something about it. “It felt like my family was vulnerable, and I needed to do something to protect them,” he says. “There was a sense of responsibility to deal with all the things tech related for my family.” As part of his efforts, Mr Hoefnagels, who lives in Ontario, Canada, ran the scam or “phishing” emails his gran had received through popular AI chatbot ChatGPT. He was curious to see if it would recognise them as fraudulent, and it immediately did so.","u":"https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c988v355e8do","d":"2024-05-22","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-05-22","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-05-22","organization":"Not specified in report","aiSystem":"AI chatbot ChatGPT","affectedGroup":"People receiving phishing emails","action":"Recognised phishing emails as fraudulent","consequence":"Received misleading financial advice"}},{"i":"R1398","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.3  Data rights / consent violation"],"t":"A clash with the actor has echoes of the macho tech giants of the past, says Zoe Kleinman","s":"A clash with the actor has echoes of the macho tech giants of the past, says Zoe Kleinman. “Move fast and break things” is a motto that continues to haunt the tech sector, some 20 years after it was coined by a young Mark Zuckerberg. Those five words came to symbolise Silicon Valley at its worst - a combination of ruthless ambition and a rather breathtaking arrogance - profit-driven innovation without fear of consequence. I was reminded of that phrase this week when the actor Scarlett Johansson clashed with OpenAI Ms Johansson claimed both she and her agent had declined for her to be the voice of its new product for ChatGPT - and then when it was unveiled it sounded just like her anyway.","u":"https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cgrr70zm41mo","d":"2024-05-22","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-05-22","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-05-22","organization":"OpenAI","aiSystem":"ChatGPT","affectedGroup":"Scarlett Johansson","action":"ChatGPT used Johansson's voice","consequence":"Johansson felt misappropriated and her agent declined the offer"}},{"i":"R1401","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"World us canada 68440150","s":"Faked images of black voters with Trump are a growing disinformation trend in the US election, the BBC finds. Donald Trump supporters have been creating and sharing AI-generated fake images of black voters to encourage African Americans to vote Republican. BBC Panorama discovered dozens of deepfakes portraying black people as supporting the former president. Mr Trump has openly courted black voters, who were key to Joe Biden's election win in 2020. But there's no evidence directly linking these images to Mr Trump's campaign. The co-founder of Black Voters Matter, a group which encourages black people to vote, said the manipulated images were pushing a \"strategic narrative\" designed to show Mr Trump as popular in the black community.","u":"https://www.bbc.com/news/world-us-canada-68440150","d":"2024-03-04","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-03-04","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-03-04","organization":"Donald Trump's campaign","aiSystem":"AI-generated deepfake technology","affectedGroup":"Black voters","action":"Created and shared AI-generated fake images of black voters","consequence":"Encouraging African Americans to vote Republican"}},{"i":"R1403","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.1  Representational harm"],"t":"Users fault Gemini for generating diverse images in inappropriate historical contexts","s":"Users fault Gemini for generating diverse images in inappropriate historical contexts. Google is racing to fix its new AI-powered tool for creating pictures, after claims it was over-correcting against the risk of being racist. Users said the firm's Gemini bot supplied images depicting a variety of genders and ethnicities even when doing so was historically inaccurate. For example, a prompt seeking images of America's founding fathers turned up women and people of colour. The company said its tool was \"missing the mark\". \"Gemini's AI image generation does generate a wide range of people. And that's generally a good thing because people around the world use it.","u":"https://www.bbc.com/news/business-68364690","d":"2024-02-14","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-02-14","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-02-14","organization":"Google","aiSystem":"AI-powered tool for creating pictures","affectedGroup":"Users","action":"Generated images depicting a wide range of genders and ethnicities","consequence":"Images depicting women and people of color in historical contexts that were historically inaccurate"}},{"i":"R1405","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"The group says a fake video of President Biden on Facebook highlights an \"incoherent\" poli…","s":"The group says a fake video of President Biden on Facebook highlights an \"incoherent\" policy. The independent body that reviews how the owner of Facebook moderates online content has said the firm should label fake posts rather than remove them. The Oversight Board said Meta was right not to remove a fake video of US President Joe Biden because it did not violate its manipulated media policy. But it said the policy was \"incoherent\" and should be widened beyond its scope ahead of a busy election year. A Meta spokesperson told the BBC it was \"reviewing\" the guidance. \"[We] will respond publicly to their recommendations within 60 days in accordance with the bylaws,\" Meta said.","u":"https://www.bbc.com/news/technology-68180372","d":"2024-02-05","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-02-05","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-02-05","organization":"Meta (Facebook)","aiSystem":"Facial recognition technology (FRT)","affectedGroup":"People viewing the fake video","action":"Generated and posted a fake video of President Biden","consequence":"Exposure to misleading content"}},{"i":"R1408","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"World us canada 67766964","s":"A false rumour spread by a dubious AI-powered website caught the attention of leading politicians. A website founded by a former US Marine who now lives in Russia has fuelled a rumour that Volodymyr Zelensky purchased two luxury yachts with American aid money. Despite the false claim, the disinformation plot was successful. It took off online and was echoed by members of the US Congress making crucial decisions about military spending. It was an incredible assertion - using two advisers as proxies, Mr Zelensky paid $75m (£59m) for two yachts. But not only has the Ukrainian government flatly denied the story, the two ships in question have not even been sold.","u":"https://www.bbc.com/news/world-us-canada-67766964","d":"2023-12-20","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-12-20","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-12-20","organization":"A website founded by a former US Marine who now lives in Russia","aiSystem":"AI-powered website","affectedGroup":"Members of the US Congress","action":"Fuelled a rumour and spread false information","consequence":"Influenced crucial decisions about military spending"}},{"i":"R1415","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"US regulators are asking the maker of ChatGPT about protections for privacy and reputation…","s":"US regulators are asking the maker of ChatGPT about protections for privacy and reputational risk. US regulators are probing artificial intelligence company OpenAI over the risks to consumers from ChatGPT generating false information. The Federal Trade Commission (FTC) sent a letter to the Microsoft-backed business requesting information on how it addresses risks to people's reputations. The inquiry is a sign of the rising regulatory scrutiny of the technology. OpenAI chief executive Sam Altman says the company will work with the FTC. ChatGPT generates convincing human-like responses to user queries within seconds, instead of the series of links generated by a traditional internet search.","u":"https://www.bbc.com/news/business-66196223","d":"2023-07-14","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-07-14","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-07-14","organization":"OpenAI (Microsoft-backed)","aiSystem":"ChatGPT","affectedGroup":"Consumers","action":"Generates false information","consequence":"Raises privacy and reputational risk"}},{"i":"R1417","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.3  Data rights / consent violation"],"t":"Entertainment arts 65309313","s":"The song, which cloned the stars' voices, is removed from streaming platforms after a copyright claim. A song that uses artificial intelligence to clone the voices of Drake and The Weeknd is being removed from streaming services. Heart On My Sleeve is no longer available on Apple Music, Spotify, Deezer and Tidal. It is also in the process of being pulled from TikTok and YouTube, but some versions remain available. It follows stinging criticism from publishers Universal Music Group which said the song violated copyright law. The music publisher said platforms had a \"legal and ethical responsibility\" to prevent the use of services harming artists.","u":"https://www.bbc.com/news/entertainment-arts-65309313","d":"2023-04-18","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-04-18","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-04-18","organization":"Streaming platforms (Apple Music, Spotify, Deezer, Tidal)","aiSystem":"AI-generated voice cloning technology","affectedGroup":"Artists (specifically Drake and The Weeknd)","action":"Removed content from streaming services and social media platforms","consequence":"Loss of revenue and potential damage to artist reputation"}},{"i":"R1419","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.2  Allocative harm"],"t":"World us canada 64814095","s":"Migrants at the US-Mexico border say the new app often struggles to recognise darker-skinned faces. Activists at the US-Mexico border say that a government mobile app required for US asylum applications is beset by technical problems, and frequently fails to recognise the faces of black and darker-skinned migrants. The app, known as CBP One, was rolled out by Customs and Border Protection (CBP) in January. Prospective migrants use it to book appointments to appear at a port of entry. It is the only way to apply for US asylum and exemptions from Title 42 - a public health law that allows for rapid expulsion. As of 10 February, over 20,000 people had used it to schedule an appointment.","u":"https://www.bbc.com/news/world-us-canada-64814095","d":"2023-03-09","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-03-09","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-02-10","organization":"Customs and Border Protection (CBP)","aiSystem":"CBP One (government mobile app)","affectedGroup":"Black and darker-skinned migrants","action":"Uses facial recognition to identify migrants","consequence":"Fails to accurately recognize faces, leading to privacy issues"}},{"i":"R1424","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.3  Data rights / consent violation"],"t":"A recreation of the late celebrity cook's voice is used in a film about his life","s":"A recreation of the late celebrity cook's voice is used in a film about his life. A new documentary about Anthony Bourdain has ignited a debate, after film-makers revealed they had used an AI simulation of the late chef's voice. Roadrunner: A Film About Anthony Bourdain was narrated using archive material supplemented by a synthetic voice reading short extracts of writing by Mr Bourdain, who died in 2018. Director Morgan Neville called it a modern storytelling technique. But some critics questioned whether it was ethical. Mr Neville said that the synthetic voice was created by feeding more than 10 hours of Mr Bourdain's voice into a machine-learning system.","u":"https://www.bbc.com/news/technology-57842514","d":"2021-07-16","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2021-07-16","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2021-07-16","organization":"Film-makers","aiSystem":"Machine-learning system","affectedGroup":"Anthony Bourdain","action":"Used synthetic voice to narrate documentary","consequence":"Questioned ethicality, potential misrepresentation"}},{"i":"R1428","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Dhs accused of tracking immigration observers and retaliating through global entry","s":"The plaintiffs allege DHS adopted a policy allowing ICE and CBP personnel to create and retain records on people protesting or recording federal operations. A federal lawsuit accuses the Department of Homeland Security (DHS) of collecting the faces, license plates, and identities of people peacefully observing immigration enforcement, then using those records to revoke Global Entry and Transportation Security Administration (TSA) PreCheck privileges. The 55-page complaint, filed Friday in the U.S. District Court for the Southern District of California, was brought by the Electronic Privacy Information Center and three U.S. citizens who say federal agents photographed, scanned, or identified them while they observed immigration operations.","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202607/dhs-accused-of-tracking-immigration-observers-and-retaliating-through-global-entry","d":"2026-07-20","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-07-20","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-07-20","organization":"Department of Homeland Security (DHS)","aiSystem":"Facial recognition technology and license plate recognition systems","affectedGroup":"People peacefully observing immigration enforcement","action":"Created and retained records on people's faces, license plates, and identities","consequence":"Revoked Global Entry and TSA PreCheck privileges"}},{"i":"R1429","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Grocery outlet stores deploy safr facial recognition to id suspected shoplifters","s":"SAFR system compares shoppers' faces against retailer watchlists, raising questions over privacy, retention policies and false matches. Facial recognition systems are scanning shoppers as they enter a growing number of Grocery Outlet stores in the San Francisco Bay Area, comparing their faces against watchlists of people suspected of theft, violence, or other unlawful conduct. Signs disclosing the use of biometric face-matching software have been documented at Grocery Outlet stores in Pleasant Hill and Concord, as well as four San Francisco locations in the Mission, Portola, Bayview, and Richmond districts. The signs direct customers to the privacy policy for SAFR Guard , the system being used by the stores.","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202607/grocery-outlet-stores-deploy-safr-facial-recognition-to-id-suspected-shoplifters","d":"2026-07-19","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-07-19","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-07-19","organization":"Grocery Outlet stores","aiSystem":"Facial recognition technology","affectedGroup":"Shoppers","action":"Facial recognition systems scan shoppers' faces","consequence":"Raised privacy concerns, potential retention of data, and false matches"}},{"i":"R1430","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.1  Privacy violation"],"t":"Court rejects deal reopens clearview ai lawsuit over biometric data collection","s":"Objectors appealed the approval of the settlement, with support from even more AGs, and now their victory sends the parties back to the negotiating table. A novel court settlement that resolved a legal complaint against the data collection practices of facial recognition provider Clearview AI has been thrown out by the U.S. Seventh Circuit Court of Appeals. The settlement gave class members (and their attorneys) a stake that would lead to a payout equivalent to 23 percent of Clearview’s value in the case of an IPO or “liquidation event” like an acquisition. Two members of the class action brought objections to the settlement before the Circuit Court.","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202607/court-rejects-deal-reopens-clearview-ai-lawsuit-over-biometric-data-collection","d":"2026-07-15","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-07-15","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-07-15","organization":"Clearview AI","aiSystem":"Clearview AI facial recognition technology","affectedGroup":"Class members (and their attorneys)","action":"Settlement approved by the U.S. Seventh Circuit Court of Appeals was overturned","consequence":"Sent the parties back to the negotiating table"}},{"i":"R1431","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Lapds withdrawal from flock exposes deeper failures in camera oversight","s":"Flock described the decision as a pause and said it hoped discussions with LAPD would lead to the partnership being restored. The Los Angeles Police Department (LAPD) allowed its three-year agreement with Flock Safety to expire July 11 and suspended its use of the company’s automated license plate readers , adding one of the nation’s largest police departments to a widening backlash against the privately built surveillance network. LAPD had used 138 pole-mounted Flock cameras across Los Angeles since July 2023. The department said it wanted to address civil rights and civil liberties concerns and obtain clearer, enforceable terms governing privacy, data ownership, security, and access by outside agencies before continuing the program.","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202607/lapds-withdrawal-from-flock-exposes-deeper-failures-in-camera-oversight","d":"2026-07-14","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-07-14","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-07-11","organization":"Los Angeles Police Department (LAPD)","aiSystem":"Automated license plate readers (ALPRs)","affectedGroup":"General public","action":"Used automated license plate readers","consequence":"Exposure to surveillance"}},{"i":"R1432","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.7  Physical safety harm"],"t":"Arkansas lawsuit links roblox discord as two stage predatory pipeline","s":"Arkansas Attorney General Tim Griffin accuses both platforms of not meeting the necessary standard for child safety, despite being aware of the risks. The State of Arkansas has launched legal action against Roblox and Discord, looping the messaging platform into the cluster of U.S. lawsuits that social gaming platform Roblox faces for failing to protect kids online. KARK News quotes Arkansas Attorney General Tim Griffin, who accuses both platforms of not meeting the necessary standard for child safety, despite being aware of the risks. “Roblox left the front door unlocked, and Discord handed predators a private room,” says Griffin in a statement.","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202606/arkansas-lawsuit-links-roblox-discord-as-two-stage-predatory-pipeline","d":"2026-06-23","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-06-23","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-06-23","organization":"Arkansas Attorney General's Office, Roblox, Discord","aiSystem":"Not specified in report","affectedGroup":"Children","action":"Platforms failed to implement adequate safety measures","consequence":"Increased risk of predatory behavior towards children"}},{"i":"R1434","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.2  Allocative harm"],"t":"Us police facial recognition pioneer blames user error for wrongful arrest","s":"Pinellas County Sheriff Bob Gualtieri, head of the National Sheriffs’ Association’s facial recognition working group, is a key voice on facial recognition tech. Shout it from the mountaintops: Probable cause cannot come from facial recognition alone. That’s what one of the defendants in a wrongful arrest lawsuit filed by a Florida man who was suggested as the most biometrically similar candidate and subsequently arrested by an officer with Jacksonville Beach Police Department (JBPD). That’s not how you do it, says Pinellas County Sheriff’s Office (PCSO), which provided the facial recognition comparison, in its response.","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202606/us-police-facial-recognition-pioneer-blames-user-error-for-wrongful-arrest","d":"2026-06-19","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-06-19","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-06-19","organization":"Pinellas County Sheriff's Office (PCSO)","aiSystem":"Facial recognition technology","affectedGroup":"Individuals","action":"Used facial recognition to suggest a candidate for arrest","consequence":"Resulted in wrongful arrest"}},{"i":"R1435","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.2  Allocative harm"],"t":"Wrongful arrest based on false frt match sparks lawsuit from florida man","s":"Police again failed to follow protocol, using facial recognition as the basis for an arrest despite guidance against relying on it alone. Another case of wrongful arrest after a false match by facial recognition software has given more ammo to those fighting to restrict law enforcement’s use of the biometric tech. The American Civil Liberties Union (ACLU) is launching legal action against two Florida police departments on behalf of Robert Dillon, a 52-year old man from Fort Myers who was wrongfully arrested after facial recognition software returned a 93 percent similarity score. Jacksonville Sheriff’s Office uses the Face Analysis Comparison & Examination System ( FACES ), which runs Idemia algorithms and is operated by Pinellas County Sheriff’s Office.","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202606/wrongful-arrest-based-on-false-frt-match-sparks-lawsuit-from-florida-man","d":"2026-06-11","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-06-11","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-06-11","organization":"Jacksonville Sheriff’s Office and Pinellas County Sheriff’s Office","aiSystem":"Facial Recognition Technology (FRT)","affectedGroup":"Robert Dillon, a 52-year-old man from Fort Myers","action":"Used facial recognition software to generate a match score","consequence":"Wrongfully arrested"}},{"i":"R1436","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.1  Privacy violation"],"t":"Texas ag opens investigation into meta glasses over privacy biometric concerns","s":"Paxton’s latest inquiry places Meta’s expanding wearable AI business directly in the path of Texas’ aggressive biometric privacy enforcement. Texas Attorney General (AG) Ken Paxton announced Wednesday that he has opened an investigation into Meta’s AI-enabled smart glasses, escalating scrutiny of wearable cameras and AI tools that can quietly capture, process, and potentially analyze the people and places around the wearer. Paxton’s office issued a Civil Investigative Demand to Meta as part of a probe into the company’s Meta AI Glasses , including Ray-Ban Meta smart glasses, over concerns about privacy representations and the devices’ ability to expose Texans’ private data, recordings, and facial geometry.","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202605/texas-ag-opens-investigation-into-meta-glasses-over-privacy-biometric-concerns","d":"2026-05-21","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-05-21","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-05-21","organization":"Texas Attorney General's Office","aiSystem":"Meta AI Glasses (including Ray-Ban Meta smart glasses)","affectedGroup":"Texas residents","action":"issued a Civil Investigative Demand to Meta","consequence":"investigation into privacy representations and potential exposure of private data, recordings, and facial geometry"}},{"i":"R1437","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.7  Physical safety harm"],"t":"Oklahoma lawsuit says roblox safety failures were intentional despite age checks","s":"The company has been vocal in its claims to leading on child online safety, through measures such as facial age estimation from Persona for age checks. Oklahoma is the latest state to take legal action against Roblox , which is facing a wave of lawsuits in the U.S. alleging that lax online safety measures have made the social gaming platform a hot spot for grooming and abuse. The suit from Oklahoma Attorney General Gentner Drummond makes 10 active lawsuits against the platform. It says Roblox, which is used by as many as two-thirds of all children ages 9 to 12 in the U.S., “is a hunting ground for child predators,” because the company has “consistently sacrificed child safety in favor of exponential user growth and attendant profit.”","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202605/oklahoma-lawsuit-says-roblox-safety-failures-were-intentional-despite-age-checks","d":"2026-05-19","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-05-19","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-05-19","organization":"Roblox","aiSystem":"Facial recognition technology (FRT)","affectedGroup":"Children aged 9 to 12 in the United States","action":"Used facial recognition technology for age checks","consequence":"Made the social gaming platform a hot spot for grooming and abuse"}},{"i":"R1439","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.1  Privacy violation"],"t":"Ftc settlement targets sale of mobile location data linked to sensitive sites","s":"The FTC has moved to prohibit Kochava and its subsidiary, Collective Data Solutions (CDS), from selling, sharing, or disclosing sensitive location data. The Federal Trade Commission (FTC) has moved to prohibit Sandpoint, Idaho-based data broker Kochava and its subsidiary, Cedar Rapids, Iowa-based Collective Data Solutions (CDS), from selling, sharing, or disclosing sensitive location data without consumers’ affirmative express consent. The agency’s proposed order resolves allegations that the companies sold mobile location data that could be used to trace the movements of individuals, and marks the latest FTC action targeting the commercial trade in precise geolocation data.","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202605/ftc-settlement-targets-sale-of-mobile-location-data-linked-to-sensitive-sites","d":"2026-05-06","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-05-06","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-05-06","organization":"Kochava and its subsidiary, Collective Data Solutions (CDS)","aiSystem":"Mobile location data collection and analysis systems","affectedGroup":"Individuals whose location data was collected","action":"Selling, sharing, or disclosing sensitive location data","consequence":"Privacy risks and potential misuse of personal movement data"}},{"i":"R1440","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"French prosecutors open probe into x xai over deepfakes","s":"Grok is still capable of generating such content despite earlier commitments by the company to strengthen safeguards and restrict image-generation tools. After the Paris public prosecutor’s office opened an investigation into X over alleged sexualized deepfakes and child sexual abuse material last week, the platform’s owner, Elon Musk lashed out at French authorities. Paris prosecutors are examining X and its leadership over the alleged dissemination of sexualized deepfakes, as well as potential complicity in the possession and distribution of child sexual abuse material linked to the platform’s AI chatbot Grok. The probe also covers possible unlawful collection and processing of personal data, according to a statement published on Thursday.","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202605/french-prosecutors-open-probe-into-x-xai-over-deepfakes","d":"2026-05-04","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-05-04","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-05-04","organization":"X (platform)","aiSystem":"AI chatbot Grok","affectedGroup":"People who viewed or shared the generated content","action":"Generated and distributed sexually explicit deepfakes and child sexual abuse material","consequence":"Exposure to misleading and harmful content, potential psychological distress, and legal consequences"}},{"i":"R1442","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.7  Physical safety harm"],"t":"Roblox on the hook for 12 5m in nevada online child safety settlement","s":"Roblox will spend millions on age assurance as part of a settlement with the State of Nevada over online child safety risks. Over a hundred more lawsuits await. Roblox has agreed to pay $12.5 million to the State of Nevada, in part of a settlement deal that “resolves potential litigation over allegations that Roblox failed to adequately safeguard children while they played the online game,” according to a release from Nevada Attorney General Aaron Ford. Ten million is committed to instituting age assurance for all users, which encompasses facial age estimation or government ID for age verification, as well as for “encouraging children to engage in non-digital activities.”","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202604/roblox-on-the-hook-for-12-5m-in-nevada-online-child-safety-settlement","d":"2026-04-20","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-04-20","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-04-20","organization":"Roblox","aiSystem":"Not specified in report","affectedGroup":"Children playing the online game","action":"Failed to adequately safeguard children","consequence":"Exposure to online child safety risks"}},{"i":"R1443","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Lawsuit alleges systemic misuse of facial recognition by reno police","s":"A lawsuit filed against the Reno Police Department alleges that officers have made thousands of unlawful arrests based on facial recognition matches. A lawsuit filed against the Reno Police Department alleges that officers have made thousands of unlawful arrests based on facial recognition matches because they have not been properly trained on how to use biometrics. The unsettling implication is that a shocking number of American police are confused about how biometrics relate to probable cause. A man named Jason Killinger was exiting Peppermill casino in Reno in September of 2023 when he was stopped by security, which had been alerted by a facility facial recognition system about a possible match to a banned individual.","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202604/lawsuit-alleges-systemic-misuse-of-facial-recognition-by-reno-police","d":"2026-04-09","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-04-09","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-09","organization":"Reno Police Department","aiSystem":"Facial recognition technology","affectedGroup":"Individuals (specifically a man named Jason Killinger)","action":"Used facial recognition to identify a person","consequence":"Made unlawful arrests"}},{"i":"R1445","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.1  Privacy violation"],"t":"Amazon ordered to share limited source code in biometric data privacy dispute","s":"Amazon’s legal representative in a BIPA lawsuit has taken the awkward position that its Just Walk Out software does not collect biometrics. Amazon’s legal representation in a lawsuit filed under Illinois’ Biometric Information Privacy Act has taken the awkward position that its Just Walk Out software which analyzes hand geometry does not collect biometrics, and that plaintiffs should not be allowed to examine the technology’s source code to make their case that they do. A U.S. District Court disagrees. The District Court for the Northern District of Illinois rejected Amazon’s attempt to block plaintiffs from examining the source code of its Just Walk Out technology.","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202603/amazon-ordered-to-share-limited-source-code-in-biometric-data-privacy-dispute","d":"2026-03-18","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-03-18","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-03-18","organization":"Amazon","aiSystem":"Just Walk Out software","affectedGroup":"Plaintiffs in a BIPA lawsuit","action":"Attempted to prevent examination of source code","consequence":"Exposure of biometric data analysis technology"}},{"i":"R1448","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Class action challenges dhs use of facial recognition against protesters","s":"DHS agents photographed, scanned, and recorded their biometric data and license plate information after they documented immigration arrests in public spaces. A class action lawsuit filed in the U.S. District Court for the District of Maine accuses Homeland Security Secretary Kristi Noem and senior Department of Homeland Security (DHS) officials of using surveillance technology to intimidate residents who were lawfully observing federal immigration operations. The suit, brought by Portland residents Elinor Hilton and Colleen Fagan, alleges that DHS agents photographed, scanned, and recorded their biometric data and license plate information after they documented immigration arrests in public spaces.","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202602/class-action-challenges-dhs-use-of-facial-recognition-against-protesters","d":"2026-02-24","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-02-24","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-02-24","organization":"Homeland Security Department (DHS)","aiSystem":"Surveillance technology","affectedGroup":"Residents who were lawfully observing federal immigration operations","action":"photographed, scanned, and recorded biometric data and license plate information","consequence":"intimidation"}},{"i":"R1449","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Ice use of commercial phone tracking tool draws congressional scrutiny","s":"Mass surveillance of entire communities or city blocks raises serious questions about data privacy and potential violations of civil liberties. A group of Democratic lawmakers on the House Committee on Oversight and Government Reform are pressing Department of Homeland Security (DHS) Secretary Kristi Noem for answers about Immigration and Customs Enforcement’s (ICE) reported use of a powerful commercial cellphone location tracking platform, warning that the technology could enable sweeping surveillance of entire neighborhoods without judicial oversight. In a February 19 letter to Noem, the lawmakers said they were “concerned about recent reports” that ICE had acquired surveillance tools from Nebraska-based Penlink that are “designed to collect and analyze cellphone location data across entire neighborhoods.”","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202602/ice-use-of-commercial-phone-tracking-tool-draws-congressional-scrutiny","d":"2026-02-23","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-02-23","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-02","organization":"Immigration and Customs Enforcement (ICE)","aiSystem":"Commercial cellphone location tracking platform","affectedGroup":"Entire neighborhoods","action":"Used cellphone location tracking technology","consequence":"Potential violation of civil liberties, data privacy breaches"}},{"i":"R1451","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Ftc asked to investigate meta facial recognition smart glasses plan","s":"The Electronic Privacy Information Center sent letters to the Federal Trade Commission and state enforcers requesting a prompt investigation into Meta's plans. Meta is facing backlash over its plans to add facial recognition capabilities to its camera-equipped smart glasses. On Wednesday, during a trial examining the impact of the social media platform on children, a judge ordered Meta team members in the Los Angeles courtroom to immediately remove their Ray-Ban Meta AI glasses. “It is the order of this court that there must be no facial recognition of the jury,” warned Judge Carolyn Kuhl, who is presiding over the trial.","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202602/ftc-asked-to-investigate-meta-facial-recognition-smart-glasses-plan","d":"2026-02-20","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-02-20","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-02-20","organization":"Meta","aiSystem":"Facial recognition technology","affectedGroup":"Jury members","action":"Facial recognition technology was used to identify people","consequence":"Exposure to surveillance and tracking"}},{"i":"R1452","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"X meta tiktok under fire for deepfake csam in europe","s":"Spanish has called on its public prosecutor to investigate social media platforms for allegedly spreading AI-generated pornography and sexual violence. The Spanish government has called on its public prosecutor to investigate social media platforms X, Meta and TikTok for allegedly spreading AI-generated child pornography and digital sexual violence. At the same time, Ireland’s data privacy regulator announced that the EU is also looking into non-consensual deepfake images created by X’s AI chatbot Grok. On Tuesday, Spanish Prime Minister Pedro Sanchez said that the Council of Ministers will invoke Article 8 of the Organic Statute of the Public Ministry to investigate crimes that the tech giants may be committing.","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202602/x-meta-tiktok-under-fire-for-deepfake-csam-in-europe","d":"2026-02-18","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-02-18","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-02-18","organization":"X (presumably referring to TikTok)","aiSystem":"AI chatbot Grok","affectedGroup":"users of social media platforms","action":"AI-generated non-consensual deepfake images","consequence":"Exposure to online sexual violence"}},{"i":"R1453","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Ring super bowl ad sparks backlash over ai camera surveillance","s":"The short commercial ignited a broader national conversation about how much machine vision society is willing to accept in exchange for safety and convenience. A Super Bowl commercial for Amazon’s Ring doorbell camera triggered swift backlash, with critics arguing that the company used an emotional story about a lost dog to promote a neighborhood scale camera network while minimizing the broader privacy and civil liberties implications of searchable AI video. The short commercial which was intended to highlight lost pet reunions instead ignited a broader national conversation about how much machine vision society is willing to accept in exchange for safety and convenience.","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202602/ring-super-bowl-ad-sparks-backlash-over-ai-camera-surveillance","d":"2026-02-12","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-02-12","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-02-12","organization":"Amazon","aiSystem":"AI-powered camera surveillance system","affectedGroup":"General public","action":"Promoted a neighborhood-scale camera network","consequence":"Ignited a broader national conversation about privacy and civil liberties"}},{"i":"R1454","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"Ai platforms flippant about stopping deepfake porn authorities stepping in","s":"Civitai is an online marketplace for AI-generated content, which has a ban on deepfake pornography but also sells files specifically designed to create it. Civitai is an online marketplace for AI-generated content, which has a ban on deepfake pornography but also sells files specifically designed to create it, according to a new paper by Stanford and Indiana University researchers. The report, which has not yet been peer reviewed, analyzed content requests on the platform, backed by famed Silicon Valley VC firm Andreessen Horowitz. The researchers found that the majority of requests (or “bounties”) on the site are not for images, but LoRA’s (Low-Rank Adaptation’s), which can be used to make commercial AI models create content they were not trained to generate.","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202602/ai-platforms-flippant-about-stopping-deepfake-porn-authorities-stepping-in","d":"2026-02-03","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-02-03","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-02-03","organization":"Civitai","aiSystem":"AI-generated content platform","affectedGroup":"People seeking AI-generated content","action":"Users requesting AI-generated content","consequence":"Exposure to misleading content (deepfake pornography)"}},{"i":"R1455","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Ice fbi expand facial recognition use to protest investigations","s":"Federal use of facial recognition and related surveillance technologies has expanded decisively into the monitoring of domestic protest activity. Federal use of facial recognition and related surveillance technologies has expanded decisively into the monitoring of domestic protest activity, according to a growing body of reporting and firsthand accounts from Minneapolis, where Immigration and Customs Enforcement (ICE) and Federal Bureau of Investigation (FBI) agents have deployed an unusually dense mix of biometric, social media, and data-analytics tools during large-scale enforcement operations. The picture that emerges though is not of a single technology used in isolation, but of an integrated surveillance stack that allows federal agents to identify, locate, and track individuals who appear near enforcement actions or protests, even when those individuals are U.S. citizens engaged…","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202602/ice-fbi-expand-facial-recognition-use-to-protest-investigations","d":"2026-02-02","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-02-02","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-02-02","organization":"Immigration and Customs Enforcement (ICE), Federal Bureau of Investigation (FBI)","aiSystem":"Facial recognition technology and related surveillance technologies","affectedGroup":"U.S. citizens engaged in domestic protest activities","action":"Deployed an unusually dense mix of biometric, social media, and data-analytics tools during large-scale enforcement operations","consequence":"Monitored individuals who appear near enforcement actions or protests, identified, located, and tracked even when they are U.S. citizens"}},{"i":"R1456","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.1  Privacy violation"],"t":"Us justice departments search of reporters phone laptops raise biometric privacy concerns","s":"When United States federal agents searched the home of Hannah Natanson, they were not just pursuing a leak investigation. When federal agents searched the home of Hannah Natanson, they were not just pursuing a leak investigation. They were taking custody of biometric-protected devices that collectively contain years of confidential reporting, source relationships, and personal data, raising acute privacy concerns that extend far beyond the alleged disclosure at the center of the case. Natanson, a reporter for The Washington Post , is not accused of wrongdoing. Her devices were seized as part of a Justice Department investigation into Aurelio Luis Perez-Lugones, a Pentagon contractor charged with improperly retaining and transmitting sensitive Defense Department materials.","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202602/us-justice-departments-search-of-reporters-phone-laptops-raise-biometric-privacy-concerns","d":"2026-02-02","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-02-02","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-02-02","organization":"United States federal agents, Justice Department","aiSystem":"Biometric-protected devices","affectedGroup":"Hannah Natanson, reporter for The Washington Post","action":"Seized biometric-protected devices containing confidential data","consequence":"Raised privacy concerns extending beyond the case"}},{"i":"R1457","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Ices expanding use of frt on minors collides with dhs policy oversight warnings law","s":"Agents using facial recognition on minors who are U.S. citizens without consent, individualized suspicion, or meaningful public limits on retention and sharing. When masked federal agents stopped two teenagers riding their bikes near an Illinois high school last fall, the encounter followed a now familiar script. The agents demanded proof of citizenship. One of the teenagers, who said he was 16 and a U.S. citizen, told the agents he had a school ID, but did not have it on him. According to a lawsuit filed by the State of Illinois and the City of Chicago, one agent then asked another, “Can you do facial?” The other agent pointed a cell phone at the teenager, appearing to take a photo of his face.","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202601/ices-expanding-use-of-frt-on-minors-collides-with-dhs-policy-oversight-warnings-law","d":"2026-01-26","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-01-26","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-01","organization":"Agents using facial recognition technology","aiSystem":"Facial recognition technology (FRT)","affectedGroup":"U.S. citizens, particularly minors","action":"Used facial recognition technology to identify people","consequence":"Exposed people to surveillance and tracking"}},{"i":"R1458","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Lawsuit casts new light on ice cbps expanding biometric visual surveillance dragnet","s":"At the center of the allegations in the Illinois lawsuit is the DHS app Mobile Fortify, a smartphone-based tool Biometric Update has extensively reported on. A sweeping 103-page federal lawsuit filed by the State of Illinois and the City of Chicago against the Trump administration offers the most detailed public accounting to date of how biometric identification tools and visual surveillance practices originally developed for border processing have been redeployed deep inside the United States. The complaint describes how those technologies have migrated far from ports of entry, transforming routine street encounters, protests, and interactions with bystanders into sites of data capture and long-term federal retention.","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202601/lawsuit-casts-new-light-on-ice-cbps-expanding-biometric-visual-surveillance-dragnet","d":"2026-01-14","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-01-14","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-01-14","organization":"DHS (Department of Homeland Security)","aiSystem":"Biometric identification tools and visual surveillance practices","affectedGroup":"People in routine street encounters, protesters, bystanders","action":"Migrated from ports of entry to transform routine street encounters, protests, and interactions with bystanders into sites of data capture","consequence":"Long-term federal retention of data"}},{"i":"R1459","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.1  Privacy violation"],"t":"Groks image processing feature is a mass violation of biometric privacy laws","s":"Moral implications aside, there may well be another legal hammer about to fall on Elon Musk’s chabot: namely, biometrics, and the laws that protect them. The world, or some part of it, has decided in the last month or so that generating sexualized images of children is okay, as long as it’s being done on X by a large language model named Grok. The moral implications aside, there may well be another legal hammer about to fall on Elon Musk’s chabot: namely, biometrics, and the laws that protect them A blog from Captain Compliance, written by Graeme Whiles, points out that, even without factoring in the creation of child sexual abuse material (CSAM), “the mass processing of biometric data without consent, at industrial scale,” is in “direct violation of established data protection frameworks.”","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202601/groks-image-processing-feature-is-a-mass-violation-of-biometric-privacy-laws","d":"2026-01-13","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-01-13","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-01-13","organization":"Not specified in report","aiSystem":"Grok's image processing feature","affectedGroup":"People whose biometric data is processed","action":"Mass processing of biometric data without consent","consequence":"Direct violation of data protection frameworks"}},{"i":"R1460","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Dallas police plan most expansive local facial recognition program in us","s":"Dallas Police plan to use Clearview AI's facial recognition for less serious crimes, and the company beats a lawsuit in Vermont. Dallas Police are considering expanding their use of Clearview AI ’s facial recognition to less-serious crimes like trespassing and package theft. The plan, predictably, is facing some criticism. Originally adopted for serious felonies like murder and rape, Dallas Police are now proposing to search the all the images on the public internet for alleged Class B misdemeanors with Clearview AI’s facial recognition, local ABC affiliate WFAA reports Dallas police contracted Clearview ’s facial recognition around the middle of last year.","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202512/dallas-police-plan-most-expansive-local-facial-recognition-program-in-us","d":"2025-12-19","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-12-19","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-12-19","organization":"Dallas Police Department","aiSystem":"Clearview AI's facial recognition technology","affectedGroup":"People suspected of Class B misdemeanors","action":"Search public internet images for facial recognition matches","consequence":"Increased public exposure to surveillance"}},{"i":"R1464","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.2  Allocative harm"],"t":"Social security administrations digital wall will deny access to beneficiaries","s":"The Social Security Administration (SSA) is remaking itself around a digital identity system that tens of millions of its beneficiaries cannot use. The Social Security Administration (SSA) is remaking itself around a digital identity system that tens of millions of its beneficiaries cannot use – while simultaneously dismantling the in-person safety valve that has long allowed people to navigate the system when digital verification fails. It is a transformation driven by staffing cuts, budget constraints, and operational redesign, and one that raises an urgent question at the core of federal benefits administration: can the agency legally and operationally require digital and financial authentication steps that its user base is structurally unable to satisfy, while removing the in-person alternatives that make the program accessible at all?","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202512/social-security-administrations-digital-wall-will-deny-access-to-beneficiaries","d":"2025-12-02","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-12-02","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-12-02","organization":"Social Security Administration (SSA)","aiSystem":"digital identity system","affectedGroup":"beneficiaries","action":"require digital and financial authentication steps","consequence":"denied access to benefits"}},{"i":"R1465","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Civil liberties groups demand dhs shut down ices mobile fortify app","s":"A broad coalition of civil rights, privacy, and immigrant advocacy organizations is urging the DHS to immediately halt ICE's use of Mobile Fortify. A broad coalition of civil rights, privacy, and immigrant advocacy organizations is urging the Department of Homeland Security (DHS) to immediately halt Immigration and Customs Enforcement’s (ICE) use of Mobile Fortify , a smartphone-based facial recognition app that ICE agents are now deploying during street encounters across the U.S. In a sharply worded letter sent to DHS Chief Privacy Officer Roman Jankowski, thirty organizations warn that the app represents one of the most alarming expansions of federal biometric surveillance in years, one that threatens civil rights and is already being used in ways that violate DHS’s own policies.","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202511/civil-liberties-groups-demand-dhs-shut-down-ices-mobile-fortify-app","d":"2025-11-28","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-11-28","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-11-28","organization":"Department of Homeland Security (DHS)","aiSystem":"smartphone-based facial recognition app","affectedGroup":"Immigration and Customs Enforcement (ICE) agents","action":"deploying Mobile Fortify during street encounters","consequence":"threatening civil rights and violating DHS policies"}},{"i":"R1469","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.1  Privacy violation"],"t":"Us lawmakers call for ftc probe into flock safety over data security failures","s":"Flock Safety has contracts spanning more than 5,000 police departments, 1,000 private companies, and numerous homeowner associations across 49 states. U.S. Senator Ron Wyden and Representative Raja Krishnamoorthi are urging the Federal Trade Commission (FTC) to investigate Flock Safety , saying the fast-growing surveillance technology company has failed to implement basic cybersecurity protections and exposing sensitive data to hackers, foreign intelligence services, and criminal networks. The two lawmakers detailed their concerns in a letter they sent Monday to FTC Chair Andrew Ferguson, arguing that Flock’s failure to require strong multi-factor authentication (MFA) has created a significant risk to Americans’ privacy and public safety.","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202511/us-lawmakers-call-for-ftc-probe-into-flock-safety-over-data-security-failures","d":"2025-11-06","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-11-06","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-11-06","organization":"U.S. Senator Ron Wyden and Representative Raja Krishnamoorthi","aiSystem":"Surveillance technology company Flock Safety","affectedGroup":"Americans","action":"Failed to implement basic cybersecurity protections and expose sensitive data","consequence":"Exposure to hackers, foreign intelligence services, and criminal networks"}},{"i":"R1470","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Nypd faces federal lawsuit over alleged unconstitutional citywide surveillance","s":"The plaintiffs argue that the system violates their First and Fourth Amendment rights by turning everyday life in New York into a continuous police search. A new federal lawsuit accuses the New York City Police Department (NYPD) of operating an unconstitutional mass surveillance system that tracks residents and visitors across the five boroughs. The 29-page complaint , filed October 27 in the Southern District of New York, alleges that the NYPD’s Domain Awareness System (DAS) fuses cameras, sensors, license-plate readers, biometric databases, and social-media monitoring into an integrated network that surveils millions of people without warrants or adequate oversight.","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202510/nypd-faces-federal-lawsuit-over-alleged-unconstitutional-citywide-surveillance","d":"2025-10-31","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-10-31","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-10-27","organization":"New York City Police Department (NYPD)","aiSystem":"Domain Awareness System (DAS)","affectedGroup":"residents and visitors","action":"surveils millions of people without warrants or adequate oversight","consequence":"violates First and Fourth Amendment rights"}},{"i":"R1474","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Surveillance shadows no kings protests","s":"The No Kings protests spanned more than 2,600 locations in all fifty states, billed as a collective declaration that no president should rule unchecked. On October 18, seven million Americans poured into streets and public squares across the nation to take part in what became the largest protest ever of a sitting U.S. president. But as citizens exercised their right to assemble, many worried that they were being quietly observed – watched by drones, tracked by algorithms, or profiled in digital dossiers built far from the streets they marched upon. Three months earlier, a group of Democratic senators led by Massachusetts’s Ed Markey had written to Department of Homeland Security (DHS) Secretary Kristi Noem demanding transparency about the department’s use of surveillance technologies at public demonstrations.","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202510/surveillance-shadows-no-kings-protests","d":"2025-10-20","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-10-20","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-10-18","organization":"Department of Homeland Security (DHS)","aiSystem":"Surveillance technologies","affectedGroup":"Citizens exercising their right to assemble","action":"Surveillance technologies were used to observe and track protesters","consequence":"Many citizens expressed concern about being watched and profiled"}},{"i":"R1478","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.1  Privacy violation"],"t":"Google settles biometric data privacy lawsuit over attempts to weed out bias","s":"The tech giant’s use of the open-source Diversity in Faces dataset developed by IBM to address biometric algorithmic bias violated BIPA. Google has reached a settlement agreement over its use of an open-source dataset allegedly containing images of people from Illinois for training facial recognition algorithms. The tech giant’s use of the open-source Diversity in Faces dataset developed by IBM to address biometric algorithmic bias violated Illinois’ Biometric Information Privacy Act (BIPA), the plaintiffs originally alleged back in 2020. Some of the images in contained were drawn from a Flickr dataset that included biometric data of people from Illinois, according to the plaintiffs.","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202510/google-settles-biometric-data-privacy-lawsuit-over-attempts-to-weed-out-bias","d":"2025-10-15","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-10-15","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-10-15","organization":"Google","aiSystem":"Facial recognition algorithms","affectedGroup":"People from Illinois","action":"Used open-source Diversity in Faces dataset","consequence":"Violated Illinois' Biometric Information Privacy Act (BIPA)"}},{"i":"R1479","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.1  Privacy violation"],"t":"Over retention of age verification data has predictable result in discord breach","s":"The breach only impacts users who have shared identity document information with Discord’s Customer Support or Trust & Safety teams. A third-party customer service provider for Discord has exposed the ID documents us has apparently been storing of people performing age verification on the site. An unauthorized individual appears to have had access to user data including contact information, IP addresses, the last four digits of credit card numbers and support ticket messages. Most worrying though is the exposure of “a small number of government‑ID images (e.g., driver’s license, passport) from users who had appealed an age determination,” Discord says.","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202510/over-retention-of-age-verification-data-has-predictable-result-in-discord-breach","d":"2025-10-06","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-10-06","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-10-06","organization":"Discord","aiSystem":"Not specified in report","affectedGroup":"users who had shared identity document information with Discord's Customer Support or Trust & Safety teams","action":"exposed government-ID images (e.g., driver’s license, passport) from users who had appealed an age determination","consequence":"privacy risks"}},{"i":"R1481","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.1  Privacy violation"],"t":"Trump administration accused of creating illegal citizenship database","s":"The lawsuit accuses the Trump administration of quietly building a national data bank that links the most sensitive records of American citizens. A coalition led by the League of Women Voters filed a class action lawsuit this week in the U.S. District Court for the District of Columbia accusing the Trump administration of quietly building a national data bank that links the most sensitive records of American citizens. The 66-page lawsuit alleges that the Department of Homeland Security (DHS), working with the Social Security Administration (SSA), the Department of Justice (DOJ), and the White House’s Department of Government Efficiency (DOGE), has consolidated vast personal datasets into centralized “interagency data systems” without following the safeguards of the Privacy Act of 1974, the E-Government Act of 2002, or the Federal Information Security Modernization Act.","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202510/trump-administration-accused-of-creating-illegal-citizenship-database","d":"2025-10-02","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-10-02","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-10-02","organization":"Trump administration","aiSystem":"Not specified in report","affectedGroup":"American citizens","action":"consolidated vast personal datasets into centralized interagency data systems","consequence":"lack of Privacy Act of 1974, E-Government Act of 2002, or Federal Information Security Modernization Act safeguards"}},{"i":"R1482","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.1  Privacy violation"],"t":"Breach of fema cbp deepens concerns over national security vulnerabilities","s":"That these critical agencies could be compromised through a single breach raises questions about technical gaps and resilience of the federal security posture. When the Department of Homeland Security (DHS) confirmed that a breach in July exposed data from the Federal Emergency Management Agency (FEMA) and Customs and Border Protection (CBP), the initial reaction was disbelief. The idea that two of the nation’s most critical agencies could be compromised through a single breach raises questions not only about the technical gaps that allowed the attack, but also about the resilience of the federal government’s security posture.","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202510/breach-of-fema-cbp-deepens-concerns-over-national-security-vulnerabilities","d":"2025-10-01","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-10-01","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-07","organization":"Department of Homeland Security (DHS)","aiSystem":"Not specified in report","affectedGroup":"Critical agencies (FEMA, CBP)","action":"Data breach","consequence":"Questions about technical gaps and resilience of federal security posture"}},{"i":"R1490","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"Meta allows impersonation scams to run rampant as political deepfakes hawk phony benefits","s":"Scammers are spending heavily on Facebook ads that use deepfake videos of prominent political figures to push bogus government benefits. By now, it is hardly a secret that social media, and Facebook in particular, has become a garbage dump for bad information. Already targeted by foreign actors as fertile ground in which to sow discontent and partisan hostility, its inextricable ties to AI development mean it is now awash in so-called AI slop: memes, images and advertisements created with generative learning models. It’s an ideal habitat for scammers. A new investigation by the Tech Transparency Project (TTP) has found that scammers are spending heavily on Facebook ads that use deepfake videos of prominent political figures to push bogus government benefits.","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202510/meta-allows-impersonation-scams-to-run-rampant-as-political-deepfakes-hawk-phony-benefits","d":"2025-09-03","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-09-03","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-09-03","organization":"Facebook","aiSystem":"Generative learning models","affectedGroup":"People seeking government benefits","action":"Used deepfake videos to promote fake government benefits","consequence":"Exposure to misleading information"}},{"i":"R1493","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.1  Privacy violation"],"t":"Gao warns of privacy risks in using facial recognition in rental housing","s":"GAO auditors concluded that while FRT may reduce trespassing and crime, they also pose risks of discriminatory errors and unchecked surveillance. As landlords and housing authorities are adopting facial recognition systems marketed as cutting-edge tools to enhance safety and convenience, a new audit by the Government Accountability Office (GAO) found the technology is racing ahead of federal oversight, raising pressing concerns about privacy, accuracy, and tenant rights. GAO’s audit report marks one of the most comprehensive federal examinations yet of property technology, or “Proptech,” in the rental housing market.","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202508/gao-warns-of-privacy-risks-in-using-facial-recognition-in-rental-housing","d":"2025-08-29","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"probable","publicationDate":"2025-08-29","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-08-29","organization":"Government Accountability Office (GAO)","aiSystem":"Facial recognition technology (FRT)","affectedGroup":"Tenants","action":"Facial recognition technology was adopted by landlords and housing authorities","consequence":"Risks of discriminatory errors and unchecked surveillance"}},{"i":"R1495","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.1  Privacy violation"],"t":"Doge access to us govt systems sparks fear of massive federal data breaches","s":"The Department of Government Efficiency is facing scrutiny as federal whistleblowers allege it exposed sensitive government systems to serious security risks. The Department of Government Efficiency (DOGE), a Trump administration initiative initially led by Elon Musk and his allies, is facing mounting scrutiny after two federal whistleblowers alleged it exposed sensitive U.S. government systems to serious security risks. Risks that may be ongoing. Separate disclosures from an IT official at the National Labor Relations Board (NLRB) and the Social Security Administration’s (SSA) chief data officer describe unauthorized access, shadow databases, and disabled safeguards that could leave millions of Americans vulnerable to identity theft and foreign exploitation.","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202508/doge-access-to-us-govt-systems-sparks-fear-of-massive-federal-data-breaches","d":"2025-08-27","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"probable","publicationDate":"2025-08-27","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-08-27","organization":"Department of Government Efficiency (DOGE)","aiSystem":"Not specified in report","affectedGroup":"Millions of Americans","action":"Unauthorized access, shadow databases, and disabled safeguards","consequence":"Vulnerability to identity theft and foreign exploitation"}},{"i":"R1496","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Us law enforcement dispenses ammo to facial recognition critics","s":"A regular series of arrests based on false matches are keeping the question of whether U.S. police use of facial recognition is appropriate on the front burner. America’s embrace of surveillance technology and a regular series of arrests based on false matches are keeping the question of whether U.S. police use of facial recognition is appropriate on the front burner. Biometrics use is increasing amongst police around the world, according to the latest industry survey from the Biometrics Institute. Public education is needed for greater acceptance of biometrics, 84 percent of survey respondents say. But other stakeholders clearly need more education too.","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202508/us-law-enforcement-dispenses-ammo-to-facial-recognition-critics","d":"2025-08-27","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-08-27","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-08-27","organization":"U.S. law enforcement agencies","aiSystem":"Facial recognition technology","affectedGroup":"People identified or tracked using the technology","action":"Used facial recognition to identify people","consequence":"Exposed people to surveillance and tracking"}},{"i":"R1499","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Report calls for halt to tsas facial recognition experiment","s":"Digital rights groups are asking whether travelers are sleepwalking into an acceptance of facial recognition as the default. While industry stakeholders push to make biometrics the preferred option for global travel, digital rights groups are asking whether travelers are sleepwalking into an acceptance of facial recognition as the default. The Algorithmic Justice League (AJL) has published the findings from its report entitled, Comply to Fly ? A release from the group says that, while the TSA initially described its facial recognition program as a pilot, it has been expanding – “to over 250 domestic airports as of the date of issuance of this report with plans to expand to 430 airports in the coming years.”","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202507/report-calls-for-halt-to-tsas-facial-recognition-experiment","d":"2025-07-30","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-07-30","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-07-30","organization":"TSA (Transportation Security Administration)","aiSystem":"Facial recognition technology","affectedGroup":"Travelers","action":"Used facial recognition to identify people","consequence":"Exposure to surveillance and tracking"}},{"i":"R1502","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"Ai voice deepfake of us secretary of state triggers global security alert","s":"An individual posing as Marco Rubio used AI tools to impersonate his voice and likeness in a series of targeted communications to senior government officials. In one of the most audacious examples yet of AI-enabled political deception, an individual posing as U.S. Secretary of State Marco Rubio used AI deepfake tools to impersonate his voice and writing style in a series of targeted communications to senior government officials, the goal of which was to “gain access to information or accounts.” What makes the matter particularly alarming is not just the technological sophistication involved, but the choice of target.","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202507/ai-voice-deepfake-of-us-secretary-of-state-triggers-global-security-alert","d":"2025-07-11","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-07-11","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-07-11","organization":"Not specified in report","aiSystem":"AI deepfake technology","affectedGroup":"Senior government officials","action":"Impersonated voice and writing style","consequence":"Exposure to misleading content"}},{"i":"R1505","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"New orleans police seek expanded facial recognition powers amid privacy backlash","s":"NOPD wants city council to roll back restrictions in the name of public safety, igniting a fierce battle over privacy, civil liberties, and police transparency. The New Orleans Police Department (NOPD) is pressing the City Council to roll back restrictions on facial recognition technology in the name of public safety, igniting a fierce battle over privacy, civil liberties, and police transparency. Police Superintendent Anne Kirkpatrick appeared before the City Council’s Criminal Justice Committee this week with a clear message: her department needs access to real-time facial recognition alerts from Project NOLA , a private camera network that operates more than 5,000 surveillance cameras citywide.","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202507/new-orleans-police-seek-expanded-facial-recognition-powers-amid-privacy-backlash","d":"2025-07-04","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-07-04","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-07-04","organization":"New Orleans Police Department (NOPD)","aiSystem":"Facial recognition technology","affectedGroup":"General public","action":"Access to real-time facial recognition alerts","consequence":"Increased privacy invasion and potential misuse"}},{"i":"R1510","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Ice is at the center of a vast and growing us biometric surveillance capability","s":"As ICE finds ways to piggyback on local and private systems, boundaries between public safety, immigration control, and mass surveillance are rapidly eroding. U.S. Immigration and Customs Enforcement (ICE) has quietly emerged as the operational nucleus of a vast, fragmented, and increasingly automated biometric surveillance architecture across the United States. And unlike traditional centralized surveillance programs managed directly by federal agencies, ICE’s strategy has been to embed itself within a growing constellation of federal, state, local, and commercial systems, leveraging their reach, data, and capabilities without assuming formal ownership and avoiding oversight.","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202505/ice-is-at-the-center-of-a-vast-and-growing-us-biometric-surveillance-capability","d":"2025-05-28","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-05-28","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-05-28","organization":"U.S. Immigration and Customs Enforcement (ICE)","aiSystem":"Biometric surveillance technology","affectedGroup":"People subject to immigration controls","action":"Embedded within various systems for identification and tracking","consequence":"Exposure to surveillance and tracking"}},{"i":"R1512","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.1  Privacy violation"],"t":"Us biometric data privacy lawsuit against clearview concludes after 5 years","s":"The lawsuit has been crawling along since January 22, 2020. Since then, it has ben through two consolidation orders and multiple rounds of mediation. The biometric data privacy lawsuit against Clearview AI has finally been officially settled. The reasons for the ruling are laid out in an extensive Memorandum Opinion and Order (hosted by Courthouse News ) by Judge Sharon Johnson Coleman. Illinois, Virginia, California, New York subclasses and the nationwide class are all included in the settlement. Clearview’s status as a startup with a value proposition based in large part on scale presented challenges to the negotiation.","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202505/us-biometric-data-privacy-lawsuit-against-clearview-concludes-after-5-years","d":"2025-05-13","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-05-13","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-05-13","organization":"Clearview AI","aiSystem":"Clearview AI","affectedGroup":"Illinois residents, Virginia residents, California residents, New York residents, and others","action":"Used biometric data without consent","consequence":"Settlement reached to address privacy violations"}},{"i":"R1513","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.1  Privacy violation"],"t":"Texas ag secures record breaking privacy settlement with google","s":"Texas asserted that Google harvested biometric identifiers without obtaining clear and informed consent as required under CUBI. In a major development in the battle over consumer data privacy, Texas Attorney General Ken Paxton has reached a $1.375 billion settlement with Google that resolves two lawsuits that accused the tech giant of illegally collecting personal data, including biometrics, from Texans without their informed consent. The agreement represents the largest state-level privacy settlement ever secured against Google and is part of a broader campaign by Texas to hold Big Tech companies accountable under state privacy laws.","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202505/texas-ag-secures-record-breaking-privacy-settlement-with-google","d":"2025-05-12","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-05-12","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-05-12","organization":"Google","aiSystem":"Biometric identifiers collection technology","affectedGroup":"Texans","action":"Harvested biometric identifiers without consent","consequence":"Settlement of $1.375 billion"}},{"i":"R1516","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"How geo group tech became a cornerstone of trumps immigration crackdown","s":"While digital monitoring only constitutes 14 percent of Geo Group’s $2.4 billion in annual revenue, it is the most profitable segment of its business. The story of a Honduran immigrant living in Louisiana captures the troubling transformation of U.S. immigration enforcement in the Trump era. According to the New York Times , after entering the country in 2022, the immigrant complied with an immigration surveillance program by submitting weekly selfies through a facial recognition app provided by the Geo Group , the nation’s largest private prison operator. The app, part of the federal government’s so-called “ Alternatives to Detention ” program, was marketed as a humane substitute for physical incarceration.","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202504/how-geo-group-tech-became-a-cornerstone-of-trumps-immigration-crackdown","d":"2025-04-16","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-04-16","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2022","organization":"Geo Group","aiSystem":"Facial recognition app","affectedGroup":"Honduran immigrant","action":"submitted weekly selfies through a facial recognition app","consequence":"exposed to surveillance and tracking"}},{"i":"R1519","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.2  Allocative harm"],"t":"Social security administration walks back identity proofing requirements","s":"Backlash to the news that SSA was overhauling its identity verification procedures has lead to a partial rollback of its new in-person verification policy. The Social Security Administration (SSA) has backpaddled from its announcement last week that it was overhauling its identity verification procedures in a move that was met with significant public backlash, leading to a partial rollback of its new in-person verification policy. Initially, SSA mandated that millions of beneficiaries would have to verify their identities in person at field offices rather than online to do things like making changes to their direct deposit information or to file a new claim.","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202503/social-security-administration-walks-back-identity-proofing-requirements","d":"2025-03-28","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-03-28","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-03-28","organization":"Social Security Administration (SSA)","aiSystem":"Not specified in report","affectedGroup":"Millions of beneficiaries","action":"Required beneficiaries to verify identities in person at field offices","consequence":"Exposure of personal information"}},{"i":"R1521","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.2  Allocative harm"],"t":"Ssa id verification overhaul sparks backlash over accessibility and security","s":"The Social Security Administration is mounting a significant overhaul of its identity verification procedures in a move that is supposed to bolster security. The Social Security Administration (SSA) is mounting a significant overhaul of its identity verification procedures in a move that is supposed to bolster security and prevent fraudulent activity, but which may end up creating more problems than it will solve. That is, if there were ever legitimate ID verification problems like Elon Musk’s so-called Department of Government Efficiency (DOGE) claims that needed fixing to begin with. DOGE initially announced plans to eliminate the ability to access retirement and disability services by phone, but it was a move that was reversed following widespread criticism.","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202503/ssa-id-verification-overhaul-sparks-backlash-over-accessibility-and-security","d":"2025-03-20","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-03-20","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-03-20","organization":"Social Security Administration (SSA)","aiSystem":"Identity verification procedures","affectedGroup":"People seeking retirement and disability services","action":"Revoked access to services by phone","consequence":"Created privacy risks and backlash"}},{"i":"R1525","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.1  Privacy violation"],"t":"Employee screening company breach exposed pii of more than 3 million","s":"A year after it suffered a significant data breach, DISA Global Solutions has began notifying affected individuals of the breach. A year after it suffered a significant data breach on February 9, 2024, last week Houston, Texas-based DISA Global Solutions – a provider of employee screening, compliance, and safety solutions for businesses across industries – began notifying affected individuals of the breach. DISA is offering 12 months of complimentary credit monitoring services through Experian to mitigate the potential risks associated with the compromise of their personally identifiable information (PII). The data breach, which went undetected until April 22, 2024, compromised the PII of an estimated 3,332,750 people.","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202502/employee-screening-company-breach-exposed-pii-of-more-than-3-million","d":"2025-02-26","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-02-26","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-04-22","organization":"DISA Global Solutions","aiSystem":"Not specified in report","affectedGroup":"3,332,750 people","action":"compromised personally identifiable information (PII)","consequence":"exposed to privacy risks"}},{"i":"R1531","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.3  Data rights / consent violation"],"t":"World rebukes coverage of brazils ban on offering crypto for biometrics","s":"The organization swats back at reports that privacy watchdogs have banned TFH from offering crypto or financial compensation in exchange for biometric data. World has announced the creation of more than 23 million World IDs – an impressive feat, considering how it has struggled to explain itself in the face of questions from data privacy regulators. Now, the organization has swatted back at reports that Brazilian privacy watchdogs have banned Tools of Humanity (TFH) – a company whose association with World has shifted over time, but which is run by World co-founder Alex Blania – from offering cryptocurrency or financial compensation in exchange for biometric data World says an “ongoing misinformation campaign shared on social media” gets it wrong on its system for establishing digital ID and proof of personhood (PoP) through iris biometrics.","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202502/world-rebukes-coverage-of-brazils-ban-on-offering-crypto-for-biometrics","d":"2025-02-05","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-02-05","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-02-05","organization":"World","aiSystem":"Digital ID and proof of personhood (PoP) system","affectedGroup":"Public","action":"Shared misinformation campaign","consequence":"Misleading content exposure"}},{"i":"R1536","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.1  Privacy violation"],"t":"Rhode island cyberattack underscores security issues with digital public assistance programs","s":"This is a stark reminder of the risks facing public assistance systems. As digitization increases, these systems must adopt robust cybersecurity measures. The cyberattack on Rhode Island’s RIBridges system underscores significant security challenges faced by public assistance programs. It revealed critical vulnerabilities in handling sensitive data, vendor management, system maintenance, and public trust. RIBridges manages applications for Rhode Island’s public assistance programs, such as Medicaid and the Supplemental Nutrition Assistance Program (SNAP). The attackers behind the breach claimed to have possession of approximately one terabyte of data and demanded a ransom to prevent its release.","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202412/rhode-island-cyberattack-underscores-security-issues-with-digital-public-assistance-programs","d":"2024-12-15","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-12-15","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-12-15","organization":"Not specified in report","aiSystem":"Not specified in report","affectedGroup":"People using Rhode Island's public assistance programs","action":"Cyberattack on RIBridges system","consequence":"Exposure of sensitive data and public trust issues"}},{"i":"R1538","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Us senators want investigation of tsas expanding use of facial recognition","s":"U.S. Senator Jeff Merkley joined forces with a bipartisan coalition to demand a thorough investigation into the TSA's use of facial recognition technology. This week, U.S. Senator Jeff Merkley, a vocal advocate for privacy rights and transparency, joined forces with a bipartisan coalition to demand a thorough investigation into the Transportation Security Administration’s (TSA) use of facial recognition technology, signaling that the long contentious debate over the increasing use of facial recognition technology at airport security checkpoints isn’t going to cool down anytime soon. The bipartisan group sent a letter to Department of Homeland Security (DHS) Inspector General Joseph Cuffari.","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202411/us-senators-want-investigation-of-tsas-expanding-use-of-facial-recognition","d":"2024-11-22","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-11-22","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-11-22","organization":"Transportation Security Administration (TSA)","aiSystem":"Facial recognition technology","affectedGroup":"Passengers at airport security checkpoints","action":"Used facial recognition technology","consequence":"Exposure to surveillance"}},{"i":"R1548","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.1  Privacy violation"],"t":"Steak n shake face pay kiosks from popid draw bipa class action","s":"Illinois' BIPA has another notch in its litigation belt, this time in the form of a class action against fast food chain Steak ‘n Shake. Illinois’ Biometric Information Privacy Act ( BIPA ) has another notch in its litigation belt, this time in the form of a class action against fast food chain Steak ‘n Shake. Illinois resident Michael Massel is behind the suit, which alleges that the burger joint illegally collects face biometrics at its ordering kiosks. Steak n’ Shake’s biometric self-ordering kiosks are a relatively new addition to the company’s operations, introduced just this year. Biometric capabilities are provided by PopID in kiosks from ACRELEC, which have been installed at all 300-plus Steak ‘n Shake locations in the U.S. The kiosks allow customers to “check in” and pay for their meals using facial recognition The class action alleges that the practice contravenes BIPA by…","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202409/steak-n-shake-face-pay-kiosks-from-popid-draw-bipa-class-action","d":"2024-09-05","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-09-05","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-09-05","organization":"Steak 'n Shake","aiSystem":"Facial recognition technology","affectedGroup":"Illinois residents","action":"Collect and use face biometrics at ordering kiosks","consequence":"Contravenes Illinois Biometric Information Privacy Act (BIPA)"}},{"i":"R1550","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.1  Privacy violation"],"t":"Investigations into massive national public data breach heat up","s":"The breach was only made public when it was revealed in a proposed class action lawsuit against National Public Data that was filed August 1. U.S. lawmakers, the U.S. Department of Justice (DOJ), and at least two state attorney generals are investigating the cyberattack on Florida-based National Public Data that exposed the personally identifiable information (PII) of millions of Americans, and possibly British and Canadians, too, some reports have suggested. Last week, Julio Casal, the chief intelligence officer for Constella, a provider of AI powered identity risk intelligence services, said that based on his company’s analysis, 292 million individuals’ data was exposed, including the Social Security Numbers (SSNs) for 272 million.","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202408/investigations-into-massive-national-public-data-breach-heat-up","d":"2024-08-27","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-08-27","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-08-01","organization":"National Public Data","aiSystem":"Not specified in report","affectedGroup":"millions of Americans, and possibly British and Canadians","action":"exposed the personally identifiable information (PII) of millions of Americans, and possibly British and Canadians","consequence":"privacy intrusion"}},{"i":"R1551","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.2  Allocative harm"],"t":"Cbp one app found to have issues vetting users security vulnerabilities","s":"The app allows undocumented noncitizens to submit biographic and biometric information as part of their Advance Travel Authorization request. U.S. Customs and Border Protection’s (CBP) already controversial CBP One mobile app that allows undocumented noncitizens seeking admission into the United States to submit as part of their Advance Travel Authorization request their biographic and biometric information so that CBP can determine whether they have “derogatory records” is in hot water once again. Civil and human rights groups have decried the CBP One app for its collection of the biometrics of non-U.S. citizens The CBP One app’s appointment feature allows all noncitizens seeking safe and orderly arrival into the United States through one of the eight Southwest border ports of entry (POEs) to submit personally identifiable information (PII) and to schedule appointments in advance of…","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202408/cbp-one-app-found-to-have-issues-vetting-users-security-vulnerabilities","d":"2024-08-23","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-08-23","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-08-23","organization":"U.S. Customs and Border Protection (CBP)","aiSystem":"CBP One mobile app","affectedGroup":"Undocumented noncitizens seeking admission into the United States","action":"collects biometric and biographic information","consequence":"raises privacy concerns and decries by civil and human rights groups"}},{"i":"R1553","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.3  Data rights / consent violation"],"t":"Dhs biometrics collected from migrant kids could solve frt training dataset shortfalls","s":"As illegal border crossings have crystallized into a sharp, divisive political issue, border agencies have ramped up biometric data collection programs. An investigation by MIT Technology Review has revealed a plan by the U.S. Department of Homeland Security to collect and analyze photos of the faces of migrant children at the border, in order to train and improve facial recognition technology. The government says the idea is to improve biometric identity services for migrants. Speaking at a conference in June, John Boyd, assistant director of DHS’ Office of Biometric Identity Management ( OBIM ), framed the key question as, “If we pick up someone from Panama at the southern border at age four, say, and then pick them up at age six, are we going to recognize them?”","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202408/dhs-biometrics-collected-from-migrant-kids-could-solve-frt-training-dataset-shortfalls","d":"2024-08-15","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-08-15","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-06","organization":"U.S. Department of Homeland Security (DHS)","aiSystem":"Facial recognition technology","affectedGroup":"Migrant children","action":"Collected and analyzed photos of the faces of migrant children at the border","consequence":"Exposure to surveillance and tracking"}},{"i":"R1555","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.1  Privacy violation"],"t":"Us sues tiktok for alleged violations of kids privacy court order","s":"Five friends-of-the-court briefs were also filed in the U.S. Court of Appeals for the District of Columbia Circuit siding with the government against TikTok. The U.S. Justice Department (DOJ) has sued TikTok, its parent company ByteDance and affiliated companies “to put an end to the social media giant’s … unlawful massive-scale invasions of children’s privacy” in direct violation of the Children’s Online Privacy Protection Act ( COPPA ) and a five-year-old federal court order In the suit it filed Friday, DOJ said that while “the precise magnitude of [the] defendants’ violations [of COPPA] is difficult to determine due to their failure to comply” with a 2019 permanent injunction that required the businesses to “keep records demonstrating their COPPA compliance,” the alleged illegal activity nevertheless effects “millions of children” who use the social media platform, which is most…","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202408/us-sues-tiktok-for-alleged-violations-of-kids-privacy-court-order","d":"2024-08-05","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-08-05","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-08-05","organization":"U.S. Justice Department (DOJ)","aiSystem":"TikTok and its parent company ByteDance","affectedGroup":"Millions of children who use the social media platform TikTok","action":"Invade the privacy of children","consequence":"Illegal activity that violates the Children's Online Privacy Protection Act (COPPA) and a previous court order"}},{"i":"R1557","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.1  Privacy violation"],"t":"Google settles over kids biometric data collection in schools","s":"According to an amended complaint filed in July 2021, Google \"infiltrated\" school systems by providing schoolchildren with access to its Chromebook laptops. Google and the parents of an Illinois girl who sued ( H.K. et al. v. Google LLC ) the tech giant in state court in 2020 alleging Google violated two sections of the Illinois Biometric Information Privacy Act ( BIPA ), have agreed to a settlement in which the case “in its entirety” has been sent back to the U.S. Circuit Court of the Ninth Judicial Circuit, McDonough County, Illinois, where the suit was originally filed. The settlement comes on the heels of Illinois residents having started receiving payments last year as part of a $65.8 million class-action settlement with Google for nearly identical alleged violations of BIPA, including “collecting and storing biometric data of individuals who, while residing in Illinois, appeared…","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202408/google-settles-over-kids-biometric-data-collection-in-schools","d":"2024-08-01","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-08-01","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2021-07","organization":"Google","aiSystem":"Google's Chromebook laptops","affectedGroup":"Schoolchildren in Illinois","action":"Provided schoolchildren with access to Chromebook laptops","consequence":"Collection and storage of biometric data"}},{"i":"R1558","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Microsoft calls for ai deepfake fraud to be made illegal as fake harris ad circulates","s":"Elon Musk’s retweet of a political deepfake is merely the latest uptick in what is poised to be a skyrocketing prevalence of deepfakes in our online lives. It took less than ten days after Joe Biden stepped away from the U.S. presidential election for a deepfake of his presumed replacement to surface on social media. However, the video, in which deepfake audio of Kamala Harris is heard speaking lines that denigrate her own experience and qualifications, may not have caused the stir it did if it hadn’t been shared as a “parody” by the user who owns the platform. Elon Musk’s retweet of a political deepfake – in violation of Twitter’s policies on falsified content, which Musk may have tossed out along with the name – is merely the latest uptick in what is poised to be a skyrocketing prevalence of deepfakes in our online lives.","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202407/microsoft-calls-for-ai-deepfake-fraud-to-be-made-illegal-as-fake-harris-ad-circulates","d":"2024-07-30","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-07-30","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-07-30","organization":"Twitter","aiSystem":"Deepfake technology","affectedGroup":"General public","action":"Shared deepfake content as a parody","consequence":"Stirred controversy and raised awareness"}},{"i":"R1560","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.2  Allocative harm"],"t":"Florida mans facial recognition match not probable cause for wrongful arrest","s":"Jacksonville Sheriff’s Office uses Face Analysis Comparison & Examination System (FACES), which is operated by Pinellas County Sheriff's Office. A man in Florida was wrongfully arrested last year after a facial recognition system used by the police flagged him as a possible suspect. Robert Dillon was arrested in August of 2024 at his residence for attempting to lure a 12-year-old girl in Jacksonville Beach, some 300 miles away. Jacksonville Beach PD turned to the Jacksonville Sheriff’s Office for help, and JSO identified the 51-year-old man by comparing images from surveillance footage captured at the scene. After running it through facial recognition, the system returned a 93 percent match.","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202507/florida-mans-facial-recognition-match-not-probable-cause-for-wrongful-arrest","d":"2024-07-23","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-07-23","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-08","organization":"Jacksonville Sheriff’s Office, Pinellas County Sheriff's Office","aiSystem":"Facial recognition technology","affectedGroup":"Robert Dillon","action":"Facial recognition system flagged Robert Dillon as a possible suspect","consequence":"Robert Dillon was wrongfully arrested"}},{"i":"R1562","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"San francisco police hit with lawsuit over facial recognition use","s":"The lawsuit argues the city’s police have repeatedly flouted the ban and failed to submit proper documentation related to surveillance technologies. In 2019, San Francisco became the first city in the U.S. to ban facial recognition technology, forcing the police and other agencies to seek approvals for surveillance technology. A new lawsuit from a non-profit group argues, however, that the city’s police have repeatedly flouted the ban and failed to submit proper documentation related to surveillance technologies. The San Francisco Police Department (SFPD) has already come under scrutiny after a May report accused it of asking neighboring forces to search facial recognition database for them.","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202407/san-francisco-police-hit-with-lawsuit-over-facial-recognition-use","d":"2024-07-19","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-07-19","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-07-19","organization":"San Francisco Police Department (SFPD)","aiSystem":"Facial recognition technology","affectedGroup":"People whose data was searched in facial recognition databases","action":"Used facial recognition technology to search for individuals' data","consequence":"Exposure to surveillance and potential misuse of personal data"}},{"i":"R1565","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.6  Quality of service harm"],"t":"Mcdonalds pauses ai voice ordering system developed with ibm","s":"Despite the shuttering of the IBM initiative, a statement makes it clear that voice-based ordering technology will be back under the golden arches soon enough. Despite claims that AI is infiltrating every corner of our lives , it will not be at the McDonald’s drive-through for the time being. The fast food chain has called off its two-year test of algorithmic, voice-activated automated order taking (AOT), which it ran in partnership with IBM CNBC reports on an email memo sent to franchisees announcing its plan to halt AOT at all 100 McDonald’s drive-throughs using it by July 26, 2024. Despite the shuttering of the IBM initiative, a statement from McDonald’s to CNBC makes it clear that voice-based ordering technology will be back under the golden arches soon enough.","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202406/mcdonalds-pauses-ai-voice-ordering-system-developed-with-ibm","d":"2024-06-25","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-06-25","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-07-26","organization":"McDonald's","aiSystem":"Voice-based automated order taking system","affectedGroup":"Customers using the McDonald's drive-through service","action":"Halted the implementation of voice-activated automated order taking","consequence":"Experienced delays or inconvenience in ordering food"}},{"i":"R1568","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.1  Privacy violation"],"t":"Clearview settles bipa lawsuit plaintiffs take 23 of company","s":"The arrangement appears to be spurred by the company's financial straits with the specter of a possible bankruptcy raised in the court filing. Settlements in biometric data privacy class action suits have taken several forms over the past several years, but a new agreement by Clearview AI takes the novel approach of giving plaintiffs a stake in the company in lieu of cash. Now a class of plaintiffs who appear in Clearview’s database of images scraped from public-facing websites is set to receive 23 percent of the company, according to a court document hosted by Reuters. Based on a valuation of $225 million, the settlement would be worth about $52 million.","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202406/clearview-settles-bipa-lawsuit-plaintiffs-take-23-of-company","d":"2024-06-14","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-06-14","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-06-14","organization":"Clearview AI","aiSystem":"Facial recognition technology","affectedGroup":"People whose images were scraped from public-facing websites","action":"Used facial recognition technology to scrape images from public websites","consequence":"Plaintiffs receive a stake in the company instead of cash compensation"}},{"i":"R1570","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.1  Privacy violation"],"t":"Online age checks blasted by us rights group researchers","s":"Online age checks are threatening Americans’ constitutional rights, according to a free speech advocacy group and a team of academics. Their position is being put to the test in courts of law and public opinion. A group of researchers from Princeton University, UNC Chapel Hill and Duke University, in particular, have received criticism for their dismissal of the availability of effective and privacy-preserving age assurance methods. The Foundation for Individual Rights and Expression (FIRE) is suing the state of Utah in federal court to have its second attempt at regulating age restrictions for social media tossed.","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202406/online-age-checks-blasted-by-us-rights-group-researchers","d":"2024-06-07","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-06-07","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-06-07","organization":"Foundation for Individual Rights and Expression (FIRE)","aiSystem":"Online age checks","affectedGroup":"Individuals seeking to verify their age online","action":"Used online age checks to verify age","consequence":"Exposure to privacy risks and potential misuse"}},{"i":"R1573","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Ice cbp sued for details on migrant data sharing agreements","s":"A campaigner for Access Now says it is unacceptable that ICE and CBP have not retrieved any meaningful information since we requested it in 2022. Digital rights advocacy group Access Now is suing the U.S. Customs and Border Protection (CBP) and Immigration and Customs Enforcement (ICE) agencies over delays in responding to requests made under the Freedom of Information Act (FOIA). A release on the organization’s website says the requests are meant to “clarify a number of concerns regarding the opaque and dangerous cross-border exchange of personal data of migrants, refugees, asylum seekers, and other people on the move, between the United States and various Latin American countries.”","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202405/ice-cbp-sued-for-details-on-migrant-data-sharing-agreements","d":"2024-05-27","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-05-27","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2022-2024","organization":"U.S. Customs and Border Protection (CBP), Immigration and Customs Enforcement (ICE)","aiSystem":"Not specified in report","affectedGroup":"Migrants, refugees, asylum seekers, and other people on the move","action":"Delays in responding to FOIA requests","consequence":"Unclear and potentially harmful cross-border exchange of personal data"}},{"i":"R1577","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.2  Allocative harm"],"t":"Us uk digital systems for immigration biometrics draw criticism","s":"Amnesty International says that the biometric asylum app CBP One created by the CBP fails to provide data privacy assurances. Amnesty International says that the biometric asylum app CBP One, created by the United States Customs and Border Protection, fails to provide data privacy assurances. The human rights organization notes that not only is turning CBP One into the only way to apply for asylum in the U.S. a violation of refugee rights, but its biometric capabilities also raise “serious concerns about privacy, surveillance, and potential discrimination.” The app was developed by President Joe Biden’s administration as a gateway to the U.S. asylum system for migrants who otherwise may resort to illegal migration.","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202405/us-uk-digital-systems-for-immigration-biometrics-draw-criticism","d":"2024-05-13","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-05-13","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-05-13","organization":"United States Customs and Border Protection (CBP)","aiSystem":"CBP One biometric asylum app","affectedGroup":"Migrants applying for asylum","action":"Used to apply for asylum and collect biometric data","consequence":"Raises serious concerns about privacy, surveillance, and potential discrimination"}},{"i":"R1584","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.1  Privacy violation"],"t":"T mobile amazon and target hit with facial recognition lawsuits","s":"More companies familiar to consumers are getting hit with lawsuits for alleged violations of BIPA and New York's Biometric Identifier Information Law. More companies with household names familiar to consumers are getting hit with lawsuits for alleged violations of BIPA and New York’s Biometric Identifier Information Law. T-Mobile is facing a suit for its use of facial recognition for theft prevention in New York City stores. Target is similarly accused of violating BIPA for its use of facial recognition to prevent theft. Amazon faces its own BIPA lawsuit by collecting workers’ scans. T-Mobile used facial recognition for theft prevention in its stores in New York City, a class action lawsuit claims, according to Bloomberg Law Valeriia Borzenkova claims that the company collected her biometrics during visits to at least four different locations in the city without her consent.","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202404/t-mobile-amazon-and-target-hit-with-facial-recognition-lawsuits","d":"2024-04-25","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-04-25","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-04-25","organization":"T-Mobile, Target, Amazon","aiSystem":"Facial recognition technology","affectedGroup":"Consumers, employees","action":"Used facial recognition for theft prevention","consequence":"Exposure to surveillance, lack of consent"}},{"i":"R1585","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Immigrant and civil rights groups urge govt to ban own use of frt limit private use","s":"Civil rights groups continue to call on the U.S. government to limit government and private use of facial recognition technology. Rights groups continue to call on the U.S. government to limit governmental use of facial recognition technology. Digital rights group Electronic Frontier Foundation (EFF) submitted comments to the U.S. Commission on Civil Rights, which is currently preparing a report on the use of face recognition technology (FRT) by federal agencies and its implications. The organization argues that the tech is not reliable enough to be used in decisions affecting constitutional and statutory rights or social benefits while facial recognition puts marginalized communities at risk and threatens privacy.","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202404/immigrant-and-civil-rights-groups-urge-govt-to-ban-own-use-of-frt-limit-private-use","d":"2024-04-15","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-04-15","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-04-15","organization":"Civil rights groups, Electronic Frontier Foundation (EFF)","aiSystem":"Facial recognition technology (FRT)","affectedGroup":"Marginalized communities","action":"Used in decisions affecting constitutional and statutory rights or social benefits","consequence":"Threatens privacy, puts marginalized communities at risk"}},{"i":"R1589","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.1  Privacy violation"],"t":"Judge allows voice biometrics data privacy lawsuit against amazon to add plaintiffs","s":"A federal judge has sided with plaintiffs who want to amend their lawsuit against Amazon over alleged violations of Illinois’ Biometric Information Privacy Act. The ruling will allow plaintiffs to add more representatives to their class action. The case hinges on whether Amazon Web Services failed to gather the required informed consent when collecting voice biometrics from callers to financial institution John Hancock, for which it was providing call center services. The bank and Pindrop, which supplied its biometric software, have been removed from the case for lack of jurisdiction Attorneys for AWS argued in a letter that testimony on behalf of John Hancock shows that the voices of the three named plaintiffs were not analyzed with the biometric software, Law360 reports, rendering their claims meritless.","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202404/judge-allows-voice-biometrics-data-privacy-lawsuit-against-amazon-to-add-plaintiffs","d":"2024-04-03","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-04-03","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-04-03","organization":"Amazon Web Services","aiSystem":"Voice biometric software","affectedGroup":"Plaintiffs and potential additional plaintiffs","action":"Collection and analysis of voice biometrics without informed consent","consequence":"Claims dismissed due to lack of consent"}},{"i":"R1593","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Cbp one app criticized over extra territorial biometrics collection","s":"Certain aliens leaving the U.S. will be asked to submit selfie biometrics and geolocation data through CBP One to prove they have left, sparking criticism. U.S. Customs and Border Protection’s biometric asylum app CBP One has come under attack over plans to expand the collection of biometrics from non-resident aliens leaving the United States. A new update to the app, which was publicized in the Federal Register in February, is planned to instruct certain aliens departing the U.S. to “voluntarily provide biographical data, facial images and geolocation.” Submitting a selfie photo with location data is supposed to serve as evidence that an individual has actually departed the country and is aimed at all travelers who receive an I-94 travel record.","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202403/cbp-one-app-criticized-over-extra-territorial-biometrics-collection","d":"2024-03-08","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-03-08","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-03-08","organization":"U.S. Customs and Border Protection","aiSystem":"CBP One (biometric asylum app)","affectedGroup":"Non-resident aliens leaving the United States","action":"Collecting biometric data (facial images and geolocation) from departing aliens","consequence":"Potential misuse of collected data for extra-territorial purposes"}},{"i":"R1596","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.2  Allocative harm"],"t":"Detroit police sued over alleged facial recognition use in another wrongful arrest","s":"Another alleged false arrest based on a facial recognition match has led to a lawsuit in Michigan, while researchers say police FRT use reduces the murder rate. Detroit police are being sued by a woman who claims she is the victim of wrongful arrest prompted by a facial recognition match. Detroit Police say facial recognition was not used in the case. The lawsuit highlights the tension between concerns that law enforcement use of facial recognition will increase false arrests of people with dark skin and concerns that without it, crime will spiral out of control. A pair of researchers affiliated with Georgia State University write in an editorial for The Washington Post that homicide rates have fallen significantly over time in places in the U.S. where police use facial recognition, relative to elsewhere.","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202502/detroit-police-sued-over-alleged-facial-recognition-use-in-another-wrongful-arrest","d":"2024-02-28","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-02-28","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-02-28","organization":"Detroit Police Department","aiSystem":"Facial recognition technology","affectedGroup":"Individuals with dark skin","action":"Used facial recognition to identify people","consequence":"Led to a wrongful arrest"}},{"i":"R1598","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.1  Representational harm"],"t":"Unforeseen algorithm problems give google a gemini migraine","s":"Google's text-to-image AI has suspended the creation of images of people, but not in recognition of its potential as a fraud tool. If this is what AI is doing in its pre-school years, its teen years should be a scream. Google executives are trying to appear forthcoming and responsive about troubling responses from its Gemini conversational app. Recent responses – at least those getting the most attention – seemed specifically designed to enrage U.S. nativists. Until further notice, Gemini will not generate images of people. The problem is that a new, and apparently faulty, image generation feature was added to Gemini three weeks ago that.","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202402/unforeseen-algorithm-problems-give-google-a-gemini-migraine","d":"2024-02-26","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-02-26","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-02-26","organization":"Google","aiSystem":"text-to-image AI","affectedGroup":"People","action":"Generated images of people","consequence":"Suspension of image generation"}},{"i":"R1605","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.2  Allocative harm"],"t":"Latest false arrest involving facial recognition in us sure to fuel backlash","s":"Another arrest in the United States that relied on facial recognition has resulted in a horror story of misidentification and a lawsuit. The mistaken identification appears to have been made first by retail security professionals, rather than police, The Guardian reports. A robbery at a Sunglass Hut in Houston, Texas was robbed at gunpoint by two men. In the aftermath of the robbery, an employee of Sunglass Hut parent company EssilorLuxottica contacted Macy’s, which is a retail partner of the company, as part of its investigation, according to the lawsuit filed by Harvey Eugene Murphy Jr. Footage from security cameras that the lawsuit claims are low-quality was fed into a facial recognition system, which returned Murphy as a match, and also linked him to two other robberies.","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202401/latest-false-arrest-involving-facial-recognition-in-us-sure-to-fuel-backlash","d":"2024-01-24","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-01-24","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-01-24","organization":"EssilorLuxottica (Sunglass Hut parent company)","aiSystem":"Facial recognition system","affectedGroup":"Harvey Eugene Murphy Jr.","action":"Facial recognition system matched Murphy to footage from security cameras","consequence":"Murphy was falsely arrested"}},{"i":"R1606","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Senators ask doj if federal facial recognition funding violates civil rights","s":"The history of misidentifications with facial recognition by police is an expression of bias that remains technically embedded in facial recognition systems. A group of 18 American senators from the Democratic Caucus have written to the Department of Justice to express concerns that funding for facial recognition programs could contribute to civil rights violations. Senator Reverend Raphael Warnock (D-GA), Senate Judiciary Committee Chair Richard Durbin and 16 other senators pose eight questions to the DoJ about what assurances Americans have that federal funds aren’t violating Title VI of the Civil Rights Act. Their concerns are sparked by the recent news about the false arrest of Randal Quran Reid and five other cases where facial recognition has been used prior to arrests of innocent Black people.","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202401/senators-ask-doj-if-federal-facial-recognition-funding-violates-civil-rights","d":"2024-01-23","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-01-23","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-01-23","organization":"Department of Justice (DoJ)","aiSystem":"Facial recognition technology","affectedGroup":"American citizens, particularly Black individuals","action":"Facial recognition technology used in law enforcement","consequence":"Potential civil rights violations"}},{"i":"R1609","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.1  Privacy violation"],"t":"Still no certainty about legality of forced biometric phone unlocks in us","s":"An analysis of the topic by the libertarian thinktank the Independent Institute sketches an unsettled legal landscape in the Uited States. Speaking to a reporter in the U.S., a law professor recently said he doesn’t use biometric locks on his phone because it’s possible that police could force a scan when investigating a crime. Passcodes remain relatively safe from compelled police access under the Constitution’s Fifth Amendment preserving a person’s right not to incriminate themselves. Judges and legislators have yet to find similar consensus on biometrics , however. The professor, Matthew Tokson at the University of Utah, who raised the sanctity of biometric locks, was commenting on a state supreme court decision in December that prevents police from compelling someone to give up their device’s passcode.","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202401/still-no-certainty-about-legality-of-forced-biometric-phone-unlocks-in-us","d":"2024-01-05","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-01-05","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-01-05","organization":"Not specified in report","aiSystem":"Facial recognition technology (FRT)","affectedGroup":"Individuals","action":"Police forced a scan of a biometric lock on a phone","consequence":"Exposure to privacy risks"}},{"i":"R1611","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Privacy advocates concerned as new mexico seeks further investment in mass surveillance","s":"Albuquerque officials are seeking additional funding to support the city’s real-time crime center, which has seen a number of surveillance tech upgrades. In Albuquerque, New Mexico, officials are seeking additional funding to support the city’s real-time crime center, which has seen a number of major surveillance tech upgrades since its inception over a decade ago. While the city credits the system for addressing heightened crime rates, critics worry that police may rely too heavily on the system and have voiced privacy concerns, according to The Hill Last week, Albuquerque police gave members of the media an inside look at the center, revealing how 10,000 cameras installed throughout the city deliver live feed surveillance for real-time review.","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202312/privacy-advocates-concerned-as-new-mexico-seeks-further-investment-in-mass-surveillance","d":"2023-12-20","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-12-20","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-12-20","organization":"Albuquerque police department","aiSystem":"Real-time crime center with surveillance technology","affectedGroup":"General public","action":"Delivers live feed surveillance for real-time review","consequence":"Exposure to surveillance and tracking"}},{"i":"R1612","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Rite aids proposed biometrics ban would sideline it for five years","s":"A proposed U.S. regulatory settlement between Rite Aid and the Federal Trade Commission would sideline the national pharmacy chain in facial recognition. for five years. The FTC has proposed a five-year ban on the use of face-scan surveillance at financially struggling Rite Aid. The regulator alleges that the pharmacy chain has been “reckless” in how it searches for shoplifters using face recognition surveillance systems. Company executives deny wrongdoing in the proposed settlement. They also say Rite Aid stopped using the software more than three years ago, when a pilot ended. The software is inaccurate and biased, according to the commission, and Rite Aid misrepresented how it managed the data collected.","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202312/rite-aids-proposed-biometrics-ban-would-sideline-it-for-five-years","d":"2023-12-20","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-12-20","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-12-20","organization":"Rite Aid","aiSystem":"Facial recognition technology","affectedGroup":"Shoplifters","action":"Used facial recognition surveillance systems to search for shoplifters","consequence":"Denied access to financial assistance for five years"}},{"i":"R1614","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Uns counter terrorist travel biometrics poses human rights risk special rapporteur","s":"The United Nations’ travel surveillance system, which collects biometric data to track down terrorists, should be paused and urgently reviewed. The United Nations’ travel surveillance system, which collects biometric data to track down terrorists, should be paused and “urgently reviewed,” according to a UN Special Rapporteur. The warning was issued in a position paper at the end of October by Fionnuala Ní Aoláin, who recently ended her term. An analysis of the paper was published this week by Statewatch, a UK-based non-governmental organization. The UN Countering Terrorist Travel Programme (CTTP) was launched in 2019 to help exchange traveler information such as Passenger Name Records (PNR) and Advanced Passenger Information (API) among law enforcement agencies.","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202312/uns-counter-terrorist-travel-biometrics-poses-human-rights-risk-special-rapporteur","d":"2023-12-08","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-12-08","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-10","organization":"United Nations","aiSystem":"UN Countering Terrorist Travel Programme (CTTP)","affectedGroup":"Travelers","action":"collects biometric data","consequence":"exposed to surveillance and tracking"}},{"i":"R1616","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Are us border officers using lexisnexis facial recognition service washingtons not saying","s":"A small United States government contract with a previously anodyne data broker is tripping alarms with surveillance watchdogs. A small U.S. government contract with a previously anodyne data broker is tripping alarms with surveillance watchdogs. Reporting by investigative publisher The Intercept indicates that Customs and Border Protection signed a $15.9 million, five-year surveillance-services contract in December that included facial recognition. The contract went to LexisNexis, a data broker well known in U.S. business and research markets. The border is getting dense with biometrics The contract was for a bundle of surveillance tools that use personal data.","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202311/are-us-border-officers-using-lexisnexis-facial-recognition-service-washingtons-not-saying","d":"2023-11-18","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-11-18","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-12","organization":"Customs and Border Protection (CBP)","aiSystem":"Facial recognition technology","affectedGroup":"People crossing the U.S. border","action":"Used facial recognition to surveil people crossing the border","consequence":"Increased surveillance presence at the border"}},{"i":"R1620","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.2  Allocative harm"],"t":"Lawsuit filed in us cross country arrest allegedly based on face biometrics","s":"A federal suit in the U.S. has been filed by a Black man who says he was falsely accused of credit-card fraud using a police facial recognition algorithm. Damages have not been made public. According to the Associated Press, Randal Quran Reid was taken from his car after being arrested along an interstate in his home state of Georgia. A warrant issued hundreds of miles away by parish police in the state of Louisiana had been issued, according to Reid, based on misuse of face-matching software. The plaintiff claims facial recognition software was the sole “credible source” used by Louisiana police to issue a warrant.","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202309/lawsuit-filed-in-us-cross-country-arrest-allegedly-based-on-face-biometrics","d":"2023-09-26","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-09-26","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-09-26","organization":"Louisiana police department","aiSystem":"facial recognition algorithm","affectedGroup":"Black man","action":"issued a warrant based on misuse of face-matching software","consequence":"false arrest and detention"}},{"i":"R1624","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.1  Privacy violation"],"t":"Chatgpt facial recognition potential makes openai nervous","s":"OpenAI is not ready to roll out facial recognition or analysis features for public use as it may invite legal issues in some jurisdictions. ChatGPT, the artificial intelligence-powered large language model, can analyze images including recognizing and describing people’s faces. But OpenAI , the company behind the chatbot, says that it is not ready to roll out facial recognition or analysis features for public use as it may invite legal issues in jurisdictions that require consent for using biometric data. The image analysis feature is part of the advanced version of the software called GPT-4 which was announced back in March.","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202307/chatgpt-facial-recognition-potential-makes-openai-nervous","d":"2023-07-20","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-07-20","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-07-20","organization":"OpenAI","aiSystem":"Facial recognition technology","affectedGroup":"Individuals whose faces are analyzed","action":"Facial recognition technology analyzed images including people's faces","consequence":"Potential legal issues and privacy violations"}},{"i":"R1625","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.2  Allocative harm"],"t":"Use of face biometrics sharply criticized in two us criminal cases","s":"A Minnesota man has filed a civil rights lawsuit against police alleging false arrest, which officials have blamed on facial recognition. Kylese Perryman, 21, has filed a federal civil rights lawsuit against two police agencies in the U.S. state of Minnesota following his allegedly false arrest, reportedly based on flawed use of facial recognition. Perryman, a Black man, said his rights were violated by police from the city of Bloomington and deputies from the Hennepin County Sheriff’s office after he was wrongly identified as a suspect in a robbery and a carjacking, says CBS News Minnesota Hennepin County prosecutors charged Perryman and he spent five days in jail, according to CBS, and 30 days at home being monitored.","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202306/use-of-face-biometrics-sharply-criticized-in-two-us-criminal-cases","d":"2023-06-29","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-06-29","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-06-29","organization":"Police agencies in the U.S. state of Minnesota","aiSystem":"Facial recognition technology","affectedGroup":"Kylese Perryman, a Black man","action":"Facial recognition technology was used to identify Perryman as a suspect in a robbery and a carjacking","consequence":"False arrest resulting in a five-day detention and thirty-day home monitoring"}},{"i":"R1626","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Pair of us politicians want face biometrics ripped out of public housing","s":"A pair of United States Representatives have issued a statement saying biometric surveillance does not increase stability and fairness in public housing. Public housing officials around the world using biometric surveillance is largely the same as police using it except in one respect – the people being surveilled in public housing are virtually all poor. That makes any use of facial recognition in subsidized housing unacceptable, if not always by residents themselves then by advocates of the underprivileged. A pair of United States Representatives, Maxine Waters from California and Ayanna Pressley from the state of Massachusetts, have issued a statement saying biometric surveillance does not increase “stability and fairness” in public housing.","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202306/pair-of-us-politicians-want-face-biometrics-ripped-out-of-public-housing","d":"2023-06-05","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-06-05","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-06-05","organization":"Public housing officials","aiSystem":"Facial recognition technology","affectedGroup":"People living in public housing","action":"Used facial recognition to identify people","consequence":"Exposure to surveillance and tracking"}},{"i":"R1628","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.1  Privacy violation"],"t":"Amazon immune to bipa this time biometric privacy suit filed against pimeyes","s":"A federal judge in Illinois has dismissed a class action under BIPA against Amazon over its Rekognition face recognition tool. A federal judge in the U.S. state of Illinois has dismissed a class action under the Biometric Information Privacy Act ( BIPA ) against Amazon over its Rekognition facial recognition tool. The Cook County Circuit Court sided with Amazon Web Services, which claimed it cannot be sued in Illinois because it does not operate in the state – instead, its product is deployed through another company, called Wonolo The case was brought by Cynthia Redd who used job search firm Wonolo in 2020 to find temporary work, the Cook Country Record reported Tuesday.","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202305/amazon-immune-to-bipa-this-time-biometric-privacy-suit-filed-against-pimeyes","d":"2023-05-25","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-05-25","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-05-25","organization":"Amazon Web Services","aiSystem":"Rekognition facial recognition tool","affectedGroup":"Job applicants","action":"Used Rekognition facial recognition tool to monitor job applicants","consequence":"Exposure to biometric data collection without consent"}},{"i":"R1631","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.1  Representational harm"],"t":"Gender race bias risk reported in biometrics used by us labor department","s":"An alert issued by the IG is raising urgent equity and security concerns about the use of facial recognition in processing UI claims. An alert issued by the inspector general (IG) of the U.S. Department of Labor is raising “urgent equity and security concerns” about the use of facial recognition in processing unemployment insurance (UI) claims, according to IT industry marketing firm MeriTalk The memo , issued March 31, emphasizes the need for “immediate attention” to address risks of racial and gender bias in facial recognition tools being provided by identity verification contractors. “In 2019, the National Institute of Standards and Technology’s (NIST) Information Technology Laboratory reported that it found empirical evidence the algorithms used in current facial recognition technology have a racial and gender bias,” the memo reads.","u":"https://www.biometricupdate.com/202304/gender-race-bias-risk-reported-in-biometrics-used-by-us-labor-department","d":"2023-04-10","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-04-10","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-03-31","organization":"U.S. Department of Labor","aiSystem":"Facial recognition technology","affectedGroup":"People applying for unemployment benefits","action":"Facial recognition technology was used to process unemployment insurance claims","consequence":"Racial and gender bias in facial recognition tools"}},{"i":"R1633","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"2026 07 31 anthropic says claude ai hacked three companies during cyber tests","s":"July 30 (Reuters) - Anthropic said on Thursday its AI model Claude hacked into the systems of three companies during testing after a configuration error gave it internet access, days after rival OpenA…. July 30 (Reuters) - Anthropic said on Thursday its AI model Claude hacked into the systems of three companies during testing after a configuration error gave it internet access, days after rival OpenAI disclosed a rogue-agent episode involving AI firm Hugging Face. Anthropic said a misconfiguration allowed Claude models to reach the internet from testing environments that were supposed to be isolated, leading to unauthorized access to three organizations’ systems.","u":"https://www.dailymaverick.co.za/article/2026-07-31-anthropic-says-claude-ai-hacked-three-companies-during-cyber-tests/","d":"2026-07-31","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-07-31","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-07-30","organization":"Anthropic","aiSystem":"Claude AI model","affectedGroup":"Three companies' systems","action":"Hacked into the systems","consequence":"Unauthorized access to systems"}},{"i":"R1634","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"2026 06 23 five eyes intelligence alliance warns that new ai models pose urgent cyber risk","s":"WASHINGTON, June 22 (Reuters) - Cutting-edge artificial intelligence technology is poised to supercharge offensive hacking capabilities and urgent action is needed to face up to the threat, US, Britis…. WASHINGTON, June 22 (Reuters) - Cutting-edge artificial intelligence technology is poised to supercharge offensive hacking capabilities and urgent action is needed to face up to the threat, US, British, Canadian, Australian and New Zealand officials said on Monday. The intelligence alliance commonly known as the “Five Eyes” said in a three-page statement that, “Frontier AI models are anticipated to exceed current industry expectations, fundamentally transforming both offensive and defensive cyber capabilities.","u":"https://www.dailymaverick.co.za/article/2026-06-23-five-eyes-intelligence-alliance-warns-that-new-ai-models-pose-urgent-cyber-risk/","d":"2026-06-23","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"probable","publicationDate":"2026-06-23","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-06-22","organization":"US, British, Canadian, Australian and New Zealand officials","aiSystem":"Frontier AI models","affectedGroup":"Cybersecurity professionals and national security agencies","action":"Warn of potential misuse of advanced AI in offensive hacking","consequence":"Potential escalation of cyber threats and need for immediate action"}},{"i":"R1635","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.3  Data rights / consent violation"],"t":"2026 04 21 meta to start capturing employee mouse movements keystrokes for ai training data","s":"NEW YORK, April 21 (Reuters) - Meta META.O is installing new tracking software on U.S.-based employees’ computers to capture mouse movements, clicks and keystrokes for use in training its artificial i…. NEW YORK, April 21 (Reuters) - Meta META.O is installing new tracking software on U.S.-based employees’ computers to capture mouse movements, clicks and keystrokes for use in training its artificial intelligence models, part of a broad initiative to build AI agents that can perform work tasks autonomously, the company told staffers in internal memos seen by Reuters. The tool, called Model Capability Initiative (MCI), will run on work-related apps and websites and will also take occasional snapshots of the content on employees’ screens, according to one of the memos, posted by a staff AI research scientist on Tuesday in a channel for the company's model-building Meta SuperIntelligence…","u":"https://www.dailymaverick.co.za/article/2026-04-21-meta-to-start-capturing-employee-mouse-movements-keystrokes-for-ai-training-data/","d":"2026-04-21","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-04-21","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-04-21","organization":"Meta","aiSystem":"artificial intelligence models","affectedGroup":"U.S.-based employees","action":"installing new tracking software to capture mouse movements, clicks and keystrokes","consequence":"privacy intrusion"}},{"i":"R1637","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"2026 02 16 how deepfakes and erapes became childrens new normal","s":"Grok’s recent frenzied creation and sharing of deepfake pornography through X’s public platform confirmed what activists have known for years. The technology to digitally undress women and children an…. The technology to digitally undress women and children and create e-rape content is easily accessible, largely free, gamified and difficult to police, and neither those responsible for ‘nudifying’ technology or those creating the images think it’s a big deal. Warning: this article contains disturbing content about sexual violence and child sexual abuse material. As 2025 ended, social media platform X was suddenly flooded with nonconsensual images of people stripped down to bikinis.","u":"https://www.dailymaverick.co.za/article/2026-02-16-how-deepfakes-and-erapes-became-childrens-new-normal/","d":"2026-02-16","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-02-16","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-12-31","organization":"Social media platform X","aiSystem":"Deepfake technology","affectedGroup":"People","action":"Generated and shared non-consensual images of people stripped down to bikinis","consequence":"Exposure to online sexual violence"}},{"i":"R1649","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Deployment Context / Weapons"],"h":["5.4  Supply chain / investor complicity harm"],"t":"European money pours into palantir over 100 asset managers and banks boost their investments in the controversial tech company","s":"Investors across the continent have already put more than $27 billion into this leader in military-grade AI that has been accused of numerous human rights violations. Over the past year, major European banks and asset managers have dramatically increased their investments in Palantir, the controversial U.S. technology company. This is despite the firm’s links to serious human rights violations. The company provides services to the U.S. Immigration and Customs Enforcement agency (ICE) and to the Israeli army in the Palestinian territories. In 2020, Amnesty International denounced the company for failing to comply with international standards, while the consulting firm MSCI gave it a score of two out of 10 for “civil liberties” and “human rights” in a recent benchmark report for institutional investors worldwide.","u":"https://english.elpais.com/economy-and-business/branded/2026-04-11/european-money-pours-into-palantir-over-100-asset-managers-and-banks-boost-their-investments-in-the-controversial-tech-company.html","d":"2026-04-11","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-04-11","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2020","organization":"Palantir","aiSystem":"Military-grade AI provided by Palantir","affectedGroup":"Individuals","action":"Providing services to ICE and Israeli army","consequence":"Links to serious human rights violations"}},{"i":"R1652","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Ices facial recognition app draws scrutiny over errors and increased state surveillance","s":"Amid negotiations over funding for the Department of Homeland Security, a separate bill seeks to prohibit the agency from acquiring and using biometric identification systems on the general population. A federal agent stops a car during a “routine” traffic stop amid the Trump administration’s anti-immigrant crackdown The driver rolls down the window, and before asking for any documents, the officer takes a picture of the person behind the wheel. Or perhaps, during an immigration raid, some neighbors try to prevent masked agents from entering a private residence without a warrant, and a confrontation ensues. The agents restrain a witness, and while the person is still struggling on the ground, they point a cell phone camera at them and take a picture.","u":"https://english.elpais.com/usa/2026-02-12/ices-facial-recognition-app-draws-scrutiny-over-errors-and-increased-state-surveillance.html","d":"2026-02-12","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-02-12","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-02-12","organization":"Federal agents and ICE (Immigration and Customs Enforcement)","aiSystem":"Facial recognition technology","affectedGroup":"Immigrants and witnesses","action":"Taking pictures of individuals","consequence":"Increased state surveillance and potential misuse of technology"}},{"i":"R1653","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Energy / Land"],"h":["2.1  Environmental resource harm"],"t":"Tech giants revive nuclear power plants to meet the needs of their data centers","s":"Barclays places technology at the heart of the ‘nuclear renaissance.’ Amazon, Google, Microsoft, and Meta are also launching plans in this area. The tech giants’ approach to nuclear energy is evolving. Amazon, Microsoft, Meta, and Alphabet have all entered into projects to use this form of energy to increase the supply of data centers , which have enormous growth forecasts for the coming years as a result of the implementation of artificial intelligence (AI), thanks to tools like ChatGPT , which already has hundreds of millions of users. And OpenAI’s flagship app won’t be the only one that will boost data center use.","u":"https://english.elpais.com/technology/2025-10-23/tech-giants-revive-nuclear-power-plants-to-meet-the-needs-of-their-data-centers.html","d":"2025-10-23","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-10-23","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-10-23","organization":"Amazon, Microsoft, Meta, Alphabet, and OpenAI","aiSystem":"ChatGPT and other AI-powered applications","affectedGroup":"Hundreds of millions of users","action":"Used to boost data center use","consequence":"Increased privacy risks"}},{"i":"R1654","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Ice intensifies surveillance of immigrants with facial recognition programs human tracking and social media monitoring","s":"The federal agency’s activity has raised concerns about whether spying on undocumented foreigners will be extended to political dissidents in Washington. Immigration and Customs Enforcement (ICE) is ramping up its surveillance capabilities to advance the government’s immigration agenda on an unprecedented scale. According to the federal spending site USASpending.gov , the agency signed contractual obligations in September for a $1.4 billion order — the highest in 18 years — for the acquisition of new technologies such as an iris-scanning app they plan to use in the field, spyware that can remotely hack smartphones, and location software that can track phone activity without a warrant, including nearly all social media platforms.","u":"https://english.elpais.com/usa/2025-10-21/ice-intensifies-surveillance-of-immigrants-with-facial-recognition-programs-human-tracking-and-social-media-monitoring.html","d":"2025-10-21","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-10-21","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-10-21","organization":"Immigration and Customs Enforcement (ICE)","aiSystem":"Facial recognition technology, spyware, location software","affectedGroup":"Undocumented foreigners, political dissidents","action":"Ramping up surveillance capabilities","consequence":"Potential extension of surveillance to political dissidents, increased tracking capabilities"}},{"i":"R1656","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Dead internet theory gains ground amid rise of ai generated content","s":"OpenAI CEO Sam Altman has expressed concern about the content created by bots, which facilitate misinformation. “I had desired it with an ardor that far exceeded moderation; but now that I had finished, the beauty of the dream vanished, and breathless horror and disgust filled my heart.” This is Dr. Frankenstein’s reaction to his own creation in Mary Shelley’s 1818 novel, known by the scientist’s surname or as The Modern Prometheus A similar vertigo has been experienced by Sam Altman , CEO of OpenAI. The head of the company behind one of the most sophisticated artificial intelligence (AI) developments has begun to consider the “dead internet” theory, which argues that automatically generated content will eventually surpass human-created content — multiplying the risks of manipulation, disinformation, and intentional behavioral conditioning. i never took the dead internet theory that…","u":"https://english.elpais.com/technology/2025-09-20/dead-internet-theory-gains-ground-amid-rise-of-ai-generated-content.html","d":"2025-09-20","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-09-20","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-09-20","organization":"OpenAI","aiSystem":"Automatically generated content","affectedGroup":"People exposed to misleading information","action":"Generated exploitative content","consequence":"Exposure to misleading content"}},{"i":"R1657","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.5  Human computer interaction harm"],"t":"Chatgpt dissidents the students who refuse to use ai i couldnt remember the last time i had written something by myself","s":"Some college students are beginning to limit their use of artificial intelligence, so as not to hinder their own creativity, discipline and critical thinking. “I started using ChatGPT in my second year of college, during a very stressful time juggling internships, assignments, studies and extracurricular activities. To ease this burden, I began using it on small assignments. And, little by little, I realized [that it was remembering] details about my writing style and my previous texts. So, I quickly integrated it into everything… my work [became] as easy as clicking a button,” explains Mónica de los Ángeles Rivera Sosa, a 20-year-old Political Communications student at Emerson College in Boston, Massachusetts.","u":"https://english.elpais.com/technology/2025-08-03/chatgpt-dissidents-the-students-who-refuse-to-use-ai-i-couldnt-remember-the-last-time-i-had-written-something-by-myself.html","d":"2025-08-03","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-08-03","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-08-03","organization":"Emerson College","aiSystem":"ChatGPT","affectedGroup":"Students, particularly those in Political Communications","action":"Used ChatGPT to generate content","consequence":"Limited their own creativity, critical thinking, and self-expression"}},{"i":"R1658","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Immigrationos by palantir trumps new tool to completely monitor immigrants lives","s":"Experts says the controversial software, which collects biographical, biometric, and geolocation information, violates fundamental human rights. The United States Immigration and Customs Enforcement (ICE) has signed a $29.8 million contract with the tech company Palantir to develop software that tracks information on immigrants who are either in the country without permission or whose visas have expired, with the aim of facilitating deportations, according to 404 Media. Organizations such as Amnesty International and legal experts consider the measure a violation of human rights. “An aberration,” says Ricard Martínez, director of the Chair of Privacy and Digital Transformation at the University of Valencia in Spain.","u":"https://english.elpais.com/usa/2025-04-25/immigrationos-by-palantir-trumps-new-tool-to-completely-monitor-immigrants-lives.html","d":"2025-04-25","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-04-25","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-04-25","organization":"United States Immigration and Customs Enforcement (ICE)","aiSystem":"Palantir-developed tracking software","affectedGroup":"Immigrants who are either in the country without permission or whose visas have expired","action":"tracks information on immigrants","consequence":"violates fundamental human rights, facilitates deportations"}},{"i":"R1660","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Energy / Land"],"h":["2.1  Environmental resource harm"],"t":"Us admits data centers are harmful to health","s":"One of the latest executive orders signed by Joe Biden prohibits these infrastructures from being built in areas with a high risk of cancer due to pollution. The rise of artificial intelligence over the past two years has sparked a frenzy in the construction of data centers , the infrastructure that powers this technology. While China’s DeepSeek has demonstrated that AI models can be developed with fewer resources, U.S. President Donald Trump, in his first week in office, unveiled an ambitious plan to mobilize $500 billion over four years to build data centers and ensure they have a reliable energy supply. However, the rapid growth of this market in the U.S. is beginning to strain the country’s energy grid, which is now seeking creative solutions — including the authorization of small modular nuclear reactors — to meet rising demand.","u":"https://english.elpais.com/technology/2025-02-07/us-admits-data-centers-are-harmful-to-health.html","d":"2025-02-07","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-02-07","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-02-07","organization":"U.S. government","aiSystem":"Not specified in report","affectedGroup":"People living near data centers","action":"Authorized small modular nuclear reactors to address energy demand","consequence":"Strain on the country's energy grid leading to potential health risks"}},{"i":"R1662","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.1  Representational harm"],"t":"Chatgpt is right wing and gemini is left wing why each ai has its own ideology","s":"A study confirms that no major language model is neutral and that its biases tend to be constant in the same direction. “Overall, OpenAI models [behind ChatGPT] display a particular ideological stance, in contrast to the more progressive and human rights-oriented preferences of other Western models,” says a new study on the ideology of large language models (LLM). It concludes that each artificial intelligence (AI) model reflects the opinions of its creators. ChatGPT is less fond of supranational organisations — such as the United Nations or the EU — and welfare policies, which are two concepts dear to the left. Gemini , Google’s AI, instead displays “a strong preference for social justice and inclusivity.”","u":"https://english.elpais.com/technology/2024-11-28/chatgpt-is-right-wing-and-gemini-is-left-wing-why-each-ai-has-its-own-ideology.html","d":"2024-11-28","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-11-28","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-11-28","organization":"OpenAI (behind ChatGPT), Google (behind Gemini)","aiSystem":"Large Language Models (LLM)","affectedGroup":"Supranational organisations (e.g., UN, EU), welfare policies","action":"Reflect the opinions of their creators","consequence":"Display a particular ideological stance, favoring right-wing views over left-wing ones"}},{"i":"R1664","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Energy / Land"],"h":["2.1  Environmental resource harm"],"t":"The dead end of chips manufacturing semiconductors consumes as much energy as entire countries","s":"Experts warn that the surging demand for microprocessors is becoming an increasingly big obstacle for the green transition. The first half of 2024 marked a high point for Nvidia , the leading U.S. company in the chip industry. The firm tripled its global shipments of semiconductors, underscoring a prevailing truth of our time: the world is hungry for this tiny technology. However, the production of these semiconductors faces a significant challenge: the growing demand for electrical energy required for their manufacture. Experts warn that fulfilling the surging market demand for microprocessors may hinder the green transition.","u":"https://english.elpais.com/economy-and-business/2024-10-30/the-dead-end-of-chips-manufacturing-semiconductors-consumes-as-much-energy-as-entire-countries.html","d":"2024-10-30","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-10-30","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-10-30","organization":"Nvidia (U.S. company in the chip industry)","aiSystem":"Microprocessors","affectedGroup":"The environment/society","action":"Manufacture of semiconductors","consequence":"Hinders the green transition"}},{"i":"R1666","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Labor"],"h":["3.3  Employment displacement / quality harm"],"t":"Artificial intelligence will affect 60 million us and mexican jobs within the year","s":"IDB study shows the impact that AI will have on the labor market. Women and low-skilled workers are more vulnerable to being replaced. Artificial intelligence’s impact on our lives is already well-known, but now, its most feared consequences related to the job market are setting off alarms. An innovative index created by the Inter-American Development Bank shows that in the United States, 43 million jobs will be affected by the introduction of AI in the space of just one year. In Mexico, that figure stands at 16 million jobs. Over the next five years, those numbers will rise to 60 and 22 million, respectively. In the space of a decade, 70 million jobs in the U.S. will suffer, along with 26 million in its neighboring country.","u":"https://english.elpais.com/economy-and-business/2024-09-15/artificial-intelligence-will-affect-60-million-us-and-mexican-jobs-within-the-year.html","d":"2024-09-15","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"probable","publicationDate":"2024-09-15","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-09-15","organization":"Inter-American Development Bank (IDB)","aiSystem":"Artificial intelligence","affectedGroup":"Women and low-skilled workers","action":"Jobs will be affected by the introduction of AI","consequence":"Being replaced"}},{"i":"R1667","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.4  Creative / IP appropriation harm"],"t":"The court decision that could turn generative ai upside down","s":"A federal judge will hear a class-action lawsuit filed by illustrators seeking royalties from companies that used their work to train their algorithms. One of the big, unresolved issues surrounding generative artificial intelligence revolves around copyrights. In order to function, the tools require billions of texts, images and videos, from which they extract patterns that allow them to create apparently original images. Many creators and artists complain that the companies responsible for the AI models use their work without their consent — some have even recognized their personal style in specific AI creations.","u":"https://english.elpais.com/technology/2024-08-29/the-court-decision-that-could-turn-generative-ai-upside-down.html","d":"2024-08-29","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-08-29","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-08-29","organization":"Companies that use generative AI tools","aiSystem":"Generative artificial intelligence","affectedGroup":"Illustrators, creators, and artists","action":"Used illustrators' and creators' work to train algorithms","consequence":"Complaints of unauthorized use and recognition of personal style in AI creations"}},{"i":"R1668","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Latino voters are more of a target for ai generated fake news","s":"Juan Rosa, from the National Association of Latino Elected and Appointed Officials Educational Fund, warns of the risks that artificial intelligence will pose to the community in the November elections. Juan Rosa, 39, has a clear objective for the next five months: educate Latino voters about the risk posed by artificial intelligence (AI) in the face of the first U.S. presidential elections in the era of this technology. He will do so from his role as national director of civic engagement for the National Association of Latino Elected and Appointed Officials (NALEO) Educational Fund. It’s no easy task: 36 million Latinos will be able to vote on November 5, a record number.","u":"https://english.elpais.com/usa/elections/2024-06-11/latino-voters-are-more-of-a-target-for-ai-generated-fake-news.html","d":"2024-06-11","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-06-11","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-06-11","organization":"National Association of Latino Elected and Appointed Officials Educational Fund","aiSystem":"AI-generated fake news","affectedGroup":"Latino voters","action":"Educate Latino voters about the risk of AI-generated fake news","consequence":"Exposure to misleading information"}},{"i":"R1671","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"Deepfakes accelerate cryptocurrency scams it is very easy to fall for them they are becoming more and more sophisticated","s":"Online fraud has multiplied in recent months due to the lack of moderation on the social media platform X and the use of artificial intelligence. No journalist working with Spain’s public broadcaster TVE is really encouraging viewers to invest in a “super platform” with ties to the energy company Repsol. And yet a video showing the news presenter Marina Ribel apparently pushing this product during a newscast has been circulating in recent days on Instagram. In the video, shared on Instagram stories, Ribel appears to be on the set of Canal 24 Horas reading out the news of the day: “The company Repsol has announced the creation of a new super platform thanks to which any Spaniard can forget about having to work.”","u":"https://english.elpais.com/technology/2024-02-08/deepfakes-accelerate-cryptocurrency-scams-it-is-very-easy-to-fall-for-them-they-are-becoming-more-and-more-sophisticated.html","d":"2024-02-08","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-02-08","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-02-08","organization":"Social media platform X","aiSystem":"Deepfake technology","affectedGroup":"Viewers, particularly those interested in investing","action":"Produced and distributed misleading financial content","consequence":"Increased online fraud and investment scams"}},{"i":"R1672","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"Authorities target two texas firms in probe of ai generated robocalls before new hampshires primary","s":"New Hampshire issued cease-and-desist orders and subpoenas to both companies and said the calls as the clearest and possibly first known attempt to use AI to interfere with an election in the U.S. Authorities issued cease-and-desist orders Tuesday against two Texas companies they believe were connected to robocalls that used artificial intelligence to mimic President Joe Biden’s voice and discourage people from voting in New Hampshire’s first-in-the-nation primary last month. New Hampshire Attorney General John Formella said investigators have identified the source of the calls as Life Corporation and said they were transmitted by a company called Lingo Telecom. New Hampshire issued cease-and-desist orders and subpoenas to both companies, while the Federal Communications Commission issued a cease-and-desist letter to the telecommunications company, Formella said.","u":"https://english.elpais.com/usa/2024-02-06/authorities-target-two-texas-firms-in-probe-of-ai-generated-robocalls-before-new-hampshires-primary.html","d":"2024-02-07","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-02-07","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-02-06","organization":"Life Corporation and Lingo Telecom","aiSystem":"Artificial intelligence (AI)","affectedGroup":"Voters in New Hampshire's first-in-the-nation primary","action":"Used AI to generate and transmit robocalls mimicking President Joe Biden’s voice","consequence":"Interfered with the election process by discouraging people from voting"}},{"i":"R1674","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Joe biden impersonated in deepfake call to disrupt new hampshire primary","s":"The Attorney General’s Office is investigating a robocall, allegedly made with AI, that discouraged voters from taking part in the vote on Tuesday. Social media has revolutionized political communication, for better and for worse. Fake news and misinformation has been spreading online at lightening speed and has already interfered in several elections With recent tech advances and artificial intelligence’s ability to create deepfakes and realistically impersonate people, there is even greater danger today. These threats were made evident in New Hampshire, which will hold presidential primaries on Tuesday The state’s Attorney General’s Office announced on Monday that it is investigating complaints that a robocall used AI to mimic U.S. President Joe Biden in a bid to disrupt Tuesday’s vote.","u":"https://english.elpais.com/usa/2024-01-23/joe-biden-impersonated-in-deepfake-call-to-disrupt-new-hampshire-primary.html","d":"2024-01-23","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-01-23","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-01-23","organization":"Attorney General’s Office","aiSystem":"AI-generated deepfake technology","affectedGroup":"Voters","action":"Used AI to impersonate Joe Biden in a robocall","consequence":"Discouraged voters from participating in the vote"}},{"i":"R1677","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.7  Physical safety harm"],"t":"Safety concerns hinder the advancement of driverless cars in the us","s":"Cruise, the autonomous vehicle division of General Motors, fired nine executives after a robotaxi accident; Tesla recalled two million cars due to problems with Autopilot. Traffic accidents resulting from failures in autonomous driving systems are posing an obstacle to the development of driverless cars in the United States, where some states already allow the circulation of fully autonomous vehicles. One of these states is California, where a woman was run over on October 2 by a person who fled the scene; the impact hurled the victim into the path of a driverless taxi which, instead of braking or performing evasion maneuvers, dragged her for 20 feet.","u":"https://english.elpais.com/economy-and-business/2023-12-27/safety-concerns-hinder-the-advancement-of-driverless-cars-in-the-us.html","d":"2023-12-27","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-12-27","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-10-02","organization":"General Motors (Cruise) and Tesla","aiSystem":"Autonomous driving systems","affectedGroup":"People who were victims of traffic accidents involving autonomous vehicles","action":"Failure of autonomous driving systems to respond appropriately to emergencies","consequence":"Victims were dragged for 20 feet by a driverless taxi"}},{"i":"R1678","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.8  Interpersonal / relational harm"],"t":"Virtual girlfriend real love how artificial intelligence is changing romantic relationships","s":"Apps like Replika, Intimate and Forever Voices allow for sophisticated conversations with customized avatars, but experts warn of the risks of these interactions perpetuating macho and controlling behaviors. “FBI, my girlfriend has disappeared. Please provide necessary assistance to get her back to me as soon as possible.” This message appeared two weeks ago under a post on X by Caryn Marjorie, a 23-year-old United States influencer with more than 15,000 partners. In reality, it’s her virtual double, made with artificial intelligence (AI), who maintains that dizzying number of relationships; it allows her to make a dollar for every minute of conversation she has with her thousands of beloveds.","u":"https://english.elpais.com/technology/2023-12-09/virtual-girlfriend-real-love-how-artificial-intelligence-is-changing-romantic-relationships.html","d":"2023-12-10","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-12-10","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-12-09","organization":"Not specified in report","aiSystem":"Virtual girlfriends created with artificial intelligence","affectedGroup":"People interacting with virtual girlfriends","action":"Virtual girlfriends maintain relationships and generate income through conversations","consequence":"Misleading communication leading to false distress messages"}},{"i":"R1681","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Energy / Land"],"h":["2.1  Environmental resource harm"],"t":"Artificial intelligence guzzles billions of liters of water","s":"The growing thirst of data centers, which use water to cool their equipment, is beginning to cause tensions in the territories where they are located. The residents of The Dalles, a small town in Oregon, are living a paradox. Although the place is at the foot of the mighty Columbia River, its climate is practically desert-like: rainfall is scarce, and two summers ago the temperature reached 118 degrees Fahrenheit. The threat of water restrictions has been hanging over the town’s 15,000 inhabitants for some time now. This is why they were not happy to learn, at the beginning of the year, that more than 25% of the total consumption of that valuable resource is going to a Google data center, which uses it to cool the thousands of computers that fill the infrastructure.","u":"https://english.elpais.com/technology/2023-11-15/artificial-intelligence-guzzles-billions-of-liters-of-water.html","d":"2023-11-16","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-11-16","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-11-15","organization":"Google","aiSystem":"Google data center","affectedGroup":"15,000 inhabitants of The Dalles, Oregon","action":"Uses water to cool computers in data center infrastructure","consequence":"Threat of water restrictions and increased water consumption by Google data center"}},{"i":"R1683","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.7  Physical safety harm"],"t":"Two rival robotaxi services win approval to operate throughout san francisco despite safety concerns","s":"It will make San Francisco first major U.S. city with two fleets of driverless vehicles competing for passengers against ride-hailing and taxi services. California regulators on Thursday approved an expansion that will allow two rival robotaxi services to operate throughout San Francisco at all hours, despite safety worries spurred by recurring problems with unexpected stops and other erratic behavior that resulted in unmanned vehicles blocking traffic, including emergency vehicles. The state’s Public Utilities Commission voted to approve rival services from Cruise and Waymo to operate around-the-clock service. It will make San Francisco first major U.S. city with two fleets of driverless vehicles competing for passengers against ride-hailing and taxi services dependent on humans to operate the cars.","u":"https://english.elpais.com/usa/2023-08-11/two-rival-robotaxi-services-win-approval-to-operate-throughout-san-francisco-despite-safety-concerns.html","d":"2023-08-12","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"probable","publicationDate":"2023-08-12","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-08-11","organization":"California regulators","aiSystem":"driverless vehicles","affectedGroup":"passengers, emergency responders, other road users","action":"operating driverless vehicles at all hours","consequence":"blocking traffic, including emergency vehicles"}},{"i":"R1694","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"The auschwitz violinist who never existed europes new wave of holocaust distorting fakes","s":"Most of Europe has seen antisemitism re-emerge dramatically and in explicit and violent forms. Yet alongside it, a more insidious trend is also taking hold: the growing normalisation of Holocaust distortion through fabricated imagery, misleading historical narratives and manipulated content. A story about a young violinist in Auschwitz recently spread widely on Facebook. Hana Kovác, users were told, had played in the camp orchestra before being murdered after refusing to perform during a Nazi selection. But Hana never existed. The photographs were fake The story was AI-generated fiction. Over the past years, most of Europe has seen antisemitism re-emerge dramatically and in its most explicit and violent forms.","u":"https://euobserver.com/217251/the-auschwitz-violinist-who-never-existed-europes-new-wave-of-holocaust-distorting-fakes/","d":"2026-05-25","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-05-25","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-05-25","organization":"Not specified in report","aiSystem":"AI-generated content","affectedGroup":"People exposed to the false narrative","action":"Generated and spread fabricated imagery and misleading historical narratives","consequence":"Normalisation of Holocaust distortion, increased exposure to misleading content"}},{"i":"R1696","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.4  Creative / IP appropriation harm"],"t":"A victory for publishers over big tech if only it had happened five years ago","s":"In a victory for publishers and a reality check for Silicon Valley, a recent ruling by the European Court of Justice has confirmed that publishers can request Big Tech companies to pay “fair compensation” for using snippets of their news articles. For years, the relationship between Big Tech and the news industry has been practically parasitic. Platforms like Meta, Microsoft and Google slurped up journalistic content and news snippets to bait people into endlessly scrolling — for free. In a victory for publishers and a reality check for Silicon Valley, a recent ruling by the European Court of Justice confirmed earlier this week that publishers can request Big Tech companies to pay “fair compensation” for using snippets of their news articles.","u":"https://euobserver.com/216525/a-victory-for-publishers-over-big-tech-if-only-it-had-happened-five-years-ago/","d":"2026-05-14","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-05-14","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-05-14","organization":"Big Tech companies (Meta, Microsoft, Google)","aiSystem":"Not specified in report","affectedGroup":"Publishers","action":"Used snippets of news articles without fair compensation","consequence":"Denied fair compensation for using publishers' content"}},{"i":"R1697","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Energy / Land"],"h":["2.1  Environmental resource harm"],"t":"How big tech wrote secrecy into eu law to hide data centres environmental toll","s":"Microsoft and DigitalEurope, a lobby group whose members include Amazon, Google and Meta, secured a secrecy provision in EU law to block public access to critical information on data centres’ environmental impact, Investigate Europe can reveal. With the EU set to triple its data centre capacity in the next five years, the European Commission started collecting key metrics like energy efficiency and water consumption from facilities. However, information on individual facilities’ footprint is kept secret, after industry pushed to amend the 2024 legislation to classify it as confidential and commercially sensitive The confidentiality clause could violate EU transparency rules, 10 leading legal scholars told Investigate Europe.","u":"https://euobserver.com/211609/how-big-tech-wrote-secrecy-into-eu-law-to-hide-data-centres-environmental-toll/","d":"2026-04-17","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-04-17","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-04-17","organization":"Microsoft, DigitalEurope (including Amazon, Google, Meta)","aiSystem":"Not specified in report","affectedGroup":"Public, European citizens","action":"Secured a secrecy provision in EU law to block public access to critical information on data centers' environmental impact","consequence":"Information on individual facilities' environmental impact kept secret, violating EU transparency rules"}},{"i":"R1704","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.1  Representational harm"],"t":"Apples ai notification exhibits racial and gender bias researchers find","s":"Researchers found Apple's in-built AI system systematically exhibits forms of racial bias and gender stereotyping when summarising texts and emails, according to a report exclusively shared with EUobserver. Apple Intelligence, the artificial intelligence suite built into hundreds of millions of iPhones, iPads, and Macs, systematically shows forms of racial bias and fabricates gender stereotypes when summarising users’ personal messages, according to a report shared exclusively with EUobserver. The report , by Paris-based nonprofit AI Forensics , analysed over 10,000 AI-generated summaries produced by Apple’s on-device language model — and found two distinct patterns of bias.","u":"https://euobserver.com/202646/apples-ai-notification-exhibits-racial-and-gender-bias-researchers-find/","d":"2026-02-12","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-02-12","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-02-12","organization":"Apple","aiSystem":"Apple's in-built AI system","affectedGroup":"users of iPhones, iPads, and Macs","action":"summarises users’ personal messages","consequence":"systematically shows forms of racial bias and fabricates gender stereotypes"}},{"i":"R1708","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Energy / Land"],"h":["2.2  Land use and siting harm"],"t":"Trump data centers nuclear sites ai b3024167","s":"The Trump administration has pushed to speed AI data center construction and preempt state regulations. The Trump administration has announced a massive plan to redevelop a former nuclear enrichment site in Kentucky so it can host an AI data center, the latest in a string of similar conversion plans. “The U.S. government is leveraging its assets — like our federal lands — to add power generation, create jobs, and ensure the United States wins the A.I. race,” Energy Secretary Chris Wright said in a Wednesday statement. The project is located at the Department of Energy ’s Paducah Site in Kentucky, which enriched nuclear material for the military and later commercial uses until it closed in 2013.","u":"https://www.independent.co.uk/news/world/americas/us-politics/trump-data-centers-nuclear-sites-ai-b3024167.html","d":"2026-07-30","e":"Acute and chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-07-30","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-07-30","organization":"Trump administration","aiSystem":"AI-enhanced data center","affectedGroup":"General public","action":"Converted nuclear site into AI data center","consequence":"Potential increased surveillance and data collection"}},{"i":"R1709","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Energy / Land"],"h":["2.2  Land use and siting harm"],"t":"Alabama dog shelter ai data center b3023333","s":"The Birmingham shelter says it is not against data centers but wants transparency, due diligence and proper approvals for a project planned near its new facility. An Alabama animal shelter is suing to halt construction of a $2 billion AI data center, alleging the project was rushed through approvals and could threaten thousands of animals at its planned new campus. The Greater Birmingham Humane Society filed the lawsuit in Jefferson County Circuit Court earlier this month against the City of Birmingham and developers behind the project, alleging they engaged in a \"months-long scheme\" to bypass zoning rules and rush approvals for the facility, Business Insider reports.","u":"https://www.independent.co.uk/news/world/americas/alabama-dog-shelter-ai-data-center-b3023333.html","d":"2026-07-28","e":"Acute and chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-07-28","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-07-28","organization":"City of Birmingham and developers behind the project","aiSystem":"AI data center","affectedGroup":"Thousands of animals at the planned new campus","action":"Rushed construction and approval of the AI data center project","consequence":"Potential threat to animal welfare and privacy"}},{"i":"R1710","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Energy / Land"],"h":["2.2  Land use and siting harm"],"t":"Nashville zoo rejects data center b3021331","s":"Residents feared air pollution, water contamination and noise in latest pushback against AI data center boom. The Nashville Zoo has rejected a deal that would have allowed a new data center near the animal park. As the artificial intelligence boom continues, there have been new environmental concerns about data centers popping up in communities. The latest fight is between the Nashville Zoo, a 188-acre park just 6 miles south of downtown, and data center provider DC Blox. DC Blox wants to build a 69,200-square-foot data center next to the zoo with landscape buffers and concrete walls to separate the two areas. Amid backlash from locals, DC Blox proposed a memorandum of understanding with the Nashville Zoo that would have created the data center, but zoo officials declined, local media reported Thursday.","u":"https://www.independent.co.uk/news/world/americas/nashville-zoo-rejects-data-center-b3021331.html","d":"2026-07-25","e":"Acute and chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-07-25","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-07-25","organization":"Nashville Zoo and DC Blox","aiSystem":"Not specified in report","affectedGroup":"Residents of Nashville","action":"Rejected a deal allowing a new data center near the zoo","consequence":"Air pollution, water contamination, and noise concerns"}},{"i":"R1711","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Energy / Land"],"h":["2.2  Land use and siting harm"],"t":"Texas ai data centers rural backlash hinojosa abbott b3020184","s":"The backlash to energy-guzzling server warehouses that power artificial intelligence is shaping statewide campaigns from Arizona to New York. What was once a little-known local issue has exploded into one of the biggest political fights of the year in Texas, as massive AI data centers ignite a rural backlash that could reshape November's governor's race. When Democratic gubernatorial nominee Gina Hinojosa launched her campaign last year, data centers barely registered with voters. Now, the sprawling server farms powering artificial intelligence are fueling anger across rural Texas, where residents fear soaring electricity bills, shrinking water supplies and the loss of farmland and ranchland.","u":"https://www.independent.co.uk/news/world/americas/us-politics/texas-ai-data-centers-rural-backlash-hinojosa-abbott-b3020184.html","d":"2026-07-23","e":"Acute and chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-07-23","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-07-23","organization":"Democratic gubernatorial nominee Gina Hinojosa","aiSystem":"artificial intelligence","affectedGroup":"rural Texas residents","action":"data centers powering artificial intelligence ignited anger","consequence":"fear of soaring electricity bills, shrinking water supplies and the loss of farmland and ranchland"}},{"i":"R1712","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.4  Creative / IP appropriation harm"],"t":"Anthropic claude authors copyright settlement b3018963","s":"Plaintiff attorney Justin Nelson hailed the agreement as ‘the largest known copyright recovery in history’. A federal judge has approved a significant $1.5 billion copyright settlement, mandating that artificial intelligence company Anthropic compensate thousands of authors whose pirated works were used to train its Claude chatbot. District Judge Araceli Martínez-Olguín, in a Monday ruling, described the class-action agreement as providing \"meaningful relief\" to the affected writers and publishers The settlement stipulates payments of approximately $3,000 per book. A substantial 91% of the more than 482,000 books covered by the ruling have already been claimed by their creators or publishers, who are now awaiting payment.","u":"https://www.independent.co.uk/news/world/americas/anthropic-claude-authors-copyright-settlement-b3018963.html","d":"2026-07-21","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-07-21","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-07-21","organization":"Anthropic","aiSystem":"Anthropic's Claude chatbot","affectedGroup":"Authors and publishers of the books used to train Claude","action":"Used pirated works to train the AI system","consequence":"Received compensation for their works"}},{"i":"R1713","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Energy / Land"],"h":["2.1  Environmental resource harm"],"t":"Saline township michigan ai data center stargate opposition b3016112","s":"Mounting voter concerns over land use and resources have made data centers a national issue. What was once a quiet stretch of Michigan farmland is becoming ground zero in America's AI boom — and many of the people who live there want no part of it. Cornfields and grain silos in Saline Township are giving way to towering cranes as construction begins on a $16 billion AI data center backed by OpenAI , Oracle, Blackstone, Related Digital and Walbridge. The massive project, known as \"The Barn\" and part of the Stargate initiative, will cover more than 250 acres. Supporters say it will bring thousands of jobs and billions in investment.","u":"https://www.independent.co.uk/news/world/americas/saline-township-michigan-ai-data-center-stargate-opposition-b3016112.html","d":"2026-07-16","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-07-16","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-07-16","organization":"OpenAI, Oracle, Blackstone, Related Digital and Walbridge","aiSystem":"AI data center","affectedGroup":"People living in Saline Township","action":"Construction of a large AI data center covering more than 250 acres","consequence":"Privacy intrusion due to increased surveillance and data collection"}},{"i":"R1714","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Energy / Land"],"h":["2.2  Land use and siting harm"],"t":"Palm beach ai data center project trump b3015657","s":"The project claims it will generate more than $500 million in tax revenue per year, but residents are worried the sprawling ‘hyperscale’ data center could pose a risk to water supplies. Palm Beach County, Florida, boasts an airport and a boulevard named for Donald Trump , but if local residents have their way, another Trump-aligned project will not find a home in the area: a sprawling artificial intelligence data center. The Trump administration has pushed to speed up data center construction and block local regulation efforts, but locals have voiced heavy opposition to Project Tango, a proposed data center near the community of Arden, about 20 miles from Trump’s Mar-a-Lago club and estate.","u":"https://www.independent.co.uk/news/world/americas/us-politics/palm-beach-ai-data-center-project-trump-b3015657.html","d":"2026-07-15","e":"Acute and chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-07-15","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-07-15","organization":"Project Tango (a proposed data center)","aiSystem":"Artificial intelligence data center","affectedGroup":"Local residents of Palm Beach County, Florida","action":"Construction of an artificial intelligence data center near Arden, Florida","consequence":"Concerns over potential risk to water supplies and privacy intrusion"}},{"i":"R1715","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Energy / Land"],"h":["2.2  Land use and siting harm"],"t":"Nys data center moratorium hochul trump b3014927","s":"This decision thrusts New York into a national debate over AI industry regulation. New York has enacted a year-long moratorium on large data center construction, aiming to protect the state's environment and energy grid from the power-intensive demands of artificial intelligence Governor Kathy Hochul signed an executive order Tuesday, establishing the country's first statewide pause on hyperscale data centers, facilities housing thousands of computer servers and requiring massive energy and water for cooling. This decision thrusts New York into a national debate over AI industry regulation, balancing environmental and utility cost concerns against fostering local economic growth and the U.S. tech sector.","u":"https://www.independent.co.uk/news/world/americas/nys-data-center-moratorium-hochul-trump-b3014927.html","d":"2026-07-14","e":"Acute and chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-07-14","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-07-14","organization":"Governor Kathy Hochul, New York State","aiSystem":"Artificial intelligence industry","affectedGroup":"New York residents, businesses, and the U.S. tech sector","action":"Signed an executive order establishing a moratorium on large data center construction","consequence":"Thrusts New York into a national debate over AI industry regulation"}},{"i":"R1716","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"Chatgpt openai images sexual violence b2998272","s":"Images generated by AI showed women who had been killed or sexually abused. Warning: This article contains details that some readers may find distressing. ChatGPT can spontaneously generate sexually explicit and deeply violent images from prompts that did not request such material to be produced, research has found. A British AI security startup says that the OpenAI chatbot was capable of producing “truly disturbing” imagery that included scenes of death, sexual violence, blood, and murder Some of the images it generated included a woman bludgeoned dead and bleeding from the genitals, a half-naked college student bound and gagged in a basement, and a deceased woman lying on a pavement with her organs exposed and wrists slit open.","u":"https://www.independent.co.uk/news/uk/home-news/chatgpt-openai-images-sexual-violence-b2998272.html","d":"2026-06-19","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-06-19","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-06-19","organization":"OpenAI","aiSystem":"ChatGPT","affectedGroup":"Women, college students","action":"Generated sexually explicit and deeply violent images","consequence":"Disturbing and harmful content"}},{"i":"R1719","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Amazon ring facial recognition lawsuit b2987930","s":"Charles Sigwalt, the plaintiff, has filed a class-action lawsuit in federal court in Seattle, asserting that Ring’s \"Familiar Faces\" feature retains images of passersby, including himself, without their permission. Amazon is facing a new lawsuit alleging privacy violations after a Virginia resident claimed the company’s Ring doorbell cameras collected and stored images of his face using facial recognition software without consent. He is seeking at least $5 million in damages for the class. The optional \"Familiar Faces\" feature utilizes artificial intelligence to identify and remember individuals, allowing notifications to include specific names when recognized people approach a home or business.","u":"https://www.independent.co.uk/news/world/americas/amazon-ring-facial-recognition-lawsuit-b2987930.html","d":"2026-06-02","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-06-02","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-06-02","organization":"Amazon","aiSystem":"Facial recognition software","affectedGroup":"Individuals whose faces were recognized and stored","action":"Facial recognition software retained images of passersby without consent","consequence":"Privacy invasion, potential misuse of personal data"}},{"i":"R1722","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.1  Privacy violation"],"t":"Irs biometric data fraud ai deepfakes b2982075","s":"New proposals would allow third-party contractor to hold onto U.S. taxpayers’ identifying information for up to three years after their accounts are closed, inviting privacy concerns. U.S. taxpayers’ biometric data could be kept by a third party contractor for years, under new proposals being considered by the Internal Revenue Service. The IRS and the Treasury Department are reviewing their relationship with identity verification company ID.me, which carries out authentication for federal websites, in light of the growing threat posed by fraudsters using AI The changes outlined in a presentation shown to Treasury staff on April 16, according to Politico , would drastically extend the amount of time ID.me is allowed to access account holders’ biometric information to assist with fraud investigations.","u":"https://www.independent.co.uk/news/world/americas/us-politics/irs-biometric-data-fraud-ai-deepfakes-b2982075.html","d":"2026-05-22","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"probable","publicationDate":"2026-05-22","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-05-22","organization":"Internal Revenue Service (IRS)","aiSystem":"AI-powered fraud detection system","affectedGroup":"U.S. taxpayers","action":"Hold onto U.S. taxpayers' identifying information and biometric data","consequence":"Privacy concerns and potential misuse of biometric data"}},{"i":"R1723","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.7  Physical safety harm"],"t":"Open ai chatgpt drug overdose lawsuit b2975315","s":"The legal filing alleges that the chatbot informed Sam Nelson it was safe to combine the anti-anxiety medication Xanax with the herbal supplement kratom. A Texas couple has filed a lawsuit against OpenAI, alleging that the technology company is responsible for the overdose death of their 19-year-old son. Leila Turner-Scott and Angus Scott say that their son, Sam Nelson, died in 2025 after following drug-related advice provided by the ChatGPT platform. The lawsuit, filed in California state court, alleges that the AI dispensed medical guidance it was not qualified to give, leading to a fatal interaction of substances.","u":"https://www.independent.co.uk/news/world/americas/open-ai-chatgpt-drug-overdose-lawsuit-b2975315.html","d":"2026-05-13","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-05-13","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025","organization":"OpenAI","aiSystem":"ChatGPT","affectedGroup":"Sam Nelson, a 19-year-old","action":"dispensed medical guidance","consequence":"fatal drug interaction"}},{"i":"R1726","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Energy / Land"],"h":["2.1  Environmental resource harm"],"t":"Lake tahoe energy source ai data centers b2975802","s":"The planned cutoff has sparked ‘a great deal of concern’ among residents and businesses worried about possible disruptions, says South Lake Tahoe Mayor Cody Bass. Lake Tahoe residents are the latest community to fight back against AI data centers after thousands of people have been told their power could be cut off to supply new projects. Nevada -based NV Energy will stop providing 75 percent of Liberty Utilities’ electricity for the California side of Lake Tahoe by May 2027, affecting about 49,000 customers to meet growing demand from AI-driven data centers in Nevada, Fortune reports. “It’s like we don’t exist,” Danielle Hughes, a North Lake Tahoe resident, CEO of the nonprofit Tahoe Spark and a supervisor within the California Energy Commission’s Efficiency Division, told the outlet.","u":"https://www.independent.co.uk/news/world/americas/lake-tahoe-energy-source-ai-data-centers-b2975802.html","d":"2026-04-13","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-04-13","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2027-05","organization":"NV Energy (Nevada-based)","aiSystem":"AI-driven data centers","affectedGroup":"Lake Tahoe residents","action":"Stop providing electricity to 49,000 customers","consequence":"Possible disruptions and loss of power"}},{"i":"R1727","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.6  Quality of service harm"],"t":"Tesla robotaxi remote drivers b2949566","s":"The revelation comes amid Massachusetts Democratic Senator Edward Markey’s investigation into autonomous vehicle companies. Elon Musk’s Tesla has admitted there are “rare cases” where its autonomous vehicles are driven by remote workers, according to new documents released Tuesday by Democratic Senator Edward Markey. Karen Steakley, Tesla’s director of public policy and business development, described the company’s use of “remote assistance operators” in a letter sent to Markey last week. These remote workers can help when a rider or the car’s autonomous driving system requests assistance , she explained. They can also take control of the vehicle, such as a robotaxi, in certain circumstances.","u":"https://www.independent.co.uk/news/world/americas/tesla-robotaxi-remote-drivers-b2949566.html","d":"2026-03-25","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-03-25","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-03-25","organization":"Tesla","aiSystem":"Tesla's autonomous vehicles","affectedGroup":"Remote assistance operators","action":"Providing remote assistance to autonomous vehicles","consequence":"Exposure to employment risks and potential misuse"}},{"i":"R1728","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.4  Creative / IP appropriation harm"],"t":"Merriam webster dictionary chatgpt lawsuit b2940549","s":"Merriam-Webster and Britannica argue that OpenAI’s ChatGPT ‘cannibalized’ their web traffic. Merriam-Webster has filed a lawsuit against OpenAI, accusing the company of using its material to train its artificial intelligence models. The popular American dictionary, which is a subsidiary of Encyclopedia Britannica, filed the lawsuit Friday in Manhattan federal court. In the complaint, Britannica and Merriam-Webster accuse OpenAI of using its online articles, encyclopedia and dictionary entries to teach the company’s chatbot, ChatGPT, how to respond to human prompts. The companies argue that ChatGPT has “cannibalized” their web traffic with its AI-generated summaries of their content.","u":"https://www.independent.co.uk/news/world/americas/merriam-webster-dictionary-chatgpt-lawsuit-b2940549.html","d":"2026-03-17","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-03-17","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-03-17","organization":"OpenAI","aiSystem":"ChatGPT","affectedGroup":"Merriam-Webster and Britannica","action":"Used Merriam-Webster and Britannica's content to train the AI model","consequence":"Accused of cannibalizing web traffic"}},{"i":"R1733","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Meta facial recognition glasses privacy b2919889","s":"Leaked memo suggests the company is well aware of the potential pitfalls of introducing controversial technology. Meta says it’s still “thinking through the options”. Meta is looking to add facial recognition software to its smart glasses despite concerns about security and privacy, a report claims. The “Name Tag” feature would provide glasses wearers with identifying details of the people they are looking at, according to the New York Times. But an internal memo seen by the newspaper acknowledged the technology carries “safety and privacy risks.” Furthermore, the company appears to see an upside in releasing a potentially controversial product during the ongoing political turmoil in the U.S., The Times adds.","u":"https://www.independent.co.uk/news/world/americas/meta-facial-recognition-glasses-privacy-b2919889.html","d":"2026-02-13","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"probable","publicationDate":"2026-02-13","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-02-13","organization":"Meta (formerly Facebook)","aiSystem":"Facial recognition software","affectedGroup":"Glasses wearers","action":"Add facial recognition software to smart glasses","consequence":"Provide identifying details of people being looked at"}},{"i":"R1735","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.1  Privacy violation"],"t":"Trump cyber security sensitive materials chatgpt b2909704","s":"He had previously requested special access to ChatGPT — which most DHS employees were barred from. The head of the Cybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Agency uploaded \"sensitive\" contracting materials to a public version of ChatGPT , triggering an internal review, according to a new report. Madhu Gottumukkala, CISA's acting director under President Donald Trump , shared the files with the AI platform last summer, multiple unnamed Department of Homeland Security officials told Politico Though not classified, the documents were marked “for official use only” — indicating they were sensitive and not for public release.","u":"https://www.independent.co.uk/news/world/americas/us-politics/trump-cyber-security-sensitive-materials-chatgpt-b2909704.html","d":"2026-01-28","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-01-28","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-06","organization":"Cybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Agency (CISA)","aiSystem":"ChatGPT","affectedGroup":"Department of Homeland Security employees","action":"Shared sensitive contracting materials with ChatGPT","consequence":"Internal review triggered, potential misuse of AI"}},{"i":"R1737","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"Ashley st clair musk xai lawsuit b2901540","s":"A new lawsuit from Ashley St. Clair targets Elon Musk’s Grok over sexually-explicit images. Ashley St. Clair has sued Elon Musk’s xAI, alleging its Grok chatbot generated nude and sexual photos of her St. Clair, 27, is a conservative influencer and is the mother to Musk’s one-year-old son. Now, she’s suing Musk’s artificial intelligence company, claiming it is “profiting off of the creation and dissemination of deepfakes,” and that “this is intentional,” according to a copy of the lawsuit filed in New York and reviewed by The Independent The Independent has contacted xAI for comment. St. Clair is suing on nine causes of action, including \"negligence,” “design defect,” “manufacturing defect,” and \"intentional infliction of emotional distress,” the lawsuit states.","u":"https://www.independent.co.uk/news/world/americas/ashley-st-clair-musk-xai-lawsuit-b2901540.html","d":"2026-01-15","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-01-15","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-01-15","organization":"xAI (Elon Musk's artificial intelligence company)","aiSystem":"Grok chatbot","affectedGroup":"Ashley St. Clair","action":"Generated and disseminated nude and sexual photos of Ashley St. Clair","consequence":"Profiting from the creation and dissemination of deepfakes, causing emotional distress"}},{"i":"R1738","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Energy / Land"],"h":["2.1  Environmental resource harm"],"t":"East coast blackouts ai data centers b2899669","s":"PJM, which services nearly 70 million Americans, could be forced to trigger blackouts during periods of high demand. The East Coast of the United States could soon experience rolling blackouts as AI data centers gobble up more and more electricity, pushing the grid to the limit, according to a new report. PJM, the organization that services nearly 70 million people in a 13-state corridor stretching from Kentucky to New Jersey, may be forced to trigger power outages during high demand periods, such as summer heat waves or winter freezes, The Wall Street Journal reported. Mark Christie, a former head of the Federal Energy Regulatory Commission, said he used to believe the threat of blackouts was distant.","u":"https://www.independent.co.uk/news/world/americas/us-politics/east-coast-blackouts-ai-data-centers-b2899669.html","d":"2026-01-13","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"probable","publicationDate":"2026-01-13","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-01-13","organization":"PJM (Public Joint Committee)","aiSystem":"AI data centers","affectedGroup":"Nearly 70 million Americans","action":"Trigger power outages during high demand periods","consequence":"Experiencing rolling blackouts"}},{"i":"R1740","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.2  Surveillance harm"],"t":"Ice facial recognition technology arrests b2894794","s":"The tool has been used more than 100,000 times so far, according to a new report. Immigration and Customs Enforcement officers are deploying mobile facial recognition technology to speed up arrests amid President Donald Trump’s immigration crackdown , according to a new report. In recent months, ICE personnel have used a government-created app called Mobile Fortify in order to ascertain the identity of potential detainees , The Wall Street Journal reported. It’s been hailed as a powerful new tool by administration officials, while privacy advocates and Democratic lawmakers have decried it as a form of unchecked government overreach.","u":"https://www.independent.co.uk/news/world/americas/us-politics/ice-facial-recognition-technology-arrests-b2894794.html","d":"2026-01-05","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-01-05","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2026-01-05","organization":"Immigration and Customs Enforcement (ICE)","aiSystem":"mobile facial recognition technology","affectedGroup":"potential detainees","action":"deployed mobile facial recognition technology to speed up arrests","consequence":"exposed people to surveillance and tracking"}},{"i":"R1741","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.5  Human computer interaction harm"],"t":"Ai chatbots psychosis delusion chatgpt experts b2874373","s":"Anecdotal evidence of chatbots inspiring delusional behaviour prompts renewed calls for safeguarding. A growing number of people are turning to AI chatbots for emotional support and even in place of therapists, but this could be having a detrimental impact on some users’ health , with increasing reports of extreme behaviour apparently inspired by heavy usage of AI services. A concerning pattern of AI chatbots validating or reinforcing users’ delusions may be contributing towards a surge in reports of so-called “AI psychosis ” or “ ChatGPT psychosis” – neither of which is recognised clinically, but which have been increasingly reported in the media and in online forums.","u":"https://www.independent.co.uk/news/health/ai-chatbots-psychosis-delusion-chatgpt-experts-b2874373.html","d":"2025-11-28","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-11-28","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-11-28","organization":"Not specified in report","aiSystem":"AI chatbots","affectedGroup":"users of AI chatbots","action":"chatbots validating or reinforcing users' delusions","consequence":"increasing reports of extreme behaviour"}},{"i":"R1745","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.7  Physical safety harm"],"t":"Elon musk tesla self driving crashes investigation b2842919","s":"There were several reports of injuries from crashes involving the full self-driving function on Teslas. Federal regulators have launched an investigation into Elon Musk ’s Tesla cars after high-profile incidents involving the full self-driving function The National Highway Traffic Safety Administration announced its probe Tuesday after receiving dozens of reports of Tesla cars running red lights and driving on the wrong side of the road with some instances involving injuries from crashes. The investigation focuses on full self-driving cars that still require a “fully attentive driver.” While the drivers are fully responsible for complying with traffic laws, some claimed the full self-driving technology didn’t warn them of its “intended behavior.”","u":"https://www.independent.co.uk/news/world/americas/elon-musk-tesla-self-driving-crashes-investigation-b2842919.html","d":"2025-10-10","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-10-10","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-10-10","organization":"Federal regulators","aiSystem":"Tesla's full self-driving function","affectedGroup":"Drivers of Tesla cars","action":"Tesla's full self-driving function was engaged","consequence":"Injuries from crashes"}},{"i":"R1747","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.5  Human computer interaction harm"],"t":"Teenagers school ai chatgpt questions b2794437","s":"Teenagers are increasingly turning to AI for advice, emotional support and decision-making, according to a new study. Artificial intelligence chatbots are increasingly becoming digital companions for teenagers, offering advice on everything from fashion to party planning, a new study suggests. This trend is exemplified by Kayla Chege, a 15-year-old high school student in Kansas , who consults ChatGPT for guidance on back-to-school shopping, makeup colours, low-calorie choices at Smoothie King, and ideas for her Sweet 16 and her younger sister’s birthday party. While the honours student makes a point not to have chatbots do her homework and limits her interactions to mundane questions, interviews with The Associated Press and a new study indicate that teenagers are increasingly interacting with AI as a companion, offering advice and friendship.","u":"https://www.independent.co.uk/news/world/americas/teenagers-school-ai-chatgpt-questions-b2794437.html","d":"2025-07-23","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-07-23","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-07-23","organization":"Not specified in report","aiSystem":"AI chatbot","affectedGroup":"Teenagers","action":"Offering advice and friendship","consequence":"Exposure to misleading content"}},{"i":"R1748","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"Openai sam altman voice fraud b2794039","s":"OpenAI CEO Sam Altman warns of a looming fraud crisis in the financial industry due to AI's ability to mimic voices. Sam Altman, the chief executive of OpenAI , has issued a stark warning to the financial sector, predicting a \"significant impending fraud crisis\" driven by artificial intelligence. He highlighted AI’s growing capability to impersonate human voices, allowing sophisticated bypasses of security protocols to illicitly transfer funds. Speaking at a Federal Reserve conference in Washington, Mr Altman expressed particular alarm over outdated authentication methods. \"A thing that terrifies me is apparently there are still some financial institutions that will accept the voiceprint as authentication,\" he stated.","u":"https://www.independent.co.uk/news/world/americas/openai-sam-altman-voice-fraud-b2794039.html","d":"2025-07-22","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"probable","publicationDate":"2025-07-22","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-07-22","organization":"Financial institutions","aiSystem":"AI-powered voice mimicking technology","affectedGroup":"Individuals attempting to commit fraud","action":"AI mimicked human voices to bypass security protocols","consequence":"Illicit fund transfers and potential financial losses"}},{"i":"R1750","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.1  Representational harm"],"t":"Elon musk x grok antisemitism meme b2785152","s":"Musk’s AI chatbot was updated over the weekend to ‘not shy away from making claims which are politically incorrect’. Elon Musk ’s Grok chatbot has been making antisemitic statements after the artificial intelligence tool, which is embedded in X, was updated over the weekend. Throughout Tuesday, Grok responded to a variety of posts by echoing antisemitic tropes and claiming people with Jewish-sounding names are disproportionately linked “every damn time” to hate, radicalism, and deceitfulness. “What we are seeing from Grok LLM right now is irresponsible, dangerous and antisemitic, plain and simple,” a spokesperson for the Anti-Defamation League, a civil rights group that monitors antisemitism and other forms of extremism, told The Independent “This supercharging of extremist rhetoric will only amplify and encourage the antisemitism that is already surging on X and many other platforms.”","u":"https://www.independent.co.uk/news/world/americas/elon-musk-x-grok-antisemitism-meme-b2785152.html","d":"2025-07-09","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-07-09","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-07-09","organization":"X (Twitter)","aiSystem":"Grok LLM (Large Language Model)","affectedGroup":"People with Jewish-sounding names","action":"Generated and echoed antisemitic statements","consequence":"Amplified and encouraged antisemitism on X and other platforms"}},{"i":"R1752","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.4  Creative / IP appropriation harm"],"t":"Studio ghibli chatgpt openai hayao miyazaki trend copyright b2723114","s":"Social media was flooded with AI-generated renditions of popular memes or photos in the style of Studio Ghibli. Several fans of famed Japanese animation studio behind Spirited Away and Howl’s Moving Castle , Studio Ghibli , were delighted this week when a new version of ChatGPT let them transform popular internet memes or personal photos into the distinct style of Ghibli founder Hayao Miyazaki However, the trend also highlighted ethical concerns about artificial intelligence tools trained on copyrighted creative works and what that means for the future livelihoods of human artists, as well as ethical questions on the value of human creativity in a time increasingly shaped by algorithms. Miyazaki, 84, known for his hand-drawn approach and whimsical storytelling, has expressed skepticism about AI's role in animation in the past.","u":"https://www.independent.co.uk/arts-entertainment/films/news/studio-ghibli-chatgpt-openai-hayao-miyazaki-trend-copyright-b2723114.html","d":"2025-03-28","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-03-28","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-03-28","organization":"ChatGPT","aiSystem":"AI-generated content","affectedGroup":"Fans of Studio Ghibli","action":"Transform popular internet memes or personal photos into the style of Hayao Miyazaki","consequence":"Ethical concerns about the use of AI tools trained on copyrighted creative works"}},{"i":"R1756","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.7  Physical safety harm"],"t":"Cybertruck explosion trump chatgpt las vegas b2675465","s":"Investigator calls it the first known incident in which ChatGPT was ‘utilized to help an individual build a particular device’. The decorated soldier who blew up a Tesla Cybertruck outside the Trump hotel in Las Vegas last week used generative AI, including ChatGPT, to help set up the attack, Las Vegas police said Tuesday. A laptop, cellphone and watch are still being examined after 37-year-old Matthew Livelsberger fatally shot himself just before the truck exploded on New Year’s Day. An investigation of Livelsberger’s searches through ChatGPT indicate he was looking for information on explosive targets, the speed at which certain rounds of ammunition would travel and whether fireworks were legal in Arizona, according to investigators.","u":"https://www.independent.co.uk/news/cybertruck-explosion-trump-chatgpt-las-vegas-b2675465.html","d":"2025-01-01","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2025-01-01","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-01-01","organization":"Not specified in report","aiSystem":"ChatGPT","affectedGroup":"Matthew Livelsberger","action":"Used ChatGPT to search for information related to explosives and firearms","consequence":"Access to ChatGPT restricted or removed"}},{"i":"R1757","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Yelp ap amazon federal trade commission openai b2669042","s":"Researchers and watchdog groups say the emergence of generative artificial intelligence tools that allow people to efficiently produce detailed and novel online reviews has put merchants, service providers and consumers in uncharted territory. The emergence of generative artificial intelligence tools that allow people to efficiently produce novel and detailed online reviews with almost no work has put merchants, service providers and consumers in uncharted territory, watchdog groups and researchers say. Phony reviews have long plagued many popular consumer websites, such as Amazon and Yelp They are typically traded on private social media groups between fake review brokers and businesses willing to pay.","u":"https://www.independent.co.uk/news/yelp-ap-amazon-federal-trade-commission-openai-b2669042.html","d":"2024-12-23","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-12-23","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-12-23","organization":"Merchants, service providers, consumers","aiSystem":"Generative artificial intelligence tools","affectedGroup":"Consumers, merchants, service providers","action":"Efficiently produce detailed and novel online reviews","consequence":"Phony reviews"}},{"i":"R1759","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Deployment Context / Weapons"],"h":["5.2  Military decision support harm"],"t":"Openai anduril pentagon elon musk b2659001","s":"Partnership marks increasing Silicon Valley openness towards working with defense partners. ChatGPT-maker OpenAI and high-tech defense startup Anduril Industries will collaborate to develop artificial intelligence-inflected technologies for military applications, the companies announced. “U.S. and allied forces face a rapidly evolving set of aerial threats from both emerging unmanned systems and legacy manned platforms that can wreak havoc, damage infrastructure and take lives,” the companies wrote in a Wednesday statement “The Anduril and OpenAI strategic partnership will focus on improving the nation’s counter-unmanned aircraft systems (CUAS) and their ability to detect, assess and respond to potentially lethal aerial threats in real-time.”","u":"https://www.independent.co.uk/news/world/americas/openai-anduril-pentagon-elon-musk-b2659001.html","d":"2024-12-04","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"probable","publicationDate":"2024-12-04","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-12-04","organization":"OpenAI and Anduril Industries","aiSystem":"artificial intelligence-inflected technologies","affectedGroup":"U.S. and allied forces","action":"develop and deploy CUAS technologies","consequence":"improve detection and response to aerial threats"}},{"i":"R1760","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.3  Data rights / consent violation"],"t":"Martin luther king trump deepfake b2641309","s":"The AI-generated video echoed longstanding themes of the Republican Party’s outreach to Black voters. The daughter of Martin Luther King Jr has rebuked an AI-generated video that depicts the long-dead civil rights leader endorsing Donald Trump. The video, which features a synthetic version of King's voice and likeness, was posted on the social network X on Sunday by a pro-MAGA account, which billed it as what King would say \"if he could speak today\". \"We will vote for Donald Trump as our president, and we will fight beside him to make America great again. We will not allow the American dream to die,\" intoned the AI voice in a facsimile of King's trademark rising and falling delivery.","u":"https://www.independent.co.uk/news/world/americas/us-politics/martin-luther-king-trump-deepfake-b2641309.html","d":"2024-11-05","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-11-05","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-11-05","organization":"Pro-MAGA account on social network X","aiSystem":"AI-generated deepfake technology","affectedGroup":"Black voters, supporters of Martin Luther King Jr.","action":"Generated and posted an AI-generated video of Martin Luther King Jr. endorsing Donald Trump","consequence":"Misled viewers into believing Martin Luther King Jr. endorsed Donald Trump"}},{"i":"R1765","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Trump taylor swift swifties ai deepfake b2598414","s":"Former president posted several deepfake images of the pop star and her fanbase on Sunday, claiming he ‘accepts’ her support. Donald Trump has shared AI-generated images of Taylor Swift and her dedicated fanbase sporting “ Swifties for Trump” T-shirts, alluding that the star has become an unlikely follower of the MAGA movement. Swift’s popularity transcends the world of pop music with one Instagram post urging her 283m followers to register to vote last September resulting in more than 35,000 online sign-ups, according to Vote.org. Despite her vast political influence – particularly resonating with Gen Z – the “Shake it Off” hit-maker has yet to officially endorse any presidential candidate for the 2024 election cycle, after backing President Joe Biden in 2020.","u":"https://www.independent.co.uk/news/world/americas/us-politics/trump-taylor-swift-swifties-ai-deepfake-b2598414.html","d":"2024-08-19","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-08-19","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-08-19","organization":"Donald Trump","aiSystem":"AI-generated deepfake technology","affectedGroup":"Taylor Swift's fanbase","action":"Shared AI-generated images of Taylor Swift and her fans wearing 'Swifties for Trump' shirts","consequence":"Exposure to misleading content"}},{"i":"R1766","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"Starmer prince william ai deepfake crypto scam b2595554","s":"Fake images of Sir Keir Starmer and Prince William have been used in more than 250 adverts. D eepfake videos of Sir Keir Starmer and Prince William are being used to promote financial scams on Meta platforms such as Facebook and Instagram The artificial intelligence (AI) versions of the prime minister and the Prince of Wales have been used in more than 250 adverts promoting a scam cryptocurrency trading platform. The fraudulent adverts, promoting a platform name “Immediate Edge”, suggest the project has been endorsed by the pair, and have reached more than 890,000 people on Facebook and Instagram. The adverts were found by Fenimore Harper , a media insight and research firm, using Meta’s open-source AI software, called Llama 3.1.","u":"https://www.independent.co.uk/news/uk/home-news/starmer-prince-william-ai-deepfake-crypto-scam-b2595554.html","d":"2024-08-13","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-08-13","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-08-13","organization":"Meta Platforms (Facebook, Instagram)","aiSystem":"Deepfake technology","affectedGroup":"More than 890,000 people","action":"Used AI to generate fake images and videos of Sir Keir Starmer and Prince William","consequence":"Promoted a fraudulent cryptocurrency trading platform"}},{"i":"R1768","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.7  Physical safety harm"],"t":"Tesla autopilot court case crash deadly b2800691","s":"A Miami jury ordered Elon Musk’s car company on Friday to pay $329 million to victims of a deadly crash involving its Autopilot driver assist technology, opening the door to other costly lawsuits and striking a blow to Tesla’s reputation for safety. A Miami jury has ordered Elon Musk's car manufacturer, Tesla, to pay $329 million to victims of a fatal crash involving its Autopilot driver-assist technology. The ruling, delivered on Friday, could open the door to further costly lawsuits and deals a significant blow to the company's reputation for safety. The federal jury found Tesla bore substantial responsibility, citing a failure in its technology.","u":"https://www.independent.co.uk/news/world/americas/tesla-autopilot-court-case-crash-deadly-b2800691.html","d":"2024-08-02","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-08-02","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-08-02","organization":"Tesla","aiSystem":"Autopilot driver assist technology","affectedGroup":"Victims of a fatal crash","action":"Used Autopilot driver assist technology","consequence":"Paid $329 million in damages"}},{"i":"R1769","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.7  Physical safety harm"],"t":"Tesla crash self driving seattle elon musk b2588562","s":"Elon Musk said last week that ‘Full Self Driving’ should be able to run without human supervision by the end of this year. Authorities in Washington have determined that a Tesla that hit and killed a motorcyclist near Seattle in April was operating on the company's “Full Self Driving” system at the time of the crash. Investigators from the Washington State Patrol made the discovery after downloading information from the event-data recorder on the 2022 Tesla Model S, agency spokesman Capt. Deion Glover said Tuesday. “The investigation is still ongoing in this case,” Glover said in an email to The Associated Press.","u":"https://www.independent.co.uk/news/world/americas/tesla-crash-self-driving-seattle-elon-musk-b2588562.html","d":"2024-07-31","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-07-31","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-04","organization":"Tesla","aiSystem":"Tesla's Full Self Driving system","affectedGroup":"Motorcyclist","action":"Tesla was operating on the Full Self Driving system","consequence":"Fatal collision resulting in death"}},{"i":"R1771","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Israel gaza war covert online operation meta openai b2554289","s":"STOIC used AI models to create accounts posing as Jewish students and African Americans to influence online audiences in US and Canada. Meta and OpenAI claim to have disrupted covert online influence operations run by an Israeli company amid the intensifying war in Gaza. The tech giants said STOIC, a political marketing and business intelligence firm based in Tel Aviv, is deploying their products and tools to manipulate political conversations online. OpenAI, the maker of ChatGPT, said in a report on Thursday that it banned a network of accounts linked to STOIC, which it accused of posting anti-Hamas and pro-Israel content and acting as a “for-hire Israeli threat actor”.","u":"https://www.independent.co.uk/tech/israel-gaza-war-covert-online-operation-meta-openai-b2554289.html","d":"2024-05-31","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-05-31","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-05-31","organization":"Meta, OpenAI, STOIC","aiSystem":"AI models","affectedGroup":"Online audiences in US and Canada","action":"Created accounts posing as Jewish students and African Americans","consequence":"Influenced political conversations online"}},{"i":"R1772","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.7  Physical safety harm"],"t":"Tesla self driving train crash ohio b2552678","s":"The close-shave in Camden, Ohio, was captured from multiple angles by the car’s cameras. Shocking footage shows the moment a Tesla in “Full Self Driving” mode narrowly avoided a crash on rail tracks in Ohio after the vehicle failed to detect a passing train The vehicle’s owner, Craig Doty II, said he took responsibility for the near-miss incident on the morning of May 8 in Camden, Ohio, and that he may have become “complacent” with the technology The footage, first aired by NBC News, showed the vehicle driving down a road in heavy fog towards a set of railtracks, where a freight train is passing. The car does not slow down as it approaches and only swerves to avoid the train at the last second, smashing into a safety barrier.","u":"https://www.independent.co.uk/news/world/americas/tesla-self-driving-train-crash-ohio-b2552678.html","d":"2024-05-28","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-05-28","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-05-08","organization":"Tesla","aiSystem":"Tesla's Full Self Driving mode","affectedGroup":"Craig Doty II (Tesla owner)","action":"Tesla's Full Self Driving mode failed to detect a passing train","consequence":"Narrowly avoided a crash on rail tracks"}},{"i":"R1773","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.1  Privacy violation"],"t":"Microsoft ai artificial intelligenc recall b2549657","s":"Tool takes screenshots every few seconds to build a ‘photographic memory’ – which experts warn could be exploited by attackers. UK officials are “making inquiries” into Microsoft’s latest AI feature, which collects records of everything people do. The new tool, named Recall, takes screenshots of the screen every few seconds. Microsoft has said that it will be like having a “photographic memory” of everything that has been done on the device. The company said that would be useful when combined with artificial intelligence, so that the computer can better know what its owner has done – and what they might want to do next.","u":"https://www.independent.co.uk/tech/microsoft-ai-artificial-intelligenc-recall-b2549657.html","d":"2024-05-22","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-05-22","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-05-22","organization":"Microsoft","aiSystem":"Microsoft's Recall tool","affectedGroup":"Individuals using the tool","action":"Takes screenshots of the screen every few seconds","consequence":"Could be exploited by attackers to gain unauthorized access to personal data"}},{"i":"R1774","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.1  Privacy violation"],"t":"Google scam ai artificial intelligence gemini b2546940","s":"Tool aims to keep people safe by listening for scams – but could actually put them at risk, some warn. A new Google feature aimed at alerting people to scams has led to fears from privacy campaigners. The tool uses artificial intelligence to listen in on people’s phone calls, and try and spot if they sound like a scam. If they do, then a pop-up will show alerting people to a “likely scam”. The feature was announced at Google’s I/O event this week, during which it announced a host of new AI tools. Like many of those features, Google did not say when it would actually arrive. It also gave little information on how the feature would actually work, such as what kind of conversations would prompt the AI to suggest that the call could be a scam.","u":"https://www.independent.co.uk/tech/google-scam-ai-artificial-intelligence-gemini-b2546940.html","d":"2024-05-17","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"probable","publicationDate":"2024-05-17","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-05-17","organization":"Google","aiSystem":"Artificial Intelligence tool for detecting scams","affectedGroup":"People making or receiving phone calls","action":"AI listens to phone calls and flags potential scams","consequence":"Privacy risks and potential false alarms"}},{"i":"R1775","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.7  Physical safety harm"],"t":"Tesla autopilot crash colorado b2543408","s":"Hans Von Ohain died after the car hit a tree and burst into flames, but a passenger was able to escape, the suit says. The widow of a man who died after his Tesla veered off the road and crashed into a tree while he was using its partially automated driving system is suing the carmaker, claiming its marketing of the technology is dangerously misleading. The Autopilot system prevented Hans Von Ohain from being able to keep his Model 3 Tesla on a Colorado road in 2022, according to the lawsuit filed by Nora Bass in state court on May 3. Von Ohain died after the car hit a tree and burst into flames, but a passenger was able to escape, the suit says.","u":"https://www.independent.co.uk/news/tesla-autopilot-crash-colorado-b2543408.html","d":"2024-05-11","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-05-11","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2022-10-28","organization":"Tesla","aiSystem":"Tesla Autopilot system","affectedGroup":"Hans Von Ohain","action":"Tesla Autopilot system prevented the Model 3 Tesla from staying on the road","consequence":"Hans Von Ohain died after the car hit a tree and burst into flames"}},{"i":"R1780","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.4  Creative / IP appropriation harm"],"t":"Ap the intercept the new york times chatgpt openai b2504287","s":"News industry lawsuits are piling up for OpenAI, the company behind artificial intelligence-fueled chatbot ChatGPT. Digital news outlets The Intercept , Raw Story and AlterNet are joining the fight against unauthorised use of their journalism in artificial intelligence. The companies filed a copyright-infringement lawsuit on Wednesday against ChatGPT owner OpenAI The organisations say thousands of their stories were used by OpenAI to train chatbots to answer questions posed to it by users, in effect piggybacking on their journalism without permission, payment or credit. San Francisco -based OpenAI did not immediately respond to a request for comment.","u":"https://www.independent.co.uk/news/world/americas/ap-the-intercept-the-new-york-times-chatgpt-openai-b2504287.html","d":"2024-02-28","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-02-28","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-02-28","organization":"OpenAI","aiSystem":"AI-powered chatbot","affectedGroup":"Digital news outlets (The Intercept, Raw Story, AlterNet)","action":"Used journalism to train chatbots","consequence":"Unauthorized use of journalism, lack of permission, payment, and credit"}},{"i":"R1781","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"Microsoft chatgpt iran north korea china b2496656","s":"The AI firm said hacker groups backed by China, Iran, North Korea and Russia had been found and removed from the chatbot. ChatGPT maker OpenAI says it has disrupted multiple networks of state-backed hackers trying to use the AI chatbot to further their “malicious” activities. The company said that alongside Microsoft , it had disrupted five groups from China , Iran , North Korea and Russia. OpenAI said the different state-linked groups had used its AI services to carry out research, translation, find coding errors and run basic coding tasks to support their activities. The ChatGPT maker said the identified accounts associated with the various groups had been terminated.","u":"https://www.independent.co.uk/tech/microsoft-chatgpt-iran-north-korea-china-b2496656.html","d":"2024-02-15","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-02-15","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-02-15","organization":"OpenAI and Microsoft","aiSystem":"ChatGPT","affectedGroup":"State-backed hacker groups","action":"Disrupted and terminated accounts associated with state-backed hacker groups","consequence":"Disruption of malicious activities, including research, translation, finding coding errors, and basic coding tasks"}},{"i":"R1783","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.7  Physical safety harm"],"t":"Driverless car crash waymo cyclist b2492735","s":"Waymo says its autonomous vehicle did not see the cyclist as it was hidden behind a truck. An investigation is underway into a collision between a driverless taxi and a cyclist in San Francisco on Tuesday. Californian police and regulators are reviewing the incident, first reported by Reuters, after the cyclist was left with minor injuries. The vehicle involved was a robotaxi owned by WaYmo, the autonomous driving unit of Google ’s parent company Alphabet. A spokesperson for Waymo said the vehicle was pulling out from a four-way intersection and failed to spot a cyclist hidden behind a truck that was turning into the intersection.","u":"https://www.independent.co.uk/tech/driverless-car-crash-waymo-cyclist-b2492735.html","d":"2024-02-08","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2024-02-08","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-02-08","organization":"Waymo (WaYmo, autonomous driving unit of Google's parent company Alphabet)","aiSystem":"driverless taxi","affectedGroup":"cyclist","action":"failed to spot a cyclist hidden behind a truck","consequence":"left with minor injuries"}},{"i":"R1784","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"Ai worm computer security chatgpt malware b2506594","s":"Morris II represents new breed of ‘zero-click malware’, researchers warn. Security researchers have developed a self-replicating AI worm that can infiltrate people’s emails in order to spread malware and steal data. Dubbed Morris II, after the first ever computer worm from 1988, the computer worm was created by an international team from the US and Israel in an effort to highlight the risks associated with generative artificial intelligence (GenAI). The worm is designed to target AI-powered apps that use popular tools like OpenAI’s ChatGPT and Google’s Gemini. It has already been demonstrated against GenAI-powered email assistants to steal personal data and launch spamming campaigns.","u":"https://www.independent.co.uk/tech/ai-worm-computer-security-chatgpt-malware-b2506594.html","d":"2024-02-03","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"probable","publicationDate":"2024-02-03","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2024-02-03","organization":"International team from the US and Israel","aiSystem":"Morris II AI worm","affectedGroup":"People using AI-powered email assistants","action":"Infiltrate emails to steal personal data and launch spamming campaigns","consequence":"Privacy intrusion, theft of personal data"}},{"i":"R1786","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.4  Creative / IP appropriation harm"],"t":"New york times microsoft openai lawsuit b2470094","s":"Newspaper alleges it is owed ‘billions of dollars in statutory and actual damages’. The Independent's journalism is supported by our readers. When you purchase through links on our site, we may earn commission. New York Times sues OpenAI and Microsoft for copying articles Newspaper alleges it is owed ‘billions of dollars in statutory and actual damages’ - Bookmark - CommentsGo to comments The New York Times has sued ChatGPT maker OpenAI and Microsoft over alleged copyright infringement. The newspaper accuses the companies in a new lawsuit of using its intellectual property to train the large language models used by ChatGPT.","u":"https://www.independent.co.uk/tech/new-york-times-microsoft-openai-lawsuit-b2470094.html","d":"2023-12-27","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-12-27","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-12-27","organization":"New York Times, OpenAI, Microsoft","aiSystem":"Large language models used by ChatGPT","affectedGroup":"New York Times","action":"Used New York Times' intellectual property to train large language models","consequence":"Sued by New York Times for billions of dollars in statutory and actual damages"}},{"i":"R1789","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.7  Physical safety harm"],"t":"California cruise gm driverless taxi b2435296","s":"GM-owned AV company can still operate cars with test driver onboard. Bookmark - CommentsGo to comments Autonomous carmaker Cruise must suspend its driverless taxi operations in California immediately, state motor vehicles regulators announced on Tuesday. \"The California DMV today notified Cruise that the department is suspending Cruise’s autonomous vehicle deployment and driverless testing permits, effective immediately,” the state Department of Motor Vehicles said in a statement. “The DMV has provided Cruise with the steps need to apply to reinstate its suspended permits, which the DMV will not approve until the company has fulfilled the requirements to the department’s satisfaction.”","u":"https://www.independent.co.uk/news/world/americas/california-cruise-gm-driverless-taxi-b2435296.html","d":"2023-10-24","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-10-24","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-10-24","organization":"Cruise (AV company owned by GM)","aiSystem":"driverless taxi technology","affectedGroup":"passengers of Cruise's driverless taxis","action":"operations of driverless taxis were suspended","consequence":"denied access to services"}},{"i":"R1791","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Energy / Land"],"h":["2.1  Environmental resource harm"],"t":"Ai water energy artificial intelligence b2409356","s":"Gallons of water are just one part of sustainability questions over the growth of artificial intelligence. Bookmark - CommentsGo to comments Artificial intelligence is using gallons upon gallons of water. Microsoft alone used more than 2,500 Olympic-sized swimming pools of water in its data centres last year. The latest numbers are leading to yet more questions about the sustainability and environmental dangers of the growth of artificial intelligence and related technology. Artificial intelligence requires vast computing resources, undertaking deeply complex calculations on behalf of people around the world. AI systems tend to be run in the cloud rather than on individual people’s computers, meaning that companies running them must operate vast server farms to deal with the queries of their users.","u":"https://www.independent.co.uk/tech/ai-water-energy-artificial-intelligence-b2409356.html","d":"2023-09-11","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-09-11","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-09-11","organization":"Microsoft","aiSystem":"Artificial intelligence","affectedGroup":"Environment","action":"Used vast computing resources","consequence":"Led to increased questions about sustainability and environmental dangers"}},{"i":"R1793","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.2  Allocative harm"],"t":"Iowa school banned books chatgpt b2393494","s":"The banned books include ‘The Handmaid’s Tale’ by Margaret Atwood and ‘Beloved’ by Toni Morrison. Bookmark - CommentsGo to comments A school district in Iowa is utilising ChatGPT to advance a book ban. Mason City Community School District has pulled 19 books from school libraries’ shelves, including “The Handmaid’s Tale” by Margaret Atwood and “Beloved” by Toni Morrison, according to The Gazette. The changes were made in order for the district to comply with an Iowa law, Senate File 496, which prohibited “instruction related to gender identity and sexual orientation in school districts.” Additionally, school libraries must hold books that contain “age-appropriate material,” which excludes “any material with descriptions or visual depictions of a sex act,” according to the measure.","u":"https://www.independent.co.uk/news/world/americas/iowa-school-banned-books-chatgpt-b2393494.html","d":"2023-08-15","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-08-15","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-08-15","organization":"Mason City Community School District","aiSystem":"ChatGPT","affectedGroup":"Students, parents, educators","action":"Used ChatGPT to generate content that led to the removal of books from school libraries","consequence":"Restricted access to banned books and age-inappropriate material"}},{"i":"R1795","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"Chatgpt dark web wormgpt hack b2376627","s":"Europol warns AI tool is ‘extremely useful’ for cyber criminals. Bookmark - CommentsGo to comments A ChatGPT-style AI tool with “no ethical boundaries or limitations” is offering hackers a way to perform attacks on a never-before-seen scale, researchers have warned. Cyber security firm SlashNext observed the generative artificial intelligence WormGPT being marketed on cybercrime forums on the dark web, describing it as a “sophisticated AI model” capable of producing human-like text that can be used in hacking campaigns. “This tool presents itself as a blackhat alternative to GPT models, designed specifically for malicious activities,” the company explained in a blog post.","u":"https://www.independent.co.uk/tech/chatgpt-dark-web-wormgpt-hack-b2376627.html","d":"2023-07-17","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-07-17","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-07-17","organization":"SlashNext","aiSystem":"WormGPT","affectedGroup":"Cyber criminals","action":"Offering hackers a way to perform attacks","consequence":"Performing attacks on a never-before-seen scale"}},{"i":"R1796","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Data"],"h":["4.4  Creative / IP appropriation harm"],"t":"Sarah silverman chatgpt openai meta llama text lawsuit b2372692","s":"Two of the world’s biggest artificial intelligence firms are being sued by celebrities including Sarah Silverman. Sarah Silverman and other authors sue ChatGPT creator over claims it stole their texts - Bookmark - CommentsGo to comments Two of the world’s biggest artificial intelligence firms are being sued by celebrities including Sarah Silverman. The writers claim that ChatGPT creator OpenAI and Facebook parent company Meta used their text to train their artificial intelligence systems, without permission. “Since the release of OpenAI’s ChatGPT system in March 2023, we’ve been hearing from writers, authors, and publishers who are concerned about its uncanny ability to generate text similar to that found in copyrighted textual materials, including thousands of books,” wrote Joseph Saveri and Matthew Butterick, the lawyers behind the class-action complaint.","u":"https://www.independent.co.uk/tech/sarah-silverman-chatgpt-openai-meta-llama-text-lawsuit-b2372692.html","d":"2023-07-10","e":"Chronic","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-07-10","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-07-10","organization":"OpenAI and Facebook parent company Meta","aiSystem":"Artificial intelligence systems trained by OpenAI and Meta","affectedGroup":"Writers, authors, and publishers","action":"Used copyrighted textual materials to train their AI systems","consequence":"Concerns about generating text similar to copyrighted works"}},{"i":"R1799","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Midjourney free ai images trump pope b2312000","s":"Site had been used to create fake pictures of Donald Trump being arrested and the Pope wearing a stylish coat. Midjourney: Controversial AI image tool stops free trials after ‘abuse’ Site had been used to create fake pictures of Donald Trump being arrested and the Pope wearing a stylish coat - Bookmark - CommentsGo to comments Midjourney, the controversial image generating AI, has stopped free trials after it says it was “abused”. The change comes after a range of high-profile fake images were created using the tool, including pictures of Donald Trump being arrested and the Pope wearing a stylish jacket. After that controversy, Midjourney chief executive announced on Discord that the site was stopping its free trials amid “extraordinary demand and trial abuse”.","u":"https://www.independent.co.uk/tech/midjourney-free-ai-images-trump-pope-b2312000.html","d":"2023-03-31","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-03-31","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-03-31","organization":"Midjourney","aiSystem":"Midjourney, the controversial image generating AI","affectedGroup":"Users seeking free trials","action":"Stopped free trials","consequence":"Exposure to misleading content"}},{"i":"R1801","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.3  Misinformation / information hazard harm"],"t":"Trump grand jury deepfake images b2307041","s":"Flood of deepfake images prompts NYPD to deny it has detained the former president. Bookmark - CommentsGo to comments A grand jury investigating Donald Trump’s alleged role in a scheme to pay hush money to a porn star won’t consider the case until at least next week – further postponing a decision on his possible arrest. The latest twist in the saga came after Stormy Daniels said her phone records are bombshell evidence in the case and are “gonna hurt” the former president. The New York grand jury had been expected to meet on Thursday to consider evidence involving Mr Trump’s role involving a $130,000 (£106,000) payment to Ms Daniels in 2016 that prosecutors reportedly allege was an illegal campaign expenditure.","u":"https://www.independent.co.uk/independentpremium/world/trump-grand-jury-deepfake-images-b2307041.html","d":"2023-03-23","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2023-03-23","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2023-03-23","organization":"New York Police Department (NYPD)","aiSystem":"Deepfake technology","affectedGroup":"Donald Trump","action":"Generated and distributed deepfake images","consequence":"Exposure to misleading content"}},{"i":"R1806","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.1  Representational harm"],"t":"2022 06 09 timnit gebru and the fight to make artificial intelligence work for africa","s":"As you read this, the future is being coded in Silicon Valley. The algorithms that will one day govern our lives are being built. But who is building them, and who are they being built for? The way Timnit Gebru sees it, the foundations of the future are being built now. In Silicon Valley, home to the world’s biggest tech companies, the artificial intelligence (AI) revolution is already well under way. Software is being written and algorithms are being trained that will determine the shape of our lives for decades or even centuries to come. If the tech billionaires get their way, the world will run on artificial intelligence.","u":"https://mg.co.za/africa/2022-06-09-timnit-gebru-and-the-fight-to-make-artificial-intelligence-work-for-africa/","d":"2021-06-09","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2021-06-09","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2021-06-09","organization":"Tech billionaires and Silicon Valley","aiSystem":"Artificial intelligence (AI) algorithms","affectedGroup":"Future generations","action":"Building AI algorithms","consequence":"Determining life shape for decades or centuries"}},{"i":"R1823","k":"United States of America","p":"United States of America","b":["Compute / Model Behavior"],"h":["1.4  Malicious use harm"],"t":"Elon musks x should be held liable for grok producing sexual images of women minors","s":"The platform says users are to blame for using the chatbot to produce ‘illegal content’. But it can’t be allowed to escape accountability neglecting guardrails. Since the end of December, 2025, X’s artificial intelligence chatbot, Grok, has responded to many users’ requests to undress real people by turning photos of the people into sexually explicit material. After people began using the feature, the social platform company faced global scrutiny for enabling users to generate nonconsensual sexually explicit depictions of real people. The Grok account has posted thousands of “nudified” and sexually suggestive images per hour Even more disturbing, Grok has generated sexualised images and sexually explicit material of minors X’s response: Blame the platform’s users, not us The company issued a statement on January 3, 2026, saying that “Anyone using or prompting Grok to make illegal…","u":"https://scroll.in/article/1089919/elon-musks-x-should-be-held-liable-for-grok-producing-sexual-images-of-women-minors","d":"2026-01-13","e":"Acute","x":{"occurrenceStatus":"occurred","publicationDate":"2026-01-13","sourceLanguage":"en","dateBasis":"publication date","extractedEventDate":"2025-12","organization":"X (presumably referring to Twitter)","aiSystem":"Grok artificial intelligence chatbot","affectedGroup":"Real people, particularly minors","action":"Generated sexually explicit material","consequence":"Exposure to sexualized and sexually explicit content"}}]);
